4.5,"Colin Sullivan: Do you know what will happen if you shoot me?
Billy Costigan: Yeah, this bullet will go right through your fucking head! 

The story of cops and robbers in Brooklyn. Mixed company involves moles in both the cop's crew and the robber's crew, both having to play their sides in their favor, while hiding their identities.

This movie is so damn watchable, I just don't want to see Scorsese do anything but gangster movies. Everything pulls together so well. The movie is 2 and a half hours, but Scorsese's rapid-fire editing makes it move so smoothly. The cast is great, Nicholson of course. I've never had any problem with DiCaprio like others do, and he was kickin' ass again here. Damon played his part very well, trying to stay cool under a lot of pressure. The supporting cast was great as well. Martin Sheen, good as a the mood balanacer. Alec Baldwin as the comic relief. 

Ellerby: I'm gonna go have a smoke right now. You want a smoke? You don't smoke, do ya, right? What are ya, one of those fitness freaks, huh? Go fuck yourself. 

Mark Wahlberg was perfect as the guy who had to break everyone's balls. 

Dignam: I'm the guy who does his job. You must be the other guy.

Then you have the lone female, Vera Farmiga, who like Bracco in Goodfellas has to be a convincing backbone for some of the men, and she pulls it off. 

The movie itself is very tightly scripted, with great moments of tension and violence, but it is really a great big dark comedy like Scorsese's other gangster movies, filled with plenty of violence and cursing.

Kneecapped Bankrobber: [after being shot in the knee] I thought you were supposed to go into shock! I'm not in shock! It fuckin hurts! 

I had a great time watching this. Easily one of the best of 2006, and I am glad that Scorsese got his fucking directing oscar finally.

Ellerby: Go fuck yourself.
Dignam: I'm tired from fucking your wife.
Ellerby: How is your mother?
