4.5,"[img]http://www.popmatters.com/images/film_art/d/departed-poster.jpg[/img]
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]***1/2[/size][/font]
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]Many praises have been heaped upon Scorsese's latest effort and I am glad to report that all the commendations are well deserved.[/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]The acting, cinematography and script are simply brilliant. However, I believe it's the editing that really catapults this film to a masterpiece. Both sound and frame editing are astoudingly off beat which work to an amazing level of effectiveness. Within the first ten minutes, I knew I was in for a totally unique visual experience. Scorsese once again provides long time editor Thelma Schoonmaker with sparkling visuals that she meticulously translates into a brillant procession of violently virile images. [/size][/font]

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]The content is of the usual Scorsese persuasion: stunningly violent, highly provacative and largely masculine. It is not for the feint of heart.[/size][/font]

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]All the actors sizzle on screen. I expect there to be a fair share of Oscar noms coming from this one. It will actually be interesting to see who is picked because there are so many top notch performances. Some critics claim that Farmiga was the weak link, but I have to say that her character was not as finely written as the others. She mostly existed as a plot device. [/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]With that said, the only qualms I had about this film are in regard to the relationship between Farigma and DiCaprio's characters and a nagging problem with the ending. However, I will say one would be hard pressed to find a suitable finish to this meticulously crafted 2 1/2 hour movie.[/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]Overall, The Departed is yet another example of why Martin Scorsese is America's most competent, complex and critically acclaimed director. See it for the editing even if you're not a fan of the content.[/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-06","","RT","900603367"
4.5,"RATING (0 to ****): ***1/2
    
    By this point, we've probably heard raves of ""a Hollywood rarity: a sequel better than the original"". Martin Scorsese's ""The Departed"" brings the hope of pushing that a step further: ""a Hollywood rarity: a remake better than the original.""
    
    Before I get any further, I will say this: I was not heaping praise over ""Infernal Affairs"", the two reasons it being overly stylized, and making everything confusing by pretending to be more complex than really ever is. Scorsese's remake does what remakes are supposed to do: fix the errors of the original, in addition to placing something new on the table. (for the more cynical-minded... yes, also to heap cash on the same material, or ""Americanize"" it for all of us shallow US viewers who can't tolerate subtitles) It's a full * better.
    
    ...and if stars are all you care about, well, you have 'em- they ran out of A-list actors, so they invited Anthony Anderson to the party, too. There isn't a single performance that isn't great, or, at worst, does its job.
    
    Obviously, the best one being Jack Nicholson's. Along with the script, Nicholson's performance also sets the darkly funny tone placed throughout (Mark Wahlberg's and Alec Baldwin's respective characters are some form of comic relief), which keeps this movie from being unbearably brutal.
    
    As for the movie itself, we have an intriguing thriller which doesn't rush to the shoot-outs right away, but keeps the cat-and-mouse games up. Along with that we get to know the characters. The plot structure is similar to the original, with the biggest difference being the Buddhist overtones removed, and a consistent feature with US remakes of foreign-language films: a different ending. Scorsese makes sure that, at the very final shot, you leave with the appropriate look on your face.
    
    If I had a problem, it's that sometimes things move way too fast- fast cutting in some of the film's quieter scenes (including stuff we're presumably supposed to read), lest Thelma Schoonmaker makes us think the film is starting to move slow. Intrigue is intrigue, no matter what the pacing is. Making the film 2 minutes longer wouldn't have been a disaster.


MPAA: R (strong brutal violence, pervasive language, some strong sexual content and drug material)
