5.0,"With ""The Departed"" Martin Scorsese delivers a cautionary tale of how decisions we make in our youth become the driving force that navigates us through all of life. We first see the devil himself, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson), in the shadows. He tempts a young lad with something as simple as a bag full of groceries. Costello then grooms the boy to be his future contact inside the Boston Police Department's Special Investigative Unit. Meet Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) all grown up and a rising force to be reckoned with. Mr. Damon plays this role with a confidence that is intoxicating. When Sullivan walks into a room he commands attention and respect.
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/departed/gallery.php?page=9&size=lores&nopop=1""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_10.jpg[/img][/url] 
In contrast to Sullivan's bravado we have Billy Costigan's desperation. As a kid he spent weekdays talking one way with his mom and honing in on his South Boston accent every weekend with his dad. This duality leads him to be hand picked to go undercover as a mole in Costello's seedy crime world.
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/departed/gallery.php?page=11&size=lores&nopop=1""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_12.jpg[/img][/url]
Leonardo DiCaprio breathes such raw vulnerability into Billy that the audience can actually feel his pain. As Billy self-medicates and tries to stay one step ahead of Costello we are drawn deeper within this web of deceit that Scorsese has spun for us. Mr. DiCaprio holds his own scene for scene against Mr. Nicholson. As one might expect Jack is back and he inhabits Frank Costello with a hedonistic fever. With his animal print ties and robes you sense he loves the good life. Costello's motto is ""no-one gives it to you...you have to take it"" and take it he does. Nicholson can do things with his face that other actors only dream of. His Costello will keep you on the edge of your seat anticipating his next creepy move.
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/departed/gallery.php?page=23&size=lores&nopop=1""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_24.jpg[/img][/url]
There is a bit of a love triangle involving Colin, Madolyn (Vera Farmiga) and Billy. One of my favorite scenes in the film is when Billy sees a framed photograph, of Madolyn as a child, hanging low on the wall. He takes it off the hook to admire it and then hangs it on a higher peg, smiling. It is in that very moment the audience realizes Madolyn has chosen the wrong man to love.
 
The entire cast is simply terrific. I was especially impressed with Mark Wahlberg as Sergeant Dignam. Wahlberg has never been better and he nearly steals every scene he is in. The adapted screenplay by William Monahan is smart, brutal and funny. The editing is precise and the music well matched to the films tempo. The film is rated R for good reason. Graphic blood and extreme violence with sex and drugs thrown in for good measure. ""The Departed"" clocks in at 2 hours and 29 minutes and it utilizes every second to the fullest. You should see this on the big screen to fully appreciate it. Trust me when I tell you it is worth the price of admission and then some.
 
The word on the streets is that this film is the best Scorsese film since ""Goodfellas"". That 1990 flick was the coolest mob movie ever made. ""The Departed"" isn't cool...it is red hot! Look for Mr. Scorsese to finally take home that elusive Oscar.
 
Rating: A+","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-06","","RT","900882857"
