5.0,"[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/alec_baldwin/thedeparted1.jpg[/img] [img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson14.jpg[/img] [/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Oh boy oh boy oh boy, ladies and gentleman if you have plans for this weekend cancel them because seeing The Departed should take preference over everything this weekend, yes thats how awesome Martin Scorsese's latest film is. As you all know I've been chatting about this one for ages, and how excited I was I mean I love Scorsese's work and the cast for this film was to die for, but sometimes excitement can so easily be quashed by a film that doesnt live up to its hype, but do not fret because The Departed does not disappoint one bit, in fact its almost perfect. Its true what they say stick to what your best at, and make no excuses Scorsese is best at gangster films. I liked The Aviator, I liked Gangs of New York but when it comes down to it The Departed beats those two films by several miles. The Departed is loosely based on chinese thriller Infernal Affairs, which I myself have seen, for those preparing to see The Departed who havent seen Infernal Affairs dont, much of the characters fates are similar within both so I saw many of the death sequences coming, although some were wonderfully suprising, I knew what was going to occur so I honestly think your better of not seeing Infernal Affairs beforehand.[/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=#696969][/color][/size][/font] [/center]

[center][color=dimgray][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]The Departed is set in Boston where the streets are ridden with crime and ruled by top gangland boss Frank Costello (a wonderfully OTT Jack Nicholson). The state police force want his gone for good and send in young undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo Dicaprio) to inflitrate Costello and his gang. On the other side is Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) now a rising and well respected cop, Sullivan works for Costello and is feeding him information from inside the police force. Both Billy and Colin must lead double lifes and stay one ahead of each other so they can expose the other and save their own skin. [/size][/font][/color][/center]
[center][color=dimgray][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][/size][/font][/color] [/center]
[center][color=dimgray][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]The plot is much easier to follow when your watching the film, your entangled into both the criminal and police world and into Colin and Billy's difficult situations. Scorsese ever the master at presenting the criminal world manages to do the law abiding side effectively too, the representation of the police force feels very real and you believe in the determination from the cops to catch Costello. [/size][/font][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]This film is my first time of seeing a Scorsese film on the big screen, and wow does it trascend beauitfully on the big screen. I really do suggest you all see it at the cinema, just for the effect, the violence thats so brutal and comes in scenes so quickly really is so wonderfully filmed, it excites and enthralls the viewer constantly, this is possibly Scorsese's most entertaining picture to date. [/size][/font][/color][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=#696969][/color][/size][/font] [/center]


[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Within this film Scorsese has handpicked a number of phenomonal actors, all of whom act up a storm, no one ever misses a beat. The two leading men in Dicaprio and Damon bring a wonderful range to their performances, most notably DiCaprio who I can finally say has shaken of his baby faced image, and what a film to do it in. Though if truth be told its the supporting actors who remain with the viewer long after the credits roll. Nicholson's energy and caricature mannerisms are perfect for the evil kingpin Frank Costello, the man's so unpredictable and wild you sit there on the edge of your seat unprepared for what his next move might be, your almost feeling the same vunerability and worry that Dicaprios character experiences. [/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center] [/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Nicholson shouts, he makes threats, he constantly looks capable of doing anything yet at the same time hes appealling and charming as Costello you can almost understand why people work for him and why others are drawn into his life of crime. Come Oscar time you just know Nicholson will be gaining another nomination. Nicholsons well supported by a terrific Ray Winstone who does the tough guy image better than anyone. On the right side of the law you have Alec Baldwin, Mark Walhberg and Martin Sheen all whom offer plenty to the film and story itself. Baldwin is an absolute hoot whether hes bantering with Walhberg, constantly swearing or punching fellow cops because they screwed up the operation, he constantly entertains as does Mark Walhberg who suprised me most of all, his foul mouthed Sergeant Digman is one of the many memorable characters in the film- when ever him and Baldwin are on screen together they take you away from all the violence and offer some lighthearted entertainment.[/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=#696969][/color][/size][/font] [/center]

[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Infernal Affairs was an entertaining thriller, but The Departed is close to being a real masterpiece, it really is what I would define as a typical Martin Scorsese film (complete with awesome soundtrack), which is of course what I always want. Many films this year have disappointed but it has to be said The Departed really does live up to expectations, in fact hell its just become my no.1 film of the year. The Departed is not only compelling its also highly enjoyable, it never has a dull moment in its 2 hours and 30 minutes duration, the time length is fully justified. What Scorsese is able to do is grab the viewer from the very of, having Nicholson come on screen delivering a long monologue in the opening scene helps, from here on in you know your in for a treat. [/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray][/color][/size][/font] [/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]S[/color][/size][/font][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]corsese's film has many moments of tension, Dicaprio and Nicholson on screen provide much of that, you constantly wonder whether Nicholson's Costello has found out that DiCaprio's Costigan is a rat. But if I was to say it was full of tension and violence, I'd be lying their are romantic moments, which never thankfully drag and moments of laugh out loud comedy (Baldwin and Walhberg's scenes, as well as Nicholson wearing a strapped on dildo at a cinema). So what can I say, yeah I know this was a long review right? well thats because the film justifies such an in-depth review, I loved every minute of it, if you love Scorseses crime films, go see now, what the hell just go see it, I guarnatee you wont be let down, its the best film of the year bar none. Just one question remains will it finally win Scorsese a much deserved Oscar? I bloody well hope so.[/color][/size][/font][/center]

