5.0,"The lights went down at one minute till midnight on Thursday evening. I expected a good movie, and got a masterpiece in return. ""The Departed"" is set in South Boston, where the state police force is waging war on organized crime. Young undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is assigned to infiltrate the mob syndicate run by gangland chief Costello (Jack Nicholson). While Billy is quickly gaining Costellos confidence, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), a hardened young criminal who has infiltrated the police department as an informer for the syndicate, is rising to a position of power in the Special Investigation Unit. Each man becomes deeply consumed by his double life, gathering information about the plans and counter-plans of the operations he has penetrated. But when it becomes clear to both the gangsters and the police that theres a mole in their midst, Billy and Colin are suddenly in danger of being caught and exposed to the enemy  and each must race to uncover the identity of the other man in time to save himself. ""The Departed"" is a film that I new would be a pleasure to watch. It has an all-star cast, a legendary director, and an intriguing plot. However, I did not expect to watch the best film of the year so early in the year. More than likely, I will not watch a movie this year that can even be as potent as this one is. At a running time of two hours, and thirty minutes, the picture never drags nor bores. It is very safe to say that this film is entertaining as Hell! Leonardo DiCaprio, coming off two other films by Scorsese, gives his best performance of his last three films. Maybe it's time for him to get his over due Oscar (""The Aviator"" should have been his film). Matt Damon gives a superb performance as the man you just love to hate. Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, and Alec Baldwin enter top notch performances (most notably Wahlberg, who simply glows in this film). The acting in this film is overall solid. However, it is Jack Nicholson who steals the show. If he does not get nominated for his pitch perfect performance, then there is no God (meaning, he will be nominated). It's been a while since Martin Scorsese has done a film about gangster, and I'm glad to say this is his best film since 1990s ""Goodfellas."" If he does not win an Oscar, which is VERY over due, then he might as well just give up. ""The Departed"" is a crime drama that never fails to offer thrills, laughs, and excitement. It features a superb cast, a fantastic plot, and it's a truimphant return for Scorsese. Welcome back, Marty.
 
 yay, or nay? Yeaya!!!
 
 Oscar bait? It's an across the board stunner, so it's VERY likely.
 
 FINAL RATING: 10/10","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-06","","RT","900828715"
