5.0,"Back in February 2004 (on it's UK release), I saw the very film that was the catalyst for Scorsese's latest offering: [i]Infernal Affairs[/i].
 
This was a film set in Hong Kong, and was (still is) a top-notch offering and must be in my opinion one of the finest Asian films ever released.
 
When I first heard that the film was going to be remade, albeit in the USA, I had rather strong concerns that the film was going to stray away from the taut, riveting and intense storyline and character development that made Infernal Affairs a true joy to watch.
 
However, my buzz on the remake's upcoming release grew and I have to say, I am not disappointed in the slightest.
 
Instead of glorifying and 'pumping up' the film, [i]The Departed[/i] remains virtually faithful to [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], in terms of it's storyline, the character development, and as well as some very brutal and bloody action, the film has more twists than the Stelvio Pass, and somewhat cerebral.
 
Instead of offering a dud, which I initially feared, Marty has truly delivered the goods and surely he must be in contention for a golden boy this time round.  It's high time he had his turn for glory.
 
Bravo! :fresh: :up: :up:","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-07","","RT","900637529"
5.0,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/custom/16/1159016.jpg[/img]

 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Colin Sullivan grew up on the mean streets of Boston under the tutelage of Frank Costello.  Colin was always a smart and clever kid.  Colin scores high on his SATs, is an ace student at the police academy, and is quickly promoted to detective within the Boston police force.  William (Billy) Costigan was raised in a criminal family; however, his father had strict morals and never allowed him to follow in his relatives foot steps.  His parents pass away leaving him to face the realities of his family name on his own.  The Police Force uses his family members past to manipulate Costigans feelings towards becoming an undercover police officer.  Costigan does everything in his power to discover who Costello has working for him within the police force, while Colin does everything he can to discover who from the police force has infiltrated Costellos organization.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]I dont want to be a product of my environment.  I want my environment to be a product of me.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Martin Scorsese, director of the Aviator, Gangs of New York, Casino, Cape Fear,[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Goodfellas, Taxi Driver, and Raging Bull, delivers the Departed.  The Departed is a long film, about 150 minutes; however, the intricate storyline requires every second.  The Departed quickly establishes itself as 2006s best film.  From the narration, to the dynamic charters, to the wonderful storyline, everything is perfect.  Scorsese utilizes a magnificent cast that includes Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, Leonardo Dicaprio, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Anthony Anderson, Alec Baldwin, and Vera Farmiga.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Honesty is not synonymous with truth.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]The paradox reference, the shooting on the beach, the facing the gun conversation, the black guy in Boston reference, the fire fighter references, the style reference, the mention of invading Poland, Costigan getting into fight at the bar, the mushroom analogy, the beating delivered in Baboos store, Costigan beat with a boot, the John Lennon conversation, the do they cry conversation, the theyre a cop game, the fitness freaks reference, Alec Baldwin beating up the guy who set-up the camera, the in shock reference, the coke scene, the marriage reference, the I own this place scene, the stabbing in the alley, the rat conversation/references, the dont laugh scene, Mr. French going out with a bang, the shut the fuck up scene on the roof, the loose ends tied up at the elevator scene, and the final rat in the closing scene were amongst the better portions of the film.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]What are you drinking?[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Cranberry juice.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]What is it, youre period?[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]The Departed has an array of complex, complicated, and intricate characters.  The storyline has all the same qualities.  Scorsese does a wonderful job of taking a genre that is primarily displayed with an Italian theme in New York City and displaying it with an Irish theme in Boston.  The cast is as big named as they come; however, the Departed is delivered in a better fashion then most films based primarily of an A-list cast.  If you see one film of 2006, the Departed is it.[/color][/size][/font]
