4.5,"[center][u][size=5][font=Century Gothic]The Departed Review
[size=2]
[/size][/font][/size][/u][size=5][font=Century Gothic][size=2][b]Rating: 9.0/10
Rated: R
Starring: Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, Mark Whalberg, and Leonardo DiCaprio
Genre: Drama/Action

[/b]I was probably the only person on RT that wasn't interested in seeing this film, probably because the trailers didn't attract me to it at all.  So, when I entered the theatre... I wasn't all hyped up at all or really have any expectations, but as the movie rolled along I soon became attached to the chaaracters and stories in this film, which came as a big surprise to me!  The storyline is ridiculously drawn-out and hard to explain, but by the end of the movie you'll understand everything that has happened.  The acting, like in any Scorsese picture contains Oscar-caliber performances by some of the biggest names in Hollywood.  Leonardo and Damon, were my favorite in this film... probably because they were the two characters you got the most emotionally involved with in their very ironic situation.  Sheen and Whalberg were both fantastic, but they didn't get enough screen time.  My only beef with this film had to be the deperessing ending, and I think Scorsece could've come up with a more staisfying and logical one.  I was thinking that this film may turn out like ""Miami Vice"" with its terrible storyline and way too long running time, but ""The Departed"" is nothing of the sort... its a cop thriller that might not be in the same leauge as some of Scorsece's other pictures, but still goes to show you that the man has one helluva talent!

[b]Concensus: [/b]A real nail-biter, with a fantastic cast that doesn't dissapoint, and some twists and turns that you'll never expect!

[b]See or Skip: [/b]SEE, unless you hate excessive violence  or cussing beyond belief, other wise there's no way that you'll dislike this movie!

[b]Favorite Scene: [/b]When Leo and Matt were both texting their bosses in their pockets during a handoff with a bunch of gun-toting gangsters... this was the pinnacle of the film's suspense.

