5.0,"[SIZE=2][FONT=Book Antiqua]I dont want to be a product of my environment, I want my environment to be a product of me, a menacing Jack Nicholson says bluntly in the first moments of Martin Scorseses [I]The Departed[/I]. At this point in his brilliant career, Scorsese has arguably been and done both. He has retreated periodically from telling stories that are clearly more personal to him (like [I]Goodfellas[/I] and [I]Mean Streets[/I]) to make movies that dont reflect his past and heritage (like [I]Kundun[/I] or [I]The Aviator[/I]). Even when he stumbles, he manages to make films that are visionary and epic. [I]The Departed[/I] is an example of Scorsese tapping into the sights and sounds of his own upbringing (the East Coast urban life) and achieving the latter without suffering much of the former. Its also one of the years absolute best films. 

Scorsese trades in the appropriately Buddhist overtones of [I]Infernal Affairs[/I], the 2003 Chinese thriller on which this was based, for his own Catholic sensibility that has been with him from the very beginning (look no further than the title for a healthy dose of Roman Catholic perspective). In the process he elevates excellent material into greatness. 

[IMG]http://www.joblo.com/newsimages1/departed-still.jpg[/IMG]

Joining him in the triumph is what can only be described as a dream cast: the aforementioned Jack Nicholson, Leonardo DiCaprio (a.k.a. the new De Niro), Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin, Ray Winstone, and Martin Sheen. Di Caprio plays William Cortigan, a young cop from the Irish community in Boston who is offered the chance of his career: infiltration of the crime network headed by the most powerful criminal in the city, Frank Costello (an electric performance by Nicholson). The film follows him training in the police academy and applying for a spot with the Boston Detective Department. With his trimmed back hair, soft chin, and wounded eyes, DiCaprio is prey to the same problems he had in [I]Gangs of New York[/I], that is, he hardly looks intimidating. His performance in [I]The Aviator[/I] shined because he was called upon to play a character that was intrinsically boyish. But as [I]The Departed[/I] progresses, DiCaprio finds his groove and becomes scarier and scarier. 

While Cortigan intentionally commits a crime to wind up in prison (giving him the cover he needs after police training), another person from his neighborhood, Colin Sullivan (yet another great turn from Matt Damon) enters the Special Investigations Unit in order to spy for Costello. Damon is phenomenal at giving a superbly human bite to a character that has no moral compass. Hes not evil in the extroverted way that Costello is, but he is capable of doing as much damage (if not more). Costello doesnt know how to restrain himself and apparently doesnt need to; Sullivan is admittedly great at lying. Like his role in last years underappreciated [I]Syriana[/I], he is playing a character among many who is knee deep in deception and lies, with little concept as to who is the enemy and who is an ally. 

As for Nicholson, his performance bears no resemblance to some of his recent roles in [I]About Schimdt[/I] and [I]Somethings Gotta Give[/I] (or going back further the mysteriously over praised, [I]As Good As It Gets[/I]). This is evil Jack; as wicked a monster as he has ever played. Like Daniel Day Lewis in Scorseses previous [I]Gangs of New York[/I], he is both frightening and coldly funny. Hes in sync with the dark humor that gives the script shape and depth. In one of the earliest scenes with Costello he recollects shooting two women in the back of the head and then pausing after the second one has been shot to observe, She fell funny. Later on in the movie, he taunts two priests by commenting that he had a tasty relationship with a nun before she took her vows and calling them cocksuckers. He lets off a racial slur in the first minutes of the film (using the n-word) and then explains, If theres one thing I got against the black chappies, its that no one gives it you, you have to take it. 

Throughout the entire crisp and easy flowing two and a half hours, Nicholson never eases up in portraying an hysterically off the wall mastermind. After he meets Cortigan for the first time, he has Mr. French (Ray Winstone) smash his arm on the table several times to break his cast before hacking away at it with a boot. When its done and Cortigan sits complacently in wrenching pain, Costello pulls out a wad of cash saying, Im sorry, but it was necessary. Winstone matches Nicholson every step of the way, although his often-expressionless face conveys a more unsettling ferocity (that and the flashback of him murdering his wife makes him scary as hell).

Martin Sheen, in a role that was first offered to Robert De Niro, plays Queenan, one of only two law enforcement men who know Cortigans identity. The other is a stellar performance by Mark Wahlberg as Dignam, a brash detective who cant talk to anyone for more than a second without using phrases like suck my dick or fuck you. The scenes between the two men and Cortigan truly shine. Cortigan is furious to see that after more than a year in the presence of Costello, the Massachusetts State Police appear to be no closer to taking him down than they were before he took the assignment. He murdered a guy. I mean honestly, do you want them to chop me up and feed me to the poor, is that what you guys want? he yells at the two men.

Possibly the funniest and already (based on early reviews) most underrated performance belongs to Alec Baldwin as Ellerby, who is high up in the Special Investigations Unit. Baldwin, who has hit his stride with supporting roles in [I]The Cooler[/I] and [I]The Aviator[/I], is great. He beams while watching camera footage of Costello pulling up in his car, Patriot Act! Patriot Act! I love it! I love it! I love it!

[I]The Departed[/I] isnt free from the occasional blemish though; sexy Vera Farmiga has an underwritten role as the mental health professional that Sullivan falls for. Her performance is precariously thin, and she does what she can with it but its essentially the same problem that Kate Beckinsale had in [I]The Aviator[/I]. After just a few dates with Sullivan (who really pulls out the charm), the two move in together. This isnt before meeting Cortigan, who gets more aggravated and crazy in the second and third acts. He sees her for treatment, and after a few tense sessions in which he mostly berates her and second guesses everything she does, the two unexpectedly hit it off. It would have been nice if William Monahan's script dug a little deeper at that point to fully explain what drives her to him.

Perhaps the most noticeable flaw is the image that ends the movie, after a Shakespearean level of tragedy has taken. This image is the dull and obvious work of a teenager aiming for profundity; its appalling that it would sneak its way into a Martin Scorsese film. I think its the worst final shot in a movie since [I]Thelma & Louise[/I], or at least [I]Hotel Rwanda[/I]. 

But the occasional lapse in the movie is a small price to pay for an exhilarating film that at slightly under the usual Scorcese time (which is still quite lengthy) feels concise, with little tacked on. Like [I]Gangs of New York[/I], this was one staggers occasionally, but it is spectacularly alive. 

To Scorsese, thats what matters the most. For the many moviegoers who dont care for much of anything hes done since [I]Goodfellas[/I] (how does anyone resist the devilish B-fun of  [I]Cape Fear[/I] though?), this is an indisputable return to form. And in moving from New York to Boston, he loses nothing. Through editing, he does an impeccable job of zig zagging between three stories and retaining our interest (hell, we are glued to the screen).

[I]Infernal Affairs[/I] was an exciting, masterful thriller. In the dangerous world of South Boston, Scorsese has expanded upon it to give it the depth of other tragedies to take place in Massachusetts, like Clint Eastwoods melancholy [I]Mystic River[/I]. And the scene in which Sullivan and Cortigan spend over a minute on cell phones listening to each other breathing, completely unaware as to the identity of the other, has the same Hitchcockian hold as the finest moments in Spielbergs [I]Munich[/I] had.

Scorseses newest classic is prone to these comparisons, which may help illuminate where his work stands in the contemporary age of cinema, but it should not be lost sight of the fact that this is his bold and uncompromised vision. He doesnt tone it down to make it family friendly (which he certainly did in the crowd pleasing [I]Aviator[/I]) and he doesnt refrain from showing carnage or permitting his characters to hurl hateful racial epithets. The movie doesnt suffer for it either. Whether or not Scorsese gets his Oscar will have to wait to be seen until early next year (although this is already my pick for an Oscar win). Until then its satisfying to know that after more than three decades in the game, hes still doing some of his finest work. How many other directors can say that?

Grade: [B]A[/B][/FONT][/SIZE]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-07","","RT","900839512"
