5.0,"Martin Scorsese's [b][i]The Departed [/i][/b]is something of an anomaly.  It marks the legendary filmmakers return to greatness, a modern masterpiece that is an ambitious, uncompromising cat-and-mouse crime saga that can easily be considered Scorsese's second best film.  It's absolutely astonishing how close this film comes to being as great as [b][i]Goodfellas[/i][/b].  This is the best film of the year, by far.
 
[b][i]The Departed [/i][/b]is infused with a raw, unflinching energy.  The film plots itself out meticulously while moving at a breakneck pace.  This helps to entice and engage you, draw you in, force and command your attention, and not give you a single second to take a breather.  This is a brutal crime saga done right.  I'll leave it to you to discover the details of the story on your own, as this is one film you absolutely should not miss.  The casting is phenomenal here, as everyone seems to know they're being directed by a living legend, and they pull no punches when delivering some of the best performances of their careers.  Matt Damon is as good as he's always been (when is he not?), Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin and Ray Winstone all provide excellent supporting roles.  Jack Nicholson is evil to the core, bringing to life one of the most playfully cruel gangsters I've ever seen in a film of this magnitude.  His iconic persona suits the character perfectly, Nicholson becomes this foul mouthed biggot gangster, and you instantly you find yourself convinced that nobody else could've played it better.
 
Now read this part carefully, because you might not believe what you're about to read.  Leonardo DiCaprio delivers the best performance in the entire film, the performance of his career, and so far, the best performance I've seen all year.  This man should definately be considered for the Oscar this year.  Yes, I said it.  Watch the film, you'll realize that it's true.  DiCaprio brings an intensity to character that just lights up the screen.  His focus, his emotional range, it's as if Scorsese was able to help DiCaprio realize his full potential as an actor.  They've worked together for three films, and each time we get a huge surprise, seeing DiCaprio bring something new to the table each time.  Third time's a charm.  DiCaprio is simply superb in this flick.
 
The film is vulgar, but not without a dark and twisted sense of humour.  It's complex, but not without fully fleshing out the events taking place and bringing the characters into the emotional realm of those events.  We get a full understanding of where each of these characters is coming from.  Their motivations and personal needs are all things that we can identify and even sympathize with, to a degree.  But when we're not wrapped up in the emotional ongoings of the characters, we're awestruck by the films raw boldness, its extremely violent nature, and its raunchy sense of humour.  All of it comes together extremely well, none of it feeling clunky or out of place.  Scorsese directs his actors and the action with a sly confidence, all the while keeping the energy flowing, the film moving, never letting up for a second.
 
The film is a masterpiece, no doubt.  Martin Scorsese has managed to recapture some of that old magic that made him the legendary director he is today, which is no easy task.  With the entire cast shining bright here, and Scorsese back in top form, there's not much to rival a film of this calibur.  Scorsese should finally get that gold statue he's been deserving of his entire career.  They'd be nuts not to give it to him.  This is simply the best film to come along all year, period.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-07","","RT","900603213"
