4.5,"Wow, had two drastically different movie experiences today. I know I haven't blogged here much but I couldn't pass writing this up while I was thinking about it. 

I'll start with Flyboys, and the best thing I can say about it is that it had some of the best sound effects I've heard in a while. The 2nd best thing I can say about it is that it's a virtual gold mine of unintentional comedy. Every character is cardboard-thin, the acting ranges from serviceable to bad, most of the major plot developments are completely telegraphed and you see them coming a mile away, and it packs in every pilot or war movie cliché you can think of. The characters themselves are the stock characters you've seen a dozen times in other, much better movies. There's James Franco, who plays a kid from a Texas ranch who joins the French air force to escape some trouble back home. There's other actors in the movie, almost none of whom I or you have heard of, so I'll just describe the characters and you tell me if you've seen this movie before. There's the young man who has a family history rich in military glory and struggles to live up to that. There's the rich kid who's a disappointment to his father and gets sent into the war to prove himself to... well somebody, we're not really sure if it's himself or his father, or the audience (he's probably the most unnecessary character in the film). There's the token black guy who signs up to serve and gain some respect for himself from the white man, and there's the inevitable racist member of the group, and about an hour before it happens, you can already tell that they'll come to understand each other eventually. There's also the guy who can't shoot straight to save his life, the kind who would miss if they tried to throw a rock in the ocean, and you can tell that he'll have to prove himself. There's the Christian character who never has his Bible far from his sight, and who sings ""Onward, Christian Soldiers"" while shooting at German planes. 

Is this starting to sound familiar? I haven't even mentioned the guy who goes into shock after a battle and is unable to fly for a long time, who you just know will get over it and come through when they need him. Or the mysterious hard-bitten veteran who looks down on the newbies, has a three-day stubble in every scene he's in, and, even though he seems to do little other than fly out on missions trying to kill his ace German nemesis, he still manages to find time to familiarize himself with the top boxers in Paris, as well as know several other things that the script finds it convenient for him to know. Other members of the group familiarize themselves with the women at a nearby brothel, and there James Franco meets and falls for a French woman (played by Jennifer Decker) who he later learns is not actually prostitute (she was just there exchanging chocolate or something, what are the odds). This leads to several awkward scenes in which Franco tries to talk to her in English, which she doesn't speak any of, by using the tried-and-not-so-true method of making somebody understand a language foreign to them by speaking it very slowly and loudly.  Eventually they do have some romantic and emotional scenes, during which Franco has a vacant look in his eyes while Decker acts rings around him.

The movie is based (very loosely, I'm guessing) on the true story of the Lafayette Escadrille, a squadron of American pilots who flew for the French military in the years of World War I before America officially entered the war. It features several scenes that strain the credibility of its claim to be ""based on a true story"", including one where a pilot crashes his plane in no-man's land between two facing armies, and another pilot is somehow able to land his plane within running distance, dodge enemy bullets ala Kevin Costner in Dances With Wolves, and rescue his buddy. The plot is predictable, the characters uniformly uninteresting, and the story full of ideas and themes that could have been compelling had the film spent any time developing any of them. This is not to say the movie didn't have time to do that, since it goes on for a restless 2 hours and 19 minutes. And I couldn't help but wince during one dogfight sequence where a certain character says a certain line that no movie character has ever said and lived to see the end of that scene, and that proved true in this movie as well.

The cast and crew is truly an odd assortment of ""talent"", a term I'll use very loosely in this case.  Jean Reno is a welcome presence as the French commanding officer, though he's not given much to do besides hold a clipboard and tell the characters what to do. And when James Franco is the only American actor in the movie you've ever heard of, that's not a great sign. It's also not a great sign when the opening titles give 3 or more writers screenplay credit, this usually means that the script has changed hands and/or gone through a series of re-writes. One name that struck me was David S. Ward, one of the screenwriters on this film. I've heard of him because he wrote the original script for one of my all-time favorite movies, The Sting. I'm guessing he's not totally to blame for this film's ineptitude because the other 2 screenwriters were Blake Evans, whose career has been spent mostly doing cinematography for B-movies and TV shows (this is his first screenwriting credit, and he also received credit for the story), and Phil Sears, who has only received writing credit (or any other type of credit for that matter) on one film that was released a decade ago. The film was directed by Tony Bill, whose name sounds so goofy I'm surprised Quentin Tarantino hasn't written a character with it. He is best known for producing The Sting, and since then has acted in and directed for a number of TV series and TV movies, but nothing remarkable. After this I'm hoping he never returns to the director's chair. He never allows any of the important scenes to build up to anything, or to leave any emotional resonance. The editing is awkward, and the music (composed by Trevor Rabin) is bombastic, unsubtle, comes on in several scenes when it's least needed. I got the feeling that Rabin had watched The Patriot and Pearl Harbor a lot before writing the score for this because parts of it sound like a blatant rip-off.


Later that day I went bowling with some friends and after that was done most of us went and saw The Departed. It was a marked contrast to what I saw earlier in the day. The performances were compelling, the characters were interesting, the scenes actually were allowed to build up and lead the story somewhere (as opposed to just being inserted at the plot's convenience), and Martin Scorcese looks like he directed the film with a greater respect for the audience than he showed with Gangs of New York. Some parts of the story I didn't find entirely believable (how likely is it that Matt Damon and Leo DiCaprio would have been seeing the same psychiatrist?), but there is plenty of tension and the actors seem to actually enjoy what they're doing. Damon and DiCaprio were both good, Mark Wahlberg is hilariously profane (more profane than I've seen him in anything since Boogie Nights), Alec Baldwin shows his usual reassuring presence, and even Ray Winstone gets some funny lines. Winstone is one actor I've enjoyed watching even if the movie he's in isn't that great (see: King Arthur), so it's good to see him in a high quality production like this.

I've only seen 3 Scorcese movies, which are his 3 most recent ones. I've learned that his movies are, by and large, more to be admired than to be enjoyed. I admired parts of Gangs of New York but didn't really like the movie much overall. The Aviator I thought was one of the best movies of its year, though I wasn't terribly enthusiastic about the idea of ever watching it again. This movie I'm sure I'll want to see again sometime, and it might even vault World Trade Center as the best movie I've seen this year. I made the mistake of buying a coke before the movie (I almost never buy snacks at the theater) so I had to pee for pretty much the last hour of the movie. When I finally made it to the restroom after the movie it was almost like that long peeing scene in A League of Their Own with Tom Hanks. The point is that the movie was good enough to make me hold it rather than leave and miss something.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-07","","RT","900589922"
4.5,"I really can't point out a single thing that I didn't like, or that I felt was wrong with this movie. It keeps you on the edge of your seat, the characters are interesting, and it keeps you guessing what is going to happen until the end. I also have to say that I enjoyed the soundtrack quite a bit as well. Since I have watched a lot of movies, I can usually predict the ending....but in this one, I couldn't, and that makes me happy.
 
