4.0,"[font=Arial][size=6][center][b][u][img]http://www.cinemacomrapadura.com.br/noticias/img/3244-2006-08-16-23:30:33_1.jpg[/img][/u][/b][/center]
[center][font=Georgia][b][u]THE DEPARTED[/u] [/b][size=5][size=6][[/size] [b][font=Courier New][color=red]* * *[/color][/font][/b] [/size][size=6]][/size][/font][/center]
[center][/size][/font][font=Georgia][size=1]Starring: Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Vera Farmiga[/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1]Directed by Martin Scorsese[/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1]Rated R[/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Arial][size=2][font=Georgia][size=1][color=white].[/color][/size][/font][/center]
[b][u][/b][/u][font=Georgia][size=1]The word ""masterpiece"" has followed Martin Scorsese around for decades, so you can imagine then, that after making such an association for so long, even his weaker films begin to take on a praise of their own. Though its been called ""Scorseses best since [i]Goodfellas[/i],"" the reality of the matter, is that [i]The Departed[/i] supercedes only [i]The Aviator[/i] in greatness, making this remake of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] a mere four-year-old ""return to form"" since the triumphant [i]Gangs of New York[/i]. Even when heavily flawed however, weak Scorsese pictures still manage to be better than what the majority of the box office has to offer, and that is so the case with [i]The Departed[/i]. [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1]If you havent heard by now, [i]The Departed[/i] is a remake of the Japanese film [i]Infernal Affairs. [/i]Whats changed plot-wise from that film is very little, but at the same time, a whole hell of a lot. Essentially, [i]The Departed [/i]is the same premise as [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], only screenwriter William Monahan and Martin Scorsese have inflated the proceedings to run fifty minutes longer than its Hong Kong counterpart. With all that extra time, Scorcese has filled in the gaps with character-padding, diving deeper into the psychology of its protagonist and antagonists. Leonardo DiCaprio is Billy Costigan, the good guy infiltrating the bad, and Matt Damon is Colin Sullivan, the bad guy infiltrating the good for Irish crime boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). The two opposing moles work to expose each others identities through a series of cat-and-mouse exercises, which ultimately results in a death count to rival [i]Hamlets[/i] last act. [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1][img]http://www.projo.com/photos/20061006/LB1006_departed_10-06-06_PV297V4.jpg[/img][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1][color=black].[/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Georgia][size=1]Being a Martin Scorsese picture and all, of course theres much to be said about the performances, though Im almost certain that theres too much being said about them. The big talk of the town is Jack Nicholsons return to cinema after a three year absence, as the devious, maniacal, and outrageous Frank Costello. The legendary star is often known for not stepping too far outside the box for his performances and I cant say that he makes a huge leap here either. But then again, the words ""Jack Nicholson"" have become more of a definition than a name. Instantaneously, when we hear the stars name, we think, ""Ahh, charming badass."" Even in [i]Somethings Gotta Give[/i], a bonafide chick-flick, Nicholsons machismo radiates the screen with a coolness youre not likely to find in any other working-actor today. That said, Nicholson owns [i]The Departed[/i] (and from what numerous on-set reports have stated, I dont mean to sound figurative). Hes crazy and we know this early on when he shoots a woman point blank in the head and comments, ""She fell funny."" His performance belittles his co-stars. Theres one moment however, in particular that bothered me: a scene between him and DiCaprio, where he accuses Costigan of being a rat. What follows is a moment that looked as if it shouldve been on the blooper reel, where Nicholson does an awkward hokey-pokey dance behind DiCaprio. There are a few of these little ""touches"" so to speak, where it seems as if Nicholson departs with the seriousness of the material to become the class clown, and however brief, theyre nonetheless distracting and tone-shattering. [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1]The other actors deliver solid performances, though none that are so remarkable as to deserve their own individual paragraph. DiCaprio, whom I could not stand in 2004's [i]The Aviator[/i] (he wore a mustache like a child going trick-or-treating as Charlie Chaplin would), is effectively edgy as Billy Costigan, and Matt Damon rightfully instigates audience-hatred as the slimy Sgt. Sullivan. Martin Sheen and Vera Farmiga bring a nice touch of humanity to their roles, meanwhile Alec Baldwin and Mark Wahlberg steal the scenes theyre in with unexpectedly effective comedics. All-in-all, its a very impressive ensemble cast. [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1]With that said about the performances, I want to talk about the films tone because quite frankly, I was not expecting to laugh as often as I did. For a film that attempts to be a dramatic-thriller, it has more laughs than a full-blown comedy feature ([i]Employee of the Month[/i] perhaps?). Though the sharp, witty banter of some of the characters did make me laugh, I felt as if it took away from the films more serious moments. Part of me wanted this to be darker than what is was, and I think thats where the epicenter of my disappointment lies. There are scenes in the movie that seemed as if they were craving tension, but backfired, and instead caused involuntary laughter. Take for instance, the part where Damons character phones DiCaprios in search of the police departments mole. For thirty-seconds the two simply listen in on each others breathing, which is supposed to create this incredible amount of tension, but instead evoked laughter from the audience I saw the film with. Finally, theres the issue of the films final act, where so many people begin to drop dead, its almost ridiculously hilarious. Perhaps a solo viewing of this film is required; one without the laugh-track.[/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1][img]http://www.joblo.com/newsimages1/departed-still.jpg[/img][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Georgia][size=1][color=black].[/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Georgia][size=1]Also working against [i]The Departed[/i]s favor is the incredibly bloated screen time. At two-hours and forty minutes, [i]The Departed[/i] is at least twenty minutes longer than it should be. Somewhere after the thirty-minute mark, my attention had shifted away from the film and towards the temptation of buying red vines. In case youre wondering, I postponed snack-consumption until after the end credits, in fear that Id miss something important turns out I wouldnt have. Nonetheless, after a dry-spell lasting about thirty minutes or so, the film returns to being captivating.[/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1] [/size][/font]
[font=Georgia][size=1]All squabbles aside, [i]The Departed [/i]is a good movie; just not a great one. There was potential here for something truly epic and tragic to take shape, but only parts take form. Its easily the most overrated film of the year, but its also one that I dont necessarily mind getting an overabundance of hoopla (Scorcese deserves it). For the majority of its running time, [i]The Departed [/i]is an alluring cops-and-criminals tale thats marred by too many laughs, too many uncut scenes, and an ending that tosses around one of the cheapest attempts at symbolism that Ive ever seen. [/size][/font][b][font=Georgia][size=1]Grade: B+[/size][/font][/b][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-07","","RT","900669084"
