4.5,"[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][color=darkorange][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2911/posters/poster1_large.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font]
 
[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][color=darkorange]After seeing Infernal Affairs, the amazing 2002 Hong Kong film with the play-on-words title, I wondered why Martin Scorsese would name his adaptation The Departed, but by the last reel is becomes quite obvious. In his latest masterpiece, and I don't use that word lightly, Scorsese paints a complex world of the Boston organized crime scene and the Elite State Police Unit out to destroy it. Jack Nicholson plays Frank Costello, the godfather (so to speak), while Matt Damon plays Colin Sullivan, Costello's mole inside the State Police, and Leonardo DiCaprio plays Billy Costigan, the undercover cop who has worked his way up the ranks in Costello's gang. The mole and the undercover cop are out to uncover each other in this reverse role and identity game of cat and mouse. As you might imagine this all leads to powerful struggles of morality, evil and sacrifice; if you were the undercover cop how much evil could you allow yourself to be involved in for the greater good? And what happens when all lines are blurred and you are no longer sure who you belong to or who you are? [/color][/size][/font]
 
 
[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][color=darkorange]This all made me wonder, what is so fascinating about mafia stories? The Godfather, Goodfellas, etc. Both great and brilliant films, but why? It seems that in these stories are the characters, who exist in some facet, that not only believe they are a god but in some senses actually play god. They hold in their hands life and death, they hold ultimate power, and this to any human is fascinating. But here's the good news, especially here in Scorseses The Departed, those who choose evil eventually fall to its decapitating hand. The Jack Nicholson character here is brilliant because as intelligent and wise as he is, in scenes where you expect him to spew intelligent banter he cuts short, he begins to loose his mind. The godfather here has reached his climax of success, he needs nothing, yet he is trapped in his damned ways, he can't stop, there is no hope for him.[/color][/size][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][color=darkorange]Yet the pursuit of justice and redemption goes on around him through the lives of the undercover cop and mole. There is so much to enjoy and take away in The Departed. It is a surprisingly entertaining and funny film but is also, especially at a particular turning point in the second act, a dramatically gripping and powerful piece of cinema. But to those sensitive to violence and language be warned, this is no light cops and robbers film, this is heavy handed, gritty and real; Scorsese wouldn't have it any other way. The directing, cinematography, screenplay, and acting (Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, Leonardo DiCaprio, and Martin Sheen) are all Oscar worthy! But let me not forget to mention the memorably crass and brilliant Alec Baldwin and Mark Wahlberg. There are a couple things done better in 2002s Infernal Affairs, but the extra time spent on brilliant storytelling here makes The Departed a masterpiece.[/color][/size][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][color=darkorange][b]Rated R for strong brutal violence, pervasive language, some strong sexual content and drug material.[/b][/color][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-07","","RT","900789686"
4.5,"One of the best of the year (although, yes, it' been a slow year). An incredibly pleasing movie about the cops infiltrating the mob and the mob infiltrating the cops. The tone, the pacing, everything, is perfect. At any moment, you feel as though everyone on screen is goin to start killing each other, and that's pretty much the way it is for two and a half hours. The acting, by the way, is very good. Matt Damon stands out, as does Mark Wahlberg, who's intense as hell and has all the best lines. Jack is Jack, Martin Sheen does what he does, and Alec Baldwin has the snappiest lines in the movie and pulls them off. DiCaprio, who I'm not a fan of, is also very good when he gets angry, which luckily consists of about two-thirds of the movie. Vera Famiga, though, was my favorite, and I think she's going to be a star. There are too many twists and turns at the end, but I really liked the last surprise, and I left the theatre happy. Be careful, though; be ready to see a movie that's bloody as hell and spares very little.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-07","","RT","236969735"
5.0,"[center][u][size=6][color=Red][b]The Departed[/b][/color][/size][/u]

[size=4][color=Red][b]Synopsis[/b][/color][/size]

To take down South Bostons Irish Mafia, the police send in one of their own to infiltrate the underworld, not realizing the syndicate has done likewise. While undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) curries favor with mob kingpin Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson), career criminal Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) rises through the police ranks. But both sides soon discover theres a mole among them. 

[img]http://moviegoods.com/Assets/product_images/1010/378555.1010.A.jpg[/img]

[size=4][color=Red][b]Review[/b][/color][/size]

Wow...is the first thing that comes to my mind when thinking about [color=Yellow][b]The Departed.[/b][/color] Ive waiting for this film since I found out about it, and the day finally came. Was I disappointed? Ha, yeah right, there is nothing in this movie that was disappointing at all. Martin Scorsese is a freaking genius when it comes to making mob films. I havent seen no where near all of Scorseses films, but everyone I have Ive loved. The movies of his Ive seen are: [color=Yellow][b]Taxi Driver[/b][/color], [color=Yellow][b]Mean Streets[/b][/color], [color=Yellow][b]Casino[/b][/color],[color=Yellow][b] Goodfellas[/b][/color], so yeah I lack in seeing a lot of his films. Scorsese is easily one of the best filmmakers in Hollywood today.

There are very few movies that can have a huge cast like this and they all give stunning performances. Sure some movies have big casts, but they dont always turn out that great. Sometimes the big casts can hinder a movie, but nope not here, it makes [color=Yellow][b]The Departed[/b][/color] shine even more. Leonardo DiCaprio gave an amazing performance, I havent seen his other films with Scorsese, but I now see the guy really does have some great acting talent. It was pretty sweet to see Matt Damon play a bad guy, I dont know if he ever has, I mean sure hes played a robber in [color=Yellow][b]Oceans Eleven[/b][/color], but hes not portrayed as the antagonist of the film. 

Jack Nicholson steals the show easily with the best performance, Nicholson is just the freaking man, lets face it. In a way I almost like Nicholson better as a Mob boss than Robert De Niro, they both have theyre own great styles. Nicholson is more of the crazy psycho Mob boss. Mark Wahlberg really, and I mean really impressed me here, Ive always liked Wahlberg as an actor, but I never expected this from him. Wahlbergs is not only great, but his character is hilarious with his insults. Martin Sheen, another fine talented actor, nice to see him in a big movie again since [b][color=Yellow]The West Wing[/color] [/b]has ended. 

Ray Winstone played a great tough guy, Alec Baldwin gave a top notch performance. James Badge Dale, who most probably know as Chase from season 3 of [color=Yellow][b]24'[/b][/color] did a fantastic job too. Even Anthony Anderson was good, in his screen time. With that cast, how could you not want to see this film? How could it possibly not be great? 

There was alittle part in me that was worried that the long 2 hour and 30 minute running time would get me bored somewhere in there. But there wasnt a minute that was dull in this film, the film will keep you entertained from the start of Jack Nicholsons monologue to the end credits.  Of course theres a monologue, its just not a Scorsese mob movie, without some kind of monologue from one of the main characters.

 The acting is great, the story is amazing with some great plot twists. There is no reason that anyone shouldnt see this movie in theaters. This was my first Scorsese film in theaters, and actually I think it was my first BIG gangster movie in theaters. [color=Yellow][b]Goodfellas[/b][/color] and [color=Yellow][b]Casino[/b][/color] came out while I was still a wee lad. EVERYONE, and I mean EVERYONE needs to see this in theaters. If you dont than you will be easily missing the best film of the year. 

