4.5,"Real good. Awesome reworking of tyhe original, with new ending! so its not given away ...and stuff. Whalberg was great.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-08","","RT","266776274"
4.0,"October 6, 2006
 
[size=5][color=darkred][b]I[/b][/color][/size]f any filmmaker was going to remake ""Infernal Affairs,"" I'm glad it was Martin Scorsese. The very premise feels Scorsesian -- the Boston State Police recruit a reckless cadet to infiltrate the local crime circuit. Meanwhile, the city's leading crime boss has a mole of his own working in the same police department. Somewhere and somehow, the undercover man and the rat will come face to face.
 
First, a little about ""Infernal Affairs"" (2002). This is a terrific thriller out of Hong Kong from directors Andrew Lau and Alan Max. I'd recommend watching it before ""The Departed"" simply because it will show how one film can be remade without sacrificing the original's goods. The film deservedly won numerous honors, including Best Picture at the Hong Kong Film Awards. 
 
What I liked most about ""Infernal Affairs"" was that it never appeared stagey or overwrought. It was intelligent and quietly manipulative. ""The Departed"" is too, and while it feels more like a showcase for its cast, it showcases them well. Leonardo DiCaprio plays Billy Costigan, the undercover cop sent in to spy on Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson), the big cheese of Boston. Matt Damon is Colin Sullivan, a preppy-looking cop with respectable records and a new promotion. He works as Costello's personal rat in the police department. Nicholson's Costello is an especially fun villain to watch because he's a crime boss with a sense of humor, the kind that Nicholson could probably only provide. 
 
William Monahan's screenplay, based on Alan Mak and Felix Chong's original, expands the characters' dialogue and Scorsese, being the patient director that he is, allows them to talk. While ""Infernal Affairs"" provided all the information necessary about its main characters, Scorsese adds new layers with each passing reel and creates extra nuances because he lets the character-motivated scenes play out longer. One of the best takes place between Costigan and his counselor (Vera Farmiga), who just happens to be Sullivan's live-in girlfriend. Their back-and-forth dialogue about prescriptions reveals hidden pains and anxieties. 
 
The actors all sink their teeth into the script. Along with DiCaprio, Damon and Nicholson, Scorsese finds strong performances in Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen and the underrated Mark Whalberg, who has the film's best line - ""I'm the guy doing his job. You must be the other guy."" 
 
I did have a small problem with the ending, which feels too concerned with being a crowd-pleaser, even though it's completely logical and believable based on previous events. But ""Infernal Affairs"" handled the same plot with more subtlety and poignancy that it left a greater emotional impact.
 
If you've seen ""Infernal Affairs,"" don't feel like you've seen ""The Departed."" Both films may tell the same story and operate on the same plot devices, but the two are different enough to be impactful and entertaining in their own right. ""The Departed"" is more violent and brutal, but that's Scorsese's calling card. He's remade an involving, dramatic crime thriller with his own force, energy and style.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-08","Matthew H","RT","45716fb3-1420-4208-ab91-8817576305ad"
