1.0,"Just got back from seeing The Departed and was completely unimpressed. Granted, the original, ""Infernal Affairs,"" is one of my favorites: it is good on so many levels and gets better each time I watch it. But where IA was subtle and nuanced and dealt with universal themes, and The Departed is a sledgehammer of an American cop show. Plot points take FOREVER to get anywhere and then get setup, explained, and then covered one more time just in case you might have missed it. The story was unnecessarily convoluted and needless plot points added...and it went on forever! Talk about ""Continuous Hell""! That's what sitting through this movie was (and the saying is right, it is the worst kind of hell). And don't think too long and hard about the story or it all falls apart, very unrealistic.
    
   I was tired of Nicholson in about 30 seconds (does he do anything other than the Joker anymore?) and Damon, who I thought would be the one worthwhile thing in this, was flat. I dreaded DiCaprio in the Yan role and Sheen in the Wong role, but I ended up thinking they were the best parts of this movie, though Leo emoted just a little too much for my taste. Mark Wahlberg, huh? What was the point of that character?
   
The original was a ""Casablanca""...The Departed is the community college script writing class's version of ""Casablanca,"" updated for modern taste and sensibilities. 

    Follow the pack if you must, but afterwards, take the time to track down the original. You won't regret it.","",False,False,False,False,1.0,"2006-10-08","","RT","900914138"
2.5,"I tend to shy away from star-studded, blockbuster-type movies since their goal tends to be to make as much money as possible by giving the very occasional movie-goer what he wants: the same old familiar crap in a supersized portion with big name actors so he can walk out of the theatre feeling like he's gotten his money's worth. But every once in a while a film comes out that's so widely acclaimed that I fear that by listening to my blockbuster eschewing instincts, I may miss out on a truly excellent movie. And thus I found myself in a packed theatre seeing a late afternoon screening of [i]The Departed[/i].
 
At two and a half hours long, I knew I'd need sustenance so I stopped at the concession stand for popcorn, despite the fact that this theatre sells a particularly inferior product. The conversation with the concession stand employee (CSE) went like this:
 
Me: ""Small popcorn with butter please.""
CSE to colleague: ""Small popcorn easy.""
Me: ""Not easy.""
CSE: ""No? Ok, no butter.""
Me: ""Yes butter. 'Easy' means butter?""
CSE: ""Yes.""
(Long pause)
Me: ""Why does 'easy' mean butter?""
CSE: ""'Easy' means light butter.""
Me: ""But I want lots of butter.""
CSE: (eye roll)
 
I remain perplexed at the clerk's editorializing of my popcorn order and her exasperation when I attempted to correct it. Does this theatre have a cost-cutting butter-scrimping policy? A Starbuxian ""small = tall"" English-to-jargon order conversion system? Was the clerk simply trying to save me from drenching my popcorn with an unhealthy dose of golden-colored, artificially butter-flavored liquid trans fats? I guess I'll never know.
 
Anyway, the movie.
 
Jack Nicholson plays this Boston crime boss named Frank Costello. Matt Damon plays a detective in the organized crime Special Investigations Unit who's secretly an informer for Costello. Leonard DiCaprio plays this young tough hired as an undercover cop to infiltrate Costello's gang and feed information back to Damon's boss. So Damon and DiCaprio are both moles (or rats, depending on your point of view) in parallel universes of sort. 
 
This film features an incredible amount of bloody violence running the gamut from bullets in the head and knives in the stomach to guys getting whacked in the face with heavy objects. In some scenes, the violence is so prolific and cartoonish that after one such flurry the audience actually started laughing, as if Wile E. Coyote had just been bonked on the head with his own anvil. 
 
There's also an improbable love triangle involving DiCaprio and Damon, of course, and some hot young police psychiatrist who just happens to be dating one character while treating the other. Sure it's a bit of a contrivance to have the yin mole and the yang mole both pursuing the same girl, but it's all part of the formula to insure a good box office draw. Blood and guts for the boys, romantic polygons for the girls, and you've got yourself a date movie.
 
Let's see, what else can I complain about? Well, there are the on-again, off-again Mayor Quimby accents; the trite recurring Irish ethnic pride/Catholic guilt themes; and the excessively crude language which includes all variety of racial and ethnic slurs, with a variant of the F-word never more than a syllable or two away, including numerous fine examples of interstitial placement, e.g., abra-fuckin-cadabra. I know, I know, cops and criminals really do use salty language, but here it often sounds like it's just been thrown in for effect. Which, of course, it has.
 
Jack Nicholson puts in a fine performance playing Jack Nicholson playing a crime boss. He gets more and more demented as the film goes on and is positively Shining-esque by the end. His transformation isn't really explained but I assume it's supposed to be related to his discovery that someone on his crew is a rat. He even graces us with his finest beady-eyed, bared-teeth, rat face impression. Tony Soprano would definitely not approve. 
 
In case you can't tell, I didn't like this movie very much. Part of the problem is it's way too long. It's actually a remake of a 2002 Hong Kong action movie named [i]Infernal Affairs [/i](which I'm unfamiliar with), yet it's almost 50 minutes longer. How can you take 50 more minutes to tell the same story without ending up with an overblown movie? I guess you can do whatever you want when you're Martin Scorsese. 
 
My biggest gripe with this film is that I never even came close to getting caught up in what was going on on the screen; I was always very aware that I was watching a movie and that these people were actors. I didn't really care about any of the characters, but I think the characters are sort of beside the point here. No thinking or feeling required; this is all about being entertained. Cars blow up, heads blow off, Marky Mark spouts a string of off-color language, the audience laughs on cue. Pump up the volume on the soundtrack; it's time for another round of violence. Hey look, there's Jack Nicholson mugging for the camera. 
 
It feels like a trip to a post-Jordan-era Bull's game where the owners fear that the game itself might not be interesting enough so they throw in gymnasts and baby races and guns that fire t-shirts into the crowd. At the heart of this movie is a great little story but it's buried beneath layer upon layer of crowd-pleasing glitz. The result is a movie that's big and loud and goes on for-fuckin-ever!
 
I know, people love this sort of stuff. At last count, the average user rating for this film was 9.2 with 100% positive reviews. I hate to break the streak and incur the wrath of people who take it personally when someone dislikes something that they love, but here goes. Is this a slick, stylistic, high-energy movie with top-notch acting? Sure. Did I like it? No. Will you like it? Probably. Doesn't just about every other reviewer in the country like it? Yes. Then why am I only giving a 5? Because it's my review.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2006-10-08","","RT","900683813"
