4.0,"[img]http://www.darkhorizons.com/2006/departed/depart2.jpg[/img]
[size=1]Matt Damon and Leonardo DiCaprio star as moles trying to expose each other in Martin Scorsese's latest gangster flick, [i]the Departed[/i].[/size]
 
by David Lim
 
Despite not being as good as its Hong Kong predecessor - [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], Martin Scorsese's triumphant gangster drama has solid performances from its leading actors and is sure to receive many Oscar nods.
 
Young undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is assigned to infiltrate the mob syndicate run by gangland chief Costello (Jack Nicholson). While Billy is quickly gaining Costellos confidence, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), a hardened young criminal who has infiltrated the police department as an informer for the syndicate, is rising to a position of power in the Special Investigation Unit. Each man becomes deeply consumed by his double life, gathering information about the plans and counter-plans of the operations he has penetrated. But when it becomes clear to both the gangsters and the police that theres a mole in their midst, Billy and Colin are suddenly in danger of being caught and exposed to the enemy  and each must race to uncover the identity of the other man in time to save himself. 
 
Since [i]Gangs of New York[/i], DiCaprio and Scorsese have been a tag team and every product they have released is an an instant best picture nominee come Oscar time. [i]The Departed[/i] is no exception and both DiCaprio and Scorsese will surely be contenders again for best actor and best director respectively. 
 
There is never a dull moment with Jack Nicholson, who parades around in every scene with sly remarks and flaunts his openly condescending attitude.
 
Although Matt Damon is outshined in the presence of his two co-stars, he still performs competently as the boyishly charming officer who no one suspects as the informant of the syndicate. 
 
Supporting cast members all have their moments to shine, especially Mark Wahlberg, who lights up the screen with his overly offensive personality and witty one-liners.
 
To really enjoy [i]the Departed[/i], one must not watch [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] beforehand. Scorsese has labelled his masterpiece as ""not"" a remake of the Hong Kong crime drama. It is one thing to say a movie is not a remake if it just takes a generic premise and exhibits a different execution. However, Scorsese's film has lifted ninety percent of the key plot points from [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]. At many times throughout the film, I felt it dragging because I knew what the outcome of every event was going to be before it occurred. 
 
The one major variance in the story is the ending, which is something I wish they had kept true to [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]. The Hong Kong film actually gives you a sense of tragedy to everything that has unfolded whereas the climax to [i]the Departed[/i] is actually laughable. One could almost call Scrosese's ending as a mockery of the Hong Kong version.
 
From a Stylistic perspective, Scorsese succeeds in making the film his own by scripting humorous dialogue reminiscent of [i]Goodfellas[/i] and portraying every character as on edge or uncivilized. The shady streets of South Boston also adds a dimension of grittiness to the overall atmosphere. 
 
[i]The Departed[/i] is one of this years best films but it is still an inferior effort to [i]Infernal Affairs.[/i] Both tell the same story but the Hong Kong version covers it in a much lesser running time and its dramatic impact is stronger.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-08","","RT","900858958"
