5.0,"The older I get the fewer movies there are that can keep me on the edge of my seat throughout.  The Departed is one that can and did!
 
 This is a modern Irish mob movie, not too distant from Showtime's excellent Botherhood series. It's almost flawless throughout, except for one not-so-minor problem, discussed in my minor spoiler at the end of this.
 
 Other than that, the movie is a fast-paced thriller where the good guy is the bad guy and the bad guy is the good guy and they both deperately need to know the identity of the other.  I loved it.
 
 To say much more would be to spoil it. If you liked Goodfellas, The Sopranos (except for this past ""Broke Back Gangster"" season which sucked), and Donnie Brasco, you'll love this movie.
 
 -- Character Study --
 Jack Nicholson's performance is, well, trademark Jack Nicholson.  One minute he's a sociopath blithely saying in the old days he'd just kill everyone in his crew. The next minute, he's doing a ""rat"" impersonation that is absolutely laugh out loud funny; that is, if you didn't feel so guilty laughing at something this unrepentent killer said or did.
 
 Leonardo DiCaprio, who I usually refer to as Leonardo DiDorkio, gave a very decent performance. Unfortunately, casting a Hollywood pretty boy as an underling gangster undermines the suspension of disbelief. It was hard to buy him as ethnic Irish or State Police or Mobster.  Bag boy at a Shaw's Supermarket maybe. Tough guy mobster? No so much.  Fortunately, DiDorkio, er, DiCaprio, gave a good enough performance to eventually sell his character to me, but he just about lost it again playing the ""I'm just a vulnerable, lost undercover boy at one point.""
 
 I'll be the first to admit I've never been a big Matt Damon fan, although that has been changing after the Bourne Supremacy films.  He too, did a good job in The Departed in his role as Colin Sullivan.
 
 I really did like the performance of Vera Farmiga, who played Madolyn the psychologist. I hope to see much more character roles from her on the big screen, and less panties, er, less bedroom shots.  I think she has much more to offer us than a sex scene.
 
 Martin Sheen and Mark Walhberg delivered excellent performances, too.  
 
 I'm not a big Mark Walhberg fan, and probably never will be, but he added a great flavor to an otherwise dull setting in the State Police headquarters. He's become such a big star, extra kudos go to him playing a relatively minor role here and handling it professionally and with pizazz.
 
 Sheen, despite being an Ed Asner lefty, was a real joy to watch.  I haven't seen him on the big screen in awhile, so it was nice to see him playing the head of the undercover unit as Oliver Queenan. (I only wish he could use his talents to get Charlie Sheen, his knucklehead son, to stop saying George Bush blew up the World Trade Center. I fear nutjobs don't fall too far from the tree.)
 
 -- Director's Cut --
 Big praise to Martin Scorsese for another, truly great movie! He's able to tie together multiple views of the same story with nary a seam showing. We completelt buy view of the mole inside the mob, the mole inside the State police, and their intersecting interests in discovering each other.  However, there was one tie-in that was unnecessary. See spoiler below.
 
 What a national treasure Scorsese is.
 
 -- Looney Left Alert --
1) Poor leftist Alec Baldwin, who plays (Lt.?) Ellerby. Baldwin had to praise The Patriot Act which allowed roving wire taps on the Costello and his gang.  Baldwin probably had to down a six pack to get those words out with a straight face.
 
 Despite his left leaning looniness, kudos to Alec Baldwin who gives a solid, albeit slightly over the top, performance as Ellerby, a career law enforcement office for the Mass. State Police.
 
 2) In Hollywood, sex = love.  Rather than develop a love story, we're just supposed to interpret having sex as meaning the characters love each other.  To always take that route cheapens us - and cheapens love.
 
 -- Spoilage --
 1) When mob boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) is convinced there's a ""Statie"" mole in his ranks, he never doubts Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) who just joined his team, even though he knows Billy had a short-lived State Police career. Instead, fast talking Billy gets him to start doubting more senior members of his crew who have been loyal to him for years. That just ain't gonna happen. I really think they'd stick to the newbie like white on rice if they thought there was an undercover State Police mole in their midst. Had the local undercover copy been exposed and killed off sooner, the heat on Billy could have been dissipated.
 
 2) Adding the superfluous counseling and sexual nexus for the opposing moles through the pyschologist seemed so out of place!  Wasn't their affiliation with the State Police and the old Irish neighborhood enough of a nexus?  Wasn't their family and historical connections to Frank Costello enough of a nexus?  Maybe adding a surreptitious romantic link was to keep the attention of the ladies who hate mob films, but it was a hack to me.
 
 
 --
 jonathan segal","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-08","","RT","900914260"
5.0,"Amazing! Never before, have I seen two opposite worlds so flawlessly meshed. Scorsese, Nicholson, DiCaprio, Matt Damon, and Mark Whalberg have all come together for this movie to make something truly worth seeing for any fans of the crime/cop dramas. Stunning performances by all of the aforementioned actors; I say give them the Oscars right now. You WILL hear about this movie come Oscar time, trust me. Scorsese's back with a bloody bang. Literally.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-08","","RT","191692561"
4.0,"I just got back from the theater, I caught the 9:45 showing of 'Departed' (aka Scorsese's remake of [i]'Infernal Affairs'[/i]).   
 
 [url=""../m/departed/gallery.php?page=26&size=lores&nopop=1""] [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_27.jpg[/img][/url] 
 In short - its pretty good....you'll like it if you like Scorsese movies (lots of violence, lots of cursing, lots of tension),  if you don't, you might not make it very far into the movie. :-P
  
 Leonardo and Matt Damon both do a pretty good job (Leonardo is the better of the two,  but that maybe because he has the better character to play...),  I thought Jack Nicolson was a little over the top at a few points, but he has some good scenes. Markie Mark's character is strange too...  not sure why you would create a character that was that big of an a$$ for no apparently reason... but hey, thats the directors choice. also a few extraneous sub plots and scenes that could be cut....
 
 Plotwise its almost exactly the same as '[i]Infernal Affairs[/i]', with one or two exceptions (its been a few years since i saw IA, so i might have missed a few minor differences). The only major difference is the fact that they    sort of melded Sammi's gf character and Kelly Chen's psychiatrist characters into one...   which makes for a weird love triangle aspect.... not sure how i feel about that,  since its not that way in IA, i feel like its unnecessary,  but it does add an additional aspect of 'parallelism' between the undercover cop and the undercover gangster... :-P
 
 Its hard to really judge this one since we've all seen the original... but this is not really a copy,  its sufficiently rebranded to a Boston flavor to make it interesting.  As a whole, its not as slick and stylish as Infernal affairs, it definitely has the Scorsese style cops/mob type crime drama stamp on it instead. (which is not a bad thing by any means!). [b]lots[/b] of blood of course. 
                                                                                 
 I'm curious what the impression of someone who hasn't seen IA before would be.   I'm guessing it'll go over well. 
 
 ps - our discount theater here at the mall charged an extra HK$10 for the ticket, they had a sign saying they were doing it because the movie is 151 minutes long... strange!","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-08","","RT","900659519"
