4.5,"What do you get when you mix Jack Nicholson, Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Alec Baldwin working with Martin Scorsese? Most likely something good. The Departed is what we know and love most about Scorsese; Witty, coarse dialogue, quick flashes of ultraviolence, great actors, and top notch scripts. 
  
 Everyone knows the basic plot by know so there's no need for me to regurgitate it. Where it became somewhat confusing was the constant betrayal. My friends and I discussed it for some time and I think I have it figured out. A second viewing in the near future is definite.
  
 As far as acting nominations, it's difficult to say who will get what. Are Nicholson DiCaprio and Damon all supporting? Could Wahlberg be nominated? He was absolutely hilarious as the foul mouthed ass-hole Dignam. It's hard for me to say who I liked most because they all are worthy candidates. However, I think this will boost Leo's career the most. He has many haters, I used to be one after Titanic, but he will change many minds after they see his complex performance as a good cop forced to commit crimes to pursuade Costello of his commitment. 
  
 The way Scorsese uses violence in his movies is an art form of its own. It's shocking, yet quite appealing. The way the characters are dispatched is completely unexpected and hits you in the face like a brick. Two scenes just make you say ""whoa, shit."" I think Amanda referred to these scenes in her review. 
  
 Do I think Scorsese should win the Oscar? Yes, but only if Eastwood doesn't surpass him (they seem like they'll be going head to head again). A few reviews have come out for Flags, and although they have been positive, it doesn't seem like the critics (Emanuel Levy, Richard Roeper) like it as much as Departed.


***I changed the score to a 9 after seeing Infernal Affairs.  The Departed  freely copies almost all of the scenes from that movie.  However, it's still a good [u]remake[/u].***","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-09","","RT","900847302"
