4.5,"[url=""http://photos1.blogger.com/blogger/5705/3234/1600/thedeparted.jpg""][img]http://photos1.blogger.com/blogger/5705/3234/320/thedeparted.jpg[/img][/url]  I can honestly count all of the films directed by legend Martin Scorsese that I have seen on one hand.  The Color of Money.  Goodfellas.  Cape  Fear.  Gang of New York.  Thats it, thats all of them.  I can honestly say that they were well crafted films, even if Gangs of New York bored me to tears.  But now I have a fifth to add to the list, The Departed, a Scorsese remake of a Japanese film entitled Infernal Affairs.  Ive yet to see the original but by all proclamations it is a fantastic piece of cinema.  I didnt go to see the film based on Scorsese or the praise for the original; I went based on the star studded cast that Scorsese assembled for it.  Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, Leonardo DiCaprio, Mark Whalberg, Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin; if thats not a cast for the ages, I dont know what is.

     Scorsese delivers a hard hitting cop drama utilizing every one of the superb cast members in a variety of different ways.  Nicholson plays crime boss Frank Costello and I havent seen Jack this good since As Good As It Gets in 1997.  Damon plays Colin Sullivan a high rising cop with a dual responsibility to Costello.  DiCaprio plays Billy Costigan an undercover cop inside Costellos unit.  Whalberg, Sheen and Baldwin all lend supporting roles as police superiors but this story is really about Costigan and Sullivan and how their duality pulls them apart.

    As the film starts we are introduced to Costello and we quickly find out he runs the show.  Frank is a partially crazed crime boss who continues to do what he does because he likes it, not because he needs to do it.  We meet Sullivan who is on the fast track on the police force, getting promotions at an alarming rate and then we meet Costigan, who has a different set of values than his crime oriented family.  Sullivan and Costigan mirror each other in their duality and both have to deal with the same problems, most notably the other finding out who they really are.  

    The characters mirror each other so much they even fall for the same woman and in the end that brings sadness to everyone.  The film shows the problems that arise living a double life and maybe its truly better to just be a straight shooter because trying to play both sides of the coin just doesnt work.  It seems almost everyone in this story has an ulterior motive and what you think is straight forward is really furthest from what you are thinking.  

    While I was expecting this cast to be good I wasnt expecting it to be this good and while I had heard the talk about the original version, I wasnt expecting this to be this good.  The Departed is a very, very good movie.  With that said though there is some editing issues, especially at the beginning where the film seems a bit choppy, outside of that and a minor pacing issue towards the middle of the film, where it just drags for a little, The Departed is a fantastic film.  For anyone that loves movies this should be a must see.

    9 out of 10

