5.0,"[center][size=5][font=Palatino Linotype]The Departed
            [size=4][img]http://i5.photobucket.com/albums/y189/cedricdiggory88/4stars.jpg[/img]
  A
            [img]http://www.boxofficemojo.com/img/d/departed/000poster.jpg[/img]
            [font=Verdana][size=2]
          [/size][/font]  [/size][/font][/size][left][font=Georgia][size=2]A masterpiece. 'The Departed' is a shockingly beautiful film. Through its brutal violence and gritty portrayal of the police and mob life, it's one of the most engrossing and stunning pictures of the year. Nearing perfection with nearly everything it does, Martin Scorsese creates an outstanding achievement with the help of a phenomenal cast and an intelligent script. 'The Departed' is one of the year's very best (in technical terms, there's no comparison), and the first true contender for this year's quickly approaching awards season.
          
           Set in South Boston, 'The Departed' sets the police against organized crime. Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) has been ruling the streets for years and slipping through the cracks of the police when Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) and Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) join the state's police force. For Costigan, who was kicked out of the police academy, is enlisted by Oliver Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) to spy on Costello from within. When Sullivan, who holds an unknown connection to Costello, begins to aid the crime lord through knowledge and power gained through his job, it sets off a firestorm and deception that can only end in bloodshed and death of those dearly departed.
          
           If there's one standout quality of 'The Departed', it's the film's all-star cast. The film's three lead actors act as a perfect trio: Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, and Leonardo DiCaprio. Each of the three pull off extraordinary performances, it's difficult to pin-point only one. They are each perfectly casted for their roles, particularly Nicholson, and act off one another to create fiercely compelling drama that few could pull off. Mark Wahlberg and Martin Sheen are equally terrific in their supporting roles, and no stone goes unturned as even Alec Baldwin makes several cameo appearances. A truly remarkable ensemble cast whose stars are wholly Oscar-caliber.
          
           It's sensational how Martin Scorsese is able to create such a high tension and heart pumping film with what seems like such finesse and ease. Naturally, he has screenwriter William Monohan to thank, who adapted the script from the original Japanese film ('Internal Affairs'). Yet for a running time of 2 1/2 hours, Scorsese keep's the film moving in such a fashion that there are few, if any, low-lying moments that would cause the film to slow and drag. No, he keeps the plot moving at a quick enough pace to make it the most exciting picture of the year with a refreshing take on a genre that can easily become cliched and formulaic. 
          
          [/size][/font]          [center][font=Georgia][size=2][img]http://i5.photobucket.com/albums/y189/cedricdiggory88/thedeparted.jpg[/img] [img]http://i5.photobucket.com/albums/y189/cedricdiggory88/thedeparted3.jpg[/img]
          [/size][/font]   [/center]
            [font=Georgia][size=2]
           Its dialogue is sharp-witted and vastly superior to many films of its kind. It's these clashes between the stars that makes this the explosive film that it is, and what makes it difficult to take your eye away from. The terrific drama is so high-voltage, you'll likely find yourself anxiously shifting positions several times as the film approaches its climax, viewers still unsure as to what it will entail, only knowing that the previous 2 hours of film have all been leading up to those final, blood-curdling moments. Which, I may add, is nothing short of amazing. The violence is graphic and will catch you off-guard on more than one occasion, but I find myself saying that it forms a beautiful portrait of violence, if such a thing exists. And if it does, 'Departed' is its name.
          
           Don't miss your chance to catch 'The Departed' in theaters, an engaging crowd pleaser that'll leave you something to talk and think about once the credits roll and the lights spring back to life. Because believe me when I say, there are no obvious flaws in Scorsese's latest breathtaking effort. You'll be hard-pressed to find a film that'll get your blood pumping through your veins quite like this one, and even if you do, it probably won't be nearly as satisfying. A sensational cast and impeccable direction and writing launches 'The Departed' into cinematic gold, making it one of the best mob drama's ever made. A white canvas splattered with blood, it's damn near perfect in every way.  [/size][/font]
            
