3.0,"[font=Book Antiqua][size=3][font=Arial Narrow][font=Book Antiqua][size=3][i][font=Comic Sans MS]Goodfellas [/font][/i][font=Comic Sans MS]is the best film that Martin Scorsese has ever directed, and it just might be the worst thing that ever happened to him. Prior to [i]Goodfellas[/i], Scorsese had examined mob life in [i]Mean Streets[/i], but that film was more about the specific choices facing the young protagonist played by Harvey Keitel. And the mafia had always been on the periphery of Scorseses films, particularly in [i]Raging Bull[/i]. But Scorsese was primarily a stylistic virtuoso interested in exploring human dilemmas, from loneliness and isolation ([i]Taxi Driver) [/i]to jealousy ([i]Raging Bull[/i]) to the delusional quest for fame ([i]The King of Comedy[/i]).[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]Then came [i]Goodfellas[/i], a masterful entertainment about life in the mafia. Suddenly, Scorsese was the master director of gangster films. Everyone remembers the profanity-laced exchanges, the unpredictable violence, and the cocky swagger and memorable one-liners of the Joe Pesci character, who stole every scene he was in. But thats the problem for Scorseseeveryone remembers those elements that made [i]Goodfellas [/i]such a great entertainment, but what few focus on is the way Scorsese so skillfully employed compassion (yes, compassion, in an often brutal film) to take us inside the head of the protagonist Henry Hill, and allow us to understand both the allure and the harsh reality of life in the mafia. In a strange way, Henrys journey became a poignant metaphor for all of our life journeysfrom a wide-eyed young man with many hopes to a man crushed by a lifetime of poor choices and misfortune. The film was a masterpiece, but rather than building on it (or his prior masterpieces), Scorsese seems caught between two conflicting paths, neither of which has been very fruitful (the one exception is [i]Bringing Out the Dead[/i], one of Scorseses most underappreciated films, and a great return to the sort of questions about city living that Scorsese raised in [i]Taxi Driver[/i])..[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]Scorsese has tried to expand in new directions in his post-[i]Goodfellas[/i] career. He has tried certain period films ([i]The Age of Innocence, Kundun[/i]) that feel bogged down and stagnant, while also attempting films of epic scope ([i]Gangs of [/i][/font][font=Comic Sans MS][i]New York[/i][/font][i], [font=Comic Sans MS]The Aviator[/font][/i][font=Comic Sans MS]) which have been similarly lifeless. On the other hand, Scorsese also seems determined to duplicate his [i]Goodfellas [/i]success, as evidenced by the pale retread [i]Casino[/i], which sacrificed all of the poignancy and nostalgia of [i]Goodfellas[/i] for more Gofuckyourself one-liners and bullets to brains.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]Which brings us to [i]The Departed[/i], Scorseses latest film, which many critics are praising to the heavens as a return by Scorsese to his familiar turf, the gangster genre (a claim thats ludicrous given that, of the approximately twenty films Scorsese has directed since 1972, only four have been concerned with the mafia, five if you count [i]Gangs of New York[/i].). [i]The Departed [/i]certainly seems like vintage Scorsesewhen the film opens with the Rolling Stones Gimme Shelter blazing on the soundtrack (as it did in [i]Goodfellas [/i]and [i]Casino[/i]), one could be tempted to say Scorsese is repeating himself, but hes so stylistically assured that we surrender once again to his mastery of the craft. That is, at least, until it becomes clear that this isnt the same masterful Scorsese of films past, but rather a director who seems to be fighting a troubling battle with his own reputation.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]Part of the problem may be that, for the second time in his career, Scorsese isnt working with a clean slate. As with [i]Cape Fear[/i] (one of Scorseses worst films), [i]The Departed [/i]is in fact a re-make, of a film precious few North Americans saw, the very good Hong Kong thriller [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]. [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] was by no means a perfect film (its opening act was a bit too hyperactive and confusing), but it was ingeniously plotted, and had the novel central idea of an undercover cop matching wits with a mafia mole in the police department. Having seen [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], I was aware that my enjoyment might be hampered by the fact that I knew some of the crucial plot developments (many of which are shocking) in advance, but I was still looking forward to seeing how Scorsese put his own spin on the various beats in the Hong Kong predecessor. How shocking it was to discover that Scorsese practically re-creates every noteworthy scene in [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] shot-by-shot, making [i]The Departed [/i]almost as gratuitous a re-make as Gus Van Sants [i]Psycho[/i] (although still far more entertaining). This is particularly a problem given that the makers of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] were clearly influenced by some of Scorseses earlier work, which makes [i]The Departed [/i]the cinematic equivalent of a hand-me-down sweater that has lasted at least three generations.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]That is not to say that [i]The Departed [/i]is not a fresh film...as we have come to expect from Scorsese, it pulsates with life. But the film doesnt have the same momentum as Scorseses best work, and the rhythm of the film is oddly disjointed (its particularly strange given that the film was edited by Scorseses longtime collaborator Thelma Schoonmaker, whos normally a masterful editor but here seems unsure of herself). There are none of the visual flourishes that distinguish most of Scorseses work, eitherstylistically speaking, this could just as easily be a Michael Mann film (not that that is anything to be ashamed of). At its worst, [i]The Departed[/i] feels like the effort of a young director trying to become the next Scorsese, rather than the work of the master himself.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]The actors fare quite well, particularly the supporting players, who give the film an air of authenticity and flavour (given its [/font][font=Comic Sans MS]South Boston[/font][font=Comic Sans MS] setting, we expect somewhat eccentric working-class characters). Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg, Ray Winstone and Alec Baldwin are all extremely entertaining in fairly minor roles. But problems arise with some of the lead characters. Matt Damon, as the seemingly goodie-goodie hotshot police detective who is in fact the mole for the citys Irish mob, brings none of the depth or insinuating presence that he showed in the masterpiece [i]The Talented Mr. Ripley[/i]. Instead, he relies on the same facial tics and mannerisms that he displayed in [i]Good Will Hunting[/i]namely, a relaxed [/font][font=Comic Sans MS]Boston[/font][font=Comic Sans MS] charm and a face that effortlessly breaks into a smile. While this is endearing at first, Damon doesnt quite rise to the challenge of making us believe he is actually a morally bankrupt man who has no qualms about leaking vital information to the mafia. Similarly, Leonardo DiCaprio comes up short in the role that was played magnificently by Tony Leung in the original. While DiCaprio can be charming and convincing in the right role (like [i]Catch Me If You Can[/i]), Im still not sold on him as a dramatic actor. The fact that he cant shed his boyish looks isnt necessarily a problem (neither could Tom Cruise, and I think Cruise is an excellent actor). The problem seems to be that DiCaprio is always straining too hard to be taken seriouslyhe has a way of playing dramatic scenes in which his effort is far too visible (he tends to look up at the person he is playing opposite, and he often whispers any sort of dramatic proclamation in a hushed tone, supposedly to lend it credibility). There is one scene in the film, in which DiCaprio enters a state of shock after witnessing the death of a comrade, in which he seems so raw and natural that we begin to forgive Scorsese for sticking with DiCaprio for three films. But for most of [i]The Departed[/i], DiCaprio doesnt succeed in convincing us that the mob could ever think that anyone [i]but [/i]him was the informant in their midstanytime the subject comes up, DiCaprios character shifts and winces like a man obviously consumed by nervousness. In other words, DiCaprio can not play a good actor, and thats what the role calls for.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]And then there is Jack Nicholson, who at last has teamed up with Scorsese for their first collaboration (the fact that it took so long is a bit of a mystery). Nicholson plays Frank Costello, the leader of the Irish gang, a mob boss who at first seems cold-hearted and completely in control but is eventually revealed to be consumed by murderous paranoia. Ive long felt that Nicholson has it in him to be a fantastic actor (witness his turn in [i]The Pledge[/i]), but he often falls back on his Jack persona. The part of Costello almost begs for Nicholson to indulge in his hammiest habits, and unfortunately, he often heeds the call. I have no doubt that it is possible there are real life mob bosses as over-the-top as Nicholson plays Costello, but his performance seems tonally removed from the rest of the film (actually, [i]The Departed [/i]is so tonally unsure that Nicholsons performance isnt really too far off the mark now that I think about it). Several ill-advised choices (such as brandishing a dildo in a porn theater) make us laugh at Nicholsons characterization rather than being both amused and terrified by his character (as we were by Pescis Tommy DeVito in [i]Goodfellas[/i]). Every time Nicholson tries to add a layer to his character, it only robs the character of gravity. Come to think of it, thats what the three lead actors all are lacking in [i]The Departed[/i]: gravity (its something the two leads of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] had in abundance). Damon, DiCaprio and Nicholson all have a Hey, Im playing a tough guy in a Martin Scorsese film quality to their performance, whereas previous Scorsese actors like Pesci and DeNiro seemed to inhabit their roles effortlessly.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]I am coming down hard on [i]The Departed[/i] not because I think it is a bad film (taken on its own, its an average to above-average thriller), but because I can see that everything thats good about the film is whats been lifted verbatim from [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], and everything that goes wrong with it is something that has been added. One added subplot involving DiCaprios relationship with a police psychiatrist (Vera Farmiga) who just happens to be also the girlfriend of the Matt Damon character is both ridiculously contrived and extremely uninteresting, despite the fact that Farmiga brings a lovely, natural quality to her scenes. And, without giving too much away, I will say that while I admire Scorsese for sticking with some of the more shocking developments of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], he and scriptwriter William Monahan have added an unnecessary final scene that dilutes the power of the film. The ending of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] achieved something quite difficult: it provided the hope that redemption can be found for even the worst of sinners while also suggesting that evildoers will continue to reside within the annals of power (in this case, the police department). The ending of [i]The Departed[/i], on the other hand, provides few moral shades of gray, and instead resorts to poorly-motivated, audience-pandering revenge. Stylistically, Scorsese doesnt even add much to what worked so well in [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]he adds an insert shot of a man falling to his death, thereby robbing the surprise of his crash to the ground, and, most disappointingly, he completely re-creates a memorable shot involving elevator doors rather than finding his own original way to suggest the tragedy of what has led up to that moment.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Comic Sans MS]All throughout [i]The Departed[/i], I kept thinking about something that another great director, Steven Spielberg, had to say while being interviewed on Inside the Actors Studio. When asked about how he dealt with being Steven Spielberg, the legend, Spielberg replied that he never viewed himself that way, and that if he ever found himself trying to compete with his own legend, that would be the death of him creatively. Whether you believe Spielbergs professed modesty or not, he raises an interesting point about what happens to an artist when he creates a work of art so successful that people want you to return to it. I am beginning to fear that Scorsese is falling into the very trap that Spielberg describedhes trying to be Martin Scorsese, the legend. Rather that continuing to explore the human condition, or at the very least continuing to experiment with new cinematic styles, the Scorsese of [i]Casino [/i]and [i]The Departed[/i] strikes me as a man wrestling with his own reputation, trying to recapture the glory of [i]Mean Streets[/i] and [i]Goodfellas, [/i]while forgetting what it was that made those films great in the first place. It doesnt help that Quentin Tarantino has often been referred to as the new Scorsese, despite the fact that Tarantino, entertaining as he is, has never approached the levels of compassion and understanding that Scorsese has achieved in his career. What [i]The Departed [/i]demonstrates is that Scorsese is imitating his imitators, rather than continuing to set the bar, and that is why the film doesnt have anywhere near the impact of his best work. The title of the film, the departed, refers to those who have died and left this worldI only hope that Scorseses talent isnt among them. [/font][/size][/font][/font][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2006-10-09","","RT","900665601"
