4.0,"After a slight panic attack, which caused me to run out of a wedding reception like a maniac, I went to calm down, by watching a movie.  What I saw was maybe the best movie of 2006.  No BS.
[b]The Departed:  [/b]The movie revolves around two cops, Billy (Leo DiCaprio), and Collin (Damon), who get involved with the head of an Irish Mob family (Jack Nicholson).  The thing is that one is working with the Mob boss, and one is working undercover to bring him down.

The movie is a non stop, action packed crime drama, with one twist after another.  You never really know what, or who to believe.  The cast is top rate, and the performances were out of sight.  Damon and DiCaprio were stirring in their roles as morally conflicted policemen.  Mark Wahlberg plays an asshole cop named Dignam.  Dignam hates the Feds and doesn't trust them a lick.  Hateable, but likeable at the same time.  Good work out of him.

The one who actually made the movie great was Jack Nicholson protrayal of Mob Boss, Frances (Frank) Costello.  Costello was nothing, if not a complete SOB.  Charming, viscious, crude, and nasty to the core, Costello kept me on the edge of my seat.  There's a reason why Nicholson is Nicholson.  He's great.  The man, like Robert DiNiro(sp?) is money everytime he's on camera.  He was the perfect movie mob boss.  A guy you both despised and envied at the same time, like Pacino in The Godfather.

Departed was fantastic, and probably the best movie movie of the year.  Now I heard that this movie was possibly the best of the Scorcese films.  Yeah, I'm not ready to say that.  I mean, the man made 'Gangs of New York', 'Raging Bull', and to me, the thrid best movie of all time, 'Goodfellas', for christ sakes.  Departed is slightly above 'Taxi Driver', and slightly below 'Gangs of New York' in the Scorcese pantheon.  I would say it's number four behind the first three movies I mentioned.

In closing:  Great movie, definately worth the price of admission.

