5.0,"Sometimes, the best movies of the year come my way all at once. This was the case for this past extended weekend, in which I started by lamenting that I'd completely missed ""Factotum""'s one-week run in one of Ann Arbor's indie theaters, and I ended completely forgetting it. Here are small reviews of the three films I saw, in the order in which I saw them:
 
[b]The Departed[/b]
Friday--opening night. I'd heard this was good, but I had no idea that it was full-on Scorcese-at-his-most-Scorcesian good, probably his best since ""Goodfellas"" (though I am a constant advocate of 1999's ""Bringing Out the Dead""). ""The Departed"" is one of those films that rounds up some of the best actors in the business, all in top form, all overshadowed by Jack Nicholson at his most deliciously Christopher Walken-esque eccentric. He is truly the legend and he and Scorcese [i]own [/i]this film, despite a surprisingly unboyish performance from Leo DiCaprio and the always reliable Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, Ray Winstone, Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin (well, maybe not [i]always [/i]on that last one). Oh, it's bloody. And it'll keep you hooked to the screen till the end. And, if you're like me, you'll love the soundtrack. See this one--it more than makes up for ""Something's Gotta Give,"" ""Anger Management,"" and, Scorcese's last remake, ""Cape Fear."" [b]A+[/b]
 
[b]Half Nelson[/b]
Sunday night. This one, on the other hand, has been out for two months, and I was well aware of the myriad accolades it was receiving. In fact, that's why I went--as I don't normally choose to see throes-of-drug-addiction heartstring pullers. I'm glad that I decided to give it a shot: ""Half Nelson"" now replaces ""The Devil and Daniel Johnston"" as my favorite movie of 2006--for those of you who are keeping track (i.e. me). It becomes immediately clear at the start that you are not entering into another ""Stand and Deliver"" inspirational teacher flick. Rather, everything in this film feels real and decidedly uninspirational--aside from the teacher's I'm-thinking-outside-the-box lesson plan. It's more of an intimate portrait of the teacher Dan Dunne, played by former Mickey Mouse Club member (!) Ryan Gosling in an absolutely fantastic performance, and Drey, the impressionable young girl who befriends him, played equally well by Shareeka Epps, as they struggle to break out of their respected ruts in life. There's also an underlying theme of political upheaval and racial interaction--but, again, this is no Martin Luther King Day TV special. The entire movie will break your heart--it's one of the greatest feel bad movies in a long time. [b]A+[/b]
 
[b]The Science of Sleep[/b]
Monday night. This may be the most anticipated film of the year for me (depending on when ""Inland Empire"" is released), and, being an ardent fan of ""Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind,"" I knew I was in for a treat. But one should not be fooled--""The Science of Sleep"" is not nearly as accessible as ""Eternal Sunshine..."" (if you found that one readily accessible), and has the bizarre interactions reminiscent of Charlie Kaufman's earlier movies. But that shouldn't matter--""Sleep"" is a visual masterpiece, a film comprised of cardboard, cellophane, and wads of cotton, like a (very) French arts and crafts project. I am reminded of Richard Linklater's similarly-themed ""Waking Life""; both films are equally beautiful visually, but whereas that film placed the magic of dreams in a very loose storyline, this one places dreams in a love story between an eternally adolescent Stephane (Gael Garcia Bernal) and the equally whimsical but grounded Stephanie (Charlotte Gainsbourg). Unfortunately, I feel as if something has been lost in translation with the waking life story, as some of it can be quite confusing. I found the hopelessly naive Stephane to be a tad frustrating as a character (definitely not the relatable Jim Carrey character in ""Eternal Sunshine""), and wasn't quite sure why he was struggling toward the end (you'll have to see it to find out). But, by the end, you get a nice story of an unhealthy crush, and a cute story of two neighbors linked by imagination. Seriously though--it needs to be seen for it's dream sequences, and, aside from some frustration, it was everything I'd hoped for. [b]A[/b]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-11","","RT","900820226"
