4.5,"[size=3][color=DeepSkyBlue]Martin Scorsese has a style that continues to shock audiences with each generation.  The Godfather shocked audiences in the early eighties.  The crime family drama was the first of many film nuar.  Goodfellas shocked the people of the late eighties.  I was shocked and remember feeling like I had witnessed a murder as I drove home.  
      
            This all star cast is put to the test as no one is too-big-of-a-name actor.  The actors are big name actors but they are made to be the characters they play.  There are about six very famous celebrities in this movie.  However they were carefully choosen and are not a laundry list of cliche actors for this type of movie.  
      
            Jack Nicholson is a raw and nasty leader of an organized crime ring.  He is very impactful in this role and is ""Jack"" as always but this role is best suited for him.  Al Pacino would have been to clean cut and reserved for this role.  Robert De Niro would have been too cold and stern.  Jack is once again great in this special way of being Ultra villainous with an evil smile.  
      
      No one is what they seem.  Matt Damon is not a character that is perfect and self righteously intelligent.  His character is a poorboy from the mean streets that lives with his grandmother.  The crime leader recruits his character around nine or ten.  Matt Damon plays that boy all grown up.  He gets a job as a state trooper and quickly and unfairly rises to detective.  His face looks like that of the boy's that stood in the cold.  His expressions are subtle.  The same relationship exists but on a whole new level with Nicholson's character.  
      
            Soon the skeletons-in-the-closet of Leonardo DiCaprio's character get exposed.  He is pulled from the state trooper academy and is told he is nothing by his superiors.  He is forced into a life as an undercover cop of sorts.  His is much like so many other undercover characters that get in to deep into the life of crime.  The violence from this character's snapping from all the pressure is believable.  This is due to some toning up on DiCaprio's part physically.  These family skeletons, a relative who was a bookie etc, become the reason for this character being able to survive in the underworld.  It's like a family tree organized criminals trace backwards.
      
            The ending would have been so obvious if I had any time to think while watching.  I was so in shock that I could do nothing but watch the ending unfold.  There are many quick turns at the end but not so many that one gets frustrated.  The human mind needs a finish to be satisfied.  Our minds don't want to be fed more and more bits of information so close to a glorious conclusion.  The events unfold quickly and many things end abruptly.  
      
            The Departed will stand on its own when you place the DVD on your shelf next to the Godfather series and Goodfellas.  Only in this movie if Marlon Brando or De Niro were in this movie they would have been killed off in the first act.  No big name celebrity's character is safe. 
       [/color][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-11","","RT","900764461"
