4.0,"[size=3][color=blue]Just got back from my local multiplex here in Ventura County seeing a Hollywood-bankrolled studio flick, THE DEPARTED. I heard a lot of good things about Martin Scorcese's latest screener, and the final product lived up to the buzz and hype.[/color][/size]
 
[size=3][color=#0000ff]THE DEPARTED, bursting with an all-star cast headed by JACK, MATT, and LEONARDO, reminds me of that oft-quoted Hollywood idiom, ""All movies are really westerns.""[/color][/size]
 
[size=3][color=#0000ff]Scorcese takes us and his gun-toting cowboys to his old stomping grounds - Boston and the mob. In a high stakes game of cat and mouse, Costello (JACK NICHOLSON) has a mole in the Massachusets State Police tipping him off and keeping him out of jail. The police, however, have their own mole in Costello's organization. The moles soon suspect each other's existence, and the search is on to see who can reveal the other first.[/color][/size]
 
[size=3][color=#0000ff]After about fifteen minutes of introductory exposition, the film takes off and never slows down. The screenplay is not dumbed down for the audience and you have to stay on your toes to catch everything that's happening.[/color][/size]
 
[size=3][color=#0000ff]The ending is unorthodox, pumping Sarin gas into the incubator of Hollywood formulas - and that's just fine by me.[/color][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-11","","RT","900912669"
