This may be Scorsese's chance. I haven't been blown away by any of Martin Scorsese's films since Goodfellas. The Aviator was [i]pretty [/i]good and probably deserving of the 2005 [i]Best Directing [/i]and [i]Best Picture [/i]Oscars (certainly more so than Million Dollar Baby). But the Departed blows the Aviator away. It was phenomenal, even exceeding my high expectations. I even applaud the performance of one of my least favorite actors, Leonardo DiCaprio. Jack Nicholson played the same character he always plays, but the practice paid off. All of the lead characters were amazing in the Departed...unless you count Alec Baldwin. He had no business being in this movie, even if his was one of the smaller roles. I am a big Goodfellas fan and this film definitely lives up to it's standard. I wonder what the original version is like?  The writing, directing, and acting were all brilliant. It's twisting and unpredictable; completely engrossing. It's still early, but this could definitely be Martin Scorsese's year for an Oscar. 
Finally a 2006 film that lived up to it's hype.

(EDIT:  After watching this again on DVD, I don't really believe that I praised it so highly.  I would probably give it a 6/10 today.  It was just OK.  A little over-dramatic and involved.  This time the ""shocking"" twist endings got on my nerves.  It just seemed unnecessary.  I guess the point was to leave the audience guesssing until the end, but it went too far.  The acting didn't impress me so much anymore either.  I also don't think it deserved the Best Picture Oscar at all.)","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-12","","RT","900908713"
