4.0,"[size=2]""The departed,"" it's what we say when it's still too painful to refer to the deceased by name and when it's just too callous to say ""dead person."" So we tread lightly. We tiptoe. We say ""the deceased"" or ""the departed."" Those who work in the funerary trade also use the term. They use it as a way to refer to the client with unerring accuracy. In [b]The Departed[/b], director Martin Scorsese's not tiptoeing. He's in a funerary trade of sorts and he's into it up to his elbows.
 
[b]The Departed[/b] tells the story of two spies. One, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), is a mob rat. He's a fresh recruit to the Massachusetts State Police Organized Crime Unit and he has a clean family background. Or so it would appear. If only the Staties had known that Colin's kindred father, the one he keeps in touch with so diligently, is none other than Boston mobster Jack Costello (Jack Nicholson). Costello and Sullivan go way back. Costello kept young Sully in groceries and comic books when he was a boy and he's been grooming the kid for this service ever since. It's Sullivan's job to infiltrate the government and inform Costello of upcoming raids. It may seem a broad stroke of convenience to place Sullivan so solidly within an organized crime unit as a rookie cop, but brilliant writing and brilliant acting (undoubtedly the two recurring themes of this review) convince us that sometimes the government works as baldly, as blandly and as badly as this.
 
The other spy is the government's own mole, Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio). He too is a green state police recruit. We meet him at his intake interview. It does not go well for young Billy. He's a bright man - 1400 on his SAT's. That's too bright, the argument goes, for police work. But more damning than that is Billy's broken family: a lace curtain mother and a small time Southie thug father. This fractured Irish pedigree is what really sets the Organized Crime Unit poobah's off. They can't stand to be in the same room with Costigan. So, they send him underground. Billy goes reluctantly. He agrees to take a criminal conviction and he does real time. He uses his family's seedy side as a link to the Southie crime scene and in Southie, all crimes lead to Costello. 
 
So the intrigue begins. Each side knows that there's a traitor in their midst. Each side wages a deadly battle to stop the leaks and exterminate the rats. The stakes are high and Scorsese orchestrates the tension masterfully. He blends the enthralling swoon of tragic opera with the sprightly step of a Celtic jig and creates a film that in many ways rivals, but fails to surpass, his past mean street work. 
 
As the tension escalates, each of the rats is assigned by their respective boss to find the traitor within. In effect, each operative has been charged with finding himself. But don't start reaching for the Sartre yet. There are no philosopher-thugs here. The Scorsese Scale of Human Needs goes no higher than brute survival. (Though, writer William Monahan, adapting the script from the Hong Kong mega-hit, [b]Infernal Affairs[/b], finds the time to include a pinch of Freud and a tickle of Joyce. At 149 minutes, Yeats must have landed on the cutting room floor.) 
 
The editing and cinematography contribute to the film's success. The close, jagged shots give the movie a rapid pulse, cat and mouse, run for your life feel. The pace slows occasionally, but only long enough to peak around the next corner or to glimpse over a terrified, guilt ridden shoulder. 
 
Ultimately, however, what carries this movie and delivers it from mediocrity, are the performances. To name one and not another in this instance is a crime. And its one I'm perfectly happy to commit. First, ""Go Mahkie! Youwah the best, Mahkie, the best! YANKEES SUCK! Little Mahkie Wawhll-bug, all Dawhchestah that kid is."" He's always been just on the periphery of my radar and what can I say? This is what I've been waiting for. He reminds me of an old cartoon character- a little bulldog, or something- a funny little one note character that launched into vicious, irrational attacks, but, oh, how I laughed at those attacks and how I love Wahlberg's work here. I wonder if he studied that little fella to prep for his role as Sgt. Dignam. He's the Very Bad Cop to Martin Sheen's Cpt. Queenam. Queenam's the Very Good Cop, but you can just call him [b]Da[/b]. 
 
Then there's Wahlberg's supreme and solid co-hort, Alec Baldwin. This is another fine supporting turn for him. He's a much better character player than lead. I never get bored. Vera Farmiga has a tough job. Her psychiatrist, love interest role is the weakest link in the script, but she gives it charm. I'm not convinced by the curled finger, but otherwise, her mix of smarts, vulnerability and estrogen keep this film from smelling entirely like a men's locker room.
 
Damon and DiCaprio are terrific. Damon employs cunning and boyish charm to fool the figurative pants off the state police and the literal pants off of his state psychiatrist girl friend. As the heat comes closer to home, he coils inward, screwing ever tighter. His face remains stoic, but he moves with quick twitch speed and his eyes shoot rapid-fire bullets. DiCaprio's character does the opposite. He unravels - slowly. And since his character is too smart not to notice his own descent, he gives us a kind of soul-searching gravity and desperation that we haven't seen from him before. Dicaprio's is a mature, nuanced performance and it's the best in the movie.
 
And then there's Jack. Let's give Jack a hand. Scorsese does. (Okay, Okay, UNCLE! UNCLE! I give. I GIVE!) A lot of people go to the movies just to see Jack because, reliably, Jack knows Jack and this is a classic Jack performance. It's big and it's broad. It's probably bigger and broader than he's ever gone before. The performance is a marvel to behold . It reminds me of that [i]Seinfeld[/i] line, ""he is a loathesome, offensive brute, yet I can not look away."" (And I will add this: big, broad Jack is infinitely more enjoyable than big, broad Al.) Unfortunately, it may contribute more to the Legend of Jack than it does to the movie. And there's the rub. I'll leave it to the viewer to decide.
 
Although the movie presents a stunning crescendo, its ending fails to satisfy. There's a bit of deus ex machina and lots of bullets to the face. That's Scorsese's way of tying things up neatly. But he leaves with a wink. There's a little visual pun in the closing frames. You know how we Irish love our puns. 
 
[/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-12","","RT","900904281"
