3.5,"The Departed by Director Martin Scorcese spins webs in many different directions as its exceptional cast dances under and through the web's design with effortless ease. However, the weight of such an all star cast tends to hold down the rhythm of the story.  Just my opinion, but when you go to the movies, you are supposed to get lost in the story and I found myself more often than not getting lost in what MATT DAMON was doing or how LEO was doing this or how JACK was reacting to that.  Don't get me wrong. Its delightful to see such high caliber heavyweights all wrapped up in one movie but I think this movie might have exceeded it A List Star capacity.  Well, I might have a little prejudice since I spent most of the movie wondering how that skinny kid from Titanic turned into a major league, grade A sex pot without me realizing it. Move over McDreamy, here comes Leo! Even his Boston accent was hot! OH MY GOD!  Leo is a force of nature in this role (despite my sexist oggling)! I am not a fan but I think I might have to jump on board the Leo train now.  :D 
I think that Scorcese did a great job of modernizing his usually time warped interpretation of gangster life.  Yet I think the ques he left throughout the movie were a little simplistic (references to JFK, living near the Capital, etc...) I would have enjoyed it better if I didn't know who was the good guy and who was the bad guy until the very end (although the terms good guy and bad guy seem a little simplistic to describe anyone in this movie).  Since that was revealed early on, you were left waiting to see who would get caught and how. The ride was enjoyable but not as twisty as it could have been. The play between Matt Damon and Leo (who do not share much screen time) was amazing and the cell phone scene (one of the best of the film) will most definitely be duplicated over and over again. Its great to see how technology is changing the way films approach suspense and drama (reminscent of a scene from RESCUE ME when Leary's brother was killed and the word spread within minutes due to cell phones basically stopping everyone in their tracks. In the past, there would have been a call at home, a knock on the door by the cops. Nowadays, even tragedy is delivered in real time.)  It definitely worth the price of admission but I think that Oscar is safe from this one. Sorry Marty.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2006-10-12","","RT","900749884"
