3.0,"I'll have to break with the tomatometer on this one, ""The Departed"" just wasn't all that great. Especially if you've seen ""Infernal Affairs."" Martin Scorsese supposedly never watched the film that his remake is based on, but maybe he should have. That film is superior to his in almost every way. Several changes he made to the script seemed to dampen the effects of suspense and emotional impact that made the first film so good. A couple examples (spoilers ahead):
  
  - Splitting the police commissioner into two characters. In the original, Tony Leung's boss is the only person who knows about his undercover indentity. When that person gets taken out, you really feel the devastation hit Tony Leung's character, he's basically screwed because his boss was the only person who could get him out of his increasingly dangerous undercover situation. In the remake, there's two people who know about the cover, so when one of them gets offed it doesn't have as big an impact.
  
  - The sting operation in which both undercover agents are trying to outplay the other via secret communications to their respective bosses is done much better in the original.
  
  - I agree with Senh in that the remake has a lot more humor. Especially Marky Mark's character, who gets to throw the best in-your-face insults. But the humor detracts from the tension and suspense of the story.
  
  - The actors in the remake are all pretty good. Except Jack Nicholson, who doesn't really act, but just does his thing. You know what I mean. Any minute you're half-expecting him to turn toward the camera and wink. He's just not believable as the big mob boss.
  
  Overall, it's a decent movie if viewed on its own. But compared to the original, it falls far short. I guess the best thing you could say about ""The Departed"" is that it makes you want to watch ""Infernal Affairs again.""","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2006-10-13","","RT","900660398"
