5.0,"[B][COLOR=Red]Half Nelson[/COLOR][/B] (2006)
Director: Ryan Fleck
Starring: The wonderful Ryan Gosling, Shareeka Epps, Anthony Mackie

3.5 stars out of 4

No other film has ever left me with the feelings that I had after experiencing Half Nelson.  As I exited the theater, paranoia washed over me. Every movement, every lick of my lips, every quick dart of my eyes, I felt was being scrutinized by anyone who crossed my path. As I stood in the bathroom, merely washing my hands, I felt conspicuous.  I felt like a drug addict. Now, I have no idea what that genuinely feels like, and I hope that I never do; however, after coupling myself for 106 minutes with such a painstaking and hyper-realistic character study, my perception of myself and the world around me were bizarrely and unexpectedly altered.  

Half Nelson is the story of Dan Dunne, a history teacher at an inner-city middle school, coach of the girls basketball team, and an inspiration and friend to the children in his class.  Half Nelson is also the story of Dan Dunne, a cocaine and crack abuser.  Eventually, and with absolute poetic precision, the barrier separating the two realms of his life begins to weaken, causing them to shift and merge in interesting and harrowing ways.  

As the film began, I feared that it would become heavy-handed and preachy, chock-full of greeting card-worthy motivational one-liners and clichéd inspiration.  But as the final credits rolled and I escaped from the nightmarish virtual reality ride that the theater had become, I was glad to be proven wrong.  Instead of leaving filled with empty and temporary feelings of exaltation, I was transformed.  I was transformed into someone that I never want to be, but who I am thankful I was able to experience (even if only on the most superficial of levels).  Responsible for such an extreme reaction are primarily two things, which, not surprisingly, are the films strongest points: Ryan Goslings meticulous performance and the daring cinematography.  

A very strong, yet consistently overlooked, young actor, Gosling carefully creates Dan Dunne, studying him, becoming him, to the point where any line between the dueling pretenses of actor and character seems completely obliterated. Every movement is precise yet smooth, exact yet spontaneous, always feeling as natural as possible. He captures something truly human in his performance, and is careful never to sink to levels of caricature.  Perhaps even more importantly, Gosling knows not to upstage the supporting cast, but rather to complement them. Shareeka Epps, a talented newcomer who plays Drey, a student in Dunnes class who acts as the films catalyst, also gives a stunning performance, and should not go unnoticed in Goslings shadow.  

[center][img]http://www.rialtocinemas.com/images/2006/large/2006_half_nelson.jpg[/img][/center]

Stumbling through his existence, always equipped with a fresh dose of coke and conflict, Dunne experiences intimidating levels of loss, intimacy, apathy, love, rage, and responsibility.  His hazy and overwhelming trip through everyday life is communicated to the audience via unfocused and unsteady cinematography, causing his spectator to view him in exactly the way that we should imagine he would see us, forging a subconscious bond between character and audience.  It is this bond that causes the film to be truly effective. 
 
Half Nelson is a nearly perfect film. The performances and impressive production alone are enough to make the film satisfying, but when fused with an extremely poetic and purposeful screenplay, it emerges as one of the best of its kind.  Half Nelson is an audacious character study that proves to be an absolute must-see; and if it happens to make you feel a bit shaky and paranoid after viewing it, I would argue that it is likely to be the only instance when such feelings come from such a satisfying and enlightening place. 

[CENTER][img]http://www.theday.com/gbl/media/dynamic/lrgimages/zMovieNelsonETW091406.JPG[/img]
*swoons*[/CENTER]


[B][COLOR=Red]The Departed[/COLOR][/B] (2006)
Director: Martin Scorsese
Starring: Matt (sexiest man ever) Damon, Leo DiCaprio, Jack Nicholson, Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg

4 stars out of 4

The Departed is, thus far, the best film of the year, and being such, I should probably write more about it. But I won't. Just SEE IT. It is to Martin Scorsese what Match Point is to Woody Allen. The intensity is crazy, and the layers of the screenplay are surprisingly poetic --in many ways, its a lot like Carnivale (the greatest TV show in history. R.I.P.). And besides, Matt's back!!! 

[center][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_13.jpg[/img]
Yeah. I'm looking at you too, Matt. *swoons some more*

[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_10.jpg[/img][/center]

[B][COLOR=Red]Little Children[/COLOR] [/B](2006)
Director: Todd (God) Field
Starring: Kate Winslet, Patrick Wilson, Jennifer Connelly, Jackie Earle Haley, Noah Emmerich

4 stars out of 4 (formerly 3.5 stars out of 4) 

UPDATE: I've watched this a couple more times and I've changed my mind. This film is superb. 

(original review):

I saw Little Children in New York... I was anticipating Todd Field's follow-up to the greatest film of all time (In the Bedroom) VERY much, so I suppose I was just setting myself up for disappointment. It was definitely my most anticipated film of this year (or even the past couple years), so I am quite annoyed (yes, annoyed) that I only can give it an 8/10. Critics hail this as a total masterpiece, but really, it's just missing the realism and profundity that makes In the Bedroom the [i]true[/i] masterpiece that it is. Perhaps it's a shame that I had to watch this film constantly drawing comparisons between it and In the Bedroom, and I suppose, it really isn't fair, but I simply couldn't escape my own viewer-codes while watching it. The performances of the main cast were great (and surprisingly, I really loved Jennifer Connelly), though the performances from all secondary actors were some of the worst I have ever seen. Now, ALL of the performances in In the Bedroom are like, the greatest of all time, so why couldn't Mr. Field get more out of his actors this time around? The atmosphere and style were great, and it definitely felt like it was his work, but the source material just was not as strong as that of In the Bedroom. I'll definitely watch this film again, but truly, it just felt too neatly packaged (especially in its conclusion), and at this point in my career as a film buff, I am just sick of the whole cliche suburban disconnect theme... Regardless, Field is an AMAZING director, and I look forward to seeing everything that he does in the future. He is a true auteur, and In the Bedroom still stands as the greatest piece of cinema ever.

