5.0,"[b]Theater[/b] First Viewing, 6 Scorsese films seen

[center][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_36.jpg[/img]

[left][i]The Departed[/i] is a great movie. The editing, the performances, the pace, the dialogue, everything. Except one critical thing. It's one of the most forgettable experiences I've ever had in a movie theater. Until the last three minutes, I was anything but compelled and my brain never strained a muscle. I understand and appreciate good entertainment, but [i]The Departed[/i] is miles away from one of the year's best.

I have been increasingly disappointed in Matt Damon, who I used to label as my favorite actor. After last year, when he was upstaged by Siddig in [i]Syriana[/i] and Leger in [i]The Brothers Grimm[/i], my expectations for him regretably have lowered. Good thing he truly impressed me in [i]The Departed[/i], but, and a big but, Leonardo Dicaprio still stole the entire show for me. It's one of the best performances of the year, and one of Leo's best as well. And that's saying a lot. Who else could have survived the heartthrob fame so well?

I'm at least glad that Scorsese has left the Oscar begging genre. As great as [i]The Aviator [/i]is, there is something more admirable about a director making a high profile thriller for fun instead of trying to win his much deserved best director Oscar - which he has yet to receive. [i]The Departed[/i] isn't the film to get it for him, but I bet he is one away.

