4.0,"[center][font=Times New Roman][size=7][color=darkorange][i][u][b]The Departed[/b][/u][/i][/color][/size][/font]
[/center]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/thedeparted_bigearlyposter.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Thriller[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][color=darkorange][i][size=1]Released 10/6/06[/size][/i][/color][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][color=darkorange][i][size=1]Reviewed 10/14/06[/size][/i][/color][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Starring Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin, Vera Farmiga, Ray Winstone, Anthony Anderson, James Badge Dale, Kevin Corrigan, David OHara, Mark Rolston, Kristen Dalton[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Based on the screenplay Infernal Affairs by Siu Fai Mak and Felix Chong[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Written by William Monahan[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Directed by Martin Scorcese[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=1][color=darkorange][i]Rated R/18A for Strong Brutal Violence, Pervasive Language, Sexual Content and Drug Material[/i][/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/matt_damon2.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]I love big casts. You put as many strong actors in a movie as possible and I will pay six dollars to see it. If not, Ill likely wait for video unless it gets extremely good reviews. The Departed was a bit of both, with an exceptional cast and incredible reviews. The only catch: Scorcese was the director. While I liked Gangs of New York, I definitely thought it was overrated, and The Aviator was just a bore. Granted, Ive never seen Casino or Goodfellas, so I really cant say I know much about Scorcese. Still, I was definitely intrigued to see The Departed, especially since the premise looked awesome. While it has many strong points, The Departed is just a little too messy to call a must-see.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/mark_wahlberg6.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]The movie is a remake of the Asian hit Infernal Affairs, but the fact is that the base plot is such a great concept that Id be surprised if it hasnt been used many times before. Colin Sullivan (Damon) was just a small child growing up in Boston when he met mob boss Frank Costello (Nicholson). Eventually, he began to look up to Costello as a father figure. Sullivan went into law enforcement for a career, and is accepted into an elite unit of mob-busters led by a determined agent named Ellerby (Baldwin). On the inside, he helps Costello out by tipping him off about raids and purposely slowing down the hunt for him.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) graduates from the academy around the same time as Sullivan. Because of his familys mafia connections, he is taken aside by Oliver Queenan (Sheen), who leads the undercover ops in the mafia. He is tasked with infiltrating the Costello mob, and quickly rises to become one of Costellos right hands. Only Queenan and hot-headed officer Dignam (Wahlberg) are aware of undercover personnel, for obvious reasons.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson9.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]Eventually, word gets out that there is a mole on both sides. Costello tasks Sullivan with finding the mole in his group using his resources as a cop. Queenan makes it Costigans goal to smoke out the mole in the police, though Costello keeps a tight leash on Sullivan. Essentially, Sullivan and Costigan must find each other, with their life on the line should the other discover them first.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]It really is an incredibly interesting concept, and the cat-and-mouse games are always fun to watch, especially since we never exactly know which one is the cat and which is the mouse. Both are painted in a sympathetic light. While Sullivan may inform Costello, he is a genuinely good cop who is just maintaining a loyalty. Costigan definitely has his character flaws as well, which really leaves it open to the audience to decide who to root for. Either way, the plot remains very tight and constantly changes gears.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/leonardo_dicaprio17.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]The problem is that it does become a little muddled and messy. The subplot that has both Sullivan and Costigan becoming involved with police psychiatrist Madolyn (Farmiga) is a little sloppy, and some plot elements arent as fleshed out as they should be. Theres also an incredibly pointless scene in the movie involving Costello throwing cocaine on a bed. The ending is really what kills the movie, as even though it does produce some shocks, it just seems far too easy for a movie that was fairly clever until that point.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/leonardo_dicaprio21.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]The acting in the movie is a bit of a mixed bag. The two leads are actually somewhat disappointing. DiCaprio is a good actor, but he just doesnt fit the role of the mafia tough guy. He pulls off the smart-aleck jackass without a hitch, as well as the desperate cop near the end, but seeing him roughhouse a man for information just doesnt seem to fit. Damon is definitely a notch above, as he absolutely nails the reclusive side of Sullivan. He just has a slightly harder time when he has to be assertive.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]Still, both can easily carry the movie. The problem is that the supporting cast is so much stronger. Jack Nicholson is definitely the standout as Costello. With a slightly over-the-top performance that always remains within the confines of reality, he is easily the most interesting character of the group, and may well get yet another Oscar nod for it. Wahlberg is also incredible as the hot-headed and fast-mouthed Dignam. His exchanges with the also memorable Baldwin and Damon are easily among the best portions of the film, and his initial interrogation of DiCaprio is one of the strongest. Martin Sheen is a perfect fit for the role of Queenan, bringing a sense of sanity compared to the other off-the-wall characters. There are also some solid supporting turns from Ray Winstone as Costellos right hand man and Alec Baldwin as the boss of the tactical unit. The only weak link is Vera Farmiga, who is just too wimpy to fit in with Sullivan and Costigan.[/color][/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange] [/color][/size][/font]
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson14.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#ff8c00][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=darkorange]When viewed as a whole, The Departed doesnt really seem to be a clear winner. Sure, it has an incredible cast and awesome premise, but it never really adds up to the sum of its parts thanks to a little bit of a sloppy filmmaking. It definitely is a worthwhile film to see that has plenty of memorable moments and can be watched over and over again. At the same time, I cant help but think it could have been even better.[/color][/size][/font]

