5.0,"it's hands down one of the best MOVIEs to come out in the last 10 years","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-14","","RT","235730161"
4.0,"Having long, long ago earned for himself a place in movie history, director Martin Scorcese doesn't really have anything left to prove... except maybe that his best movies might yet be in his future, rather than in his past, as many would assume given his considerable filmic achievements to date. Certainly it's been a little hit-and-miss with him recently: although both are solid, powerful movies head-and-shoulders above others released in the same year, recent works like [i]Gangs Of New York[/i] and [i]The Aviator[/i] (both starring Scorcese's muse of the moment, the eternally baby-faced Leonardo Dicaprio) haven't shone quite as brightly as his output in the 70s. Well, with [b]The Departed[/b], Scorcese is reminding just about anyone who had any doubts at all that he remains on top of his game and is [i]still[/i] one of the most exciting, thought-provoking directors working today.
 
And all that with a [i]remake[/i] (cue sharp intake of breath - the horror!). Scorcese and his cast have been careful to stress in their press junket for this movie that [b]TD[/b] is inspired by - but [i]not [/i]based on - Hong Kong's modern classic [i]Infernal Affairs[/i], which first put onscreen the tangled lives and uncertain morals of an upright cop and a crooked mobster, who are serving as deeply undercover moles in the mafia and police force respectively. Whether or not it [i]does[/i] echo the original movie (I'm told that the broad storyline is similar but the details differ, especially with a newly grafted-in love interest for the dual protagonists), [b]TD[/b], however, stands proudly as its own movie - though it may be every inch a cops-and-robbers movie, teeming as it is with deceit and action, what makes it stand out from the rest of its ilk in this same genre is that Scorcese makes darn sure that his film engages both the brain [i]and[/i] the heart. Smart and artfully plotted, the movie is also surprisingly touching and viscerally emotive, on many occasions touching a nerve when you least expect it. 
 
The down-and-dirty streets of Boston. DiCaprio is Billy Costigan, a kid from the wrong side of the tracks who's trying to go straight as a cop, but is recruited by top special investigation unit operatives Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) to go undercover to suss out one of the biggest of bigwigs in the local Irish mafia - the practically sociopathic Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson), a towering example of a man who has managed to transcend the petty ethical concerns of humans to become something akin to a god on his own turf. But even as Billy goes to great, painful lengths to insinuate himself into Frank's network, Colin (Matt Damon) is working his way swiftly up the ranks of the state police and into the special investigation unit, into a position that gives him plenty of leeway to tip his real boss off and continually frustrate attempts by the police to build a solid case against Costello's gang. It isn't long before both Queenan and Costello discover that there's a mole in their midst - and both Billy and Colin soon find themselves living ever [i]more[/i] in fear of being found out for who they truly are.
 
Packed with twists, constantly shifting morals and more ethical grey areas than you can shake a stick at, [b]TD[/b] is a masterful exploration of the choices each man is compelled to make, trapped as they are in the desperate lives they have chosen for themselves. Billy, in particular, finds himself increasingly struggling to make some sense - to find some meaning - in the routinely horrible chores he is forced to do as an increasingly trusted member of Costello's gang. Every time he accompanies Costello and his henchmen, including the ubiquitous Mr French (Ray Winstone) on their rounds, Billy cannot quite disguise that split-second flicker of fear, of distaste and revulsion, that crosses his face when his companions kill, maim and set things aflame with merry abandon. His desperate pleas during his infrequent meetings with Queenan and Dignam, the only two men who know just what a sacrifice he is making, are heartbreaking, as are those brief moments when his heart stops in his chest and yet he is forced to tell smooth, convincing lies that he cannot believe, when the raving Costello tries to smoke him out, to smoke out [i]anyone[/i] who would dare to be a rat in his enterprise. Colin, meanwhile, safely blessed with an identity as an upright man of the people, settles into a cushy lifestyle that sees him buy a flashy upmarket apartment and court police psychiatrist Madolyn (Vera Farmiga)... even as the trust his bosses have in him lead to his having to pretend to look for himself amongst his colleagues.
 
If anything can be said against [b]TD[/b], it's really that the movie takes a little too long to really get going. Necessary though all the exposition may be, as we watch Billy and Colin get recruited (the former in a shouty conversation with the in-your-face, thoroughly rude Dignam and the latter in a soda shoppe as Costello buys his loyalty with a bag of groceries and comic books) and grow accustomed to the lives they are to lead, with Costello ever posturing and shouting in the background, it doesn't really feel as if the movie has quite kicked into high gear. The set-up is belaboured and a little pat (Costello accepts Billy as one of his own awfully quickly), and it doesn't help that much of it is told in such rapidfire Irish mobster patois that the dialogue becomes more frequently hit than miss... although, to its credit, it does lend the movie a rugged realism that prevents [b]TD[/b] from straying too far from any semblance of credibility.
 
However, these are but niggling concerns, for when [b]TD [/b]finally hits its stride, it never looks back - double-crosses abound, as loyalties are questioned, moral grounds are shifted, and lives, some infinitely more worthy than others, are lost in the crossfire. Where Scorcese really makes the movie sing is in his masterful control of the characters' relationships with one another - to the extent that he can evoke nail-biting tension with nary more than a glance from Billy to Costello, or put your heart through the wringer when Queenan's clearly fatherly concern for Billy trumps his better judgement about being cautious above all else. The second half of the movie is a masterpiece of controlled tension - whether it's the gunfight showdown between the cops and the robbers, Billy's night-time stalking of a hooded Colin through the streets, trying to catch a glimpse of his face, or pretty much any scene involving an abandoned tenement building, Billy, Colin and their respective posses. Best of all, even amidst all the action, Scorcese never forgets to keep his characters human - particularly Billy, who is effectively the star and tortured moral centre of the movie. His pain is palpable when one of his mentors pays a high price to meet with him, although in terms of onscreen time, Billy's reaction has barely seconds to register with the audience before the movie is back up and running and pulsing with yet another heart-stopping action scene.
 
The movie wouldn't work half as well as it does without a competent cast, and here Scorcese draws from DiCaprio and Damon superlative performances - one of the former's most impressive, and [i]definitely[/i] the latter's career best. For all his acting chops and sheer magnetism, DiCaprio always looked a little uneasy as Howard Hughes in [i]The Aviator[/i], and was never quite convincing enough as a man consumed by revenge and hatred in [i]Gangs Of New York[/i]. But here, he is every inch Billy Costigan - consumed by fear for his life, driven by a passion to transcend his own criminal roots. He is ably matched by Damon, who turns his amiable image on its head with a chilling, dark turn as Colin. Whatever Damon might say in interviews about his character simply doing his best for being caught in an unfortunate situation, there is a cold, bitterly ruthless, thoroughly unsympathetic side to Colin that I'm surprised Damon managed to evince given his usual sun-kissed sweetheart image.
 
Meanwhile, aside from Fermiga, who drowns a little in all the testosterone and is one of the weakest characters in the film, supporting performances are, across the board, fabulous... led, of course, by the scene-stealing, scenery-chomping Nicholson, who can rest assured that he will be receiving his twelfth Oscar nomination in short order. There isn't another actor alive who could breathe the very worst that life has to offer into Costello the way Nicholson does - for Costello is truly living, breathing evil in human form, a man who kills and maims for the sheer fun and [i]joy[/i] of it, and doesn't think twice about sitting at breakfast with a severed hand near his plate - and yet make the character curiously charming and thoroughly compelling. Even minor characters find their voice here - Sheen is a welcome, credible presence as what amounts to a beacon of goodness in a movie about hardened criminals (for that's what Billy is, even if at heart he's a cop), and Wahlberg is howlingly funny as the acerbic, tough-as-nails Dignam who is ever the stereotypical bad cop, badgering Billy into submission every chance he gets. Alec Baldwin also turns in a great comedic performance in his cameo as Sergeant Ellerby, a cop so eager to bring Costello down that he delivers in all seriousness possibly the funniest throwaway line in the film about just much he goshdarn [i]loves[/i] the Patriot Act.
 
Without a doubt a leading contender for Best Picture - among a host of other well-deserved accolades - at this year's award ceremonies, here's hoping Scorcese finally snags the little bald guy (that would be the Best Director Oscar) for this movie. It's as glorious a piece of smart, modern film-making as we've had the honour to see in a very, very long time.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-14","","RT","900685673"
