        [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/125353__night_l.jpg[/img]  [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/71301341_0ba066ee60_o.jpg[/img]
       [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/nightdead5.jpg[/img] [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/nightlivingdead015.jpg[/img] 
        T-Meter [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]100%
        My Rating [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]9/10
        
        This is quite simply one of of the most claustrophobic films I've ever seen. Romero put me in that house with the shell-shocked girl, annoying man, lovebird teenagers, and the fearless leader. I gasped when the zombies almost broke through the window and I intensely watched the television, waiting for updates on how to possibly kill these bastards, or, just get to saftey. When the truck exploded, I cried out in pain, and when the mother is killed by her daughter, I simply sat in my pitch-black room with my jaw dropped and my eyes large with fear, kind of like a Tex Avery cartoon. The low-budget, cult classic [i]Night of the Living Dead [/i]far surpassed my expectations. An excellent film.
        
        [size=5]Dawn of the Dead (Romero, 1978)[/size]
        [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/dawn208.jpg[/img] [/size][/font][font=Arial][size=3][img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/dy20dawn20of20dead.jpg[/img][/size][/font][font=Arial][size=3] 
        T-Meter [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]96%
        T-Meter [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]9/10
        
      Concentrating less on intense scariness, Romero more has fun with the sequel to his 1968 classic. Like the Terminator films, the second installment is cooler and funner than the original. Watching zombies get murdered to elevator music and receiving pies in the face is well-worth watching. It's kind of sad though, to see the four people taking all these things from the mall, like they're going to be able to go out and spend it. Dawn is jam-packed with awesomeness. It's now one of my favorites. 
      [/size][/font]
      [font=Arial][size=5]Days of Being Wild (Kar-Wai, 1990)
      [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/Nos_Annees_Sauvages_Aff17.jpg[/img][size=3] [/size][/size][/font][font=Arial][size=3] [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/4083573_400x300.jpg[/img]  
      T-Meter [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]89%
      My Rating [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]9/10
 
 This early entry from Wong Kar-Wai is a stirring, uncomfortable tale about relationships. Two different women are attracted to the same man (Tide), and no matter how cruel or indifferent he acts towards them, they still want to believe that he's not that bad (think [i]2046[/i]). It's sad and you don't like Tide, but Kar-Wai makes you feel genuine sympathy for him, as he constantly battles with his aunt, sometimes uncessarily and other times it's justified. She won't tell him where his real mother lives (he's adopted) and he hates her for it. It's a strange mix of emotions that will intrigue you from the get go, and there's one scene in particular that's espcecially emotional, at least for me
 
 [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/cyukadeneimyozyodaysofbeing15.jpg[/img]
 
 When Tide goes to visit his mother, sees her in the window, and realizes that she wants nothing to do with him, he starts to walk back down the trail. Kar-Wai films this in slow-motion, and my sympathy for Tide doubled because of his toughness to not turn around and stare at his mother one last time. His spirit is broken, but he can't let her know it. It's really quite admireable, but simultaneously very sad. 
      
