 [size=5]     The Departed (Scorsese, 2006)[/size]
      [img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/the_departed-1.jpg[/img]   [/size][/font][font=Arial][size=3][img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/poster.jpg[/img] [/size][/font][font=Arial][size=3][img]http://i98.photobucket.com/albums/l241/BryceTheCyclops/2006-9-30-departed1.jpg[/img] 
      T-Meter [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]93%
      My Rting [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/fresh.gif[/img]9/10
  
  The trailer didn't look all that entising, but after hearing people gush over how great it was, I decided to give it a try. Scorsese's big return to the Gangster genre equals a very entertaining, darkly comedic and complex film that's one of the finest of the year. His ability to create cool scumbag  characters hasn't faltered at all. Jack Nicholson steals the show as the mob boss, Frank Costello, delivering his finest performances in awhile. Whether he's laughing at how someone falls over after he's shot them in the back of the head, or beating a man's already injured hand with a shoe, he does it all with grace and coolness. Dicaprio gives a pretty good performance as Billy Costigan, and Damon is one of the most despicable villans I've seen in awhile. The supporting cast of Wahlberg, Sheen, and Baldwin do fine jobs as well. The thing that's best about all these characters is that they are all distinctly different from one another. They aren't the typical, cardboard characters you see in most crme films, and if Scorsese hadn't been at the helm, this movie might have suffered from that problem.
  
  The Departed is no [i]Taxi Driver [/i]or [i]Gangs of New York[/i], but it is in the upper eschelon of his filmography (probably between [i]Raging Bull[/i] and [i]Goodfellas[/i]). It should be nominated for Best Picture.
