4.5,"Maybe it's because she's been under the direction of one of film's giants, but she crafted some of the most memorable sequences in all of film, from the fight scenes in [i]Raging Bull, [/i]to Ray Liotta's paranoid coked-out day in GoodFellas, and now the first hour or so of [i]The Departed. [/i]We follow Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) and Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) through their world of deceipt, lies, and double crosses and not once are we sure what's coming next, and, even more important, we are never confused. Scenes jump around and are intercut non-linearly, creating an uneasy atmosphere, as well as momentus energy that, unfortunately, the movie can't quite keep going for the entire running time. I dare anyone to find a better example of editing from the last 10 years. Hell, maybe ever. It's nothing short of brilliant.
 
That said, Scorsese is back in familiar gangster territory, and his knack for shocking, brutal violence is still there. He's in top form here, especially when it comes to establishing mood, developing character, but when the plot starts to get in the way, we're out of his strong suit. The movie does hit a bit of a lull (though, maybe only when compared to the bang-up first hour) when the usual plot mechanisms have to come into play, but that's a minor knock on an otherwise terrific film. The acting was just about perfect. Jack Nicholson finds a way to let enough of ""Jack"" to come through in Frank Costello without being distracting. DiCaprio and Damon each bring the right sense of desperation to their roles, as each tries their damndest to keep their cover. And I could tell Alec Baldwin was enjoying playing the role of a PATRIOT-Act-loving cop. Hell, I even think Mark Wahlberg was damn good.
 
In summation, check it out. It kicks ass.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-15","","RT","900589974"
5.0,"The best movie of 2006 by far.  Honestly the best directing, acting and writing I've ever seen in a film.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-15","","RT","350008021"
5.0,"If this movie doesn't sweep every oscar out there Illl have lost all faith in the oscar voters. a brilliant movie its serious and has twists and turns everywhere but at the same time absolutly f*cking hilarious""","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-15","","RT","337145995"
4.5,"A very classy film in a very brutal setting, Martin Scorcese has returned to his guns and has created one of the best films of the year. The Departed has had a lot of Oscar and public hype, and it is well deserved, this is a bloody and conniving look at deception, loyalty, and motivation and it is brought to us in a brilliant screenplay that is anything but predictable. The script to The Departed keeps you on the edge of your seats, as it was designed too, and the constant twists and gut wrenching suspense will leave your eyes glued to the sizzling plot layed before you. The casting for this film, initially, may have look a little strange given actors like Mark Wahlberg and Matt Damon in a film like this, but every single actor has given a fantastic performance - I would go as far as saying that portrayals were perfect. This is a true actors movie and Leonardo DiCaprio stands out firmly as the best of the lot with his edgy performance as Billy Costigan. However, it is some of the subtelties to The Departed that leave it a little far from that masterpiece it begs to be. Throughout the script there are little bits that feel unexplained properly, such as Costigan's recruitment, but these inconsistencies tend to blow over fairly quickly. And, of course, The Departed leaves you with a very ambiguous twist at it's conclusion - take it as you will. All in all, The Departed is a fantastic, wonderful achievement that should be seen by all.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-15","","RT","56424930"
4.0,"This movie makes Street Kings look like child's play. Man, a cop movie has never been this GOOD.
Leonardo DiCaprio is a superb actor, and I don't know why it took me so long to add him to my favourite actors area on my Flixster page. 
The chemistry he has onscreen with Damon and Nicholson helps the movie flow well, without flopping into a typical Hollywood cop movie.
The plot of the movie is for the intellectual type as it's not one of your average, predictable plots.
DiCaprio, Damon, Nicholson and Wahlberg help bring the various themes of the movie to life: Corrupted cops, and the father and son relationship with Costello and Costigan, and Costello and Sullivan.
The ending was kind of pointless though, with everyone getting gunned down but hey, who said Scorsese wasn't going to end the movie with a big bang!
See DiCaprio grow immensely as an actor in this movie, as he steals the show hands down, without a doubt!","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-15","","RT","361766646"
4.5,"[b]The Departed[/b]

Martin Scorsese's lastest is actually a remake of a cult Hong Kong crime flick trilogy entitled [b]Infernal Affairs[/b]. This series of films presented a flashy, stylish, and overly convoluted story about dual moles in the police and crime world, each trying to discover who the other one is. The amazing thing about what Martin Scorsese has done, is that he took the basic concept and created something entirely his own. It definitely worked, and provides the audience with a return to Martin Scorsese in his greatest form since 1990's [b]Goodfellas[/b].

With an all-star cast, every performance is impressive and feels unforced and truly gripping. Non-fans of Leonardo DiCaprio will definitely gain a new appreciation of his acting ability after seeing this film. And while DiCaprio definitely proves himself as a strong actor, others like Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen and Jack Nicholson only further prove their cinematic prowess, as they provide gritty urban performances, and evidently had some fun with them.

Beginning with a beautifully photographed slow motion tracking shot of Jack Nicholson's profile as he walks through a warehouse, it sets the stage for a brutal yet elegant cinematic experience. Scorsese is at the top of his game with this film, showing that he can still deliver the sweeping camera movements and dark, shockingly violent content, while also showing that he has matured as a filmmaker. Like [b]Goodfellas[/b], the film is narrated by its main character - in this case, Frank Costello (played by Jack Nicholson). He's a hardboiled, uncompromising gangster in Boston with a sick sense of humor and, at 70 years of age, a libido that would make most 19 year old men feel ashamed. But he still has the signature Jack Nicholson charm, where you can't help but like the guy regardless of how horrible a man he is.

The script is smart. Really smart. It takes what was in [b]Infernal Affairs[/b] and plants in like a seed, growing something entirely different out of the soil of the American film industry. It's less about the style and more about the story and the issues it raises about both organized crime and the police force whom we trust with our lives to protect us. In an incredible scene involving Leonardo DiCaprio - an ex-cop working undercover for crime lord Nicholson - in which he confronts his superior officer about Nicholson, he says ""Why haven't you arrested him on one of the million other things that you've seen him do, that I've seen him do. The man murders somebody, what are you waiting for?"" It transgresses the usual good cop, bad cop formula and becomes a hard-hitting moral tale.

Martin Scorsese's [b]The Departed[/b] is, without a doubt, the best film of the year, and in a surprising turn, one of the best films of Scorsese's entire career. It oozes with energy and keeps a taut pace through its entire 153 minute running time. It's gritty, violent, and shocking, but it's all for a good cause. [b]The Departed[/b] tells one hell of a story, and it shouldn't be missed.

9","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-15","","RT","900601323"
4.0,"So last night I saw the Departed and I enjoyed it, it was a little too long as I kept checking my watch, but that could be because I was running on four hours of sleep and it was getting late. But anyway.. the movie...
  
  Acting was very good, great cast selection. I really didn't like DeCaprio in the Aviator, but in this he was very good at playing the insecure tough guy. Same with Damon, dont usually like the roles he plays but this one held my attention. And of course, the one indication of a great movie.. Mr. Mark Wahlberg. If he's in the movie you can guarentee swearing, tough guy attitudes, and one bad ass fuckin movie. (see: Four Brothers)
  
  The story was very interesting, very violent, defiinitely an R movie, with a lot of swearing although I thought it fit well because I didnt really notice all the swearing until someone pointed it out. I think it just helped with the feel of the movie. Also there was a lot of humor to keep the audience interested, since it was a little long. At some points of the movie I found myself a little lost as to what was going on, but that could also be a side affect of sleep depravation.
  
  Overall, very enjoyable movie, probably buy worthy when it comes out on DVD (or blu-ray). And thats saying a lot, as I usually dont buy movies. But yeah definitely a movie that I'll be able to watch time and time again.
  
  Great acting, interesting storyline, a little bit of crude humor = ""8"".","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-15","","RT","900916394"
