3.0,"[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]THE DEPARTED[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]For years, the New York Rangers hockey team had the highest payroll in the league.  The team was loaded with superstars, making it nothing short of all-star caliber.  Unfortunately, with all that talent, the Rangers went nowhere.  The stars did not, or could not gel and for a good number of seasons, the Rangers, despite superstars at every position, continually missed the playoffs.  I mention the scenario only because it fits so perfectly for THE DEPARTED the latest oeuvre from director and craftsman Martin Scorsese.  The movie boasts Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Marky-Mark, Martin Sheen, Jack Nicholson and Alec Baldwin in its cast.  There are enough liberal votes there to ensure Hillary receives the Democratic nod.  Still, when all is said and done, THE DEPARTED is lacking.  Even with all this talent, it doesnt make the playoffs.[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]First, the film is not very complementary to the Irish, nor to the city of Boston.   After viewing this movie, youll be left with the impression Boston is run by a gang of Irish thugs and not the Kennedys (wait, perhaps they are synonymous) and that every cop on the Boston police force is dirty, or on their way to becoming dirty.  The Boston PD cant be happy with this depiction.[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][size=3][font=Times New Roman]William Monahan has written an intricate, meandering script.  While the tale is interesting, its simply too long.  He drives home character traits like the proverbial sledge hammer, assuming the audience is dense and will need several depictions of the same thing to get it.  [/font][/size]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]KEY SCENES TO LOOK FOR:[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]1.[/size]      [size=3]DICAPRIOS FIRST MEETING WITH MR. FRENCH[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]2.[/size]      [size=3]THE CAST SEARCHING SCENE[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]3.[/size]      [size=3]MARKY-MARKS STUNNING CONCLUSION[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]A few key notes to mention, THE DEPARTED features four actors who were prominently represented in Team America as members of the Film Actors Guild (FAG).  Audiences still remember and their names drew snickers (not the candy bar) during the credits.   Leonardo Decapitated is actually starting to show signs of talent.  I thought Id never utter such sacrilege, and will attribute it to Scorseses direction.  Matt Damon still cant act, Baldwin continues to be a parody of himself, Martin Sheen is in desperate need of a comedy and Jack Nicholson is amazing in anything he does, though its never really explained how he is in an Irish yet has the name Costello.  Vera Farmiga is the love interest and the sole selection of poor casting.  Shes very plain, yet is supposed to be able to seduce both lead stars.  Not likely, though she does display a nice derriere during a bedroom romp. [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]THE DEPARTED is a tale of approximately 100 minutes thats jammed into nearly 180 minutes.    The movie is the type of film most producers would release if not for the viewers; no happy ending, a bit too realistic and designed to purposely leave you with a downer feeling.  So much talent[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]THE RATING FOR THE DEPARTED = C[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left][font=Times New Roman][size=3]BFCA RATING = 6/10[/size][/font]
[/left]
[left]
[/left]","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2006-10-15","","RT","900732021"
