4.5,"""The Departed"" is very stylish, maybe a little too stylish for it's own good, but it delivers an extremely well-rendered and powerful story.  It tells us about two different kinds of cops, both work for the police, but one deceives the police by working with the bad guys and one decieves the bad guys by working with the police.  
 
I don't know how Martin churns out brilliant movie after brilliant movie.  He's a fucking genius, I don't know how else to say it.  Hot damn I love that man.  
 
The performances are, of course, spectacular.  Matt Damon reminded me a little bit of his role in The Talented Mr. Ripley here, although this role doesn't require as much skill.  Leonardo De Caprio, in my opinion, actually brings the best performance of the main three to the table.  It was the most complex, and he does it with convincing quality.  Jack is wonderful as always as an eccentric leader in organized crime, but there are times when I see him overacting a little.  Despite that, I wouldn't be surprised to see a nomination for him, or especially for DeCaprio, maybe for Matt Damon, too.  Just superb stuff, I love it.  
 
The psychology woman was the least convincing of all aspects of the film, but perhaps that's because her big sappy blue eyes and hot body seemed to be upstaging her acting.  Some was there, but other stuff wasn't.  Definitely nothing that hurt the film, but it certainly didn't add to it as much as it should, or could have.  
 
All in all, yet another awesome movie from America's greatest modern director.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-16","","RT","900890844"
