[left][b]Producers:[/b] Roy Lee, Doug Davison, Gianni Nunnari, Brad Pitt[/left]
[left][b]Composer:[/b] Howard Shore[/left]
[left][b]Screenwriter:[/b] William Monahan, Felix Chong and Alan Mak (Source Material from screenplay: [b]Infernal Affairs[/b])[/left]
[left][b]Director:[/b] Martin Scorsese[/left]
[left][b]Running Time:[/b] 2 hrs. 30 min.[/left]
[left][img]http://cdn.nflximg.com/us/pages/parental_ratings/rating_R.gif[/img] - Strong Brutal Violence, Pervasive Language, Some Strong Sexual Content, Drug Material[/left]
 
[left][b]Plot Outline:[/b] Two undercover agents must discover the identity of the other before it's too late.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/mark_wahlberg19.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]Director Martin Scorsese has come up with a string of films that have been met with muted enthusiasm. This time around he returns to his street crime roots and gives his old and familiar genre new tricks. [b]The Departed[/b] is a Boston set remake of Hong Kong's movie [b]Infernal Affairs[/b], which had mainstream American critics raving over. With a clever, local screenwriter (William Monaham), a legendary director, and a gifted cast you have yourself a fairly cocky film. However, its arrogance is warranted as it is a rare breed where it has all the looks as well as brains.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson9.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]Young undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is assigned to infiltrate the head of the mob, Costello (Jack Nicholson). While Billy is gaining Costello's trust, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), is a hardened young criminal who has happened to become a police officer and quickly rising to power in Boston's Special Investigation Unit. Each man is gathering information about the plans of the organization he has penetrated. However, both the police and the mob become aware that there is a rat in their midst. Now that both organizations are aware that their internal security has been compromised, both Billy and Colin have found themselves in danger of being caught. They must now race against one another, not knowing who is the rat on the other side, and uncover the identity of the other man before he himself gets discovered.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/matt_damon20.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]It is very fascinating to see the differences and similarities between Billy and Colin. They are naturally two sides of the same coin. Working in the Special Investigation Unit, Colin lives the upper-class life in a high-class apartment. Billy, on the other hand, stoops down to the role of the government-issued foot soldier. As months go by Billy becomes more and more accustomed to the lifestyle of a street criminal. Costello goes by the philosophy (which is introduced early in the film) that good and evil are never more abstract than when you are on the business end of a loaded gun. DiCaprio does such a good job in portraying a conflicted character that the viewers will find it reasonably easy to side with Billy and dismiss his erratic behavior rather than judge it.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/leonardo_dicaprio17.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]While Nicholson's notable theatrical performance seems Oscar-worthy, both DiCaprio and Damon are equally convincing as they inhabit the world of Boston tough guys. Their world consists of casual sexism, racism, and rough-and-tumble physicality. Alec Baldwin and Mark Wahlberg deliver great work as the police higher-ups; and Martin Sheen performs exceedingly well as being the only officer who seems to be the most level-headed in the entire unit. [/left]
 
[left]With a stellar cast you have a commanding presence on screen throughout. This is one of Scorsese's rare films where it does not overtly cry out ""a Scorsese film"". And that is a compliment to the director because he has wisely chosen a capable cast that can share the spotlight and glory equally alongside with the crew that works behind the cameras. [/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/mark_wahlberg/thedeparted1.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]Scorsese demonstrates a renewed confidence in this film with extraordinary vigor. It is a stylish and intelligent bully of a Hong Kong-like gangster movie. The movie is kept in remarkable steady balance with verbal and visual gifts throughout, offering plenty of substance for the viewers to digest. Each character and every aspect of the story offers a unique form of existentialism with genuine suspense and intrigue.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/alec_baldwin/thedeparted1.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]The movie has abundance of style. Scorsese has always been a keen chooser of music. He leans on some of his old favorites like ""Gimme Shelter"" from the Rolling Stones, stamping his movie with that song. He also freshens up the film a bit by using the Boston punkers Dropkick Murphys' ""I'm Shipping Up To Boston"". That song is the perfect encapsulation of the film's attitude. It is hardcore and in your face. Scorsese is clearly a man that loves style and knows how to be relevant in virtually any era. Through the years as being one of Hollywood's most passionate filmmakers, he has roamed far and wide in the diversity of his films.[/left]
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson13.jpg[/img][/center]
[left]Delivering classics like [b]Raging Bull[/b], [b]Mean Streets[/b] and [b]Casino[/b], Martin Scorsese returns to his roots of street drama. He has always had a sentimental touch whenever he brought things down to the street level. Whether it is the protagonist lone [b]Taxi Driver[/b] or the mobbed-up [b]Goodfellas[/b], Scorsese is always seemingly able to use an artistic brush to color for us a world that is brutal and confident, strutting around like the toasts of the town. [b]The Departed[/b] is definitely no exception. While this is a return to his older genre, there are new elements found within this film. It is another complimentary addition to his other street-leveled films.[/left]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-16","","RT","900690782"
