4.0,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_22.jpg[/img]
 
I need to go back and watch Infernal Affairs again, but I'm pretty sure that it was a better movie than this - much more taught and twisty. And after all, as an Asian film buff, to say this remake is better would be hearsay. I guess I'd have to cash in my Thai film collection or something.
 
Anyway, this is still pretty good, with plenty of cool supporting players. And it's a Scorsese movie. But it feels like Scorsese making a Scorsese movie - not a Scorsese movie, like Goodfellas, Mean Streets or Casino. There's something missing. I couldn't really make a connection with it, not like I could those other films.
 
Part of it is neither of the rats were likeable. DiCaprio's just a whining smart ass, and Damon's duplicitious son-of-a-bitch. When they finally meet and starting beating each other up, I was glad, though I wasn't sure who to root for.
 
And that's one thing Infernal Affairs had going for it. I remember spending most of the movie trying to figure out which guy was which, and in doing so developed sympathy for them. But here, I can tell Leo from Matt. They look nothing alike. Except both are weasels, or rather, rats.
 
I guess the really cool thing is that Anthony Anderson is in a Martin Scorsese movie. It's like something out of Entourage. As with a lot of these kinds of movies, it's the background players I enjoy most: Ray Winstone, Kevin Conway, Martin Sheen and especially Mark Wahlberg.
 
Jack Nicholson is a force of nature that no Boston accent can tame.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-20","","RT","900602572"
