5.0,"The Departed is easily the best film released thus far in 2006.  Some are saying that this is Martin Scorseses best film since Goodfellas, but I would stretch that even further as his best film since 1980s Raging Bull.  Scorsese should finally get his long overdue Oscar for this film, and at this point in awards season, it has to be a shoo-in for a Best Picture nomination, if not a win.

	The story weaves a very complicated web with the entanglements of two Massachusetts State police officers.  Leonardo DiCaprio plays Billy Costigan, a cop assigned to infiltrate the Irish mafia of Boston headed by the notorious Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson).  Matt Damon plays Colin Sullivan, the surrogate son of Costello, who is rising in the ranks of the police department while working as an informant to his father.

	The police department is looking to finally nail Costello after years of evading the law, but Sullivan continually thwarts its efforts.  The plot thickens when both the police and the mafia realize that there is a rat amongst them.  Sullivan is put in charge of the investigation to find the mole (himself) and Costello goes on a hunt to find the inside man in his crime ring.

	The story develops into a cat and mouse game between Sullivan and Costigan after they realize that the key to saving themselves is to out the other.  And if that is not already too complicated, Costigan decides to see a police psychiatrist (Vera Farmiga) after overwhelming anxiety that Costello will find out his true identity.  The psychiatrist he sees is just so happens to be the girlfriend of Costigan.

	Martin Sheen, Alec Baldwin, and Mark Wahlberg round out the cast, each turning in fantastic supporting performances as detectives working with either Sullivan or Costigan and getting caught in intrigue themselves.  A finer cast could not have been assembled for this picture, with DiCaprio and Damon delivering career-best performances.  As always, Jack Nicholson plays his role to perfection, developing Frank Costello into a ruthless, coked-out mafia dictator, deserving of another Academy Award for the veteran actor.

	The Departed takes unforeseen twists and turns that are jaw dropping but inevitable.  The story, although complicated, never becomes too confusing or implausible, and is genuinely fun and thoroughly engrossing.  The script precisely balances both humor and horror, providing the audience with the most fun it will have at the theater all year.

This movie is nearly perfect.  Its A-list actors all turn in fantastic performances.  Its dialogue is extremely realistic with its vulgarity and wittiness.  Its rock and roll soundtrack featuring songs from The Rolling Stones and other classic artists works perfectly with the story.  Most importantly, Scorsese is back to his original, brilliant form dealing with the mean streets of Boston.  The only complaint I have with this film is that at two and a half hours, it is a little too long, but you dont even realize it because of how enthralling the story is. The Departed has staked its claim as the film to beat this awards season, and I do not foresee any other film slated for year-end release taking away its title.  This will be the best movie of the year.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-21","","RT","900829656"
