[font=Andy][size=3][color=white][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/custom/16/1159016.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font]
[font=Andy][size=3][color=white]So how can I rate the original ([u]Infernal Affairs[/u]) an eight when it 's more or less the same film? The answer is simple, Martin Scorsese. By now, Im sure most have seen the [u]The Departed[/u]. It's the most visceral and exciting film experience Ive had all year. It's holds you to the screen and doesn't let go for the quick two and a half  hour running time. The directing is amazing. It's clear youre watching one of the world's greatest filmmakers deliver a new classic as soon as the title appears on the screen. If the academy doesn't give Mr. Scorsese his long overdue Oscar this time, it will be quite clear that the end is neigh. I also can't say enough about the acting it the film. Leo gives a performance Brando would be proud of. Damon goes against type, playing a complete prick so naturally I wanted to shoot him myself. Jack is, well, Jack .Same as Alec Baldwin. Both are incredibly funny. Walberg and Sheen are great as well. In short, it's a modern classic.[/color][/size][/font]
[font=Andy][size=3][color=white] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][color=white][font=Andy][url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/infernal_affairs/dvd.php?select=1""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/29/239129.jpg[/img][/url][/font][/color][/color][/size]
[size=3][color=#000000][color=white][font=Andy]The [/font][font=Andy]Hong Kong[/font][/color][font=Andy][color=white] original is the same film, only leaner, with less character, and no sense of humor. That's not to say it's a bad film. The director Wai Keung Lau has a style that Im sure Martin related to. The camera is moving constantly and Lau gives us a real sense of the city the story is set in. If you loved [u]The Departed,[/u] it's a great rental. It's has enough differences in the plot, mostly the ending, to keep you interested. Mainly the music is bad and the film can be over dramatic at points when it needs to be gritty and real. I'm glad this film was made if only for me to view [u]The Departed[/u] repeatedly when it's released on DVD.[/color] [/font][/color][/size]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-22","","RT","900830848"
