4.5,"Martin Scorsese has always been referred to as ""the iconic director who never won a Best Director Oscar"", and it's usually been for many reasons, whether it be an apparent feud with someone influential on the Academy of Voters, or quite simply put, he brings out his great movies when there are those even greater out.

This is not Scorsese's best. It's not even up there with his best. You think it outdoes <i>Kundun</i>? I laugh at you. However, how good it is COMPARED to his other films actually doesn't matter. At all. What matters is how good THIS piece alone is.

The answer? Pretty damn good. Granted, a stellar performance from Leo DiCaprio still doesn't shake the feeling of predictability from his character. After the third sudden assault on someone who calls him a name, we kinda get the idea that nearly everyone he comes across is going to get some sort of ownage done to them. Matt Damon on the other hand is a joy to watch, restrained and roughed all at once, avoiding the usual pussyfooting attitude previous roles of his have been used to.

What about Jack Nicholson? Outstanding as per usual. This role was clearly tailor made for Mr. Nicholson, with every little cheeky sadism thrown in for good measure. Watch with glee as he calmly converses with a disturbed DiCaprio while he plays with a severed hand like it was his dinner. Watch his trademark grin come into action despite being shot in the chest. Hear his voice express every little conniving tone of his attitude. Clearly an Oscar nomination lies in wait. Perhaps not a win (the critics and viewers alike welcome Michael Caine in <i>Children Of Men</i> for the Supporting honors), but still some lauding nonetheless. Clearly the standout of this impressive lineup.

Now to the actual film and plot at hand. It works, and the direction is well-founded, in fact I'd call it Scorsese at the top of his game directing-wise. The fact that the title of the movie plays upon a subtle twist that comes into effect right at the end also helps, and the twists and turns that plague the film are a welcome addition. The shock-factor is upped too, as is the level of violence. An element of predictability where it shouldn't be predictable maybe let's the third act down, but every other stage that the film runs through is pretty well-formed. That fat black guy from the Scary Movie series really hasn't much cause for being in this film, especially since the character he plays is quite vital to the endpoint, despite a grand total of two minutes screentime.

Still, all in all it's one of the best movies this year, and Scorsese is clearly back in shape after the disappointing <i>The Aviator</i>.

UPDATE: Another half star added... as a contrast to the inferior <i>Infernal Affairs</i>.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-22","","RT","102166502"
5.0,"what can I say, this  is one of the best movies of the year, and the best with a mob theme since goodfellas, its one of Scorsese's finest jobs, you can notice that it is directed by him from the very first minute of the movie, the camera movements, the language, everything. It has a lot of action and adventure. The suspense is on its highest level so you'll be on the edge of your seat for the entire movie, it also have moments of good humor. The cast is excellent, beggining with Leonardo DiCaprio perhaps its best performance ever in my opinion, Jack Nicholson is  great as always, Matt Damon and Mark Walhberg are also very good, Even Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin are great although their characters are secondary, and the end of this movie is so impressive and unexpected, that you won't believe it","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-22","","RT","51167377"
