5.0,"In the opening monolouge of Martin Scorsese's ""The Departed"", Jack Nicholson's Frank Costello spews an offensive racial slur that lets us know right off the bat that this time around, we won't get the usual nice-guy Jack we've come to know lately. From the tone of his voice and the firmness of his words, there's no doubt that Costello is a cold-hearted criminal. In the first ten minutes, Costello makes an interesting observation:

""When I was your age, they would say that you could become cops or criminals. What I'm sayin' to ya is this: When you're facing a loaded gun, what's the difference?""

""The Departed"" explores the thin line between cops and criminals while providing thoughtful moral questions about what's really the right thing. ""The Departed"" is the year's best film, pure and simple, plain and true. It's brutal, bloody, and profane, but that's what makes it work so well.

The film tells the tale of two young Boston police officers: Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio in his best performance to date) and Colin Sullivan (the always teriffic Matt Damon). Sullivan soared to the top in Police Academy while Costigan was kicked out due to his family's questionable past. But Costigan is given a chance to go deep undercover to spy on Boston's most notorious crimelord, Frank Costello (played by Jack Nicholson in an outstanding performance). For years, the Special Investigations Unit inside the Massachusetts State Police Department has been trying to build a case against Costello, but they need hard evidence in order to do so. And that's where Costigan comes in. He's to act the usual street criminal and report to police captain Oliver Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) on any criminal or shady activities.

Meanwhile, Colin Sullivan is promoted to Staff Seargent and is soon given the job of locating a suspected mole within the Special Investigations Unit. Ironic, because the mole is in fact Sullivan himself; he's been in with Costello ever since he was a kid growing up in the corrupt streets of Boston. But as it so happens, Queenan wants Sullivan to do his best on finding the mole as well while Costello orders Sullivan to locate the cop in his crew. And so the cat and mouse games begin.

Meanwhile, Costigan's position soon takes its toll on him mentally and he starts to see a psychiatrist named Madolyn (newcomer Vera Farmiga), with whom Sullivan is romantically involved. As the two moles slowly come closer to unmasking each other, things get worse in the crime world, and it all leads up to many bloody showdowns and shootouts in which anyone and anything goes.

This is one of the few, but the best mobster movie I've ever seen thus far. Martin Scorsese practically created this genre and he returns here to his roots in top form. Scorsese explores the dark side of every character involved in this story, making the film all the more gripping. The real standouts are Leonardo DiCaprio (who could be Scorsese's new DeNiro) and Jack Nicholson. DiCaprio's character is the most complex because he's the most human person here. He doesn't enjoy the work he's doing, but yet he does it anyway because on some level, he's aware that it's the right thing to do. Nicholson is deliciously evil as Frank Costello; he can exude insanity while still managing to be sane. There's one scene in particular that I remember where Costigan meets Costello in a bar. Costigan is repeatedly denying that he's the rat within Costello's syndicate, and all the while we're watching with bated breath, wondering how Costello will react and what he'll say next because by then, we know how terrifying he can be.

But probably the most effective scene is one that doesn't involve dialogue at all. It involves both Costigan and Sullivan dialing each other up on cellphones and just listening to each other breathe. The silence here is able to speak for them and it is able to work.

Matt Damon gives another excellent performance as Colin Sullivan. His character is dark, sometimes brooding, and downright slimy. And being the good actor that he is, Damon pulls it off without overdoing it. Vera Farmiga is also good in her first movie role.

It goes without saying that ""The Departed"" is surprisingly the year's best film. It's an instant modern American classic in its genre and a delight for anyone who still enjoys and appreciates the power of movies.

MY RATING: **** (out of four stars.)","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-10-23","","RT","900917180"
