3.5,"[center][img]http://i54.photobucket.com/albums/g118/sickman777/infernalaffairs.jpg[/img] [img]http://i54.photobucket.com/albums/g118/sickman777/thedeparted.jpg[/img][font=Times New Roman][color=wheat][/center]
 
 
[font=Trebuchet MS][size=3]If I hadn't seen [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] before, I would probably enjoy [i]The Departed[/i] much more. The original is one of the most daring and heartfelt takes on the criminal world coming from Hong Kong as well as one of my favorite movies of 2004. Scorsese's film surely won't bear such a title in my book as it is step-by-step remake in which solely the ending differs from the excellent original. I really anticipated the screenplay going beyond well-known formula and less derivative story. Thus, the show seemed absorbing, but only slightly exciting for me. I also think that [i]The Departed[/i] isn't any improvement over [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]. The predecessor had a bit better developed characters and was more tense and profound. Not to mention the whole concept and numerous both accurate and gripping scenes that make the movie so incredibly intense. However, [i]The Departed[/i] is also a very slick and superbly-directed movie which can result in a well-deserved Oscar for Scorsese who embroidered the whole story so as to fit in his style and American standards. What's more, the casting is great and the movie is extremely well-acted (once again - just like the original). Jack Nicholson is a real stand-out as an Irish mob boss, but the other actors are very close to equal him. All in all, I definitely recommend you [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] instead of [i]The Departed[/i] which exploits the same idea once again with only minor changes. [/size][/font]

 
