3.5,"[font=Arial][size=3]I must confess to having suffered potential pre-screening overhype on this one, seeing the names Scorsese, Nicholson, Damon, and DiCaprio on the billboard advertising this flick. Saw the high percentage on the Tomato-meter (93%). Read the plot synopsis: Boston police officer (Leonardo DiCaprio) works undercover as a member of the Irish mob, while leader of said gang (Jack Nicholson) has his own mole in the police force (Matt Damon), each one being hot on the trail of the other. Very intriguing. Hard to screw this one up... and with Scorsese's direction, highly unlikely.[/size][/font]
 
[font=Arial][size=3]Two and a half hours later, I'm feeling strangely unsatisfied. Certain violent and profane scenes/sequences in [i]The Departed [/i]shocked me back to an attention that was lost in the haze of the Howard Shore soundtrack and the alleged slice-of-life sequences of cops being cops, gangsters being gangsters, cops being gangsters, and vice versa, but no matter who is who, we're all pretty much the same, 'cause of these guns we're pointin' in each other's faces. The damn movie moved along like George Romero zombies on Darvon.[/size][/font]
 
[font=Arial][size=3]Sorry, folks. All the Oscar talk around this one? [i]The Departed [/i]is nowhere near as entertaining as [i]Goodfellas, Taxi Driver, The King of Comedy... [/i]shall I go on? And the acting just didn't quite cut it. I was not watching gang leader Frank Costello. I was watching Jack Nicholson. In Hal Ashby's [i]The Last Detail,[/i] I knew I was watching a younger Jack play the character of Billy Buddusky. For about two minutes. Then the tough yet paternal character became a flesh-and-blood human being who taught Randy Quaid's Meadows how to drink, screw, and return an unsatisfactory snack dive hamburger, among other things. Not Frank Costello. He was Jack the Joker, with an occasional ""heeere's Johnny"" upward eyeballs roll. Even the more sympathetic characters in [i]The Departed [/i]had not a tenth of the interactive chemistry or individual gravity of the three leads in [i]Goodfellas [/i](four, if you count Lorraine Bracco). [/size][/font]

[font=Arial][size=3]Capable performances by Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg (impressive as a loutish police officer), Vera Farmiga, and Ray Winstone keep [i]The Departed [/i]from sinking all the way to the bottom of the well. Also of minor success was that to me, the movie was steeped in the grit and meanness of the hard side of Boston.[/size][/font]","5a7e0075-e0e5-4d62-906e-8675dd996aa7",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2006-10-27","","RT","900770470"
