4.0,"Jack Nicholson and Matt Damon headline this star-studded movie about deception between the Massachusetts State Police and the Irish mafia.  The story really requires no preface; any spoilers would really ruin the art of Scorcece's craftiness.  In a sentence, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) infiltrates the Massachusetts State Police in order to help a mob boss and father figure, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson); in response, the police send an undercover cop (Leonardy DiCaprio) to take down Costello.  All of the lauded male actors deliver fantastic performances.  Damon appears as the tragic figure who struggles to balance love and greed.  Dicaprio plays the fidgety and underappreciated foil who finds himself at the brink of death in every scene.  The two men never even interact until towards the later scenes of the movie, but Scorcece does a wonderful job in building up to the tense moment.  Nicholson delivers somewhat of a Joker-esque performance in which his character appears just barely sane.  Some other notable cast members include the hilarious Alec Baldwin and Martin Sheen.  Vera Farmiga, who plays a psychiatrist and love interest to the two leads, is the only real weak link in the entire movie.  She is an extremely incompetent psychiatrist who cannot even keep her own patients in check.  While [i]The Departed[/i] does boast a great cast, I was not completely blown away by the storyline.  In fact, much of it was rather predictable; and the movie drags along in its final 15-20 minutes.  In other words, while engaging, the plot takes a back seat to the individual performances.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-10-28","","RT","900905369"
3.5,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/custom/16/1159016.jpg[/img]
 
