4.5,"[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=white]Over the last decade Scorsese has released some very worthy titles to his already sterling reputation but none of them have been all that many believe the director is capable of. Fortunately for us the master has just swooped back in with his new crime drama [i]The Departed[/i], sporting such powerhouse actors as Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, and Alec Baldwin. The setting is South Boston where the Irish Mob rules the underground led by Frank Costello, played by the suitably insane Jack Nicholson, who is in totalitarian control of his gang and his city. The police have been trailing Nicholsons character for years and have finally found a possibility of bringing the kingpin down by inserting a fresh new police officer (DiCaprio) into prison to infiltrate Costellos crime syndicate and become his right-hand man. Meanwhile Costellos mob has its own plans by recruiting one of its own (Damon) into the ranks of the Boston Police Force in order to keep Costello informed on busts and police crackdowns. In the process of their subterfuge both sides learn of their moles and assign both their operatives to hunt each other down and arrest or eliminate one another, no matter what the cost. [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][font=Times New Roman][color=white]            Though this is a remake of Hong Kongs [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] this film is a perfect stand-alone taking the crime genre to a new level of intensity and showing some of Scorseses best work since [i]Goodfellas[/i] (1990). Scorsese keeps the action rolling from the very beginning with his trademark use of Rolling Stones music and a narrative by Nicholson to set up the play while asserting Nicholsons dominance over South Boston as he kills and extorts carefree of the consequences. As with many of Scorseses films he keeps the directing style much like the plotline of the movie, and so [i]The Departed[/i] starts as a confusing rush through events and parallel recruitment of Damon and DiCaprio into Costellos mob and the police academy. As the film goes on the pace slows as the characters begin to realize the situation that is at hand and so the intensity builds with Damon and DiCaprio playing the suitable cat-and-mouse games through the streets of Boston. Scorsese also throws in some of the most amazingly subtle artistic embellishments that he has shown in his career as a director. He has one shot of Damons character speaking to his fellow officers with a single shaft of light on the wall behind him perfectly angled through his body as if one could see the two sides there are to his character. The film is simply a treasure-trove of such beautiful shots and sequences and still the action is not slowed or the plot made boring as the intensity continues to rise. [/color][/font][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman][color=white]            Scorseses choice to come back to crime film has only enforced how he continues to be regarded as the master of the genre ever since [i]Mean Streets, Goodfellas, [/i]and [i]Gangs of New York[/i].  The master has struck again and made another American crime classic and hopefully the Academy will finally recognize that and give Mr. Scorsese what he has been deserving for so many years.[/color] [/font][/color][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-11-02","","RT","900702715"
