4.5,"[center][b]The Departed[/b]
    [b]Review By: Wildfire Sky[/b]
   [/center]
   [center]
   [/center]
     [center] [img]http://www.themovieblog.com/archives/Departed-Poster.jpg[/img]
   [/center]
 
 [b]Release Date: [/b][i]October 6, 2006
 [/i][b]Rating: [/b][i]R
 [/i][b]Genre: [/b][i]Drama, Gangster, Crime
 [/i][b]Run Time: [/b][i]2 hours, 29 minutes[/i][b]
 Distributor:[/b][i] Warner Bros.[/i]
 [b]Director: [/b][i]Martin Schorsese[/i]
 [b]Writer:[/b][i] William Monahan[/i]
 [b]Cast:[/b][i] Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicolson, Mark Wahlburg[/i]

*********************************************************

Schorsese has once again, taken us for a ride that you just don't want to stop. [b]The Departed[/b] has answered the call for a 2006 movie that won't leave us wanting at the end. Finally, we can go home from a movie saying to ourselves: ""well, that was fun!"" and have everyone agree with you. If this film doesn't win an Academy Award, then it will definately give the winner a run for their money!
   
   [center][img]http://media3.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/photo/2006/10/05/PH2006100501860.jpg[/img]
   [/center]
 
 Set in downtown streets of Boston, [b]The Departed[/b] tells the tale of a group of men intertwined with each other in more ways than one. A rookie Bostonian police officer, Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio), who by the basis of his temper and street urchin unbringings makes him an excellent candidate to bring down a heavy-weight Irish mobster, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) that the Boston Police Department has been trying to bring down for years. However, as he sinks deeper and deeper into the mob bosses activities, Costigan seriously begins to question whether or not his current career choice is a healthy one.
   
   [center][img]http://www.kino-govno.com/images/departed/departed_2s.jpg[/img]
   
   [/center]
   But Costigan and Costello are only one part of this story. You also have Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), another member of the BPD, but Sullivan is about as two-faced as some one can get. Unfortunately for Sullivan, his light and darksides clash repeatedly that cause for interesting interaction with other characters. Sullivan, Costello, and Costigan are involved in a tip-toeing dance around each other that ends with a violent, climatic clash that has everyone in the theatre going: ""WHOA! What just [i]happened[/i]?""
   
   [center][img]http://www.movie-list.com/d/departed.jpg[/img]
   
   [/center]
   With a slew of other big name actors like Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin, and Martin Sheen acting as supporting characters that hold a much more significant presence on screen that almost lends them to leading roles. You don't see them a lot, hell, they disappear for periods of time only to jump back into the fray with something equivalent to an explosion. Yet, even with the lack of screentime, they have such powerful presence that you don't have time to realize you've missed them.
   
   [center][img]http://z.about.com/d/movies/1/8/E/h/N/departedreview2.jpg[/img]
   
   [/center]
   At times, the movie can seem to drag, but you're not held in that place for more than a few moments before something dramatic happens and you're brought right back in. Schorsese plays this yo-yo trick with the audience for the full three hours...a three hours you don't even notice until you're walking out and happen to look at your watch (""what the..."").
   
   [center][img]http://images.ctv.ca/archives/CTVNews/img2/20061003/160_departed2_061003.jpg[/img]
   
   [/center]
   [b]The Departed[/b] is something that anyone can enjoy, if they let themselves look past the use of derogatory terms throughout the movie. Believe it or not, it gives the movie a sense of realism and makes it that much more powerful. It's not very often that you see a movie enjoyed by so many that it could easily offend and not be some sort of horrible comedy.
   
   [center][img]http://www.projo.com/photos/20061006/LB1006_departed_10-06-06_PV297V4.jpg[/img]
   
   [/center]
   [b]The Departed[/b] has charged up the ranks of movies (yet, for some God awful reason, [b]Saw III[/b] surpassed it recently), pulling in $9.8 million dollars and settling in a firm 2nd place on RT.com. To not see this movie would be like denying yourself that last slice of chocolate cake because of a diet. Eat it, damn it! Eat it and enjoy it!","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-11-02","","RT","900920578"
4.5,"A whole lot of body bags in this one, brilliant as ever Mr. Scorsese.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-11-02","","RT","98589970"
1.0,"I was very excited to go and see this film. It has a great cast. It was directed by the same man who brought us Goodfellas (probably my favorite mob movie of all time btw!). The previews looked amazing. Unfortunately you will only walk away from this movie VERY dissapointed! For the first good while I was excited. It started slow and then grew very interesting by the mid mark. I was thinking that it was going to be a great movie. But all of the buildup goes absolutely nowhere by the end of this film! It has one of the WORST endings I have ever seen! Talk about a total anticlimatic joykill! I should have been somewhat forewarned with Scorcsese's recent track record. His last several movies have been pretty crappy as well. I however, love a good crime drama and after seeing the previews and reading all of the amazing reviews I felt that I had to go see this. I mean are all of the critics today on crack or what? Anyway, don't waste your money on the theater and don't bother renting this.","",False,False,False,False,1.0,"2006-11-02","","RT","900921389"
