5.0,"Maybe go f&$^ yourself
 
 This is an excellent film. The only reason I dont give it a 10 is because I can imagine not ""everyone"" liking the movie as much as I did.
 
 Summary:
 Martin Scorsese's ""The Departed"" is an adaption of the smash hit from Hong Kong ""Infernal Affairs"". The location is moved from Hong Kong to Boston and culture from Hong Kong inner city to Boston Irish Mafia. The rest is the same. The police (Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen) send young Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) undercover into Mafia Kings Costello's (Jack Nicholson) brood. What they don't know, however, is that Costello already has a rat in the police in the form of Sullivan (Matt Damon). This is the basic setup of this intensely gripping and tremendously satisfying gangster drama. To spell out anymore would be doing the readers a gross injustice. 
 Oh but lookout for Alec Baldwin. He's supporting role is truly delightful and he brings out a lot of the subtle humor in the film; in typical baldwin style of course.
 
 Acting:
 Three cheers all around. I couldn't think of anyone who did a less than stellar job (except for the female lead, Vera Fermiga, whose was just adequate). Mark Wahlberg has the best lines by far and its a treat to watch him deliver them with such style.
 
 Direction/screenplay/cinemetography
 5 stars. This is a true return to form for Scorcese who after meeting with mixed responses to his ""Aviator"" and ""Gangs of New York"" (both excellent movies too) has won widespread acclaim from the critics and the public. In a sense he has really gotten back to those roots he defined with ""Goodfellas"". William Monahan has adapted the HK screenplay in the best possible way. The script is tight, the lines solid, the pace quick, what else can you ask for? And I also love the fact he set it in the Boston Irish culture giving him so many interesting ways to adapt the story.
 
 Overall:
 If you haven't seen the Departed, you need to drop whatever you're doing and go see it now. I garuntee you won't be sorry.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-11-16","","RT","900925255"
