3.5,"Having never seen Internal Affairs, I can only review this film on its own merits, of which there are certainly many. Having said that, I found myself disagreeing with the general critical consensus on this one. At no point during the viewing of this film was I particularly absorbed or affected. The performances by the absurdly star studded cast left almost nothing to be desired, and Scorsese was competent as always, if unspectacular. I just didn't ever experience the feelings that I associate with watching a great film. It certainly isn't among Scorsese's best, and I found it to be his weakest effort since Bringing Out The Dead.
     Scorsese did bring us another classic film villain, and while Nicholson never lets up in his entertaining portrayal of Frank Costello, I couldn't help but be constantly reminded of Daniel Day Lewis' Bill the Butcher character in the superior Gangs of New York. The characters are very similar antagonists, and these things always work better the first time around. We are treated to Matt Damon in full blown Good Will Hunting mode, and he, DiCaprio, Wahlberg, and the rest of the cast elevate this film from mediocrity. But excellent acting does not a great film make. Scorsese seemed to be on cruise control behind the camera; displaying little of the technical flair that he demonstrated in The Aviator. The script fails to carry the impact that it seems to strive for. I really never felt much of anything as these characters 'departed' one by one.
     Many have hailed this as a return to classical form for Scorsese. Trouble for me is, I am really not that  big of a fan of quote-unquote classic Scorsese films. I have rather enjoyed his more eclectic recent efforts. While Taxi Driver ranks in my top 50 favorite films, and few movies have been more technically sound in their execution than Raging Bull, I have never really understood the championing of Goodfellas and Raging Bull that occurs. These films have never struck me as tremendously original or affecting and are certainly not among the handful of films that I believe deserve consideration for the finest ever made. I honestly found Gangs of New York to be among his finest work, and he has never looked more proficient than he did with The Aviator (with the possible exception of the aforementioned Raging Bull). I found this to be a bit of a retread for Scorsese, and it disappointed me.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2006-11-19","","RT","900764390"
