5.0,"[b]*** This comment may contain spoilers ***[/b]
 
 
 
90% of the Acting was Brilliant: DiCaprio, Nicholson and Walhberg were Brilliant; Damon, Farmiga, Sheen and Baldwin were Good... 90% of the Dialogues were Brilliant: Dark humor, fast meaningful lines, hardcore language, numerous references (courageous) to pedophilia, religion and philosophy... 90% of Directing, Cinematography and Editing was Brilliant. The rest 10% never fell short from Greatness.
 
The Plot was Perfect (Greetings to the fountain of inspiration, Infernal Affairs) with lots of twists and double lives.
 
A real spectrum of emotional and physical behaviors, from the main characters: Vulnerability and inner struggle for Costigan (DiCaprio); Dark humor and extreme non-political acceptable language & personality for Costello (Nicholson); Cinicism and cold-blood living for Sullivan (Damon); Simplicity and Security for Madolyn (Farmiga); Honesty & father figure for Queenan (Sheen); Comediant and loyalty for Dignam (Whalberg).
 
The scenes went from extremely violent, to deep dialogs and dark emotional moments, always feeling extremely Real, never forced and unfocused.
 
The rat is always present, in humans, living in the gangs and institutions, and in subtle moments, in a ""drawning"", in a ""no cat situation"" and in the end of the movie, showing us that it will always survive over the departed ones...
 
There are lots of subtle references, and background action, that work as signals and clues to what is going on that, to fully acknowledge this movie, more than 2 viewings is recommended...
 
Scorsese gives some of is traditional camera movements and creates future classic scenes to be remembered: The conversation about the rat between Nicholson and DiCaprio (intense); The porno theater scene with Jack and Damon (extremelly funny); DiCaprio and Damon with the cellular phones (thrilling); and the Elevator and Final scenes (unpredictable).
 
Mean Streets, Goddfellas and Casino, have now a new younger brother to remember younger generations of Scorsese grand movie-making stature.
 
The Departed stand above the Oscars, it's above any other movie of the 2000s. ""Act accordingly...""","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-11-20","","RT","900926525"
4.0,"Truly impressed!!!!!!!!","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-11-20","","RT","328768243"
