4.5,"[center][img]http://images.fok.nl/upload/061110_115217_thedeparted1.jpg[/img]
[/center]
 

[i]""I am as God made me. Is that your rationale? May I remind you - in this archdiocese, God don't run the bingo.""[/i]
 
This one's a doozy.
""Martin Scorsese"" and ""crime film"" are by now synonymous but almost to the degree that the terms and association are taken for granted, I think. This has become quite the box office hit (raking in $114 million at the time of this writing) and there were many opinions, I recall, that Scorsese was returning to the well with this one long before it was released as if the genre he has been successful in is one he can't escape. 
 
This is ludicrous and it assumes, I think among other things, that he doesn't invest passion into films unlike this, which is wrong. He never commits to anything unless he is passionate which is why the result is always fascinating and arresting, if not always successful. You [i]feel[/i] the joy, the anger, the sweat and the blood in his films and in The Departed, the senses are assaulted to such a degree that it feels like blood stains on a shirt that'll never come off.
 
I may be taking it too far with the first 2 paragraphs but I do so to stress that Scorsese isn't treading the same, comfortable ground he has in Casino, Goodfellas and Mean Streets. This is new turf, like Gangs of New York, in which crime and violence are a normal part of life and share closet space with lies and stress. Good people are led astray, choices and actions have complications and realizations allow the stress to build up to such a claustrophobic degree that life is risked and death is somewhat welcomed. This is a harsher, more unforgiving world.
 
The film's title is appropriate because one of its major themes is death, physically and morally. It also concerns young men who have bad role models posing as father figures, citizens going through the motions until the most extreme of situations allow them to wake the hell up, and the dilemma of trying to redeem yourself when you sucked back in to the cesspool. Its an angry, bleak film and it is unlike any ""crime film"" Scorsese or his collaborators have made. There's no glamour to the job, no sense that the risk-taking and law-breaking leads to good rewards. 
 
As for the policemen, there's no satisfaction that they get their man, that they can trust each other. Their situation is no better than the mob; they're limited to where they can go, what they can say and who they say it to. They see the promise of a righteous bust, they cut down the weed only to see more grow into its place.
 
And the lies, oh the lies. Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) wants to escape the shadow of his criminal father and become a cop but he's challenged right after his tenure at academy by Sgt. Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) for his reasons. ""Why are you trying to be a cop?"" he asks Costigan and its a damn good question. Dignam and his superior Queenan (Martin Sheen) ask him to go undercover as a hood to infiltrate the ranks of Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) which is a chance at sheer suicide. Costigan agrees, makes great but difficult strides to doing so but halfway through the film he is a nervous, paranoid wreck. He's skillful at dispensing pain and being intimidating but the closer he gets to completing his assignment the worse he feels, like he's accomplished nothing.
 
Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is handpicked by Costello at an early age (during the memorable, 30 minute opening) to be mentored and shaped into a mole for the Massachusetts State Police. Sullivan, like Costigan, is successful with his cover but for Sullivan, the results are comfortable. He enjoys it. He's smart, charming and wins everyone over (except Dignam, who nobody wins over). 
 
Both the characters have father figures: Costello with Sullivan and Queenan with Costigan. Costello doesn't dote over Sullivan but keeps a good eye on him, even becoming impatient with him. Queenan feels obligated and sympathetic for Costigan but knows his hands are tied.
 
What happens to these characters and why provided one of the great movie yarns of the year. The script by William Monahan at first seems like its glossing over plot holes but is sharper and intricate upon closer viewing. A lot of past collaborators of Scorsese have returned: cinematographer Michael Ballhaus, editor Thelma Schoonmaker, composer Howard Shore, production designer Kristi Zea and along with a cast that I can't compose enough names and superlatives about really bring a complex narrative to life.
 
DiCaprio makes a career-best performance in this one. Damon is more subtle and understated. Nicholson acts like ""Jack"" but since Costello is a bit of a looney, maybe that's a perfect fit. Vera Farmiga makes a shoddy supporting role into something of great substance even when her dual relationships with the cops seems forced. Mark Wahlberg also gives a career-best as the foul-mouthed Dignam.
 
So many great sequences, too. I mentioned the 30 minute opening, which explains everything before the main titles show up. Other key events: the unbelievable, tension-filled phone call between Damon and DiCaprio; and the unforgettable climax in the elevator. 
 
I don't know how this ranks in Scorsese's work. I don't want to know. I'd like to think its his best since The Aviator. And that's the strongest compliment I think I can give to The Departed.
 
