4.0,"So, last night I saw The Departed, the new movie from master Martin Scorsese.

[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/thedeparted_bigearlyposter.jpg[/img]

This movie is so far, the movie with the biggest gross box office of the year..big surprise, after all the crap of movies released this year (with a few exceptions), any movie with violence, and suspense that seems ""coherent"" or ""intelligent"" is welcome.

Not that The Departed has anything of that. All the contrary, it is a great movie wich shines in many parts..except one.

[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/jack_nicholson9.jpg[/img]

Ok, the story, you know it, it's about two cops: one (DiCaprio) is infiltrated in the mafia ruled by a bad-yet-charismatic guy (Nicholson() and a cop (Damon) that  works for the mafia for the bad-yet-cahrismatic guy.

Scorsese excelent directing feels in every shot and track. Superbly developed, this movie has anything that other movies lack: attention to the detail, awesome cinematography (sans CGI gimmicks) and excelent dialogues with a plot that demands the audience to pay attention and not get distracted. It also delivers what people wants in a ""blockbuster"" movie: action, thriller, violence, ""romance"", etc..well balanced. 

[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/the_departed/_group_photos/matt_damon2.jpg[/img]

It biggest asset is the great casting. Not the choices but the way they performed. Leo Dicaprio showed a big maturity as an actor. Long past are the days of the Romeo+Juliet cute kiddo that girls and people loved/hate. He decided to take the path of a good actor instead of a superstar. Good choice buddy.

The same for Matt Damon. He has always demostrated a great underneath talent and this time he made no exception. You feel for it and you hate him  thanks to his great work.

And Jack Nicholson was...well, Jack Nicholson. Was fun to watch and did a great job but, doesn't he gets tired of being repetitive? I guess no. Good work anyway.

Alec Baldwin, Mark Walbergh (believe it or not!), Martin sheen all made a great addition to a great cast. The only cylinder that wasn't firing is the token female character. Not bad but still needs a lot of work to do.

Well looks so great..then what's the problem?

It's a god damn Remake!!

[img]http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/bd/InfernalAffairs.jpg[/img]

The original movie 無間道(Wú Jiàn Dào) who had the stupid B-movie english  translation of Infernal affairs, it's one of the most succesful movies made in Hong Kong and is regarded by many as a great crime thriller of all time. What's the surpirse on it? China, Hong Kong, Korea and Japan has always made great pieces of art on film. But of course, Hollywood people, unable of creating something of their own and had to rely in copying the excelent works of asia, the only ones that reallky doesn't fear to use their IMAGINATION and take risks. 

[img]http://www.stomptokyo.com/img-m6/infernal-affairs-c.jpg[/img]

And Mainstream audiences had a lot of blame of it.

[img]http://www.stomptokyo.com/img-m6/infernal-affairs-a.jpg[/img]

You may say ""but now people would see the original thanks to The Departed"". Wake up and smell the coffee, people. No matter if a movie is excelent. If it doesn't had a superstar on it, or is spoken in english or their native language, they will dismiss a movie spoken in a foreign language and  prefer the remake because ""it looks better"". You saw it with Ringu/The Ring, Abre los ojos/Vanilla Sky, Wings of Desire/City of angels, Il Mare/The lake house and now it will happen with this one.

Here in mexico, I know I'll have the chance of watch the original now that they can market it as ""the movie The Departed was based on!""..and it will last a miserable time before taking it off of the shelves just because Andy Lau is not as cute as Leonardo Di caprio.,

God.

That sucks, but lately that's what happens. Remakes are not necesaryly bad as long as they respect the originals and gave people time to see the original, but lately they are too much and people just get more apathetic to other cultures, languages and film industries.

The proof that The Departed, a remake, was a success is just a proof that how bad is Hollywood right now. Scorsese did a great work, but is obvious that he needed BADLY a commercial hit to survive, specially after Gangs of New York and The Aviator failed. Well, good for him.

Still, I recommend it, because it's great cast and tech values. Just remember that, if you are going to thank somebody, thank Andrew Lau and
 Mak Siu Fai, the creators of Wú Jiàn Dào. They really deserve it, over Brad Pitt (the producer) and Scorsese.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-11-21","","RT","900791409"
