5.0,"[color=white]Everyone is saying this is Martin Scorseses best film since [i]Goodfellas[/i]. Theyre saying its his best film in a decade Its being hailed as a return to form, the re-emergence of Scorsese as the man who brought us [i]Taxi Driver, Raging Bull[/i] Hell There are some who are treating this as a kind of trilogy capper that comes after [i]Casino[/i] in Scorseses chronicle of the American mob scene.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]None of these things are true.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]Yes, there is a visual and aural relationship with the aforementioned films. But that's just what Scorsese generally does. The gritty Michael Balhaus cinematography... The strange hacksaw editing that doesn't seem to care about that little thing known as visual continuity. The Robbie Robertson splicing of a pop soundtrack, which, in this particular case, seems more contrived than usual. Setting the scene with the stones because we begin in the 70s and then making sure to pepper it with Irish pub jukebox shuffling that somehow manages to juxtapose Patsy Cline with Dropkick Murphys. As forced as it gets. As far as Marty's ticks and fetishes? Nothing new under the sun here folks.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]So, let's be frank here. Scorseses best film since [i]Goodfellas[/i] was [i]Gangs of New York[/i]. Few want to acknowledge that fact, but its true. And this isnt a return to form, that would assume that he had somehow lost his nerve or something. Its like people forget that Scorseses biggest ambition is not to reinvent the wheel, but to craft high-quality genre films for Hollywood. He is a master of elevating pulp entertainment to the status of art. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white][i]The Departed[/i] falls into that category as the most pure entertainment he has given us since [i]Cape Fear[/i]. It is a star-packed Hollywood thriller to rank with the best of them. Full of vitality and wonderful violence, its a fucking great film. A superior comic book thriller about two young men on opposite sides of the law in Boston, who are placed as moles in each others ranks in a battle to bring down the meanest Irish motherfucker in all of Southie. But with Scorsese at the helm, this stuff of Hong Kong action melodrama (which it in fact was  in the form of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i]) is transformed into a black meditation on the nature of duality and loyalty Albeit peppered with enough moments of strong, gritty action and gory violence with the gaping bullet wounds spraying blood on the walls, people spitting gallons of blood from their mouths before they expire, and cursing like sailors in the moments of their agonizing deaths. In other words, what youve come to see to send you home happy; which you will, in any case, as things go from powerful and shocking, to downright hilarious in the films final minutes. I dont know if the consecutive display of Head Shots was a necessary spice for this little brew, but damned if Ive ever seen anything quite like it and I cant remember the last time I laughed so hard. Dont know if I was supposed to. But if it works, it works. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]Historically, it will be remembered as the first teaming of Scorsese and Nicholson. The result of this may come off as slightly anti-climactic. But only if you're somehow expecting the world to spontaneously implode. How it took this long is anyones guess. Could it be that, until now, there just wasnt a role for Jack in a Scorsese picture with the meat and bones of Irish mob boss Frank Costello? The kind of megaton of a part that allows him to devour the set decoration and lighting crew with the gluttony that only he can muster? This is The Joker. This is Daryl Van Horne. This is all of Nicholsons master villains rolled into one propulsive ball that slams you right in the pit of the stomach and makes you puke explosively over the person seated in front of you, even when it isnt stadium seating. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]But Make no mistake. This is a good thing. Whether hes breaking into song with an exaggerated brogue, or flinging handfuls of coke into the butt-cracks of two luscious prostitutes (one of whom is [i]The Dead Zone[/i]s Kristen Dalton) while squinting his eyes, crossing them, and furrowing his brow (like his death scene in [i]The Shining[/i]) or repeatedly slamming DiCaprios already broken arm with a Timberland boot Its a supreme comic book performance And Jack really sells the shit out of it.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]Which brings me to DiCaprio. In the latest of his now seemingly endless collaborations with Scorsese, he is finally given a role that doesnt embarrass him. There is no attempt to make him look tougher than Daniel Day Lewis, or to have the complexity and worldliness of a young Howard Hughes Here, he is a vulnerable young man, deep in a pile of shit, slowly sinking up to his eyeballs as the pungent odor consumes him You believe it. Every step of the way and he is never once upstaged. Not even when asked to share the screen with a trying-WAAAAAY-too-hard Mark Wahlberg as a cop who for some reason has to hurl profanity-laced insults and act all threatening in a labored Boston accent (which is funny, considering Wahlberg is from Boston) all the while sporting a goofy haircut that makes him look like a human penis. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]In any case, DiCaprio holds his own. This is the best shit he has ever done. Period. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]Matt Damon (MATT DAMON!) is also given the chance to shine as a really slimy villain who somehow manages to be rather likeable despite the fact that hes basically a douchebag. As the yang to DiCaprios yin, he plays a cat-and-mouse game with Leo in one scene involving a celphone and no dialogue that will probably go into the books as one of the best moments of cinematic tension of the last ten or so years. And he gets to close the film with one of the best final lines given to a character in recent memory, at least when the line is taken in context.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]And speaking of celphones They have been a handy plot device in the past. Hell, [i]24[/i] wouldnt be able to exist without them. But here they are turned into the kind of essential suspense detail that would have made Alfred Hitchcock fart out loud. How you can derive so much from echoing ringtones and people glancing at cryptic text messages is a master class in plot construction. With Scorsese holding the brick and mortar and stuffing it up your rectal cavity as only he can.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]This is why we love him.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]This is also why he still wont get the Oscar. He does Hollywood better and bolder than they ever have. And they have no idea how he does it and they want him to go fuck himself because of it.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]But The Departed deserves some kind of recognition. Acting awards to all (including the as yet unmentioned Vera Farmiga who, as the shrink who has to deal with Damon and DiCaprios neuroses among other things of theirs, is engaging, magnetic, sexy, all the things a movie star is supposed to be with this and [i]Running Scared[/i], she has shot to the top of my Yes list) Theres good stuff from Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin, in particular, who is given the chance to be SNL funny in his brief supporting role. Ray Winstone is on hand being the consummate badass that we all know he can be as Nicholsons top enforcer. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]William Monahans tight, yet sprawling, screenplay is full of terrific funny lines. Hell The movie might get a nom for Best Picture.[/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]But it will not win. [/color]
[color=white] [/color]
[color=white]See it anyway though. Its a cool movie.[/color]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-11-26","","RT","900612391"
