4.0,"[i][font=Georgia]The Departed [/font][/i][font=Georgia]is quite thoroughly a Scorcese film, which is to say, it is nearly Shakespearean, the amount of death that occurs before the end of the movie. 
However, it's a film that examines familial ties and bonds of obligation if not of blood. I would venture to say that[i] The Departed[/i] is to Irish mobsters what [i]The Godfather[/i] was to the Italian mafia. 

Among a sterling cast, there were a few breakout roles. Nicholson goes beyond what we've seen him do before; his capacity for ruthlessness approaches a near Zen level in this movie, and yet he plays an oddly bifurcated character in Frank Costello.  We have no doubt there is nothing he won't do as a crime boss, no cruelty he won't stoop to, but he expresses concern over Billy Costigan coming into the life. ""Your dad would've killed me and my whole family, just for talking to you,"" he tells DiCaprio. ""Have you thought about going back to school?""

For his part, DiCaprio offers an unexpectedly textured performance. His pretty boy days are over. He's grown into his face; he's a bit worn now and he proves he can actually act, as those of us knew who saw [i]What's Eating Gilbert Grape[/i]? Incidentally, I haven't liked DiCaprio since then, but he shines in The Departed. His portrayal of Billy Costigan, son of an Irish crime family, trying to go into law enforcement, possesses grit and intensity, sharp edged conflict.  Deep undercover for over a year, we feel his disenfranchisement, his increasing dissolution of self. 

Though I've read a review where someone said that Vera Farmiga was the weak link in the movie, as I see it, the weak link was Matt Damon. He just couldn't match the intensity everyone else generated. His character, Colin Sullivan, came across looking weak, beholden to Costello (Jack Nicholson) and trying to fool his boss, Ellerby, (Alec Baldwin) into thinking he was the best of the best. He isn't quite up to the tightrope Sullivan is supposed to walk, so the performance comes across as erratic. 

The rest of the cast is admirable including Alec Baldwin who turns in a stellar  (and hilarious) supporting performance, along with the idealistic, paternal Martin Sheen. The true star of the show, though, astonishes me. Ladies and gentlemen, Mark Wahlberg can act. As the angry, foul-mouthed Dignam, he steals the limelight anytime he's on camera.  He radiates  animosity and loathing for a system that he rightly senses is at best ineffectual and at worst utterly corrupt. 

See [i]The Departed[/i]. Its themes will leave you a lot to discuss afterward. 
[/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-11-30","","RT","900927028"
