1.5,"I'm clearly in the minority on this one, but I really was not happy with this film.  First of all I'm so sick and tired of mobsters and police officers always being portrayed as assholes.  In fact even sitting here now I'm hard pressed to think of one character who was likeable from beginning to end.  For me if I don't like the characters I don't give a crap what happens to them.  The only one I cared for on any level was Leonardo DiCaprio's Billy Costigan.  He showed a vulnerability and softness to his character that at least allowed me to be outraged (spoiler alert) when he got killed.  Furthermore I hate that cops are always written in films of this type as basically morons and jerks who basically luck into solving their cases and couldn't be nice to someone if their life depended on it.  I realize not every portrayal of a cop can be as kind hearted as Andy Griffith's old television character but it doesn't mean that they have to be someone we hate.  Alec Baldwin and Mark Wahlberg's characters were despicable nasty people.  I wouldn't want to be around them for five minutes let alone endure a movie with characters like them for over two hours.  Even Martin Sheen's character was written as such a putz that a small part of you couldn't help but think he got what he deserved when he was killed for being so darn stupid.  If you watch even ten minutes of a television show like the First 48 you see that real cops can be intelligent, kind, upstanding people who use logic and reasoning to solve crimes.  In fact I think we'd be more entertained watching a smart police force matching wits with an equally intelligent criminal element but the way it was portrayed in this film I spent the whole time wondering how anybody, with the exception of Damon's and DiCaprio's characters got to their positions of power because they were all idiots.  Nobody showed the slightest glimmer of intelligence they were all a bunch of twits.  The only two intelligent people in the whole mix played by DiCaprio and Damon were running around answering and kowtowing to people they were completely intellectually superior to.  It didn't make any sense.  The most ridiculous part was Wahlberg showing up at the end to assassinate Damon's character.  Instead of bringing him to justice he shoots him.  The one person who could expose him commits murder instead.   The shrink girlfriend doesn't take the recording to anyone.  Nobody in this damn movie does the right thing once.  Completely immoral people.  So why do I care what happens to any of them.  Well I didn't.  So in spite of all the beautiful cinematography, the great pacing and the overall beautiful look of the film it all still amounted to naught because I didn't give a damn about any of the characters other than DiCaprio's, the one intelligent person who repeatedly kept telling everyone exactly what was going to happen if they didn't take heed.  So the one person we care about, you kill him and get him absolutely no justice at all.  Thanks for stealing two hours of our life to only piss us off with injustice.  A complete waste of time this film.  If you want to see deception and subterfuge done right, watch L.A. Confidential, don't waste your time on this dud, I'm certainly sorry I did.  The only redeeming qualities were the outstanding performances by DiCaprio and Damon who delivered great work in a ridiculous setting.","69cc5539-608f-46ad-8647-473c70b0d6f3",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2006-12-16","","RT","104738694"
