0.5,"The Departed was very disappointing. 

Lots of plots that literally go nowhere; unintentional red herrings. 

More plotholes than I think I've ever seen in a film; it literally digs its own grave. For example, the cops tell Leo that he can't be a real cop since his whole family was in the mob. Somehow the cops have this information; they know his connections. However, despite the fact that they have TAILED and PHOTOGRAPHED Jack Nicholson for DECADES, they MISS the fact that Matt Damon has worked for him for plus or minus TWENTY FIVE YEARS. Of course, they know the identity of all his other goons. 

There are about 20 other big plot holes or unanswered storylines that really ruined this film for me. And the two people I saw it with who had never heard of Infernal Affairs. 

1. Why would Jack continually let Leo slide when it's obvious that he's the mole, and he does nothing to shed suspicion on any other member of the group?
2. Why is a drug dealer dealing micro processors?
3. If Jack is an FBI informant, and the Feds knew about the PD's investigation, why didn't they stop it? 
4. If Jack is a top dog, why would he be an informant? Who would he inform on? Why wouldn't the Feds get him? 
5. What's the deal with Matt Damon not being able to get it up? (I'm assuming it's so we know Leo's the dad?)
6. Why would the Doc give Leo drugs?
7. Why would the Doc date Leo? What attracted them to each other? 
8. Why does Matt Damon want to go to Law School?
9. How come we never see MD at LS, even though he's been going for a year? 
10. Why wouldn't MD investigate who Leo's ""cop shrink"" was?
11. Why wouldn't Leo try to contact Mark Wahlberg before just trusting MD?
12. Why would MW just give up the investigation?
13. WHERE DID MW GO FOR HALF THE MOVIE ?????
14. AND HOW DID HE KNOW MD WAS THE MOLE ????
15. Why didn't Doc give the evidence to the cops?
16. Why didn't MD get suspicious when Doc showed up at a STRANGER's funeral?
17. How does Matt Damon have time to be a Law Student, Cop, and Rat at the same time? 
18. Why doesn't MD cap Doc?
19. Why is there no payoff with the envelope?
20. Why wouldn't Leo TELL THE COPS that MD was the mole WHEN HE WAS AT THE HQ SURROUNDED BY COPS?
21. WHY WOULD LEO RUN FROM THE POLICE STATION?
22. Why wasn't Doc conflicted about screwing two guys at the same time?
23. HOW DID A RAT GET ONTO THE BALCONY OF A HIGH FLOOR OF AN EXPENSIVE APARTMENT BUILDING?
*24. Why didn't MD kill Doc, after all the talk about silencing wives & girlfriends? 
*25. Why did the cell phone work on the T? Cellphones don't work on the subway, this whole scene doesn't work!

I've heard a lot of theories from others who've seen the film, but I'm just not buying them. I don't believe that Leo had Marky's contact information, and had he left a note for Doc to give him, I don't believe she would have been able to get his contact information. (And if Leo had it in the first place, why didn't he contact him directly?) I really think there was no payoff with the envelope. As well, *Ben*, there is NO WAY that Marky brought the rat to the apartment! It was just terrible visual thinking.

On top of that, the cinematography was just weird. There were a lot of jump cuts (the worst case of this was in the romance scene, where there's absolutely ZERO tension before the kiss, ugh) breaking the 180, a number of not-quite-dutch angles, just odd montage bits with Jack, and a bizarre Blade Runner scene. 

The last shot was the worst ending I'VE SEEN IN YEARS. The plot holes really were repetitive and roundabout, and made the film drag. The beginning (especially when compared to the original) is SO SLOW and filled with AWKWARD EXPOSITION. While the acting was good all around (though Jack didn't do anything new for me), the emotions are good, but played on the surface -- you're supposed to ""try to hide your real emotions."" Also, those who were faking Boston accents lost them halfway through.

On a personal note, there was a lot of ""fuck,"" ""fucker,"" ""fucking,"" ""you want her to suck your cock,"" ""dick,"" ""blowjob,"" ""pussy,"" ""homo,"" ""queer,"" ""jackoff,"" porn, ""cunt,"" ""whore,"" hookers, tits, etc. chauvinist machoism in the film, in theory to more masculinize the characters. But it adds NOTHING to the characters whatsoever, and just makes Martin Scorsese look like a fourteen year-old DICK. Scorsese really has a problem with female characters (in this one, there were a few old ladies and hookers, the main two were an incompetent girlfriend who gets knocked up, and a slutty older girlfriend trying to get knocked up) and the whole film was a sausage fest in the worst way. I don't mind if a script is lacking in women, but I do mind when all the women are elderly or sperm beds. 

This bothers me especially since the original didn't have ANY of that. And even though Hong Kong cinema isn't exactly female-friendly, they're never this blatantly sexist. Is this was Scorsese thinks a good film is? I'm afraid it's what America thinks a good film is -- it's being Oscar tipped to hell. Really shameful. 

Please rent Infernal Affairs. It's a beautifully shot, beautifully acted film, and only 3 years old.","",False,False,False,False,0.5,"2006-12-20","","RT","900925724"
