4.5,"[font=Arial]I'm back! And I'm thoroughly annoyed - the electricity went JUST as I was finishing my first big entry of the year. So, here's an extremely expurgated version:[/font]
 
[font=Arial][b]Holiday bad[/b][/font]
[font=Arial][i]Enteritis[/i] (was knocked flat on Christmas day afternoon, got up a few days later)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Twisted ankle[/i] (fifteen minutes into a sodden football game up in my old neighbourhood, Hillcrest)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Heat and humidity[/i] that threatened heatstroke (I played a round of golf in the heart of Zululand, at Amatikulu, and had to stand in the golf club's walk-in bar fridge afterwards) and made every physical action a challenge[/font]
 
[font=Arial][b]Holiday good[/b][/font]
[font=Arial][i]One of my best friends announced his engagement[/i] (to a girl in England) and that I shall be his best man (also in England - lock up your daughters and animals)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]A great new year's eve party[/i] (for a change)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Bodysurfing at Ballito[/i] (I'd twisted my ankle by this stage...so no surfing)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Coming back to Joburg for my birthday[/i] (Sunday), playing a killer round of golf and enjoying a nice dinner (with presents)[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Cheap whisky[/i] in Durban nightclubs[/font]
 
[font=Arial][b]A brief roundup of films I watched[/b][/font]
[font=Arial][i]Hoodwinked[/i]: Shite animated crime/musical/flashback film. Though, somehow, Anne Hathaway still manages to ooze sex appeal through her irritating little avatar.[/font]
[font=Arial][i]The Departed[/i]: Completely unshite film about shooting people. Great performances, great script, great cinematography, great direction, great cast.[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Guilty by suspicion[/i]: Bob de Niro alone tries, but fails, to overcome terminally boring script/story, attempting to rely on historical memory of events.[/font]
[font=Arial][i]Family Business[/i]: For once, Connery eases up on the ""This is my one and only character"" motif, but it can't save the film from having Matthew Broderick tugging on its ankles in deep water. Hoffman does OK at filling the space between the lines, but the other actors must have eaten the bad clams.[/font]
[font=Arial][i]No more to say, nothing to weep for: an elegy for Allen Ginsberg[/i]: (Not listed at RT) I watched this because I'd read [i]Jack's Book - An oral biography of Jack Kerouac[/i] over the holiday. Shows Ginsberg to be painfully sensitive and childish, as well as grindingly egotistical, against all his Buddhist spoutings. There's good work, respect for the man, a sad story involved...but something about Ginsberg doesn't sit well with me (ditto for Kerouac, Cassady, et al). I share Tom Wolfe's feelings about the Cult of Me.[/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-01-10","","RT","900662783"
