4.5,"Like perhaps all gangster films, this one has a strong pre-civilization Hobbesian element. That is, the life of these characters can certainly be described by the well-known phrase "";nasty, brutish, and short.""; The film focuses on what happens when we lose our trust in others, and how our sense of security unravels when we lose our place in society (and for Billy Costigan, identity). Colin, the only character at home in deception, is clearly the villain of the film, as is indicated by a rat parading on his balcony when he is shot. This is reflected in the difference between the way he and Billy react to the loss of their father-figures: when a turn of events takes the life of Billyී;s father-figure, Queenan, he is distraught, but Colin kills his own father-figure, Costello. Given his heroic, if blighted, role in the film, Billyී;s sudden murder is shocking, and is a slap on the wrist for those who hoped for justice. The only justice in this society is a tragic ending for all. (posted to http://www.philmosophy.com April 17, 2009)","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-01-12","","RT","900942442"
