4.0,"[color=black]The Departed is a genre picture, but it's one of the best genre pictures of 2006. I love it when A-list talent chooses to have some fun making a B-type movie.[/color]
 
[color=black]The script by William Monahan is simply dazzling. Dialogue flies across the screen like fireworks. There are passages that are among the funniest I've seen in years, and the humor all stems from the dialogue. Despite this being a genre picture, I think it deserves an Oscar nomination for Best Screenplay.[/color]
 
[color=black]The film is set in the Boston area, and the characters are either Irish cops or Irish mobsters -- some are both.[/color]
 
[color=black]Jack Nicholson plays one of the leading mobsters. He is very entertaining, as you'd expect Nicholson to be. But I think this performance will be just a footnote in his career. He has done work that is vastly richer than this.[/color]
 
[color=black]Leonardo DiCaprio is good as an undercover cop who becomes a confidant of Nicholson's character. DiCaprio doesn't have any of the funny lines. He provides the gravitas for the film. While ""The Departed"" is often laugh-out-loud funny, it is also shocking and upsetting at other times. It is an odd mixture of humor and grimness; reminiscent of Quentin Tarantino's films.[/color]
 
[color=black]Mark Wahlberg, whose performances just keep getting better and better, is hilarious as the co-head of the police undercover division. He gets many of the funny lines, and he delivers them brilliantly. Same goes for Alec Baldwin, who plays the head of a different police division. These two deliver lines in a rapid way that reminded me of 1930s screwball comedies. When they're together on screen it's movie magic. I'd love to see these two do a comic love scene together someday, with lots of dialogue in it.[/color]
 
[color=black]Matt Damon gives one of his better, livelier performances as a corrupt cop. His character is sexy, smart, and scrappy. When the tension builds at the end, and the character fears he's being cornered, Damon is a powerful presence on screen.[/color]
 
[color=black]""The Departed"" is assured a place in movie history. I'm very confident that it will become one of the favorite DVDs of the male moviegoer, especially those who are fans of Quentin Tarantino. But I suspect that ""The Departed"" will be even more popular than Tarantino's films, because ""The Departed"" is more accessible. It's a sterling entertainment.[/color]","",False,True,False,False,4.0,"2007-01-15","","RT","900932669"
