4.0,"This is not as good as Infernal Affairs. 

That aside, it is different enough from its source material to be enjoyable even for fans of the former. The acting is top notch throughhout (though Jack Nicholson descends into parody, in a very showy but underwritten part) and the direction as brilliant as always, with an exquisite soundtrack. The film moves along at a breakneck pace and feels organic, set in a real place, Boston and its irish neighborhoods literally ooze out of the screen.

The problems, however, are many:

Nicholson's character is essentially a lot of sound and fury ultimately signifying nothing. 

The parallels were subtle in Infernal Affairs, but here they are underlined 5 times in red marker, to the point of ridicule (Nicholson and Sheen as the father figures for Damon and DiCapiro respectively, their falling in love with the same woman, they are two sides of the same coin, and many others)

Mark Wahlberg's character, though well acted, is irrelevant (and has absolutely no depth) in the ultimate scheme of things save for the very end of the film, which is a ridiculous cheat. And the final shot, clearly a joke, is supposed to signify what exactly? 

As a whole it is a fierce, solid film well worth seeing, but shoots itself in the foot way too many times to make the jump from very good to great.","",False,True,False,False,4.0,"2007-01-18","","RT","501354031"
