1.5,"To look at the critics' reviews of [i]The Departed[/i] I can't help believing that they all drank too much of Martin Scorsese's cool-aid.  Bad scripts are tragic enough on their own, but bad scripts imposed on good actors and backed by too much money and hype are insufferable.  The movie-goers in the theater in which I viewed [i]The Departed [/i]obviously can see a poorly-crafted story better than the critics posted on Rotten Tomatoes; many of them laughed out loud as yet another character got whacked towards the end of the movie.  I was laughing with them, mostly because it seemed the writers could find no other way to end this inane story full of inconsistencies in any way other than to just kill nearly everyone.  At least that way only a few people are left to explain all of the points in the story that do not make sense.  Unfortunately, they just all look glum and glaring in the final scene and tie nothing together.  Someone should have shot them too.  It would have made no difference.  I overheard others complaining about how superficial and unbelievable the love triangle was.  And I can understand if [i]Ghandi[/i] is long, but there is no excuse for a movie with no substance to run over 2 1/2 hours.  That's just indiscipline and poor editing.  Hollywood has plenty of money.  I wish it would quit wasting it, and the time of a lot of talent artists and viewers as well, and pay a little more for writers who can tell a better story.","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2007-01-22","","RT","900945606"
