3.5,"[b][size=5][color=red][u]The Departed[/u][/color][/size][/b]
:fresh: [b][size=4]7/10[/size][/b]
 
I walked into Martin Scorsese's [i]The Departed[/i] expecting 'greatness' but only got 'good'. That statement really sums it up for me. I would be lying if I said I wasn't a 'bit' disappointed. I had high hopes, hopes of being 'blown away' by an Oscar nominated film for Best Picture 2006. But by the end for some reason or another I really could hardly have cared less as Scorsese's film left me with a sort of bland feeling. 
 
At times The Departed was very compelling and the story was even (for the most part) well thought out. I was even sucked in and entertained for most the time. But to be honest I by no means was 'astonished' by the time the curtain fell. Yes, by the time the not-even-climactic ending hit me, I had simply stopped caring about all the twists and turns the story offered.
 
The acting in the flick was 'fantastic' and showcases some fine work especially by Leonardo DiCaprio. The look, feel and setting all Scorsese, but when it was all said and done I was merely 'hooked' by The Departed and never 'reeled' in, the audience member who got away so to speak. So if asked to put my finger on my main issue with the film, I would have to say it was simply not compelling enough.
 
I give the film a '7' which means I thought it was good, it's definitely a worth while venture, and even though my review sounds somewhat negative that's only because I was expecting more. But as I drove home from the theatre collecting my thoughts all I kept asking myself was ""Is The Departed really one of the best films of 2006 for you""? And the only answer I got back was a resounding and very clear ""Nope not even close"".
 
 
