4.0,"Well, it's a sad, sad year for me. I am 35 movies behind on the Oscar nominations. I only need 14 in the big categories, so I might can swing that. We'll see.
 
I am terribly behind on the [url=""http://www.filmspotting.net""]filmspotting[/url] animation marathon. But I did catch [b]Grave of the Fireflies[/b], possibly the most depressing animated movie ever. Set in WWII Japan, Fireflies follows the story of a teenage boy who must care for his sister after their mother is killed in a bombing of their city. The animation is totally breathtaking, but besides making me depressed, the film also made me angry and frustrated with the boy. A very moving film, but it's hard to recommend as it is so emotionally draining.
 
I finally caught up with [b]The Departed[/b] and it was almost as good as every one said it was. But I couldn't help comparing it to Infernal Affairs, possibly a better film with an hour shaved off the running time. But I did love Departed for rekindling my respect for Leonardo DiCaprio (""Blood Diamond""). He had all his acting power back in this film. Our fight is over. I almost felt sorry for Matt Damon (""The Good Shepherd""). DiCaprio was just out-acting him left and right. Marky Mark (""Invincible"") also entertained the hell out of me. He's come so far. Anyway, I really enjoyed the movie, but I think Scorsese (""The Aviator"") could have shaved a good hour off of it and lost none of the power.
 
[b]Little Miss Sunshine[/b] was not as good as everyone said. Perhaps I just built it up too much in my mind. Anyway, I thought it was pretty much an average dysfunctional family film with a couple of stand-out performances. The little girl, Abigail Breslin (""Santa Clause 3""), of course, and Alan Arkin (""Firewall"") were two of my favs. There were a few funny parts. But I don't get at all why this is up for best picture. It's no Children of Men.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-01-29","","RT","900777916"
