2.0,"Sure makes you realize what a sleek, well-crafted film Infernal Affairs was.  After spending over 2 hours with each character you still never felt half as much compassion towards them as the original.  And some of the plot that was changed was kinda stupid, I can't imagine why they wouldn't want to give Damon's character the depth of Lau's.  Instead, he was just a villain.  And DeCaprio just seemed to be angry all the time, he had none of the quiet integrity that Leung had.  A heartbreaking scene near the beginning of IA has Leung tucked away in an alcove saluting his fallen comrade, not able to do it openly like he dreams one day of doing.  He's disgusted with himself for being a villain (even if it's for good reason) and wants to be a cop whereas DiCaprio's motivation is even less explained (he just hates his non-immediate family?).  Heck, when DiCaprio gets the chance to be given a medal and finally get his identity back as a cop he just wants his money.  I guess they didn't want anyone to actually be a good guy in the film, that whole shades of gray ""intellegent"" filmmakers like so much.  It is interesting to note that in the Hong Kong version that no one is entirely bad, and the American version no one is entirely good.","",False,True,False,False,2.0,"2007-02-07","","RT","420550560"
