4.0,"Martin Scorsese has directed some legendary films.  [i]Taxi Driver[/i], [i]Raging Bull[/i] and [i]Goodfellas [/i]are widely considered masterpieces and the director has several other very good films on his resume.  Although it's not a masterpiece, The Departed marks a return to form for Director Martin Scorsese.
 
[b]The Departed - 8.5/10[/b]  
Director - Martin Scorsese
Starring - Leanardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Vera Farmiga, Ray Winstone, Alec Baldwin, Anthony Anderson.
 
Inspired by the Hong Kong crime thriller Infernal Affairs (which I have not seen), [i]The Departed[/i] is an intoxicating thriller that takes place in Boston.  The Irish Mafia, led by Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) has taken over South Boston.  The police's undercover division led by Captain Queenan and Sergeant Digman (Sheen and Wahlberg) are determined to bring Costello down.  To do so they enlist an undercover cop named Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) whose family had links to the mob.  However Costello is a shrewd man so he has his own mole Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) within the police department to tip off any investigations.  It's a race against time as Sullivan and Costigan try to expose each other before they expose themselves.
 
Having not seen Infernal Affairs may somewhat skew my review as I don't have that film to reference.  For me (and I suspect most who view this film) it is new, so I'll proceed accordingly.  For me the film marked a return to the classic Scorsese style I have long admired.  It's not overblown to the point of being ridiculous like [i]Gangs of New York[/i], nor does it have that Oscar grab feel of [i]The Aviator.  [/i]Nor is it a straight mobster picture like [i]Goodfellas[/i] or [i]Casino[/i].  Sure it has elements of both, but it's more of a thriller than anything.  And thrilling it is, despite some flaws.  My first thought was how Costigan managed to so freely allude suspicion from Costello and his gang.  Sure we have the  scene where Costigan is searched, but time after time when the squeeze is getting tighter Costigan's behavior is, well suspicious.  But hey it's a movie so I'll live with that.  
 
The characters for the most part have depth and are fully realized.  Matt Damon in particular was very good as the mobster infiltrating the police force.  His role wasn't as flashy as others, but was Oscar worthy.  DiCaprio continues to grow as an actor and has some of the film's more intense moments.  Jack Nicholson is Jack Nicholson and many will say he's just a carciature of himself in this film.  Perhaps he's such a legendary personality that those comparisons are inevitable, but I didn't feel Costello was anymore flamboyant or over-the-top as Joe Pesci's characters in [i]Goodfellas[/i] or [i]Casino[/i].  Wahlberg got a supporting actor nomination for his arrogant and aggressive Digman character and he's very good in this film.  Winstone, Baldwin and Sheen make the most of their screentime as well.  More problematic is Vera Farmiga, a psychologist who counsels Costigan while she's in a romantic relationship with Sullivan.  Female characters, while not the stars of most Scorsese films, often represent it's soul.  See Lorraine Bracco's incedible performance in Goodfellas as a prime example.  However not enough is done with Farmiga's character to make her as integral to the film as she should have been.  Just a small nitpick.
 
As pure enertainment, [i]The Departed[/i] sizzles.  It may not be an important masterpiece like [i]Taxi Driver[/i] or [i]Goodfellas[/i], yet it's more enertaining than 99% of the films I've recently seen.  Most importantly it's a return (of sorts) to form for one our greatest filmmakers.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-02-07","","RT","900789687"
