4.0,"[b][size=3]Halloween II (Rosenthal, 1981)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://www.geocities.com/madnessmike/H2alicephone.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
[i]Halloween [b]II[/b][/i] immediately raises the usual discussion: why produce a sequel for a universally acclaimed masterpiece, when it has not even the slightest chance to reach the first part's brilliance anyway? Well, I've never really seen the point of such conversations, because, if you know that Hollywood is reigned by money (and nothing else), the explanation is fairly easy: John Carpenter's independent production [i]Halloween[/i] is, until today, one of the commercially most lucrative productions of all time. So, a sequel was just a matter of time. What is rather surprising is that it needed three years to follow.
But [i]Halloween II[/i] isn't a bad film by any means. As a matter of fact, it intelligently ties in with the first part: during the first five minutes, the precursor's finale is retold and thus almost seemlessly integrated into part two. Also the new settings, a hospital, are rather wisely chosen.
On this point, I want to cut short the qualities though, since the (numerous) flaws should be emphasized as well. One major problem is that the sequel has no longer got the elegance of [i]Halloween[/i]. Many scenes make a pretty awkward impression, which weakens the suspense quite a lot. Furthermore, some other scenes are even ridiculous (like Michael Myers walking through the glass door). Needless to say that this doesn't make the film any more thrilling (even though it has got its intense moments). The major problem here does not lie in the writing (after all it was John Carpenter, together with Debra Hill, who wrote it), but rather in the linkish, uninspired direction. You feel that the film was full of good ideas, but director Rick Rosenthal is not able to put on most of these. What a man like Carpenter could have done out of such a potentially rich writing...well, he didn't, so lets not speculate.
If you're a logics freak, [i]Halloween II[/i] will be a really tough horror film for you. The idea that Michael Myers is Laurie's older brother (no, I [i]am [/i]your brother., anyone?) may not be very plausible, but it is good enough nevertheless. A bigger problem are the dates however: [i]Halloween II[/i], just like the first one, of course, takes place in 1978. Loomis says that Michael is 31 (born in 1946 or 1947 then). So, how could he be 6 years old, when he killed his older sister, in 1963? Hello?
 
[size=3][b]*[/b][/size][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]6 out of 10[/size][/b][/size]
 
 
[b][size=3]Amores Perros (Iñárritu, 2000)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://www.offoffoff.com/film/2001/images/amoresperros.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
Until two months ago, I had never seen a film by Alejandro González Iñárritu, and had no idea about this guy's huge talent. After having watched the excellent [url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/vine/journal_view.php?journalid=231975&entryid=388187&view=public""][i]Babel[/i][/url][i], [/i]I took [url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/vine/journal_view.php?s=&journalid=196362""]Megan[/url]'s recommendation to eventually catch up with [i]Amores Perros, [/i]a film I had taped a long time ago on TV.
Just like in the case of [i]Babel [/i]and [i]Amores Perros[/i] (which I watched later on), this film is also divided into three episodes, each of them following one or two highly interesting, plastic and profound main characters. What gives the film additional grip is the fact that they are intelligently interwoven and cross at different, crucial moments.
This said, [i]Amores Perros[/i] is shocking, humorous and dramatic, but always in a deeply involving context. This probably set new cinematic standards to millenium-cinema like only few others did (I would name [i]Requiem for a Dream[/i] and [i]Mulholland Dr.[/i] here). A sincere work of art, essential to the understanding of contemporary cinema and its devices.
 
[size=3][b]****[/b][/size][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]9 out of 10[/size][/b][/size]
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3]Little Otik (Otesánek) (Svankmajer, 2001)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://www.ephemeroi.com/stuff/2002/front/littleotik.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
[size=2]Jan Svankmajer is a genius. A solomonic statement you can only disagree with, if you have seen some of his work. And if you really have, then you will wholeheartedly agree.[/size]
Svankmajer's imaginative power is unbelievable, and makes those of Peter Jackson and Co. look like empty rubbish bins. 
The film is about a Czech couple who can't have children, which is quite an emotional burden for the wife. Her husband, while working in the garden one day, finds a tree root that looks like a child too him. After having brought it a bit into shape, he presents it to his wife, who immediately adopts it like her own child. But soon Otik, as they called...ehm...it, develops a life of its own and, alongside, a huge appetite, especially for human beings.
With such a refreshingly original story, far off the usual Hollywood-crap, Jan Svankmajer conjures 130 minutes of magnificently enchanting entertainment: funny, scary and completely crazy. Plus, Svankmajer's typical stop motion-technique still works wonderfully. If you can find it somewhere, don't think twice, it's brilliant.
 
[size=3][b]***[/b][/size][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]8 out of 10[/size][/b][/size]
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3]Yossi & Jagger (Fox, 2002)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://www.filmhai.de/kino/kinoplakat/bilder_0003/yossi_jagger/gallery1/yossi_jagger_001.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
This film is what [i]Brokeback Mountain[/i] should have been: sensitive, involving without being sentimental, and profoundly human.
Although the film might be slightly predictable, its storyline is gripping until the last second (well, the film's runtime isn't that long anyway). Moreover, it enchants with its splendid beauty and its natural ease of dealing with the topic (as opposed to [i]Brokeback[/i]'s clumsy way). The acting is authentic and realistic, without ever being pretentious.
Ok, this feels now like a checklist I am enumerating. Enough of this. Watch it.
 
[size=3][b]***[/b][/size][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]8 out 10[/size][/b][/size]
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3]The Departed (Scorsese, 2006)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://movies.themoviebox.net/newimages2/departed/main.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
Well, here you go with my long-promised and long kept back-review of Martin Scorsese's newest work, [i]The Departed[/i], which will most probably bring the well-deserved Oscar home to the old master. 
Briefly said, [i]The Departed [/i]is an excellent action thriller, and a above average Scorsese-work. The difference in enthusiasm for both claims can easily be explained by the fact that [i]The Departed[/i] is far from being Marty's finest work. While it has no major flaws (or no flaws at all) in the main domains, such as writing, direction or acting, the film lacks that certain originality, all of Scorsese's films lacks since [i]The Age of Innocence[/i] in 1993. [i]The Departed[/i] is a perfectly directed, but rather conventional Hollywood-thriller, which would have been the last straw with just a bit more originality and experimental daringness. 
Anyway, I salute Scorsese for these 150 minutes of outstanding entertainment.
 
[size=3][b]***[/b][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]8 out of 10[/size][/b][/size][/size]
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3][/size][/b] 
[b][size=3]The Lives of Others (Das Leben der Anderen) (von Donnersmarck, 2006)[/size][/b]
[center][b][size=3][img]http://www.filmhai.de/kino/kinoplakat/bilder_0001/leben_anderen/gallery1/leben_anderen_001.jpg[/img][/size][/b][/center]
Nominated for the Best Foreign Language-Oscar, the German film [i]The Lives of Others[/i] is a remarkably deep and unspectacular (because subtle) insight into the life in the G""D""R (and especially the Big Brother-principles there) during the 80s. At the same time, it can be understood as a strong and valuable plaidoyer against the plans of some European politicians (dictators) for an omnipresent control by the state. While it may not be as emotionally involving as it probably wants to be, the film displays an astonishing intensity and elegance in several scenes. Plus, the acting, especially in the leading roles, is powerful and convincing.
Yes, a short review for a long film (appr. 145 minutes). Worth watching.
 
[size=3][b]**[/b][/size][size=2]or[b]   [size=3]7 out of 10[/size][/b][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-02-12","","RT","900797459"
