4.5,"By the end of this movie, you're drained. Theres so much blood and plot twists throughout ""The Departed"" I would recommend a good half hour of just digesting what you saw. Let me explain the plot as well as I can. There are two police officers, one played by DiCaprio is infiltrating a crime group headed by Nicholson's character. The other cop, Damon, is supposed to help bring down Nicholson as well but he instead is helping him get away with such crimes because Nicholson looked over him since he was a kid (I would make a chart of some sort). DiCaprio does jail time to avoid suspicion of being a cop. Then he roughs up a few guys who are somewhat connected to Nicholson.....goes to a bar where he frequents and finally meets him. Now why Leo's character knows he wont get killed off the bat, I don't know. Anyway, Nicholson brings him into the group. From there suspicion grows as both sides are trying to sniff out the rat (Damon and Dicaprio). Ugh, I've most likely lost everyone who reads this. IT'S MARTIN SCORSESE, WATCH THE MOVIE!!!","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-02-14","","RT","900808073"
4.5,"It was really violent, but the movie itself (the plot and the acting) were fantastic!","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-02-14","","RT","557782963"
4.5,"The rare film that's actually better on the second viewing.  Martin Scorsese might as well appear on camera, screaming ""Give me the f**king Oscar!"" between scenes. His latest film, a remake of the popular Asian crime-thriller ""Infernal Affairs,"" is a masterpiece of plotting and tension, with great performances all around and a rock-and-roll soundtrack that just won't quit. Tight and complex, ""The Departed"" expects you to keep up, and has little use for you if you don't.  A most refreshing attitude compared to the majority of films, which over-explain everything to ensure that Joe Average doesn't get left behind.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-02-14","","RT","152229349"
