4.0,"[center][font=Times New Roman][size=4][color=white][img]http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a4/The_Departed_poster.jpg/200px-The_Departed_poster.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=4][color=white]Well, here it is folks. The Departed is the first Martin Scorsese movie that I enjoyed. On the eve of writing my Goodfellas review, I saw this film. It was miles above the other Scorsese films I have seen. It still had the over-abundance of swearing, disguting dialog, etc., but it was different.[/color][/size][/font]
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=4][color=white]The biggest strength of this film is that Scorsese gives us a character that we care about. The movie follows many characters but focuses on Leonardo DiCaprio's character, his struggles, inner thoughts, and motivations. I, as a viewer, cared for this character and wanted to see him through the film. How DiCaprio wasn't nominated for Best Actor in this film is beyond me. He gives the best performance out of anyone in the all-star cast, even better than Nicholson. This was the biggest thing put this movie head and shoulders above the other Scorsese films I've seen.[/color][/size][/font]
 
[center][font=Times New Roman][size=4][color=#ffffff][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_27.jpg[/img][/color][/size][/font][/center]
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=4][color=white]I also really enjoyed the storyline of this movie. It was entertaining, enthralling, and captivating. I don't feel like the other Scorsese movies I've seen have those same qualities. I stand alone on this opinion, but I stand behind it nevertheless. Films such as Goodfellas and Raging Bull didn't provide a story that really ever interested me in the way The Departed did. Anyway, overall this movie was very good and I recommend it to those that can take its vulgarity.[/color][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-02-15","","RT","900763681"
