5.0,"[center][b]Plotline:[/b] Now we all know Scorcese's done this type of film before. One could retire on Goodfella's, Taxi Driver, and Mean Streets alone, but now he gives us The Departed. The film focuses on the parrallel lives of two men, each undercover with opposite and conflicting groups-The mob and the cops. Billy Costigan (Leonardo Dicaprio), just out of the academy is assigned to infiltrate the mob run by the ruthless and sadistic Costello (Jack Nicholson). Sullivan (Matt Damon), working for Costello, heads the operations from within the Mass. State Police against his own boss, actually corrupting the case and the cop's chances of catching Costello. Everything beyond this would spoil most of the bloody surprises.[/center]
[center] [/center]

[center][url=""http://www.movieweb.com/movies/film/11/2911/gal2274/12.php""][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2911/2274/lo/coa.jpg[/img][/url][/center]
[center] [/center]
[center][url=""http://www.movieweb.com/movies/film/11/2911/gal2274/09.php""][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2911/2274/lo/co7.jpg[/img][/url][/center]
[center] [/center]

[center][b]Review:[/b] I'd just like to first say that since the start of my journal here on RottenTomatoes I have never given out a 10/10 for a film, other than the movies in my top ten of all time. I'm pretty harsh when it comes to reviewing, and even though others have given this film a 10/10, I rarely see a film that I admire this much, and all readers should know this. Moreover, I know most loved the film because they were stupified by its craftmanship compared to most films out there, but I can confidently say, as a harsh critic, and die-hard Scorcesse fan that The Departed was completely worthy and an incredible feat.[/center]
[center] [/center]

[center]Scorcesse has his trademarks all over the place- risky and unique music choices, 1st person camera movements, and creative killings. The Departed really does jump the gun, firing faster than any crime film I've seen. It cuts so quickly and moves so fast, but keeps a tight focus on plot, motives, development. The acting was superb all around, probably the best ensemble cast this year. What was important to me was the dialogue, which was never showy or flashy, but mesmerizing. It had a style to it for sure, but unlike a Luck Number Slevin it wasn't trying too hard to impress you. Nicholson and Walhberg, not to discount Dicaprio and a few others, lock in your attention when they speak. Nicholson above all makes you cringe, holding your breath, not knowing what he plans to do with Billy. I suppose this is The Departed's greatest accomplishment-the ability to create genuine tension, keeping the viewer on edge because he/she cares about these characters and becomes obsessed with finding out what will happen to them.[/center]
[center] [/center]

[center]The parallels between the characters of Dicaprio and Damon, as they carve the same paths on opposite sides were beautifully highlighted. The best example of this is when Damon calls Dicaprio on the cell phone and they both listen for the other to talk first. Yes, the movie is violent, but selectively, for the most part in the finale. The Departed was really about characters. How they interact, betray, and kill one another, but the movie didn't focus on how they killed each other, just why. For incredible acting, a dense storyline, and brutally funny dialogue The Departed stands alongside Goodfellas as one of the master's best, if not the best mob picture ever, and you can hold me to that...[/center]
[center] [/center]

[center][b]Who I liked:[/b] Mark Wahlberg, not only with the best accent, but probably with the most memorable performance based on his short appearance.[/center]
[center][b]Best: [/b]Seeing the comparisons between moles on each side of the law.[/center]
[center][b]Worst:[/b] I heard a couple of days ago that there was going to be a sequel. Not that I would mind, but I just don't want this to become a franchise like the Ocean movies.[/center]
[center] [/center]


