4.0,"I had my hopes pretty high for this movie. I mean, I hadn't heard a single bad thing about it. It's already # 74 on the IMDB Top 250. I mean, look at the cast. The only person who could screw up a cast as good as that it Steven Soderburgh. [b]The Departed[/b] is not a masterpiece, though. It seems overdrawn and too celebratory in its simplicity to be a true ""modern American masterpiece"".

""But,"" you say, ""you gave [b]Rat Race[/b] a higher score!"" In the veign of any Scorcese movie, my response to you is ""Fuck you!"" (I think that word is used well over 200 times in the movie; is there a count?) The movie is obscene, violent, and vulgar. But it's a Scorcese movie. The man's an amazing director, and it's expected in a mob movie by Scorcese. I've seen his [b]Casino[/b], [b]Raging Bull[/b], and [b]The Last Waltz[/b]. [b]The Departed[/b] holds its own against all of Scorcese's other movies, and proves that Scorcese is far from a one-note director (well, duh).

The cast is amazing. The film takes place in Boston amidst Irish mobs (a la [b]Boondock Saints[/b]) Jack Nicholson plays Frank Costello, the mob boss. Matt Damon plays Colin Sullivan, a spy within the Boston police system who works for Costello. On the other end, there's Leo DiCaprio who plays Billy Costigan, a spy sent into Costello's circle by the Boston police. The upper echelon of Massachussets state police include Oliver Queenan (Martin Sheen), Quignan (Mark Wahlberg), and Ellerby (Alec Baldwin). Sullivan and Costigan are both trying to figure out who the moles are in the seperate camps. As their paths run dangerously close, they both fall for shrink Madolyn (Vera Farmiga).

Farmiga gives a great performance as the only main female character in the movie. Her character is underused, as is Mark Wahlberg who gives another great performance (not [b]Boogie Nights[/b] great, but it helps you forget that he did [b]Rock Star[/b]). DiCaprio and Damon are both electric in their leading roles. They both add a focused intensity and Leo really impressed me. He gives an especially good performance in this movie. Alec Baldwin gives an awesome performance (he's the man), and Martin Sheen, Anthony Anderson (shock), and Ray Winstone give solid performances. Jack Nicholson is, of course, brilliant as the bad guy. He delivers every line meticulously. He's got it down to a science and his performance is nothing short of brilliant.

The movie is too long. That's my complaint. It lost my attention at parts. Otherwise, it's another great Martin Scorcese film. It gets a solid as rock [b]8/10[/b]. Not 8.5/10, though.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-02-17","","RT","900761844"
