4.0,"[img]http://image2.sina.com.cn/ent/d/2006-09-28/U105P28T3D1267585F326DT20060928192034.jpg[/img]
 
 
 
The Departed tells the simple story of a young cop, Billy Costigan (Leonardo Di Caprio), who's life has been devoted to crime which results in him as the perfect role of a mole. Costigan goes behind the enemy lines of the Boston mob which is controlled by the dangerous Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). Costigan has to earn Costello's trust and aid his detective handlers (Mark Wahlberg and Martin Sheen) to bring Costello down for good.
 
 
[img]http://movies.go.com/i/movies/652321/gallery/departed_3.jpg[/img]
 
 
Meanwhile, SIU officer Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is trusted by everyone. No one suspects that Sullivan is in fact Costello's mole. Proving that Costello is not going to be an easy target to take down. 
 
As these two battling sides cross and collide, lies, deception, danger, lost lives and terrible consequences are the results.
 
 
[img]http://soulhonky.com/candanny/061004_departed_hmed_3p.hmedium.jpg[/img]
 
 
Directed by the master of mob movie making, Martin Scorsese, The Departed has the typical Scrorsese essentials: crime, guns, violence, and (of course) gangsters. Like the excellent Goodfellas and Casino, The Departed starts off very well. With a fast paced introduction, the viewer is immediatly interested. The films fast energetic pace and inter-twinning plot is a little bit of a struggle to keep up with. But, still engaging and entertaining, which results in great film viewing. Rather than an entire mob family shooting chunks off cops in a bloody gunfight war, The Departed leans a bit away from the violent subject matter to concentrate more on plot, drama, characters and performances. Speaking of performances, the acting in TD is surprisingly timid. High class actors such as Leonardo Di Caprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg and Martin Sheen all seem to ""act-down"" rather than ""act-up"". In some parts of TD, the actors also give off the impression that they are reading directly from the script. Although this is looking at the performances in minute detail, overall, the acting is quite good. Especially in Leonardo Di Caprio's convincing role. Jack Nicholson however, could have been better in-my-opinion. Playing a mob boss with a bubbly personality, JN is NOT convincing. I would have liked him to be vicious, serious and treatening. 
Matt Damon and Mark Wahlberg give limited performances. MD plays the a**hole rat while MW is a though talking hard cop with a one sided personality, to trash talk and threaten anyone he sees. Dull. If looking for effort, LDC certainly gives the best. 
 
 
[img]http://www.king5.com/topstories/stories/M_IMAGE.110e915f0fa.93.88.fa.d0.156fd092.jpg[/img]
 
 
The Departed is lengthy and strong. Not Scorsese's best and certainly not even near as good as Goodfellas. In the end The Departed is a very entertaining drama/action thriller. Scorsese does make a very good effort, and with four Oscars including Best Film and Best Director.... it's worth it.
 
 
[img]http://content.answers.com/main/content/wp/en/0/04/Boston_Celtics_logo.png[/img]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-02-20","","RT","900901529"
