1.5,"If you'd like to have fun during a Scorcese movie (because if you're me, you're not having it watching the film) try to hold your breath between F bombs.  Not only will you never even strain for breath, but in some sentences, you may hyperventilate yourself starting and stopping.  I've mentioned it in the past, but I'm not a Scorcese fan.  No, that's not really accurate.  I loathe the man.  I think he's overrated to an unbelievable degree, and that his scripts are the most laughable pieces of garbage imaginable.  His dialogue is incapable of standing on its own without being an insane madlib of profanity, and the few lines that don't contain it, aren't powerful, aren't smart, and aren't worthy of all the lauding he gets.  I don't understand his entire catalogue of praise, but I especially don't get why this is one of the best reviewed movies of 2006.
 
The movie is a LONG 2.5 hours and, despite the complexity of the plot, could have been trimmed significantly.  The story itself could have been compelling if accompanied by dialogue from the planet earth, but most of the time I was just laughing at how stupid it was.  I will say that I thought Leonardo DiCaprio did an excellent job in the movie, but the rest of the cast, though certainly an outstanding ensemble, was subpar.  How did Mark Wahlberg wrangle an Oscar nom out of about 10 minutes of screen time (none of which I think they'll be able to air during the Oscar broadcast)?  Especially disappointing was possibly the worst performance of Jack Nicholson's career.  He slouches through the movie doing a hammy impersonation of his own persona, and it's really kind of sad (which I would say for the movie as a whole had it evoked anything other than pitiable disdain from me).  Waste of time.  I try every year to see as many of the best picture nominees as I can, and that this was one, makes me hate the Academy even more than I normally do.
