3.5,"This was not my thing.  I mean, yeah, Jack Nicholson was brilliant, because he just is.  And the directing was good, although a bit heavyhanded.  (Seriously, Martin Scorcese, how many shots of the Massachussets State House did we need?)  I was even favorably impressed by Leonardo DiCaprio, and that takes some doing.

But for one thing, there are an awful lot of characters.  This left me curiously unmoved when they died, and man, did a lot of them die.  Dropping dead all over the place.  While swearing.  I also felt several of the plot points were left unresolved, such as exactly what is going on with Alec Baldwin.  (There are those who would say that it's a good question all the way 'round, but I'll leave that for someone cattier than I.)  

Yes, all right.  As those of you who've read my fiction now, I'm hardly the one to pick on other people about having too many characters.  But you know, I work very hard on getting you to care for as many of those characters as I can.  Here, I pretty much cared for the shrink, and even then, there wasn't anyone I felt she should end the movie with.

There's a lot of Scorcese I haven't seen, but there's also some I have.  This movie, I felt, was nowhere in the same league as [i]Taxi Driver[/i].  For one, most of his cast wasn't in the same league as that cast.  As I said, I was surprised at how much I liked DiCaprio in this, likewise Marky Mark.  (I'm sorry for the man, but he's actually pretty much the root of my belief that you should never pick a stage name you wouldn't mind being known by when you were 90.)  And Alec Baldwin's always good, likewise Matt Damon (well, okay, they're both usually good), and Martin Sheen and Jack Nicholson are usually brilliant.  But come on!  Neither Matt Damon nor Leonardo DiCaprio are ever, in their wildest dreams, going to be DeNiro.  And while neither Cybil Shepard nor Jodie Foster play particularly strong characters, they're both far stronger than the women in this movie.  Either of them.

Maybe that's one of the reasons I'm not much of a Scorcese buff.  His worlds are completely dominated by men with guns, so they don't call much to a woman who doesn't like guns.  And so many of his women, if they aren't prostitutes, are still pretty subsumed by their men.  Gods know I'm not going to psychoanalyze the man, but it explains why the movies aren't my bag, huh?

Anyway, it's up for Best Picture, Best Supporting Actor (Marky Mark), Best Director, Best Adapted Screenplay, and Best Editing.  Oh, and for the record, I know that's not the title the Academy is currently giving those awards.  However, mine are simpler and less conciously PC.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-02-25","","RT","900810868"
