4.5,"[b][size=3][font=Times New Roman]The Departed
[/font][/size][/b]
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]I saw this way back in October, then again two weeks ago.  What more could I say about the most recent Best Picture winner?  Well, the only thing I didn't like about this movie, really, is something very small, but I couldn't ignore it.  You know, I like the Stones, I know Marty likes the Stones (I can call him Marty because we're tight like that), but here's my problem/puzzlement:  I'm alright with ""Gimme Shelter"" and didn't mind it being used again in one of his movies...it was put to good effect in the beginning of the movie.  But, why, oh why, is it used a second time?!  The second time the song is used is when Damon's girlfriend comes home from having coffee with Leo (I think).  As they embrace, the song starts to kick in.  I assume it's played because both characters are in danger of having their lies exposed, their security is in jeopardy, what have you.  I guess.  But, a) it's distracting, b) it's unnecessary, c) it's overkill, d) the intro is freaking looped! e) why continuously loop it? f) how about just not playing the song?  g) did I mention it's distracting, h) and lame?   

The scene doesn't last long, but still, I expected more out of Marty, one of the architects of deftly applying rock songs to his films.  I also wondered about the cover of Floyd's ""Hey You""...I wondered for a second why he would use a cover, but then realized it was probably because Roger Waters is a pretentious jerk and wouldn't let them use the original.

Aside from that, though, I enjoyed the movie.  Thought DiCaprio should've gotten nominated for this rather than [i]Blood Diamond[/i], but I suppose they voters didn't want to single out any of the lead actors for that movie so as to not hurt any feelings, perhaps. 

That's all I got to say 'bout that.

[b]Casino Royale

[/b]I can't say that I'm a huge Bond fan, but this movie fucking rocked.  That opening has got to be one of the best Bond openings yet, with the normally ceremonial shooting into the camera actually having a function: he kills the guy.  The whole movie is pure entertainment and you end up leaving the theatre nodding your head in approval.  One hell of an ending.

This whole refashioning of tired franchises is a hot trend in Hollywood, but I wonder how long it can remain fresh.  How long before the new Batman movies turn into Joel Schumacher disasters?  How long can this reborn Bond franchise last?  Hopefully, if Hollywood has learned anything, they'll quit while they're ahead.
[/font][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-02-27","","RT","900756829"
