2.5,"Well, gosh....where to begin....I'm sure I'm in a vast minority, but I really can't stand Martin Scorcese; have never really [i]loved[/i] a single one of his films, have [i]hated[/i] a lot of them but it's more than that, really....even if I don't like or appreciate a particular director's or actor's ""style"", I'm usually quite capable of still understanding and/or acknowledging WHY they're good at what they do; just that it's not my 'taste'. With Scorcese, though, I really don't understand his hype; everything he does seems so formulaic, the biggest talent I think he has is picking good screenwriters and big actors to carry his films for him. That said, knowing not only the director but the subject matter and the cast led me to immediately not want to see The Departed. But when a film garners over 90% positive reviews from both the critics [u]and[/u] the public, it's a little hard to ignore. So I finally decided, while I might not ""like"" it, The Departed certainly must be an excellent film and, as a movie buff, I should obviously see it. I was better off assuming everyone else was correct.
 
I don't understand the praise heaped on the direction of this movie. It was overly long; scenes and dialogue were repetitive and occasionally unnecessary. We actually stopped it midway and decided to finish it another night because it felt interminable.....and were dismayed the next evening to find out there was still an hour and 40 minutes to go....enough time for an entire film and we felt like we had already watched two. There were multiple incidents of 'showy tricks' like montages of partial scenes cut together so we knew the action was happening at the same time or loud, unfitting music blaring over a segueway scene to be sharply cut off for dialogue so it passed as art. It all felt very amateurish to me, like ""Look at me! Directing a film! I'm a legend!"". 
 
I don't understand a lot of the praise heaped on the acting in this movie. Leonardo DiCaprio is excellent; I haven't yet seen Blood Diamond, but I can already see why people say he was nominated for the 'wrong' film. He more than holds his own with a huge cast; he actually does a better job than most of them. Like most of the characters, his is multi-layered and complex; unlike most of the actors, he [i][u]shows[/u][/i] that to us....we feel his pain, anger and confusion and see the desperation behind his bravado. Matt Damon is ""OK""; you never really hated him or felt sorry for him, or rooted for him; he was just......there, reading his lines. Martin Sheen is Martin Sheen; not necessarily a bad thing, but when he shows up, you know what you're getting. Mark Wahlberg is....well, let's just say, I don't get the point. We already know 2 1/2 hours of gutter language that doesn't even make [i]sense[/i] a lot of the time will garner an Oscar nom because it's ""daring""; Tom Cruise already proved that in Magnolia (a FAR better film). However, until the final scene of the film (which redeems his presence, if not his performance), there doesn't even seem to be a REASON for Wahlberg's character except to cuss. The fact that he just fires off the lines without any display of emotion or development of character actually made us laugh a couple of times....and at times we weren't supposed to. Ditto whichever Baldwin brother it was with whom he was nonsensically bantering. Now the biggest sin in this movie is Jack Nicholson, with his gigantic talent, working with one of his most beloved directors not bothering to even TRY to act. This is almost 3 hours of him doing a rather lame Jack Nicholson impression, with some of the worst one-liner-heavy dialogue I've ever seen. ""This aint reality TV"" indeed; someone should have told him [u][i]that[/i][/u] wasn't the quality for which they were aiming before most of the times he opened his mouth.
 
The music was, often, awful and inappropriate. There's really not much worth note here in regard to sets, costumes, cinematography etc, but that's all all right because that IS appropriate in a film of this nature; it's not what should be important here. The editing is fine but we definitely could have done with a LOT more heavy-handedness in it. 
 
All this being said, why am I still giving The Departed a slightly positive rating? Well, I have to admit, minus about an hour of unnecessary meandering, the storyline is brilliant. It's hugely complex, often tragic and very smart. The last 20 minutes or so really turned it around from a movie I was sincerely hating to one I can, at least begrudgingly, respect. There were several shocking moments, disturbing twists and a really great, stunning ending. And, even though at times I thought the film almost denigrated into camp, as a whole it pulled together quite well in regard to the story it was telling and the following, from the very beginning of some of the characters to their ultimate fate, the parallel of Damon's and DiCaprio's characters, the complexity of how they were both forced to live their lives and the way they both struggled with it, culminating in the sad futility of either--was outstanding. It was the [u]one[/u] thing that left me thinking after the rest of the junk was over. And I really liked that. Even despite my pre-disposition, I had built this film up in my mind as a classic; something I might not 'like', but a must-see for my catalogue. I obviously was seriously disappointed, but.......I'm glad I saw it, I guess; just think I would've been better off continuing to assume everyone else knew better and saying, ""I never got around"" to watching it.
 
ADDENDUM: After much reflection, I'm leaving the review intact as it's still how I feel, but I'm yanking my ""slightly positive"" rating. An excellent idea that's poorly executed and one outstanding performance do not a 'positive' film make. I, again, wish there was a neutral rating because I don't think this deserves the 'rotten' a 5 will give it but.....when waffling between rating points on a difficult film, my criterium has always been, ""But what was it TRYING to be?"" And the The Departed couldn't've more clearly been trying to be a giant Oscar epic with a huge budget, a huge cast of talent and a highly acclaimed director. It might have wound up a '6' of a movie under different terms but, with this much money and talent involved, I'm going to judge it a little more harshly. There's no excuse for squandering something that should have been great with this many people that know better. So.....a '5' it is.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-02-27","","RT","900826463"
