5.0,"[font=Times New Roman][color=black][b]The Departed.  [/b]5 out of 5.  With [i]The Departed[/i], Martin Scorsese returns to the genre that made him one of the greatest directors in cinema.  In a complex story of police corruption and organized crime in the south of Boston, Scorsese paints a psychological and visually stunning portrait of crime, and while complex and intellectual the film remains thrilling and violent, ranging anywhere from Jack Nicholsons Irish crime boss Costello mentoring a young future state cop (an early version of Matt Damons character, the seemingly honest good cop who turns out to be a criminal informant), to Mark Wahlberg as a badass police sergeant acting as the vulgar yang to Martin Sheens collected yin, to Leonardo DiCapprio as an honest cop who didnt make it into the states smashing a glass of cranberry juice into an insulters head.  All of the performances are unique and compelling, with a twist of irony to them as law enforcement slowly grows corrupt.  However, it is not the acting that is the main attraction; Scorsese is the real star, taking his earlier, more hard-boiled works of [i]Taxi Driver [/i]and [i]Mean Streets [/i]and mixing it with the well-rounded intrigue of [i]Goodfellas [/i]and [i]Casino[/i].  It was about time that Scorsese got the Oscar for Best Director, and this is no sympathy award on behalf of the Academy; [i]The Departed [/i]is without a doubt one of Scorseses best works, period.  His Oscar was long overdue, and I couldnt think of a better picture for which he should have been awarded.  Necessary viewing.  Rated R.[/color][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][color=#000000][/color][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][color=#000000][b]The Final Ruling:  :fresh:  [/b]Fresh.[/color][/font]
 
[font=Times New Roman][color=black]I've been a fan of Martin Scorsese for years.  I've seen his best films; [i]Taxi Driver[/i], [i]Goodfellas[/i], [i]Raging Bull[/i], the lot of them.  And out of all of those truly great pictures, none, I repeat, none, have has this well rounded of a story, characters and action.  I won't say that this is Scorsese's best; let time and the audience determine that.  All I have to say is that [i]The Departed [/i]is definitely up there.  Way up there.[/color][/font]
[font=Times New Roman][color=#000000][/color][/font] 
