3.5,"1st: Suspense. OH THE SUSPENSE!

Premise: In the lovely city of Boston Mass, we are taken into two worlds of corruption, the criminal underworld and the crime fighting elite.  There's a rat in both.  The question is which side finds out first, and who dies in the process...
 
WSW: Fans of mob-ish, good-cop bad-cop drama/action flicks, and the eye candy that make up the cast: Leo, Mark W., Matt D.; Jack, Alex and Martin S. are in this too.  FYI: it is as violent as ppl say it is.

Overall: The acting is good, the plot is pretty tight, and it was pretty suspenseful.  However, secondary to the wattage of the star power, I had some difficulty with suspension of disbelief.  But this is not to say that others will have this problem.

Here are the RIPPLES: spoilers
Actors: the acting was relatively solid, but I just can't believe how hard it is for ppl to do the Boston accent.
Leonardo Dicaprio: Really did a good job.  Him, I occasionally forgot that he was Leo.
Mark Wahlberg: I really liked him in this.  I've said it before, it's not fun to watch him unless he's a little bad mixed in with whatever good he's allowed to be.  He's a lot of fun in this one.
Matt Damon: He did what he was supposed to do, seem good, but be really bad.  It's kinda Mr. Ripley with a horrible accent.
Alec Baldwin: Oh, he's great in this.  Really, seriously.  I almost forgot how good of an actor he was, but he's quite good in this.  Pay attention when he talks, it's quite fast, low frequency, and very funny.
Jack Nicholson: is there really anything you can say.  He's Jack, he'll always be Jack, and there's nothing else you can really say about him than that.

The good.
The suspense.  I got so nervous at times, I was shaking, not from fear, but from pent-up frustration to find out how it's going to ened.

Mark Wahlberg: I can't say whether or not he deserved the Oscar for his performance, but he was pretty intense.  Interesting, Dignam's probably the most ethical A-hole ever.

Leo: There were some moments of vulnerability that were quite tangible, and I did achieve some suspension of disbelief, but these moments were fleeting in the entirety of the movie, and really only with Leo.

Dropkick Murphy: everytime their music came on, I wanted to rip off my pants, throw on a kilt and start a line of river dancing. I LOVED the music.

The doppelgangers: Mark vs Matt - I think it's funny when they are in the same movie, bc they look so much alike, and interestingly enough, in a bunch of Clooney flicks, it's either Matt or Mark playing some supporting role that the other probably could have done just was well in.  

Everyone, except Bill and Queenan, getting what they deserve.

The not so good: 
Really, it was mostly the suspension of disbelief.  I had a hard time getting over the fact that these actors where who they were rather than the characters they were playing.  Part of it was me and my overawareness of the Hollywood machine, but a part of it is also on the actors and the movie itself to provide a manner by which to help the viewer forget the pieces that make up the movie, and rather experience the totality of all the parts.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-03-01","","RT","900948340"
5.0,"Stellar all around.  The cast is the real draw, obviously, and each one gives an absolutely top-notch performance.  I was especially impressed by the leads (DiCaprio, Nicholson, Damon, and Wahlberg) and the way they found the tension in every breath, every word, and every silent moment.  It makes you hold your breath and lean forward in your seat, let me tell ya.  Of course, that makes the payoff moments all the more effective.  And boy, if the cast wasn't enough, the direction was also a treat.  I can't say enough about it - there's a reason this won that Golden Boy.","a2039555-b8b4-4434-9c31-332bcccde6d0",False,True,False,False,5.0,"2007-03-01","","RT","101758845"
