3.0,"When Martin Scorcese won the Oscar for best director for this movie, the PA announcer at the Academy Awards said the Scorcese was quoted as saying ""this was the first movie he ever made that had a plot.""  I disagree.  Taxi had a plot, Age of Innocence had a plot, and Gangs of New York, too.  The difference between these past Scorcese films and [i]The Departed[/i] is that they had [i]intriguing[/i] plots and they also had real human characters that were developed throughout the stories.  [i]The Departed[/i] may have won Scorcese the Oscar because it was ""his time,"" but it wasn't close to the best film he's ever made.  I don't want to say he sold out, but this movie had a Hollywood formula type feel to it, like a [i]Mission Impossible[/i] - mix a little mystery and action, and make sure there are corrupt cops, too.  The only thing that seemed uniquely Scorsese was the way the last scenes played out.  Oh, and the use of the Rolling Stones' ""Gimme Shelter"" in the soundtrack.  Definitely, not Scorcese's best, but that's because he has set his own standards so high by his previous works.  At any rate, by any other standards, a decent movie.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2007-03-02","","RT","900957946"
