4.5,"Despite the fact that 2006 has come and gone, remembering the slew of films that were released over the course of those twelve months comes as an easy task, especially considering that last year marked one of the best for movie goers and critics alike.  With films released from the likes of Clint Eastwood, Darren Aronofsky, Guillermo del Toro, and Christopher Nolan, it's no wonder that audiences found their money going toward the thousands of theaters that populate our country.  One movie in particular that still remains on the tip of every critic's tongue, however, has gradually built a reputation as being one of the most favored films in recent years.  Of course, I am talking about The Departed, a picture from acclaimed director, Martin Scorsese.  Despite it being a remake and almost a completely different interpretation of the film it was based off of (""Infernal Affairs""), The Departed was a movie that immediately made an impact through the use of an all-star cast and a storyline that captured you right off the bat and didn't allow any slack until the credits rolled.

The Departed finds itself deep within the depths of Boston's crime syndicate and even with the opening dialogue it is assured that this will be a crime drama that will be incomparable to anything else.  Leonardo DiCaprio stars as Billy Costigan, a young man with desires to be involved with the Massachusetts Police Department.  However, alongside him also comes Matt Damon's character, Colin Sullivan, who, despite expressing the same aspirations, finds his allegiance in that of Frank Costello (portrayed by Jack Nicholson), the powerful Irish crime lord.  Naturally, this is just the beginning of a series of weaving events that eventually leads to a climax that leaves you breathless.  Furthering the storyline, it becomes apparent that lies and deceit are what become the primary factors that run through the storyline's bloodstream and eventually, it becomes a game of cat and mouse as both the characters of DiCaprio and Damon are forced to discover who is the mole within the force and even more so, the growing involvement of Costello complicates things even further.

Being that this is a Scorsese film, it should automatically be concluded that nothing but the highest caliber of directing will be witnessed and to add on, it should be declared that Scorsese is one of the finest directors that cinema has ever seen, if not one of the most snubbed.  His ability to craft movies based on something that appears and sounds so simple and turn them into something captivating and compelling is awe-inspiring and with The Departed, he proves that his skills age like fine wine; he'll continue to make films more memorable than his previous work and continue to staple his name in as the archetype of the thing that has become known as film-making.  However, it should be noted that one of the aspects of why Scorsese films have always been so great is because of near perfect casting each and every time.  With this particular film, we witness mint acting from not just DiCaprio, Damon, and Nicholson, but also from Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, and Ray Winstone.  To pile on top of all of this, we're also greeted with terrific editing, cinematography, and an excellent screenplay.

Hopefully it can be assumed by now that The Departed isn't a farce.  It's not a film that fails in any way to live up to the hype that has been built around it and it surely deserves the amount of award nominations that it has received.  If anything, this is a movie that will go down as one that has so many memorable aspects to it that it'll be impossible to forget for years to come.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-03-06","","RT","900959417"
