3.5,"Ah, the old ""FFF"" lifestyle which I led to such rousing success. The memories...

First up is The 300, as seen from the second row of a packed house last Friday.

Did you like the battle scenes in Lord of the Rings? Extend that to a couple hours, and we have the comic book Spartans slashing and gashing their way to ecstatic machismo, eating shiny apples in periods of repose, and ensuring you never forget what country they are from with their monotone shouting.

I thought it was OK. The style is nice, and the violence unapologetic, so it passed. 

For guys that weren't supposedly emotional, a few characters experienced some breakdowns, but what the hell, it's just a big dumb flick, so make sure to leave your mouth half open while viewing and suck in the blood. 

I guess the message is that the deformed and effete make for bad people, and their honourless leadership reflects in a poor quality of life. Only by leading with your erect penis can you be a real man.

Whatever. It had some camp value. Huggy ended a long run of stinkers. Looking at the previews shown, I can see us showing up for some fine trash in ""The Grindhouse"" and getting out for the big Spidey-Venom punch up as well. 
------------------------------------------------------------------

Little Miss Sunshine had a few laughs, but was ultimately too stupid for me to get into.

You didn't know you were colour blind, eh kid? That moment completely turned me off for the rest of the show.

I think it's just not my style. I am desensitized to the point that it doesn't shock me at all, while to other viewers it could be so hilarious and wacky.

I still think Polyester is one of the finest family comedies of all time.

[B]EDIT[/B] : Roguebooks nailed it on this movie! I haven't been as excited about somebody reviewing a movie in a long time. Read it!

[URL]http://www.rottentomatoes.com/vine/journal_view.php?journalid=182405&entryid=370139&view=public[/URL] 
------------------------------------------------------------------

Marty's Oscar pic made me interested in seeing the original Infernal Affairs, and I've put it on order. As homage, that's a sucess.

A have a vague disquiet that the cadence and content of all character's speech in this film run into one another. It reminded one of an old style gangster pic in that regard. I was waiting for Edward G Robinson to round the corner.

Not being familiar with the original, there are twists and turns that come off well. The closing sequences are great. Perhaps a viewer familiar with the HK flick wouldn't be as amused by them.

I did like this movie, but I wouldn't throw the ""CLASSIC"" moniker on it by any means. I'll have to look at it again.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-03-14","","RT","900738995"
