2.0,"So i actually saw this on tv, not awful, just plain forgettabe, dull and average at best crime flick that could have been directed by anyone. The third film in the ""Di Caprio"" trilogy of Scorcese is just the final confirmation that the man once known for making classic films (Scorcese that is) is now a studio tool. Sure, there are some common themes of his work here, and ocasional flashes of his touch, but it's all lost in a sea of redundancy in a very flat movie that you will barely notice it was directed by the man.

But the real problem here is the script, quite bad in many ways, from the awful dialogues to the over-plotted and pointless things that throws around. A dumb love triangle, annoying characters, and for some reason, the strange need to remind the audience again and again that these people are irish. Not to mention how moralistic the resolution is.
""But fuck you man, the acting is solid, you can't deny that"" The acting you say? Left a lot to be desired, and i mean from everyone. Nicholson is doing a self-parody of his former roles, Damon, Marky Mark and Di Caprio are like the same guy with different names. Vera Farmiga? Boring to watch. Sheen and Baldwin barely had anything to do at all. Also, for some strange reason the writters decided to have the entire male cast act as if they were a bunch of drunk frat boys, cursing at each other most of the time and saying the most stupid dialogues i have heard since Y tu mama tambien and Kill Bill.

So in the end, this film is the proof of how hollywood loves to do circle jerking. The whole existance of this movie gets sum up in a big fashion party of oscars and pats in the back. If Scorcese wasn't the director less people will be praising this.","",False,True,False,False,2.0,"2007-03-14","","RT","312343085"
