4.5,"[font=Arial][size=2]There is so much action going on in [b][i]The Departed[/i][/b] that halfway through the movie you begin to anticipate needing to see it again. ""Want to see it again"" is also true. Scorsese keeps you on the edge of your seat expecting to see William Costigan's cover blown at any minute (Leonardo DiCaprio is undercover posing as a bad ex-cop). [/size][/font]
[font=Arial][size=2][/size][/font] 
[font=Arial][size=2]Matt Damon (Colin Sullivan), the bad cop (Costello's mole on the force) posing as a good one and Jack Nicholson (crime lord Frank Costello), both turn in excellent performances; but DiCaprio's performance as the endangered, undercover officer who lives moment by moment on the edge of life and death is so good that no one could have objected had he won the Oscar.[/size][/font]

[center][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1159016/photo_27.jpg[/img][/center]
[center][i]Crime Lord Costello meets with police mole Costigan [/i][/center]
 
[font=Arial][size=2]Vera Farmiga's also turns in a strong performance as the police counselor who is the love interest for both DiCaprio and Damon. It's a good thing she gives a good performance; as usual, women are in short supply in this Scorsese film. Ultimately, it is Farmiga who holds the card that forces Damon to do the right thing and honor a good officer. Without this recognition for DiCaprio's character, [i]The Departed[/i] would have no ""good triumphs over evil"" message and we would have to be content with just a highly entertaining, violent and bloody action flick. [/size][/font]
[font=Arial][size=2][/size][/font] 
[font=Arial][size=2]Many critics say this movie is Scorsese's return to form; perhaps so, although I found [i]The Aviator[/i] to be an excellent movie even though it did not follow his usual violent and bloody formula. Certainly, there are so many ""departeds"" that I look forward to seeing it again just to see if I missed one. [/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-03-15","","RT","900888005"
