4.5,"<a href=""http://photobucket.com"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://i144.photobucket.com/albums/r188/dnlcmch/images-9.jpg"" border=""0"" alt=""Photo Sharing and Video Hosting at Photobucket""></a>

After a slew of Oscar attempts with a wide range of period pieces, legendary director Martin Scorsese returns to his gritty gangster roots with his latest effort, The Departed, a remake of the Chinese crime hit, Infernal Affairs. Now, just because The Departed is a remake doesn't exactly make it a rehash. After filming subsequent period films that emphasize on immense detail, Scorsese once again utilizes in immense, gritty, down-to-earth realism along with his own personal style that he displays in each and every single frame of the picture. And what a tremendous picture it is! 

From the film's opening scenes, we feel Scorsese's presence. We witness his return to the gangster genre as we hear The Rolling Stone's ""Gimme Shelter"", forming a glorious juxtaposition of rock n' roll and crime-induced visuals. We hear a voice. It's the voice of an aged gangster, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson), giving us a rundown of how things work in the Irish-American mean streets of Boston, as if he owned the city with a golden touch. Costello isn't a sinister or malevolent Mafioso; he's a dangerously suave, powerful criminal who can give you a beating if you disrespect him to the slightest degree. As we witness the Costello's first encounter with the young Collin Sullivan, who is immediately taken under his wing, we fast-forward to present day. Costello has planted Sullivan (Matt Damon) into the Boston Criminal Investigations Unit in order to always stay one step ahead of the law. It's a meticulous chess game that Sullivan is happy to be a part of, as he quickly rises up the ranks of law enforcement, maintains a steady relationship with his department's therapist, and garners an abundant social life among his colleagues. As Costello enacts his tactics, law enforcement conjures a plan of their own. After being handpicked for the assignment, Billy Costigan, a young recruit with intentions of becoming a successful state trooper, must infiltrate the Boston mafia and quickly gain Costello's confidence in order to put him behind bars. Yet, as both sides of the law discover that there's a mole in their mists, it becomes a deadly race to see who can find the rat first. 


<a href=""http://photobucket.com"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://i144.photobucket.com/albums/r188/dnlcmch/Dep.jpg"" border=""0"" alt=""Photo Sharing and Video Hosting at Photobucket""></a>

The story is rich in its prosperity for its character development, specifically, and solely, upon its two leads. As Sullivan and Costigan are consumed by their double-lives, they become more and more confused about the philosophy of loyalty, making the line between good and evil somewhat blurred in their vision. And, as we garner more and more recognition with these characters, we garner more and more sympathy for them, making the grueling suspense of the chess game that is played between both sides of the law even more heart pounding. And, in reality, we come to realize that Costigan and Sullivan are actually two sides of the same coin. Although they are on different sides of the law, they are not true enemies. They are just two similar men who happened to grow up on two different sides of the street, and with a different moral compass. Both Sullivan and Costigan share a fair idea of loyalty for their beliefs and for their peers, and would do anything in order to accomplish what is needed for them. This is where the true crux of the film lies ? within the story's emphasis for its characters and their inner demons, no matter what side of the law they are on. 

What the star-studded ensemble cast was able to accomplish with this film is absolutely spellbinding. Each member of the cast brings all he's got to the table in a top-notch performance. DiCaprio has come a long way since his ""teen idol"" Titanic days, and has now emerged as the sort of ""Marlon Brando"" figure for today's generation. There's no doubt in my mind that Billy Costigan carries most of the film's emotional power, allowing DiCaprio to build an overall poignant, subtle performance that perfectly displays the effects of his character's situation and the environment he is brought into. Matt Damon gives an equally subtle performance, fueled with a silent, devout essence. We can feel Sullivan's struggle to maintain a void of lies as we glare into the facial expressions and humane eyes that Damon masterfully provides. We can sense his character's ease with a lifestyle of charm and lies among his double-social life, yet, we can also feel the weight that is pushed onto his back as a result. Of the supporting cast, Mark Whalberg, Martin Sheen, Alec Baldwin, and Jack Nicholson all maintain solid performances throughout the film and also manage to conjure most of the comic relief. From the unforeseeable Jack Nicholson to the dirty-mouthed Mark Whalberg, to the fast-talking Alec Baldwin, nobody overacts and nobody over-ranks over the other, which leads to another prosperous asset to the film ? the fact that each and every single character plays a crucial role to the film's final payoff. 

<a href=""http://photobucket.com"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://i144.photobucket.com/albums/r188/dnlcmch/dep2.jpg"" border=""0"" alt=""Photo Sharing and Video Hosting at Photobucket""></a>

The Departed is really like a grand Shakespearian story told in an intense Scorsesian style. Its plot structure is one to behold, fueled with edge-of-your seat excitement and unforeseeable predicaments. And, above that successful injection of adrenaline, there's also a vital connection with the two leads. A connection that slowly builds up more and more until it utilizes in the film's tragic climax. 

As I said, the film may just be just as much Scorsese's as it is China's. The Departed is a remake that initially stresses the director's style, from his gritty realism, to the use of rock n' roll with flying cameras. And, in all honesty, the film is like on big rock n' roll song, similar to its signature use of ""Gimme Shelter"". It starts off with a memorable opening, then slowly build up to its driven, heart-pounding crescendo. All in all, The Departed is an achievement for not only the careers of everyone involved, but for the modern crime genre itself.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-03-19","","RT","222593669"
