2.5,"After watching ""Infernal Affairs"" I have to concede that this was an inferior remake, and it's one of the weakest Scorsese films I've seen. Martin Scorsese has often said that when making a film it's almost like making two films: One for yourself, and the other for the audiences, well I think he pandered too much for the latter. OK the dialogue is lively and Matt Damon gives a nice performance, but the last act of the film just feels rushed, and the closing shot of the film is just plain terrible, it could actually be the worst ending to a film I've ever seen. Not only that, it goes over the top with the violence and swearing, it's like it's trying extra hard to shock you, Scorsese is above that. It's not that subtle either (there is no lingering question as to who the father of the child was). When I first saw it in the Cinema's I thought it was great, but on repeat viewings it just doesn't hold up at all. It was one of the greatest injustices that Scorsese was denied an Oscar all those times, it's arguably a greater one that he finally won an Oscar for a film that didn't deserve it.","324a17dc-d644-42cf-ad75-648dfae44585",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-03-23","","RT","153706950"
4.5,"Scorsese's picture was not a disappointment, but it didn?t exactly surpass my expectations. Seeing as I had no aforementioned knowledge on the plot of the film, I started watching it with no idea of what to expect. Well, I got a fantastic (although slow-paced) 140-minute crime thriller with incredible performances from every cast member and superb direction by Martin Scorsese! Leonardo DiCaprio plays William Costigan, who graduates from Massachusetts State Police Academy and is assigned to work undercover as a mole with infamous Irish mobster Frank Costello (Nicholson) to acquire evidence for the police to convict him. Frank Costello's protégée, Colin Sullivan (Damon), is another young cadet who is assigned to be Frank's mole inside the police. New clues lead to some unfortunate discoveries and both sides soon realise that they're being scrutinised by the enemy, so both Sullivan and Costigan are assigned to discover the identity of the other mole. There is plenty of violence and bloodshed towards the end of the film that the action lovers with adore, but the film carries at a slow pace (but wasn't sleep-inducing like 'The Aviator') and ultimately could have benefited from some heavy trimming. The film is mainly dialogue driven and is intelligently written complimented by superb direction by Scorsese, and it's obvious why it paid a successful trip to the Oscars but is it really the best picture of 2006? I was hoping to conclude that it was, but 'United 93' is still by far and away the greatest film of 2006 in my honest opinion. Overall Martin Scorsese's 'The Departed' is a superb triumphant in modern filmmaking: it's intelligent, every performance is incredible and Scorsese's direction is the icing on the cake, but the film could have done with some heavy trimming and the ending was a bit too shocking for my taste.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-03-23","","RT","84783584"
