4.0,"The Departed (2006) - ""As with our problem with Providence, let's not cry over spilled guineas."" - Frank Costello

A south Boston Irish mafia boss and the Massachusetts State Police simultaneously infiltrate each other's organizations.

First of all, it's interesting to see the police portrayed as more homophobic than the mob, but pretty much the same when it comes to racism.  I think it's the uniforms.  After all, the mob doesn't have to walk around in uniforms or fancy suits, both of which tend to stir the imagination.

Second of all, ""The Departed"" is good enough to give Oscar an excuse to mercilessly end Scorsese's five-time losing streak.  Direction was certainly competent.  The pacing was very good; tension built at a delicious pace and I was pretty much still into it going into the 3rd hour.

But best motion picture of the year?

There were some good performances.  Hell, look at the cast!  Nicholson was his usual lovely self.  And it was fun watching the sparks fly between Damon and Wahlberg.  In fact, Wahlberg was awesome pyscho.  DiCaprio's performance grew stronger as he got crazier, but that's his strength - no stretch there.  No glaring holes in the cast, that's for sure, but nothing that blew me away.

Sound, editing, lighting, photography - all good.  Certainly deserved the award for editing, but the rest wasn't even close to the standard set by Ang Lee's team (Brokeback) last year.

Best adapted screenplay?  Was it that slim of a year?  Maybe so.  ""The Departed"" is a story about super smart guys, but the screenplay occasionally asks them to do really dumb things.  Like when Nicholson first meets DiCaprio.  Afterwards, Nicholson asks his Number One (Mr. French), ""Can I trust him?""  You've got to be kidding me!  If he had to ask that question, the answer is obvious!  And hey, I'm not that smart.

All in all, an entertaining film loaded with popular stars and sprinkled with a few groaners.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-03-29","","RT","900690322"
