4.0,"[i]The Departed is a remake of well known (Atleast to the The Departed fans) Hong Kong crime flick Infernal Affairs. [/i]
  
 [i]Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is an emotional young cop trying to move up the ranks in the police department. However, he isn't totally trusted by Captain Queenand (Martin Sheen) or Sergeant Dignam who repeatly questions Billy's passion for being a cop.[/i]
  
 [i]However, they do decide to send Billy on a mission to get into Frank Costello's (Jack Nicholson) gang and report their criminal activities back to them. At the same time, Frank has sent in Collin Sullivan (Matt Damon) to be the mole in the police department so that they can stay one step ahead.[/i]
  
 [i]Both Frank and the police department realize they have a mole on their team and both[/i][i] Billy and Collin are in a race againest time to expose each other before all hell breaks loose.[/i]
 [i]
 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 The Departed is another entry into the crime/thriller genre by well known director Martin Scorsese. The movie does a well enough job of keep you entertained in the short run, but I can't help by think that Martin has grown tired of this genre and doesn't have anything left to offer. It's reflected by his by the numbers choices for a music score along with other such things.
  
  
 Despite the great acting on Leo's part, there's too much by the numbers type of scenes and contrived cussing in the dialogue that tries to give the movie some extra bit of emotion in an otherwise unemotional film with a seemingly tacked on love affair. Granted, there are a surprise or two in the film, but there's just not enough depth here for this film to live up to the hype, or what you expect from a Martin crime movie for that matter.
  
  
 It's an entertaining quick reaction type of flick, but don't expect to on a full stomach when this one is over. I expect that it will be a while before Martin revisits this genre, and I think it would be best if Martin moves onto other projects to be honest.

*edited*

Despite my complains, it has managed to get better on repeated viewings which comes a shock to me. I think that there is more cussing than what is needed, but you kind of forget about it on more viewings. More or less, this is a film I can't help but like despite it's flaws
  
8/10[/i]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-04-06","","RT","900796756"
