4.5,"[i]The Departed[/i], starring Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Charlie Sheen, and Adam Baldwin; is a terrific film which justly deserved it's Oscar for Best Picture. Director Martin Scorsese (who won an Oscar for this as well) does a great job in this one, his best picture since [i]Gangs of New York[/i]. The film is filled with plot twists, a shocker ending, suspense, and the storyline is just fantastic. This film gets a lot of crap for being a remake of [i]Infernal Affairs[/i] (2002, a Chinese movie), but I'm not buying their arguments. I've never seen the original, and probably won't because I'll be so biased to the remake. 
 
[img]http://i105.photobucket.com/albums/m235/bkcotton89/photo_12.jpg[/img]
[i]DiCaprio sheds his pretty boy image as the hot tempered undercover cop Billy Costigan[/i] 
 
[i]The Departed[/i] centers it's story around two ""rats"": Billy Costigan (DiCaprio), a rookie undercover cop; and Colin Sullivan (Damon), a newly appointed dective for the Boston PD's Special Investigations Unit (SIU). Costigan's mission is to go undercover and earn mob boss Frank Costello's (wonderfully played by Nicholson) trust. Sullivan, on the other hand, is Costello's rat in the SIU. Sullivan continually feeds Costello information to keep Costello one step ahead of the law. The film gets very interesting when Costigan and Sullivan realize that each side has a rat, and the two begin a desperate cat and mouse game to discover who the rat is first. 
 
There are many strengths in this film. For starters, the cast. There are so many high profile actors and all do a great job portraying their characters throughout the film. Leonardo DiCaprio, who has become a tough guy with this and his other 2006 film [i]Blood Diamond[/i] (a wonderful film, which will be my next review). DiCaprio really should have had two Oscar nominations for Best Actor, with this alongside [i]Blood Diamond[/i]. DiCaprio is going to get his Oscar, as he's high deserving yet still empty handed. Matt Damon is also terrific as Colin Sullivan. Sullivan is the guy that you love to hate in this film, and Damon does a fantastic job of making you hate him. He's brash, cocky, and spoiled rotten. Jack Nicholson, who reminded me of The Joker from the 1988 [i]Batman[/i], is a crazy in his performance as Costello. He provides tons of comic relief and does a great job of showing how nuts his character was. The supporting cast, led by Mark Wahlberg (nominated for Best Supporting Actor) do their jobs as well. Wahlberg provides tons of comic relief, and he's a jerk that you eventually will cheer for. 
 
[img]http://i105.photobucket.com/albums/m235/bkcotton89/photo_10.jpg[/img]
[i]Prepare to hate Matt Damon, as Costello's mole Colin Sullivan[/i]
 
While, this film is a great film, there are some problems that I had with it. One is the storyline between a female shrink (Vera Farmiga) who gets romatically involved with both Costigan and Sullivan. This felt very forced and cliched a bit, but it didn't really take away from the movie. Also, the ending (which I won't spoil here) was very busy with a lot of revelations happening in about 15 or so minutes. However, these do not hurt the film in a great way. One thing that I appreciated about this film was the ending. You think it's going to end one way, then the film makers throw you a curve ball, and you have no idea what's going on. This could have very easily had your standard ending for this type of film. 
 
[img]http://i105.photobucket.com/albums/m235/bkcotton89/photo_23.jpg[/img]
[i]Jack Nicholson gives us a great performance as mob boss Frank Costello[/i]
 
I highly recommend this film to anybody who loves crime/gangster movies like [i]Goodfellas, Scarface, and The Godfather[/i]. While, this film is not as good as those, it easily stands on it's own as a great crime drama and an instant classic. 
 
[img]http://i105.photobucket.com/albums/m235/bkcotton89/photo_08.jpg[/img]
[i]Mark Wahlberg provides the comic relief as the witty and foul mouthed Dignam[/i]
 
For the parents, this is not a film for the kids. The infamous ""F"" word is used 230+ times, there are many deaths, and tons of blood and gore.
 
Ratings
Acting: 10/10
Direction: 10/10
Storyline: 8/10
Sound/Soundtrack: 9/10
Entertainment Value: 10/10
Overall: 47/50= 94=9.4 FRESH","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-04-12","","RT","900970735"
