5.0,"Filled with cops, crooks, Chinese mafia, stolen microchips, and FBI agents, with double-crosses galore and an epic cast, The Departed seems like a crime saga best suited for Michael Mann. The set-up certainly lends itself well to a director like Mann, but only Scorsese could give it such realism and immediacy. Usually a director who focuses on more ""personal"" subjects, Scorsese takes on a story about two young police recruits who end up on opposite sides of the law. The Departed may be Scorsese's most mainstream movie, but it's arguably his best.

While the set-up is simple enough, the rest of the story is virtually unpredictable. Yet through all the crosses, double crosses, twists, and turns, most surprising is the script has a moral center lacking in movies of this genre. A lot of comparisons are being made to Goodfellas, and because of the moral convictions of certain central characters, this film has more resonance. Of Scorsese's gangster movies, I give The Departed the edge.

All performances are outstanding, including those from normally subpar thespians like DiCaprio and Wahlberg. Nicholson is scarier than anything he's played in recent memory, and Damon pulls off another creepy performance similar to his work in Talented Mr. Ripley.

If I have any complaints, it's that I'm getting a little tired of some ""Scorsese-isms"". A constant pop music soundtrack running over multiple scenes, use of voiceover, stock footage, etc... these are effective devices but feel ornamental in a movie that already has such a strong narrative. Still, little can distract from this masterpiece.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-05-04","","RT","780491767"
