4.0,"[i]Mean Streets[/i], [i]Taxi Driver[/i], [i]Raging Bull[/i], [i]Goodfellas[/i], Martin  		Scorsese's masterpieces; I have seen none of them all the way  		through. My interest in Scorsese's [i]The Departed[/i] came from my experiences  		with the JV-Scorsese films [i]Cape Fear[/i] and [i]Gangs of New York[/i], the impressive  		cast, and the original from which[i] The Departed[/i] was based: the 2002 Hong Kong film  		[i]Infernal Affairs[/i]. All in all, [i]The Departed[/i] is a very good  		crime drama that is held back by some over-the-top acting and some  		slight pacing issues from being one of the best films of the year. 
 		
 		[i]The Departed[/i] tells the stories of two Boston-based moles, Billy Costigan  		(Leonardo DiCaprio) and Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon). Under the tutelage  		of a fatherly police captain Queenan (Martin Sheen), Costigan has  		infiltrated the Costello crime family, headed by Frank Costello. (Jack  		Nicholson) Simultaneously, Colin has been working his way up the food  		chain in the Massachusetts State Police as an informant for Costello.  		Sound complicated? It is, but Scorsese is able to deliver these  		introductions with some clarity and gives us just enough back story to  		flush out the characters without boring the audience with too much  		exposition. After a brief montage of quick cutting scenes that works but  		feels rushed compared to the rest of the film, the movie truly kicks  		into gear when the moles discover their mutual existence, and it's a  		tense ride all the way to the finale with plenty of bloodshed along the  		way. 
 		
 		While the story does carry an occasional plot-hole or two, I didn't even  		notice until reflecting on the film afterwards. The script provides a  		fun world for the actors to play around, and it shows with Damon, Sheen,  		Alec Baldwin, and Mark Wahlberg all delivering great performances without  		overshadowing the story. Sadly, Jack Nicholson keeps this film from  		achieving greatness; his over-the-top ""Jack-being-Jack"" cartoonish  		performance distracts from the cat and mouse that should be garnering  		our attention. 
 		
 		All-in-all, go see this movie! Despite its few flaws, it is still an  		extremely suspenseful and entertaining film and if anything, this film  		has made me want to dive back into the vault and see all this Scorsese  		genius that I've apparently been missing all these years.

    If you enjoyed this, please come see more at thecardinalreview.com","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-05-12","","RT","900972137"
