5.0,"one of scorsese's best. i had no idea what to expect. my friend, jon, and i went on a whim to see it. i had suggested and he was a bit unsure. then, we saw  it. the movie's like two hours long and i was just on the edge of my seat the entire time. one of the best endings ever, too.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-05-28","","RT","781803220"
4.5,"The Departed/2006/Martin Scorsese

Finally! Finally, Scorsese returns to form with his best movie in years. The Departed is Martin Scorsese's best film since Casino and may even be better. To me it's very disappointing that Scorsese went from working with Robert DeNiro to Leonardo DiCaprio. But DiCaprio didn't bother me in this film and neither did Matt Damon (whom I also despise) and this is by far both of their best films. The plot is somewhat complicated. DiCaprio plays an undercover cop who infiltrates the Irish Mob and Damon plays a cop who works for the mob. Both of them are trying to figure out who the moles are. It's quite a long movie at around two and a half hours and it has some twists and turns along the way, but it's never slow or hard to follow. DiCaprio (who you'll find yourself routing for more than you ever have) and Damon are supported by the always excellent Jack Nicholson, who plays the mob boss, and Mark Wahlberg, who turns in a fine acting job as well. It's a quite violent film and the last five minutes or so really puts you on the edge of your seat. Anyone familiar with Scorsese's work should be able to see his classic directing style at work in this film. Some parts very much reminded me of Goodfellas. While this movie is no Goodfellas or Raging Bull...it's definitely worth seeing....[9 out of 10]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-05-28","","RT","723690853"
