5.0,"Martin Scorsese made a resurgence with the one-two punch of Gangs of New York and The Aviator, but The Departed presents a man perfectly in tune with his love of film and becomes another masterpiece in his already legendary canon.  A remake of the Hong Kong action thriller Infernal Affairs, the script takes the strengths of the original story and improves it to the point that it is practically flawless.  It works on both the surface and in depth.  On the surface is an intricate dual cat-and-mouse chase that is captivating from start to finish, while lurking underneath are themes of identity, masculinity, honor, corruptibility in those we trust, and the eternal struggle for personal redemption.  Boston is portrayed as a Purgatory, while each character is a soul trying to find a way up.  The acting is superb, with Jack Nicholson reiterating why he is a legend and Matt Damon inverting his boyish charms to make us like a despicable person.  Leonardo DiCaprio, however, has one of the best performances in a long time as he portrays his character as a man slowly decaying into hopelessness, struggling to find something that will save him from the man he has to be.  The editing is brisk and brash, keeping the film moving and making the 2-and-1/2 hours go by with verve.  The hallmarks of a great Scorsese film are there: perfectly crafted soundtrack, abrasive characters formed through their language and violent tendencies, clever cinematography, and a keen sense to detail.  However, what makes The Departed equal to his other masterpieces is his clear focus and vision.  The best undercover cop film ever made, The Departed stands above its peers and represents what great films are all about.  The best film of the decade without question.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-05-30","","RT","781957401"
