4.5,"Although it doesn't have the darkness, philosophy, nor soul of its original counterpart INFERNAL AFFAIRS (2002), The Departed does expand on its source in alternative, creative ways. The script is sharp in its dialogue, surface story, and it develops the characters a bit deeper in their thoughts and past history. Futhermore, Scorsese's skills are spot on as ever, as he gives the film a unique look and feel that captures the dark humor of the characters' situations, the feel of Boston, and the stellar performances of the cast. The Departed may not be exactly in the same league as RAGING BULL and TAXI DRIVER, but its definitely Scorsese's greatest film since GOODFELLAS, and possibly the best crime drama of the decade.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-05-30","","RT","782032599"
5.0,"This movie is not for the faint of heart. The simple plot of this movie is that there is a cop and a mobster who each infiltrate the other's world. The cop becomes a mole in the mob and the mobster becomes a mole in the police. They both eventually realize there is a mole and they set about looking for each other as the suspense builds and their lives become intertwined. Some may not have liked the ending but I think it makes perfect sense with the whole movie if thought about a little deeper than the surface.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-05-30","","RT","782036411"
