2.5,"I watched the Martin Scorcese Irish mob flick (and recent Best Picture winner) The Departed last night, and I'm surprised. Not because it wasn't all that great, but because it wasn't really Oscar material. Sure, it's a competently made flick, but it is lacking in a few areas. Let me explain.

Basically, the film is about two Boston cadets who are working for the Massachusetts Police Force: young officer Billy Costigan (played by Leonardo DiCaprio) who has experienced a large amount of tragedy in his life and has practically nothing to live for anymore, and Colin Sullivan (played by Matt Damon) a youngster who forged ties to the Irish Mafia early in life, and has graduated from the Boston police academy into a respected position in the Special Investigations Unit on the police force, working as an inside man for mob boss Frank Costello (played by Jack Nicholson). As the leader of one of the most feared groups in the city, Costello is near untouchable, which leads police officers Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) to set up an undercover operation, using Costigan as an inside man with Costello. As the story progresses, both the police force and Costello suspect moles in their midst, and it is a race against time for the both of them to figure out each other's identities before they are caught or killed by their respective bosses.

Apparently, from what I've read online, this film is a supposed remake of a Japanese crime film from many years back called Internal Affairs, and shares most of the same plot threads as that film. However, I haven't seen that movie, and I can't comment on the similarities between that film and this one. What I can tell you is that this is a...alright movie. In hindsight, there are quite a few things that I realized didn't work very well in this film, chief among them being the plot itself.

It's one thing to be a crime boss, but I don't think the scriptwriter knew what he was doing. For a ""villain"" that's been operating in excess of 20 years (as per the internal logic of this film, seeing as how he recruits Sullivan as a young kid), Jack Nicholson's Costello doesn't seem to be a very effective boss. He runs around shooting guns without much constraint, from killing a couple and laughing about it in the opening scene, to having known associates of his killing a police captain and shooting at the authorities, to the final ""showdown"" where every one of his crew gets shot to death in less than a minute. He also walks around brazenly taunting the people who are looking to bust him (Queenan and Dignam), and then runs low-level collection deals like hustling shopkeepers out of money, but the worst part of all of this, and the dilemma that drives the film's plot, is that he hires a supposedly ""washed out"" cadet who has been (planted, mind you) in jail for three months, and pretty much trusts this kid, no matter what anyone else tells him, and then he wonders why he was found out at the end? For all the complaining he does about the rats in his organization (and believe me, it's a lot), he should have been dead a LONG time ago due to negligence.

The one thing I enjoyed most about this film were the performances of DiCaprio and Damon as they each played a cat-and-mouse game, trying not to be shot and at the same time uncover the identity of the other mole. The most effective scene in the film, in my opinion, is the ""XXX house chase"", as Costigan tails Sullivan through the streets, all without each man getting a glimpse of the other one. I also quite liked Costigan's rant in the psychologist, and boy, is she a whole other subject altogether. Other than the core storyline between these two men, there was a lot of extraneous filler.

I didn't care very much for the character of Madolyn, the psychiatrist who is dating Sullivan, and then just so happens to be seeing Costigan as well. It's too cliched and convenient that she just happens to know two men who are each moles in their respective organizations, and this plot-line felt way too forced to me. Costigan blows up at her in her office, and she still continues to see him, even when he gives her lines such as ""you may as well give me a bottle of scotch and a handgun so I can blow my brains out""? If anything, she should have been running as far away as she could from this guy, considering that in her eyes, he's just some convict who was ordered to go into therapy. It's also incredibly retarded that she sleeps with the both of them (in Costigan's case, it's a ploy to give us a poorly-acted sex scene), and then there's a big mystery as to who the father is. Oooh, suspenseful! Basically, she was useless and unneeded.

Other than her, though, I thought the supporting cast did solid work, considering the material that they were given, which consisted of numerous uses of the word ""fuck"" and a smarmy, holier-than-thou attitude. I have no doubt that Mark Wahlberg is a good actor, but when he plays a guy who curses, belittles and insults practically everyone he sees, without restraint from his boss, it's a little too stereotypical for my liking. The worst performance in the film, comes from Jack Nicholson, who really should know better. He hams it up, just like he hammed it up in the original Batman, making himself sound like a crazed madman who just happens to be the world's smartest Irish mob boss. His dialogue with Costigan about the number of rats he has in his group almost made me laugh out loud, for God's sake.

Although there were suspenseful moments in the film, I thought that the ending was terrible. Costigan gets a hold of information that could bring down Sullivan, and prove his affiliation with Sullivan, and yet he does this whole dog-and-pony show, calling up Sullivan and his academy buddy Brown (played by Anthony Anderson), who show up at the building. Costigan goes in, without any backup, and expects to take Sullivan without any trouble, then is surprised when Brown pulls a gun on him. This leads to the most laughable scene of the whole film, as Costigan is then shot in the head by Barrigan, Sullivan's OTHER partner who came along because he thought Sullivan needed help. Barrigan also shoots Brown in the head, saying he was a mole, and then Sullivan shoots Barrigan in the head just for the hell of it! Did you get all that? They bring in characters who are absent for most of the film, kill off the main character in a bloody mess, then kill off the other no-name partners as well, rendering this whole plotline with Costigan's attempt to take down Sullivan useless. The very end of the film sees Sullivan getting shot in the head too by Dignam, who resigned earlier on and has apparently gone rogue. Or something.

Basically, every main character in the film and most of the supporting cast gets shot to death. There's no vengeance for Costigan (Dignam hated him anyway), there's no reaction from the police as to Sullivan's deception, we never find out if the child Madolyn was carrying belonged to either of the two men, and you don't care for anyone in the cast anymore, since they all (except for Dignam) made stupid mistakes. Queenan stood around and let Irish thugs toss him off a building, Sullivan killed his entire team, Costello didn't listen to anyone and got shot to death (and he was an informant too!), and Costigan didn't give up the evidence to the police and got shot in the head as well.

My opinion is that unless one or more of the main characters survives at the end, or that their actions have a significant enough impact to effect the future when they die, the whole movie means nothing at all, and in the end, The Departed doesn't mean anything. There's no pathos, no guilt by Sullivan over the death of the other mole (instead, he recommends Costigan for a medal), no final thoughts by any of the parties on how badly they screwed up. Instead, it's just numerous people getting shot in the head. It COULD have meant something if the final scene had Sullivan, alone and without his girlfriend, finally forced to think about the actions he made, and how it impacted the lives of those around him. Instead, we get a shot of a rat crawling around in a condo. In view of a church.

There's a reason why I don't watch the Academy Awards anymore: it's because they don't mean anything. The voters of that ceremony lost my respect a long time ago, and they continue to do it every year. Somehow, at every ceremony, there is always a film picked that is critically praised up the ying-yang by the social elite in Hollywood, as well as the online critics of the internet. Last year, it was Crash, a ripoff of ""Do The Right Thing"" which beat out the clear favorite and winner of every other award under the sun, Brokeback Mountain. Crash wasn't all that great, had a heavy-handed message and seemed to crib from many other films before it, but the Academy voters gave it an award based on their clear insecurities on a ""gay cowboy"" film. This year, it's the same thing with The Departed. Why did it win best picture? Simple. It was to finally dole out an award to Scorsese because the Academy fucked up so many times in the past with his previous films. It was pretty much a sympathy award, and I think that anyone could see that, because if you watch this film, there's no way in hell it was better acted or more enjoyable than some of the other nominees in that category (and yess, I've seen a couple of them, so I know).

As I said before, this film has a good thing going for it with the two leads, but everything else is pretty ridiculous and unbelievable. Take away the asinine stereotyped Mark Wahlberg character and the psychiatrist, give Nicholson actual DIALOGUE and MOTIVATION instead of having him die going to score more drugs, and have an actual ENDING instead of multiple men getting the back of their heads blown off, and then you might have something. As it is, this film is a waste of my time.

Bottom Line: I give this film 5 stars, 3 for the interaction between Damon and DiCaprio, and 2 for the fact that Costello knowingly walks into a porn house and whips out a vibrator at Matt Damon for laughs. Other than that, this movie is pretty much a joke. Don't bother going to see it.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-06-03","","RT","781825526"
