5.0,"""When I was your age, people would say you could become cops or criminals. What I'm saying to you now is this. When you're facing a loaded gun, what's the difference?"" These are the words spoken with Shakespearean gusto at the beginning of the film by a sinister mob leader(Jack Nicholson). What the viewer doesn't realize at first is that through the rest of the film, this Bostonian philosophy will be explored to the very bone. Martin Scorsese makes a triumphant return to form here in ""The Departed"", breaking away from period pieces, American origin epics, and intense character studies and crafts a masterpiece of American film the harkens back to his ""Goodfellas"" days. Not only is ""The Departed"" a masterwork of the crime film genre, but it is also an example of how Martin Scorsese is arguably the greatest living director in film, as well as one of the greatest directors of all time. 

Unlike previous films in the crime genre such as Scorsese's ""Goodfellas"" and Francis Ford Coppola's ""The Godfather"" trilogy, ""The Departed"" doesn't focus on the Italian mafia of New York, but rather on the Irish mobsters of Boston. It's a different choice, and a brilliant one at that. Instead of creating a film that could be regarded as yet another ""Goodfellas"", Scorsese brigns soemthing completely fresh and original to the table. This is no doubt helped by the fact that it's a remake of a Hong Kong crime film classic, ""Infernal Affairs"". What's even more remarkable about it is the way in which Scorsese and his writers were able to take a film set in Hong Kong and adapt it into a Boston setting with Irish mobsters. On that note, the writing is something truly to behold. While many often call the Coen Brothers, Quentin Tarantino, and P.T. Anderson the best writers in film today, Scorsese's films make those filmmakers look like amateurs. Granted, he does not write his own films, but the writing in them is nothing short of impeccable. ""The Departed"" is no exception. It's funnier than most comedies, more intense than most thrillers, and more in depth than even the most complex character studies out there. 

The acting is all top notch. Leonardo DiCaprio proves yet again that he's not the same kid he was in Titanic. In fact, he's become of the finest actors in Hollywood today. Matt Damn makes a surprising turn as the most vile of all the characters, a role at which he seems truly at home in (dare I say it's his best performance yet), and screen legend Jack Nicholson is as evil as ever in the role of the mob boss of Boston during the film. The editing, sound tech, and visuals of the film are also flawless.

But the biggest star of the film is the one never seen or heard. ""The Departed"" is truly Martin Scorsese's show and his finest hour. Though some will still prefer ""Goodfellas"" or ""Taxi Driver"" (both masterpieces in their own right), ""The Departed"" is Scorsese's most complete, dramatically satisfying, and compelling story yet. It's no surprise that it was the most successful film of his career and won him his well-deserved and long-overdue Oscar. Every inch of the film belongs to him, and one can see just how much of a master he is at his craft while watching the film.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-06","","RT","782364903"
