5.0,"The Departed is a great movie about good old American morals: truth and justice. You might too say that it is about loyalty and deception, but I prefer truth and justice cause both come full circle in the end. I can't say much more than that without giving away the outcome of the shenanigans happening in this hell of a thriller that focuses on the suffering of both goodguys and badguys walking in eachother's shoes as they attempt to do either do evil deeds or not do evil deeds, or do both, depending on whether they are a goodguy or a badguy. Sound confusing? It is. And it's even more confusing for the characters involved. But, despite what others might say, the movie is not nihilistic. Again, it's tough to go into explaining myself without giving anything away, but let me just say that... nihilism would mean the movie is sort of without morals, which it is not. However, the characters certainly have nihilistic viewpoints throughout the course of the film so I'll admit to that much. Back to business though... the acting is stellar. Leo sheds his pretty boy image with a strong gritty performance and Matt Damon is the perfect choice to play opposite. There is also a
charismatically menacing Jack Nicholson and a fired-up Mark Wahlberg that steals the show, with Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin giving solid supporting performances and Vera Farmiga surprisingly effective as a psychiatrist caught between the trickery. Best of all though, ofcourse, is Scorsese's directing. After seeing the movie again, I have to take back my previous comments about this not being as good as GoodFellas. They're different movies - both great movies - and I'm not sure they should be compared even if they both happen to involve mobsters. Movies aren't what you see on the surface, what they're really about is what's happening underneath, and there's a big difference in subject matter between GoodFellas and The Departed, just as much as there is a difference between The Departed and Raging Bull. This is Scorsese's latest and greatest and it's something new. It was a shock to see it win Best Picture at the Oscars but maybe they got it right. Lord knows this movie's influence has spawned numerous mimics. This is where it started though... well, actually it started in Hong Kong with Infernal Affairs but give The Departed credit for successfully bringing highly entertaining Hong Kong drama to Boston. It's a masterpiece, the kind where you don't know what hit you and the kind that keeps you guessing.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-07","","RT","783807227"
