5.0,"I loved The Departed on my first viewing but its only now that I can write about what makes it truly a great film.  Martin Scorsese for the first time does a modern day gangster film and I asked myself why this one, why now?  Obviously, the script is terrific but Marty always plays with deeper levels than just a cat and mouse game. 
     First, one of the major themes the movie plays on is anxiety.  The whole movie is shot through quick, sharp shots and the pace is always revved up.  Scenes are cut quickly and so to is even the music which usually is played at its most intense moment. A perfect example of this is the title shot in which it comes at you unexpectantly after the prologue and beginning  of act 1, accompanied by intense Drop Kick Murphy score.  Then it is cut off quickly and we are on the next scene. Leonardo creates within Billy Costigan a horrific feeling of anxiety and Marty does this to the audience with his pacing, editing, and isolation shots. 
     But we still have the why now question.  The answer is we live in a time post patriot act were every citizen can be scrutinized over given ""just cause"", the patriot act is even mentioned in the movie once.  The idea that we all can be subject to such scrutiny most have motivated Marty to do this movie.  I mean just look at the two main characters Sullivan and Billy, they are always being scrutinized, one more so than the other.  
    Furthermore, Martins themes of does might make right or is morality what really make a man are present here too.  Costigan is always confronted with the violent world around him and much to his dismay, doesn't really want to take part of it.  This eats at his character and ultimately makes him want a way out.  Then there is the ending, when everybody is exploding with violence at the end, it is no coincidence that Billy does not.  Billy is the moral character in this movie just like DeNiro was in Casino.  
     Finally, there is the references to the deceased (aka the departed) throughout the whole film and each characters' actions are judged by, gone but not forgotten, ancestors.  Even the character Costello, played brilliantly by Jack Nicholson, worries about what Billies father and uncle would do to him if they saw the two of them together.  Now, Marty is very much and will always be a spiritual director; so its should come as no surprise that the references and effects characters past an deceased relatives plays such an important moral conscience role for the characters. 
     Some people think that the Academy was just giving Marty his past dues for brilliant films like Raging Bull but The Departed is right up there with them and worthy of Best Direction and Best Picture. Well deserved Marty!","ef681ceb-fad1-48d3-a0a5-072566bdfc6d",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-08","","RT","783876038"
