4.5,"The best shot in the whole movie is the two of them on the cell phones, in silence.  I read the script of this, and that thing by itself is a work of art.  The translation from paper to performance is fascinating; so much made it through, and the reason for this is the absolutely world-class cast.  Jack Nicholson, first of all- a lot of people called it ""eating the scenery"", I call it trusting the writer and your own instincts and playing the fucking role.  Costello is a larger than life figure, but I can tell you that there are bigger and crazier guys walking around in the real world than the performance Jack Nicholson put together.  Matt Damon plays the completely socially dysfunctional Colin, a character made fascinating by his inability to truly connect with the people around him.  For all the comforts and successes he garners in the film, I wind up feeling like he's every bit as much a victim as Leo's Billy.  Who, by the way, I completely got behind, and never expected to.  I hate him in the vast majority of his films, I wish he had never been in Gangs of New York (which, save him and Cameron Diaz, would probably be my favorite movie of all time), but in this I really bought him.  Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Anthony Anderson, Alec Baldwin, everybody fucking pops.  I'm so happy Scorcese finally went all the way with this one.  He deserved it.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-09","","RT","783800341"
