3.0,"vs. Infernal Affais trilogy. What people don't realize is this: what makes the Infernal Affairs trilogy such a great work is ""layers"" or, more specifically, how the other two films of the trilogy managed to add layers to the first. Think of it in terms of three layers. Infernal Affairs I is the icing. And what it offers is simple and sleekly styled crime thriller. The other two: they delve mostly into the time that preceded the events of the first film, urging us to think differently about characters and their relationship with each other. In that, they succeeded. If you view it in chronological order, this becomes quite apparent. Some scenes between characters just seem to mesh in harmoniously, adding more clarity and impact to already dramatic scenes. At the end, the trilogy is not only a perfectly effective and sleek crime film but also an exceptional demonstration of characters' dynamic and relationships. 

Even though the Departed took part from all three films of the trilogy, what it severely lacks is the latter two films of the trilogy. In comparison, however, it's really just a matter of preference. What the Departed lacks--the dramatic and layered portrayal of characters--might just be what others prefer. The Departed replaced that aspect with cold, brutal-bordering on absurd-realism.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2007-06-10","","RT","784316657"
