5.0,"When I was your age they used to say you could become cops or criminals. What I'm saying to you is this... When you're facing a loaded gun, what's the difference???
- Frank Costello
   When a powerful mafia leader speaks, you better listen.  This particular line, spoken by a commanding mob boss, provides a great opening to any mob film.  Gangster movies would not be the same without the ever-looming loaded gun pointed at someone's head.  Movie goers get their fill of guns, vice, and crime in director Martin Scorsese's ""The Departed.""  
   The movie features a star-studded cast including Matt Damon (Colin Sullivan), Leonardo DiCaprio (Billy Costigan), Jack Nicholson (Frank Costello), Mark Wahlberg (Dignam), and many other notable actors.  Set in South Boston, the movie encompasses a battle between the Massachusetts state police force and a powerful Irish mafia, led by one Frank Costello.  Over the years, Costello has placed his best and brightest of young men into the police force (including Colin Sullivan) to serve as his eyes and ears.  Not to be outdone, the police counteracted with double agents, including aspiring state policeman William Costigan.
   As the plot develops, both the state police and Costello's mafia realize that moles have infiltrated their respective organizations.  Ironically, Sullivan and Costigan both find themselves working against each other in order to find the undercover agents.  Meanwhile, Sullivan does all he can to thwart the state police's investigation, while Costigan does his part to help bring down Costello.  Furthermore, Sullivan becomes romantically involved with police psychiatrist Madolyn Madden, who also develops an attraction to a patient of hers named William Costigan.
   There's a reason this film won ""best picture"" and ""best director"" honors at the academy awards (as well as a ""best director"" Golden Globe and two more Oscars for adaptive screenplay and film editing).  ""The Departed"" has all the elements of a classic mob thriller.  It has the over-confident all-powerful mafia boss in Frank Costello, his similarly corrupt ""son"" (Sullivan), the over-confident policeman (Dignam), and the skilled young policeman on the rise trying to bring down the boss' regime (Costigan).  Personally, this movie pulled me in at the very beginning and never let go.  I found myself wrapped up in the action on the screen.  Anyone that likes mafia movies (The Godfather, Scarface, etc.) would love this particular film.
   However, this movie has its setbacks.  The casual movie goer may think ""The Departed"" to be too long.  Seeing as the movie lasts about two and a half hours, these particular people may very well be correct.  Nevertheless, those that are extremely into the plot will find themselves completely forgetting about the movie's duration.  Another possible problem is the vulgarity of the language used in the film.  Wahlberg's character in particular curses at least once every sentence. 
  In terms of ratings, I would give this movie an A.  It is an exciting movie with plenty of intriguing action.  I'm not one who likes Leonardo DiCaprio, but I have to say that his performance in this movie was excellent, headlining some top-notch performances by the rest of the cast.  If you like the occasional mafia thriller, then I would seriously recommend ""The Departed"" to you.  Be ready for suspense, action, and a classic, abrupt ending.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-11","","RT","784582149"
