4.5,"After watching this movie, one cannot but ask, ""is this a gangster movie or a cop movie?"" Well, guess what, this is both. And that is what makes the Departed unique and hugely thrilling. This adaptation of Infernal Affairs has a storyline and plot that glues the attention. The storyline is so suspensful and unpredictable it seems like every second of the movie frame contains crucial information that will lead to the next, which also contains another important clue for the next frame, and so it goes. Consequently, the movie is watched seated on the edge of  a seat. I know I was.
What is more interesting is that this movie is also of an emotional nature, unlike most of other cop or mob films. Leonardo DiCaprio shows his yet the best performance, remarkably and beleivably playing a cop in a gang, who's combating to discover his true sanity and stay loyal to his beliefs and taks. Of course, how not to mention evil minded mob leader, played remarkably by Jack Nicholson. Other cast members, which consist of A-listers, such as Matt Damon, Wark Wahlberg and Alec Baldwin, were equally impressive, making their characters livelier. Although the movie would have been as adrenaline-rushing (because of the plot) even without these high paid actors, and only having ␀Scorsese behind the camera to direct, but with them it's easier to tell the friends about the movie, because you don't have to struggle to remember the names of the actors. 
In the end, I can only say wait 'till the end of the movie and you will be stunned.  The plot twists so unpredictably that even a movie going freak would not have seen it coming. For a commercial Hollywood movie that ending is a break-through. All Hail to Scorsese for being brave and not Hollywood-izing the end of the original version. SMART MOVE","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-13","","RT","785137540"
