4.5,"""I don't want to be a product of my environment, I want my environment to be a product of me""

When I first saw the Departed in theatre I found it quite confusing. There is quite a lot to follow. It took me the second time to watch it to fully understand all the relationships. So beware if you read this review, it may spoil the movie for you. I don't want to be the one that ruined the movie like that asshole who did it for the 6th Sense!

Enter cop #1 - Matt Damon (Colin Sullivan) who is taken under the wing of Irish mob boss Jack Nicholson (Frank Costello). After years of studying - finally Sullivan graduates as a police state detective. He is the bad cop under cover as a good cop. He really is the bad guy giving secrets out to the bad guy(s). Matt Damon was blah. I was not impressed by his acting in the movie. His character was quite flat. 

""When I was your age, they woul say we could become cops or criminals. Today what I'm saying to you is this: when you're facing a loaded gun...what's the difference?""

Enter cop #2 - Leonardo DiCaprio (Billy Costigan) a man who has had a hard life, and finally graduates as well as a state police trooper. He is given an opportunity to serve his people by being undercover in Frank Costello's group and get the in's on him. He is the good guy, pretending to be the bad guy!

Leonardo did a great job at being the guy we hate to love! He is really developing into a fantastic actor. Some compare him to Brando. Who knows - time will tell, but he is doing a great job at doig some great roles early on in his career. He really propelled the movie forward and was the glue that held it together. 

""Do you want to be a cop, or do you want to look like a cop?""

It reminded me a lot of Face Off, in terms of who is the good guy? Who are we really routing for? We see Costigan doing some pretty brutal things, but he is the good guy?

The only 2 people who know that Costigan is really undercover are 1) Martin Sheen (Oliver Queenan) - who plays a fantastic role who genuinely cares, and 2) Mark Whalberg (Dignam), a bad ass mother fucker who takes no lip! He is hard, and doesn't take anything lightly.

Alec Baldwin (Ellerby) who is a big cheese in the police department in Boston, plays himself. He was perfect for the role, playing a cope who can't think outside the box, but has all the power. Very realistic, but quite disturbing to think that there are men like him that are behind the badge protecting us. 

Jack Nicholson is fantastic. He plays himself though - the eccentric, quite eerie, twisted, sadistic, and demented character he plays in most movies. There is no way anyone else could have played this role. 

There is this fabulous scene with Costello, where he is speaking with Costigan at a dinner and Costello is going off about there is a rat in the infantry, and the way he speaks with his face is amazing, showing the ""gnawing"" affect with his mouth, and eyes. It's fabulous!

The plot get's more complex, when both sides realize there is a rat on either side. To make matters worse we find out that Costello is an informant for the FBI?! This just go from complex to just bizarre! 

Finally, Vera Farmiga (Madolyn), who is Sullivan's girlfriend is also a psyciatrist to criminals and cops, starts treating Costigan as a patient and ends up falling for him. It's a trinagle within a triangle!

Fantastic movie, great plot, great script, amazing cast, and amazing soundtrack. I really appreciate seeing the Irish mobsters instead of the typical Italian. Of course the Jack Nicholson character is slightly based on Whitey Bulger (from South Boston) who terrorized the area and was a notorious gangster. Scorsese really produced a winner! I haven't seen a movie like this since Goodfellas!

Be-prepared that it is brutally violent!","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-14","","RT","783160458"
