5.0,"The best movies have a way of rewarding you whenever you decide to revisit them. Martin Scorsese's Oscar-winning gangster opera is a most engrossing and gripping and fascinating film experience, but it's the the symbolic layers simmering just below the surface that make this worth re-watching over and over again. Scorsese is a born filmmaker, something evident with his first breakout gangland drama 'Mean Streets,' way back in 1973. Over 30 years later, he's lost none of his storytelling verve with the camera and here he delivers another signature mob story. He ditches the true life Italian mafia tales set decades ago that made him the master of the genre ('Goodfellas' and 'Casino') in favor of a modern Boston-set ""Irish mob"" story. It's a remake of the 2002 Hong Kong hit 'Infernal Affairs,' but this is vintage Scorsese from first frame to last. His visual style has rarely been this intoxicating, thanks to the often gorgeous compositions by cinematographer Michael Ballhaus and the razor-sharp editing of longtime Scorsese collaborator Thelma Schoonmaker. William Monahan's dense and witty script perfectly captures the rich local color of ""Southie"" (South Bostson). This film also has the familiar rhythms of a Scorsese mob tale: dark humor, sudden bursts of brutal violence (some of it played for laughs), amusing fake anger bits, paranoia, guilt, and Catholic undercurrents. He sets this to his pitch-perfect soundtrack selection, and Howard Shore's original score strikes the right tone when needed. This epic-length crime drama has the space and time to let the palpable mouse-and-mouse tension develop in a smooth crescendo to a satisfying slam-bang climax that will have you pinned to your seat. The cast is superb, one and all. DiCaprio finally becomes a legitimate leading man, Damon lets us see the gears turning and cruises on a cocky bravado, Nicholson is (mostly) restrained from his clown-like tendencies, Wahlberg's slick wisecracks are priceless, Baldwin and Martin Sheen play their authoritative roles with ease, and Vera Farmiga is a refreshing blend of intelligence and sex appeal. She's definitely going places. And how can I forget the great Ray Winstone as Mr. French, whose every line is delivered with such flinty gusto he stands out even amid the all-star cast. The pace is propulsive and riveting. Ultimately though, what makes this one of Scorsese's best efforts is the sheer joy one gets watching him simply be the master craftsman he is. His genius lifts this from just another cops-and-criminals thriller to the level of a Shakespearean tragedy, and that makes this not only the best film of 2006 but one my favorite movies of all time","0cd3a62a-b019-496d-9a5b-a043ab6b9fc5",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-15","","RT","09395d2e-65d8-4773-b5bc-4b29262c080e"
1.0,"This movei starts out well, but gets written into a hole, and the only way to get out is to kill everybody at the end.  A good effort, but definitely not worthy of the praise it has garnered.","",False,False,False,False,1.0,"2007-06-15","","RT","784232230"
