4.5,"Upon further review, I should give this movie five stars... but I won't -- it needs time to see if it holds up and earns that right.

Clearly, I am more impressed with it on a second run than I was the first time... where I foolishly gave it second fiddle to Scorsese's Italian Mob films.

The problem for me comes in translation -- this is a remake (in the truest sense of the word) of Infernal Affairs... but, unlike Infernal, it goes after different subject matter from a uniquely American purview.

Gone is the indecision regrding the spirituality and quest for identity from a metaphysical sense.  In that place, a quest for identity and purpose within the more commodified sphere of race, culture and identity (of the practical ""Am I good or evil... since, in America we still look for these things, sort)... Thing about it is -- none of these characters are intrinsically good or evil -- they're grey and shabby... as Scorsese tends to make them... only illuminating their situation for the auidence so that we can begin to make sense of the situation they find themselves in (and oh what a mindfuck it really is).

The visual nuiance of IA is replaced with the genius of character that scorsese is famous for (and I'm thrilled that he can actually get a decent accent out of DiCaprio... someone had to).  It's the better of the two films... easily -- though the ending makes conceptualization of a trilogy along the lines of IA seem relatively impossible.  I don't know that this is the movie that should have earned Scorsese his Oscar, but I will finally condescend to the point that it is one of his finest works.

But whether or not it is a classic is up to the determinance of time.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-24","","RT","786274836"
