5.0,"It's worth all the hype given to it.

It's not Scorcese's best, and the Oscar it won I think was half pity win, but it still deserved it.

And it's still an awesome movie. 

Yeah, if you're from New England, the accents will seem a little off. Notably, Jack Nicholson and Vera Farmiga gain and lose their accents whenever they see fit.

But Jack Nicholson's scenery chewing has to be seen to be believed, and he acts like an aging Joker, holding every other actor hostage while he robs the scene from them.

Matt Damon lays on the sleaze, and Leo DiCaprio plays up the torment with the usual Scorcese element of redemption. 

The rest of the cast is great, too, with no real sore spots that I can think of.

This movie's sense of style is as electrifying and (sometimes) experimental as Goodfellas, having the shots linger when necessary, and having the scenes zip along when you need them to move fast. Re-uniting with editor Thelma Schoonmaker is director of photography Michael Ballhaus, who also shot Goodfellas.

So, there you go.

This movie is awesome, and again, deserves the hype given to it. It's worlds better than Infernal Affairs (a remake better than the original? Go figure), and you could do worse as far as ""pity wins"" go.

A lot worse. I'm looking at you, Scent of a Woman.","052e50cc-4ec2-4d82-9ee6-7329619b3979",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-06-24","","RT","785188251"
4.5,"I did my best not to buy into the hype surrounding this movie, even after all the awards and recommendations for friends, of which there were several. But, this movie really does live up to the hype (except for ONE thing - but more on that in a minute). The plot, the action, the music, the suspense, the characters... they were all pretty amazing. Another movie that proves you should be a Leonardo DiCaprio fan, if you aren't already. The only major beef I had with the movie was the ending. It was horrible. Not horrible in the sense that it was poorly done or even horrible in the sense that it wasn't a happy ending so I didn't like it. It was an absurdist ending that the rest of the movie didn't deserve. You see, the characters and plot were so strong that the ending just destroyed any hope of the themes of the movie having any impact. I understand absurdist endings in other movies, but usually the themes of the movie finally fall into place with the ""twist"" ending - not so with this one. You're just left feeling betrayed. Maybe that's what the whole point of the movie was, but the movie left me wanting to punch someone in the face.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-24","","RT","786280554"
