3.5,"I?ll admit that it took me awhile to see this film.  To be honest, I enjoyed some of the other frontrunners so much that I almost didn?t want to see the film that, or so I had heard, topped them.  The Departed?s best picture and best director nods finally pushed me into it, and?I couldn?t have been happier, not just because it was a good film, but also because it wasn?t as good as Children of Men.

	The Departed is a gripping organized crime drama, with emphasis on a star- studded cast, including such names as Leo DiCaprio, Jack Nicholson, Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, and Alec Baldwin.  Nicholson plays Frank Costello, an Irish mob boss from south Boston who worked to place a mole (Colin Sullivan, played by Matt Damon) in the Massachusetts state police.  On the flip side, Queenan (Sheen) and Dignam (Wahlburg) place rookie cop Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) into Costello?s crew.  As if it wasn?t twisted enough, Costigan through the conditions of his fake parole after his fake arrest sees a shrink, who he ends up sleeping with.  Most interestingly, she is Sullivan?s wife.
	
As the film progresses, we go deeper and deeper into the minds of the cops and mobsters, and the confusion everyone has as to what the hell is going on.  Essentially, the issue is that whatever happens in the mob gets reported to the police, but whenever the police see fit to set up a bust, the mob gets tipped off.  Some might argue, what is the point of a film that is essentially telling the story of a stalemate?  What does it show?  Essentially what it shows is that everyone is human, and it furthers the somewhat morbid notion that wherever someone working for good is, there is, at the same time, someone working with malicious intent.  It?s the proverbial ?glass half full/glass half empty? rule at play, yet again

The main ideas of this film are the twists, action, violence, and drama.  In that way it is classic Scorsese, but this film offers something that is difficult to place.  It shows what happens when a cast of revered actors comes together as one, something that has been done before, but that the two sides of crime bring forth in an unmistakable way.
I was particularly enamored by both Sheen and Nicholson?s performances, as both seemed to really care more about their respective jobs than the rest of the cast.  Perhaps Scorsese wanted it that way.  The cinematography fit the film well, while the music is mainly the theme, one that will go down in history for sure.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-06-28","","RT","786439923"
4.0,"One of the best casts I have ever seen on film.  Better than any Ocean's movie.  Alec Baldwin owns this movie.  Loved it from beginning to end.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-06-28","","RT","784155589"
4.0,"Martin Scorsese returns to gangland in The Departed, a slick, soulful retelling of overrated Hong Kong actioner Infernal Affairs. The cast is 24 carat: Leonardo DiCaprio is Billy Costigan, an undercover cop playing crim in a Boston crime gang run by Jeff Costello (Jack Nicholson). Across the thin blue line, Matt Damon is Sergeant Sullivan, a crim playing cop on Costello's payroll. As cat chases mouse (or rat chases rat), Scorsese delivers his best mob movie since Goodfellas. 
From the moment Jack Nicholson's terrifying gang boss pads across screen to the chorus chords of The Rolling Stones' Gimme Shelter (""It's just a shot away""), you can nuzzle back into your seat, safe in the knowledge you're in for a ride. The themes are (self) betrayal, (schizophrenic) identity and (impossible) redemption; the violence is vicious (desert boot beatings); the supporting cast stunning (Wahlberg, Winstone, Baldwin - no, really). As DiCaprio and Damon push themselves to the limit, their characters' fragile sense of self literally disintegrating on-screen, this is a thriller that thrills. 
""A POST-SOPRANOS GANGSTER MOVIE"" 
And yet... And yet, there are gnawing issues: the plot hangs on enormous contrivances; then there's Nicholson. Scorsese tries to reign in Jack, but how can he stop a force of nature? Occasionally a triumph, occasionally a ham, Nicholson has the quirks (Rat impressions! Fly munching!) of a brilliant, ageing actor chiselling another ""Here's Johhnnnny!"" moment on to his tombstone. It (almost) unbalances everything. But then that's the point. This is a post-Sopranos gangster movie (Vera Farmiga's psychiatrist caught between good cop/bad cop) about psychosis, schizophrenia and madness... A masterpiece? Not quite. It's just a shot away.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-06-28","","RT","642386519"
