5.0,"Martin Scorsese's fantastic The Departed hits hard.  It's a brutal, intense deeply satisfying adaptation of the Hong Kong film Infernal Affairs.  While Infernal Affairs was a great concept, and was executed extremely well, The Departed is a complete transformation.  This is a Martin Scorsese movie.  If you have seen Infernal Affairs, going into this film you may become so engrossed in this that you may even forget a lot of the plot twists.  And Twists it does have. 

The Departed is about those who are against the law, and those who are part of it and the thin blurry line that divides them.  Mob Boss Frank Costello played by Jack Nicholson recruits a young boy by the name of Colin Sullivan played by Matt Damon and has him join the ranks of Boston's finest police force so he can be an informant so he can stay one step ahead of the cops at the same time.  What Frank Costello is unaware of though however, is that the heads of the special investigations unit played by Martin Sheen and Mark Wahlberg have done a similar thing,  they have placed an undercover cop and had him infiltrate Costellos organization.  That undercover cop is Billy Costigan and is played in a career best performance by Leonardo Dicaprio.  Soon both sides realize that they each have a rat in their midst and both moles must race to uncover the identity of their counterpart before they are both exposed and killed.

It's a genius and thrilling plot and the way Scorsese has it play out is terrifically entertaining.  The film runs at about 149 minutes but never once did things drag.  The acting is top notch and seeing Jack Nicholson play a mob boss finally feels so right, its a wonder he had never done it before.  He is definately the scariest character in the film and also has great bits of humor.  The most surprising performance in the film though, is Mark Wahlberg, who's Seargant Dignum has a strong presense and fills the film with much needed bits of humor and steals every scene he is in.  Perfect casting.

Matt Damon is also gleefully cunning as the mole inside the police.  When a deal is about to go sour by police crashing the party, he notifies Costello to get out after Dicaprios Costigan alerts the police when things are about to go down.  It's a terrific cat and mouse game and the way everything gets wrapped up at the end harks back to Quentin Tarentino's Reservoir Dogs, which was also about rats in criminal organizations. 

Howard Shores score keeps the tension high and suspensful while the rest of the soundtrack harks back to the usual Scorsese, loud rock n roll music blaring over other bits and pieces of the film a la Goodfellas, Casino etc.

This film should finally give Scorsese the academy award and should definately see nominations in the best picture, best director, best editing, best actor (Dicaprio), Best Supporting actor (Nicholson) and best adapted screenplay. 

By the end of the film, I don't think it could have been called anything else other than The Departed.  The Departed is a bleak, grim, tragedy in which everyone is on their way out. 

10/10","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-04","","RT","783426373"
