5.0,"How I FEEL...How I FEEL...

Unlike DiCaprio's character, I couldn't keep my hands from shaking. I was in the lion's den full of mass murderers and my heart rate was jacked. Two hours and a half seemed to race as fast as the currents making up my bloodstream. 

As much as I wanted Babel to take the Oscar in '06, deep down, I somehow knew The Departed was a film I loved, and was something I would want to watch more than once. It is simply a more entertaining film, and I kept thinking about it long after it was over. It has a great soundtrack, brilliant performances from a memorable ensemble cast, hundreds of swear words, clever dialogue, the men are real men, the honest guy gets the girl, and the story, while ripped off from Hong Kong's Infernal Affairs, is amazing in its execution. While the story itself isn't considered original, it is infused with enough Boston cultural nuances to make it a work of its own. It's also brutal enough to be scary.

It's not an important film at the very least--at least not as important as something as serious as Babel, but there is this particular coolness about it which I found hard to overlook. It has a lot to do with the music and how each character is portrayed, as it pits the too-good-to be-true smugness of Damon's character against the nothing-to-lose, vulnerable integrity of DiCaprio's character. The Departed shines in its own way. Scorsese deserved his Oscar.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-04","","RT","456850028"
