3.5,"I saw Infernal Affairs a couple of years back and can remember very little about it, apart from the slick production values, the end scene in the lift and rooftop and the fact I really enjoyed it.

Less than four years later it has been remade for all the idiots who can't read subtitles or more probably because of the shit scared financiers in Hollywood who won't back anything unless it's guaranteed to turn a profit.
This confuses me because with all the big names involved they could turn a profit if they stood around farting for two and a half hours, I'm not really sure why they had to go and remake a film when all the talent could have been channelled into something more original and ultimately worthwhile.
As you can probably guess I'm very much anti-remake especially the kind that remakes films less than 10 years old and the kind that steal from foreign films.
Also the fact that Martin Scorsese decided to get involved with lazy plot theft really disappointed me, though it's not the first time (Cape Fear) so maybe this isn't so surprising.

But despite all this, I gotta say I did enjoy The Departed, it's not a fantastic film, it definitely didn't deserve the Oscar (but everyone knows that was a honorary one, basically the Oscar committee saying - ""Sorry Marty here's your golden statue, we know we fucked up by ignoring you in the past so we'll undermine our credibility even more by honouring you for your least deserving film."") But the film keeps you gripped and entertained the whole way through, though with the cast and director this is probably inevitable.

The performances from the stellar cast are brilliant as expected, particularly Matt Damon and Leo DiCaprio, even the smaller roles such as Marky Mark and Alec Baldwin's light up the screen, though I must admit as good as he was Jack Nicholson seemed to err dangerously close to a pantomime performance at times.

I also noticed some nice references to other films like the scene where Dr Madolyn blanks Matt Damon's character at the funeral which was reminiscent of The Third Man and also the Leo DiCap prison scene was similar to the one in Cape Fear.

All in all it was an enjoyable well made film, I'd definitely recommend that people watch it as you're guaranteed an entertaining time, but at the same time it wasn't a GREAT film - it didn't do anything new, it didn't add anything to the crime/gangster genre that hadn't been done a million times before I'd probably go as far as say that it didn't really need to be made - It's a fun film to watch, but why not go watch the original?","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-07-08","","RT","783937882"
