5.0,"Y'know, I've noticed that I find myself constantly revisiting certain Martin Scorsese films over and over again, revising or re-rating, a bunch of re-re's going on. So I'm-a revisit this film one last time and stick with it... afterall, third time's the charm, enit? :p
<br>Compared to previous Scorsese films, I think The Departed is his most entertaining. An A-list ensemble of actors in an entangled web of a plot that just continues to intervene and twist the characters' lives. Leo DiCaprio, Nicholson, Wahlberg, Damon, Baldwin, Sheen... that's quite a list of actors in one film, and each dominate their roles. Vera Farminga, the only female role, is a tad underdeveloped but still manages to shine. From the moment you watch the film, you're immediately engulfed by Jack's presence, and as the story gradually unfolds, you're in. It's amusing when figuring out who's who and why, and it's even more amusing watching the characters try figuring it out as well, even the ones who know. Despite the rather rushed feeling of the conclusion, I think the overall entertainment value of the rest of the film could overshadow that possible flaw... or even improve it. I'm not sure if it's intentional or not, but the absurdity provides laughter; one person could scoff, ""That's fucked up,"" or the other smiles and exclaims, ""That's fucked up!"" Someone mentioned that the ending could just be a subtle jab at conventional Hollyweird endings, which made me think again of my whole perspective on not just the ending, but the film as a whole... and to think it won Best Picture. ^_^
<br>But the idea is debateable. The Departed, alone, seems rather formulaic, but for Scorsese, he updates the crime/gangster genre; the usage of cell phones. It may be a small inclusion, but in a rapidly improving technological world, the cops still need to follow standard proceedures when attempting to take down the head honcho. :p","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-09","","RT","71994508"
