4.5,"If Jack Nicholson doesn't win best actor for ""The Departed,"" at the Oscars this year, I'm going to shoot myself in the right foot ... point blank ? in the right foot. I am going to need an army of commas for this one. 

Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) is a greedy, enraged, heinous, humorous, ingenious, disoriented, audacious, demonic, passionate, um, two-faced, racist, obsessive, dexterous, impatient and, most importantly, horrifically amusing psychopath. Nicholson immaculately creates a persona that is so complicated, so inconceivable that it is hard to perceive how anyone could play off such a role. How the hell did he solve the Rubik's Cube? 

Frank Costello is a modern ""mobster"" who says, in a cynical, down-played voice, ""I haven't needed anything since I was a schoolboy stealing kid's lunch money."" He kills en masse, yet has no clear basis for doing so, only speeding along his fate of being arrested or, worse yet, put six feet under. 

His intentions are made manifest with his opening line, ""I don't wanna be a product of my environment; I want my environment to be a product of me."" Is this just a smaller picture of why we get vile degenerates like Hitler? Is there in fact a point or goal that such people strive to get across?

Or is it just a mirage, an illusion, whose de facto sole purpose is to deceive, conquer, and conquer some more? There really is no clear path once an individual catches the power rush syndrome, is there? 

Tough guys Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) and Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) both work for the Boston police. Their assignment: tip over Costello's reign of evil. For a while though, ""The Departed"" is a cold war, a battle of intelligence. Can the Police outsmart the mobsters? Vice versa? Who are the rats? Who's lying? Who is insane? Is anyone sane? Why didn't Scorsese (director) ever win an Oscar?

I refuse to give away too much, although, trust me, with the fifteen pages of notes I pointlessly took (for the most part) I would love to spoil everything. 

But what really makes this 150 minute psychological thriller a piece of work is its red hot dialogue. The film's colloquy is a mix of a neurotoxin and a really clever joke. It has power, but even more, wit. 

Costigan is the only character we can easily spot who has emotion, feeling and faith. A dark movie of this nature has room for only a little light, and DiCaprio gives us just that. He's over the edge, but he's sympathetic and, better yet, believable. 

The director lets this happen, putting his characters above endless action. Scorsese lets his cast shine while showing their true potential, generously placing the spotlight on another field of work besides his own, acting. 

Interestingly enough, this unselfish approach may get him his first Oscar in February (hopefully). I am so utterly exasperated by the typical Hollywood, eye incinerating, action flick. 

Let's go on this carousel for a moment: special effects from beginning to end; the acting - low calorie cheese; someone falls in love; movie ends happily and, of course, everyone around you rhapsodizes about it (cough). 

""Shanghai Noon,"" ""Fantastic four,"" ""Half Past Dead,"" (cough) I've got to get myself checked out. 

Why don't these clich flicks take the hint? You have to develop a character in full bloom before you kill it off. If not, you just have, well, a dead character. 

In a memorable scene at the movie's beginning, Costigan, following his mother's premature death, is at her funeral where the religious marker alongside her tombstone displays the words, ""Faithfully Departed,"" bringing me back to that old expression, quality over quantity.

So then, the message, I guess, is that it is the person who has lived to 100 but who has never made a verb out of his hopes and dreams, whom we should pity, not the the person who came up short in years, but lived that kind and fulfilling life after all. If we are not ""faithfully departed,"" having no belief in anything but the air, what will that eventually make us? I guess just ""The Departed."" Hmmm.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-07-10","","RT","900999162"
