5.0,"[font=Arial][size=3]I don't want to be a product of my environment. I want my environment to be a product of me.  This quote, the opening words of [u]The Departed[/u], defines the main struggle of the movie.  Two people, Colin Sullivan and Billy Costigan, each struggle in his own ways to make the world better suited for themselves by making drastic life decisions.  For example, although Colin appears to be a devoted police officer, he is secretly a member of the mob that he is assigned to take down.  At the same time, Billy daringly agrees to an assignment from his police unit in which he joins the mob in hopes of infiltrating it and learning its secrets.  Both Colin and Billys audacious decisions could easily get them killed, and their recklessness is part of what makes this movie so thrilling to watch.  However, this kind of story could be generic if not executed properly.  Luckily, [u]The Departed[/u] was expertly created and the result is amazing.  [u]The Departed[/u] is an outstanding movie with an exciting plot, amazing acting, and superb directing.[/size][/font]
   
     [font=Arial][size=3]The movie begins in present day Boston, explaining the citys violent history centered around the mob and their conflicts with the local police force.  After showing some violent images of mob behavior that occurred in Bostons history, the movie begins to focus on two boys.  The subsequent scenes showcase two boys with very different backgrounds. Colin Sullivan (acted by Matt Damon) has a respectable family history and a good reputation, but is secretly involved with the mob. Billy Costigan (acted by Leonardo DiCaprio) has a family filled with criminals, which leads to his bad reputation, but Billy is actually a heroic cop who agrees to a dangerous assignment of joining the mob. Eventually, both the Massachusetts state police and the Irish mafia find out that there is a rat in their group and the plot of the movie focuses on the desperate actions which are taken to find the culprit.[/size][/font]
   
     [font=Arial][size=3]The plot in [u]The Departed[/u] is very well-constructed. As David Ansen from [i]Newsweek[/i] says, The symmetries and complications are on the edge of absurdity and You often find yourself laughing and gasping at the same time. I strongly agree with him since the plot is very complicated and keeps viewers intensely involved.  Colin and Billy both have false identities and it is amazing to watch how the characters try not to give away their true identities to the other characters in the movie.  One particularly heart-pounding scene occurs when Billy tries to determine the identity of Colin and stalks him through a busy street.  The sequence is a thrilling game of hide-and-seek.  As the street twists and turns, the viewers adrenaline is pumping over whether the two rivals will finally catch a glimpse of each other.  Another intense scene that displays the movies plot of good and evil each attempting to figure out the others identity occurs when Billy and Colin talk on the phone for the first time.  Both have finally come into contact with each other and the nerves and uncertainty they show while talking to each other really add to the films already thrilling plot. Through unpredictable moments such as this, [u]The Departed[/u] builds up pressure until the climax, which is also highly energetic and nail-biting like the rest of the movie.[/size][/font]
   
     [font=Arial][size=3]As impressive as the plot is, I would say that the acting in the movie is even better. The all-star cast consists of Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Alec Baldwin, and many others. The first half of the movie belongs to Damon, the undercover mobster, who has confidence and charm but also is a master deceiver.  He easily fluctuates between a righteous cop and a corrupted mob member, fooling all the people around him with his radiant smile and his charismatic demeanor.  However, when Matt Damon shows his evil side during the movie he displays some of his best acting, because the coldness and contempt in his face really magnify the terrible actions that he participates in throughout the film.  The second half of the movie belongs to DiCaprio, the undercover cop, whose tormented nature is really shown.  At the beginning of [u]The Departed,[/u] Leonardo DiCaprio acts like a man with normal worries, but as the movie progresses, he begins to act like a caged animal.  Viewers can see the furrows in his brow deepening and the edginess in all of his actions as his life falls more and more into danger.  Even though the movie belongs to Damon and DiCaprio, the rest of the cast performed their roles very well.  They all blend into their roles perfectly, Boston accents and all, and it is hard to view them as actors because they all mold into their characters so completely.  Mark Wahlberg, a secondary character from the movie, was nominated for the Best Performance by an Actor in a Supporting Role Academy Award (http://www.oscars.org/).  This helps show how all the actors from the movie, even the secondary characters, performed at their best.
  [/size][/font] 
     [font=Arial][size=3]Lastly, the director who visualized and guided the direction of the movie did a great job with [u]The Departed[/u].  The director of [u]The Departed[/u] is Martin Scorsese, whose best work is really shown in the movie. Scorsese has directed other Mafia movies such as [u]Goodfellas[/u] and [u]Casino[/u], so he is very experienced in the area.  The movie moves at a frenetic pace, with fast cutting between scenes and constant unanswered questions.  The identities of Colin and Billy hang on a thread because of the various unfortunate situations they get into throughout the movie.  Also, Martin Scorsese constantly keeps the viewers on their toes by pacing the movie in an unpredictable way.  Just as the movie halts to a slow scene with soft music, he yanks the movie back to a running pace with wild, Irish music and the main characters furiously trying to get out of a tight situation.[/size][/font]
   
     [font=Arial][size=3]Overall, [u]The Departed[/u] is a great movie with a remarkable plot, cast, and directing. With all these good elements used to form this film, there is no question whether it deserved the four Academy Awards it received, including best director and best picture.  If the first line in the movie - I dont want to be a product of my environment.  I want my environment to be a product of me.  was said by Martin Scorsese, the director, the quote would fit perfectly.  This is because Scorsese made a revolutionary movie that will influence other movies for ages to come.
 
