5.0,"I don't believe for one second Scorsese didn't watch the original before making this brilliant adaptation but never the less good ol marty delivers one of the truly great remakes. Big Jack does a Batman again and goes whay over the top but is saved by sterling performances from the rest of the cast, wahlberg is brilliant as dignam and even baldwin puts on a good show. Superb script by william monohan and despite this being a remake, scorsese makes it his own by creating a completely different mood over the whole film. While Infernal Affairs is more stylish and visually striking, The Departed is more of a gang thriller and rather than imitate an already superb film, Scorsese innovates a fantastic story into a standalone flick. Comparisons will be drawn of course, will they be fair? I very much doubt it as both incarnations offer completely different elements of the story making any comparisons pointless. An excellent, excellent movie","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-21","","RT","786744400"
3.5,"Good movie.  Although I was disappointed that pretty much everyone gets killed.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-07-21","","RT","783165711"
5.0,"One of the best films of 2006.  A dark dive into the dichotomy of good and evil.  Truly no one in The Departed is a hero and yet none of them are really bad evil. As Costello says, ""You can be a cop or a criminal but when you're staring down the barrel of a gun what's the difference."" It is scorsese's best undisputed, and worthy of its critical acclaim.  Standout performances from every actor involved and Scorsese's use of music is intriguing.  A welcomed departure from his Italian mafiosa dramas.  We see the right side of the law is just as screwed up as organized crime. Hooray for situational ethics.  One of my favorite films of all time and I just saw the damn thing yesterday...twice.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-21","","RT","783698890"
