5.0,"Martin Scorsese moves from the streets of New York to those of South Boston in this adaptation of the Hong Kong film Infernal Affairs. Leonardo DiCaprio plays Billy Costigan, a Boston PD trainee who is called upon to infiltrate a mob group headed by Frank Costello, played by a scenery-chewing Jack Nicholson. What he and his superiors don't know is that Costello already has a mole in the Boston State Police in the form of Colin Sullivan, played by Matt Damon. It's a cat-and-mouse game as the two try to discover who the other is.
The film is vintage Scorsese from start to finish. The rock soundtrack and kinetic editing are there. However, the film has a real plot, something you don't really see much in his previous films; even Scorsese admits this. The film moves by fast for a two-and-a-half hour film. The actors are all great in their respective roles, particularly DiCaprio. Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen, Vera Farmiga, and Ray Winstone also deliver fine performances. Credit must also be given to the screenplay by William Monahan, who allows the film to play out in Shakespearean character. Plus, there are plenty of great lines in the film. Lastly, the film would not be what it is were it not for Martin Scorsese. Instead of just making of straightforward remake, he takes the material and makes it his own. All in all, one of the best and entertaining films of 2006 and of Scorsese's career.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-07-22","","RT","786318500"
