2.5,"Finally, I get to post a review of The Departed. Matt Damon out of Southie again, huh?

I need to stop listening to people when they tell me a film is phenomenal, and especially when they tell me that a particular role is played well. I am always disappointed. The Departed, a remake of a Hong Kong film, Infernal Affairs, is a good movie. But is it phenomenal? Not even close, alas.

The film has all sorts of star power, thanks primarily to the fact that it was directed by Martin Scorsese. Scorsese is known for filmmaking more about characters than plot, and it shows here. DiCaprio's Costigan, and Damon's Sullivan, are excellent opportunities to show depth of character, internal conflict, and all that. Alas, both of them fall incredibly flat, and come off as utterly one-dimensional. Damon is always the bad guy, DiCaprio is always the good guy, and the film plays out in a trite way because of it. Damon is never emotionally torn; he's not even really all that upset when his psychiatrist girlfriend finds out that he's corrupt. He has no inner conflict, no strife, no guilt. On the other hand, DiCaprio never gets sullied, he never feels the tendrils of darkness wrapping themselves around his ankles. He is clean, and the only thing he's afraid of is getting himself killed.

Martin Sheen reminds us that he can act; that's about all that can be said about his role. Nicholson... over the top as always. A little too psycho to be believable as a mafioso who's been around that long. A mixture of ""Here's Johnny!"" and ""Wait'll they get a load of me.""

Really, the most masterful performances are out of (and this is a shock to me) Mark Wahlberg and Alec Baldwin, both of whom have given some truly awful performances. Alec Baldwin is believable as a cop, and gives a performance I haven't seen out of him since Glengarry Glen Ross, and Marky Mark is the only one in the film who shows some depth of character, some internal conflict, multiple facets.

All in all, a watchable film with a more comic than gruesome ending sequence which, however, does benefit from a re-watch in some scenes where we see some subtle foreshadowing. Unfortunately, I doubt I'll ever watch it all the way through a second time; it doesn't deserve it. I am, however, looking forward to Infernal Affairs, which is on my Netflix queue.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-07-24","","RT","788632001"
