5.0,"Martin Scorsese is the king of the crime genre. He gives the audiences a look at what a true crime life is all about, giving us every description of who is involved and who does what. ""Goodfellas"" is his crime masterpiece, and beyond one of the best movies I have ever seen. Here is a movie that is close to being as great as it. Scorsese came out with the smash hit, ""The Departed"", a remake of a Japanese crime movie made not too long ago. This is a very rare story that was written in the most complete awesome way. We start off with our antagonist, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). This is Nicholson's come back for playing a villain. I knew he could pull this off. His performance of the Joker was very good, and here, he was very good. He's a crime lord that has a way of getting things down around the block that tends to be a very smart way. He's the leader of an Irish mob that he runs. He's a man that gets what he wants whenever he can get it, even without getting the cops onto him. In an earlier decade, he takes in the new member, Collin Sullivan (Matt Damon). He's been taken under Frank's wing to guide him through a new style of crime life. In order to get the cops off of him, he has Collin go through police training, the exams, the works in order to be part of the Massachusettes State Police Department. Why in the world would he want to do that? Simple. He has him go through all that training in order to be a rat for him, turning him into a double sided person. Take note, I want to know how it is all of these crime people can be so smart at things like this. How they are capable of it? I do not know. Frank's been a wanted man for quite some time now. The police have been on him ever since he started doing crimes. Not only does he have his way of getting around, but the police have a way as well. Here we are introduced to a man named Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio), a man who is dying to be a police officer, but can't seem to get back in after being expelled from his duty explained earlier in the film. However, he can be a part of the police department by doing some undercover work. He is hired by Staff Sgt. Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) and Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen). These guys know what they are doing. Queenan seems to be a bit on the soft side of things, making sure everything is in tact for their mission to get Costello. Dignam has his way of acting like a complete prick about everything, yet he's still doing his job. Now undercover, Costigan is now working with Costello, being his new man under his wing, being another double sided figure. Costello goes through Hell working for Costello, experiencing what it is they do for money and such. He struggles so hard, he seem to can't take it. Sullivan seems to play it clean and simple at his job, until further secrets unfold, leading up to mind-blowing twists and turns in this fully beautiful script. Scorsese has his way of making his movies just right. He takes a further step into his film-making, giving a more artistic vision of crime movies, going back to the roots of where crime all began, and giving us another view of being a police officer, but not just any police officer, a rat. The performances are excellent. I'm not too big for Wahlberg's acting that much. He received an academy award nomination for his performance, which is good, but may be too much on his part. The scene stealer for me was from Leo. Leo and Scorsese are a perfect duo for film-making. From ""Gangs of New York"" to ""The Aviator"", Scorsese never let Leo down with acting such awesome roles. What else can you say about Jack Nicholson? He's pretty much awesome in almost everything he does. He makes a stunning come back in his portrayel of Costello, he's one of the most clever villains I've ever seen. An all good all-star cast, featuring Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen, Ray Whinestone, and Vera Farmiga, amazing directing by the master of crime himself, flawed but solid editing by Thelma Schoonmaker, and a wonderful adapted script by William Monahan, ""The Departed"" is a fast-paced modern masterpiece that will stun audiences by it's amazing ability to look at good and evil on both sides of the law and of the mob.","ec425abb-27e3-4c00-9dbf-e73f4bc40151",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-08-01","","RT","789345699"
