5.0,"Arguably one of Scorsese's best, not just in years but in his entire filmography. It's packed with his go-to themes of personal betrayal and the price of ambition, this time wrapped up in an ironclad script that greatly improves on the source material (the international smash ""Infernal Affairs""). Damon's unctious, crooked cop is so riddled with insecurity and cowardice that the man in the Bourne films might as well be his stoic, secret twin. DiCaprio twists his babyface into a permanent mask of white hot loathing and animalistic will. They are perfect counterpoints to each other: Damon's handsome, teflon fabrication of self, the shell of a hero cop, against DiCaprio's high strung, lethal improviser and unlikely weapon. It's meta play on their hollywood bankable sides and it's a credit to the miracle of the casting that they manage to steal the movie back from Jack Nicholson, who love him or hate him, is both charming and terrifying and damn near at his best. Jack is so Jack sometimes it's easy to dismiss him a master, but the difference with him is that while other actors practice restraint, Nicholson has always been a prodigy at the surgical abandonment of it. In a tense restaurant scene when he points the finger at various members of his crew, he sways carefully between lucid and manic, in a way so genuinely erratic you can almost see the synapses misfiring. It's easy to wait out the time between appearances because the rest of the cast does their share of lifting. Martin Sheen ditches his Bartlett aires for an inscrutable frankness and detachment that fits his command and Wahlberg is psychically channelling all of South Boston in real-time during his scenes as the suicidally aggressive Sgt. Dignum. Vera Farmiga should be a much bigger name right now for doing what few actors accomplish: taking a thankless shrink role and turning her into much more than a mouthpiece. Not much can be said for Scorsese's direction that his Oscar win didn't. Some claim it was consolation, and maybe there was an element of that, but also something more. A reminder, a warning in a cinematic age that is getting higher on flash and lower on class. Marty's one of the last who makes great, simple, abrupt choices, who succeeds because he's impatient to tell every last bit of a great story, he's too busy to try and make us feel things. It happens it happens, he doesn't smother his audience who, regrettably, have been taking him for granted for years.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-08-15","","RT","790239470"
