4.0,"A little bit o' Leo, a little bit o' Damon, a little bit o' Nicholson, a little bit o' Whalberg, a little bit o' Sheen, a little bit o' Baldwin - throw 'em in the Scorsese blender with a sprinkle of intense violence and a dash of twist and turns and you've got a great night at the movies.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-08-18","","RT","786141644"
4.0,"Though it's a hugely entertaining and almost flawlessly realised piece of filmmaking, I'm unable to see The Departed as anything more than what it is: a very competent remake. True, my perspective is woefully skewed by my love of the original, and probably less than a little by the froth laden reputation that proceeded this movie, but there are a few niggles which plagued me throughout.
Obviously, The Departed has a great cast. But where are the great performances? Nicholson look ed tired and uninterested, and Martin Sheen was terribly underused (also in the original there is a great psychological showdown between these two characters which was ommitted here). Baldwin had some good lines but was guilty of some royally hammy overacting. Matt Damon never for a minute matched Andy Lau's sinister intensity and anyone who tells me that Leonardo di Caprio is even half the actor Tony Leung is oughtta shampoo my crotch.
In fact, the only actor who truly emerged smelling of roses was the superb Mark Wahlberg, who had all the charisma, and, more to the point, all the best lines, which was a good thing as his otherwise utterly extraneous character was hacked into the plot in order to cop out on the ambiguous and unusual ending in the original to allow a ""crime don't pay"" ending. Scorcese, you disappoint me.
However, on another note, Scorcese did out do himself in the directiom department. It was slick without being too stylish (in stark contrast to the hyper-stylised and very Hong Kong-esque original), it was subtle where it needed to be and pretty brutal too when required - though compare Nicholson hitting diCaprio with a shoe to Pesci's vice scene in Casino and you can see that Costello is not really supposed to be that intimidating!   
Still, despite my complaints, I have to admit it's the sort of movie that we simply don't see that much of these days. This is because it's very good, and that's a rare thing. THe direction is excellent and the script is truly brilliant. The acting is uniformly strong and in places excellent (let's be honest, Nicholson, even on 75% conviction is stil the maestro). The soundtrack's great too. 
A cracking movie all round.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-08-18","","RT","789552663"
