4.5,"Anytime a Martin Scorsese film debuts in theaters, the American public reacts in one of two fashions. More recently and much to my dismay, the majority of the public sheepishly fall in line with the words of misguided critics, saddling Scorsese?s works as more spectacle than film. While this reaction is understandable given Scorsese?s resume includes classics like Mean Streets, Taxi Driver, Raging Bull, and Casino, it often subverts the essence of Scorcese?s newest work as critics are all to happy to slaughter the film?s quality, exaggerating each and every inadequacy, flaws that go wholly ignored in any other film. For all those salivating at the opportunity to do the very things I just mentioned, I take pleasure in informing you that you will need to choice the second typical reaction to Scorsese films: check your egos at the door and just enjoy a nearly flawless film.
The Departed opens with a title card that reads ?Boston: some years ago.??? From here, Scorsese ushers us into a guttural universe where the line between good and evil is not only blurred, but doesn?t exist. Matt Damon and Leonardo DiCaprio star across from one another as Colin Sullivan and Billy Castigan. Damon?s Sullivan rises quickly from the rank of a state trooper to the slicked suits that head the Special Investigations Unit for the Massachusetts State Police using his smooth Johnny Hooker like charm to snake his way up the hierarchical police ladder. Yet, Damon?s clean cut veneer hides the truth: Sullivan is a mole for Boston Mafioso Frank Costello, played by Jack Nicholson.
On the other side of the coin is Billy Castigan, a genuine cop assigned the task of infiltrating Costello?s operation. Aided by family ties to the mafia lords of South Boston in his mission, Castigan struggles to come to terms with his identity, popping pills to numb the pain of not knowing who he really is.
As the film progresses, both characters struggle to maintain their cover and begin to unravel from the stress of maintaining identities opposite their true selves. DiCaprio and Damon give justice to the incredibly complex roles with a burning intensity that reaches its pinnacle in a scene where both men share a silent phone conversation that begins the final act of the film.
Damon and DiCaprio are only overshadowed by the eternally brilliant Jack Nicholson as Irish mob kingpin Frank Costello. More conniving than Lionel Barrymore?s Mr. Potter, more cunning than Pachino?s Corleone, and more intrinsically base than Ralph Fiennes? Amon Goeth, Nicholoson transcends boundaries, achieving a new plateau of unmercifully sadistic heathenism. No matter what underworld vice he?s doing: cocaine, bribery, robbery, murder, Nicholson abandons his characteristic Jack devices, the eyebrow tricks and all but one funny face, to create a lasting vision of evil. 
Accompanied by the reliably superb Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg, Ray Winstone, Vera Farmiga, and a surprisingly good Anthony Anderson, Nicholson, DiCaprio, and Damon enliven William Monahan?s unsparing adaptation of the Hong Kong thriller Infernal Affairs. Cinematographer Michael Ballhaus captures a vibrantly visceral Boston, the golden dome of the statehouse overlooking the rat?s den. And as for the master himself, Scorsese spares nothing, summoning his entire cinematic repertoire to construct a unremittingly violent tale of two men unable to trust, and unable to escape their actions. 
From the web of corruption that entangles it characters to the latent irony in the Catholic burial rituals, Scorsese?s The Departed provides a resolved vision of a decaying social order. The byproduct is a remarkable film that is undoubtedly one of Scorsese?s best.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-08-22","","RT","790500371"
