2.0,"Mr. Scorsese's greatest strength as a filmmaker (which incidentally, also happens to be the the greatest source of strength of any film I've seen) is his ability to vividly depict the odd realities brought out of odd human beings (all of us) by our odd situations.  When these realities are the focus of his work, it invariably shines.  This film is not without such realities, but it is much too busy jerking us around with its various plot twists and plot turns, to pay them very much attention.  They go neglected and (like in Scorse's earlier ""After Hours"") the work suffers as a result.  The emotional line between the viewer and the characters, is torn, almost severed.  All the blood becomes meaningless, like in ""Kill Bill"" or ""Elephant"".  This is just another piece of evidence leading me to believe that all this ""storytelling"" business is horseshit.  A real filmmaker is, first and foremost, a behavioral analyst.  Then, a philosopher.  Everything else (like ""a great story"") is a byproduct of that.","",False,False,False,False,2.0,"2007-08-26","ari v","RT","d34a6f12-ef84-4a77-b6c8-b9994675541f"
