1.5,"How fucking cool was Tony Leung in ""Infernal Affairs?"" Approximately one Great Wall Of China more so than petulant little DiCaprio is, replacing him in this remake.

You'd think Matt Damon would save the picture. I kept waiting for him to go all Jason Bourne on his enemies--to do something thrilling, calculated and amoral. But he's wasted, and the movie shoots wide of the original's psychological suspense. The Hong Kong flick was about paranoia and doomed souls; this one felt like ""Good Will Hunting"" meets ""Face/Off,"" but even Travolta and Cage played this game better.

The original was almost science-fiction--not a plot suited to Martin Scorcese's talents at capturing the scuzz of realistic New York streets. Boston pub brawlers filling the shoes of Triad-fighting supercops? That dog just doesn't hunt.

""Goodfellas"" was a concept that made sense--guy struggles to deal with life in the Mob. The concept here, on the other hand, just doesn't make any sense.","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2007-08-30","","RT","790749557"
