4.0,"Of Scorsese's more violent films, ""The Departed""
ranks highly.  In fact, it may be his best to date.  The performances are outstanding, the characters likable (if not particularly deep), and the screenplay tense and lean.  Whereas films like ""Casino"" and ""Goodfellas"" were sprawling stories of the rise-and-fall variety, ""The Departed"" is more complicated and morally ambiguous.  Although the film is 2 1/2 hours long, it moves at a good clip, with edits that interweave disparate time codes, further complicating the principal interpersonal relationships.  Some viewers complain that much of the violence in the film is gratuitous; I disagree.  While several of the outbursts are shocking, there is nothing so brutish and difficult as Pesci's outbursts in ""Goodfellas"" or his death in ""Casino.""  Moreover, each violent act here is done with intent and so propels the narrative forward.  The film isn't without flaws, of course.  Scorsese would have done better to leave out the closing shot of the rat and the Boston State Building and one wonders (and wants to know) what fuels Wahlberg's character, but overall it is one of the best films in the ever expanding organized crime genre.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-09-04","","RT","792440824"
