5.0,"Martin Scorsese's new film is easily one of the Top 3 films of the year. It's a tour de force picture that manages to do something that almost no long film this year has managed to do. It manages to keep you on the edge of your seat for 2 1/2 hours. Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) and Colin Sullivan (Matt Daman) have quite a bit in common. They've never met each other, but they both grew up in South Boston under the streets run by Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). Costigan's family were victims of his rule, while Sullivan warmed to Costello as a kid. They both decided to become state troopers, Costigan to get a fresh new start, Sullivan to be an informant for Costello. Sullivan is looked upon as a saint by Det. Queenan (Martin Sheen) but not so much by Sgt. Digham (Mark Wahlberg). Still, he's hired to work under them. With Costigan, they've got a job for him: Go undercover to keep an eye on Costello as a secret job that no one has to know about. First, though, he has to serve jail time but once he's out, he'll be Costello's new guy. A year passes, and they still haven't caught him, and Costigan is getting worried about what will happen if Costello finds out his true identity. This is only a shread of the plot because to give away anymore would give away some of the surprises to expect from the film. Suffice it to say, the acting from all is excellent, the direction will finally earn Scorsese the respect he deserves, which will be in this case the Best Director Oscar he has been deserving of the past 30 years. The film also has one of the best endings I have ever seen, with a triple-twist pay off that is exhilerating to watch. Since this is ahead right now in the Best Picture race, this would be my pick for the one that ends up not only getting nominated, but winning. It's Scorsese's best picture all around since Casino, and that was 11 years ago. He's back, and he proves that he still knows how to tell a great story.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-09-21","","RT","795189934"
