5.0,"[i][img]http://www.currentfilm.com/images7/departed1.jpg[/img]
      The Departed [/i]might not be the best movie Martin Scorsese has ever made, but it is certainly my favorite.  It is the smartest edge-of-your-seat thriller I have seen in several years, maybe ever.  Amidst a star-studded cast, Leonardo DiCaprio shines as Billy Costigan.  Even so, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, and Mark Wahlberg all give Oscar-worthy performances.  
 
 This movie never gets old and never ceases to entertain.  It can be watched again and again, not only because it is just a damn good story, but also for the top-of-the-line editing and cinematography. The way the music is used throughout the film is very unique, starting and stopping very suddenly.  

Adding to that, [i]The Departed [/i]also raises several questions of morality.  Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) is one of my favorite movie characters of all-time.  His on-going search for identity can be seen throughout the entire film by simply looking at his face.  Is there a justification to what he is doing?  Or do his actions put him on par with Frank Costello (Nicholson) and Colin Sullivan (Damon)?
 
I saw [i]The Departed [/i]twice in theaters and have since watched it several times on DVD.  Each time I watch it, I pick up on new intricacies that are nearly impossible to notice with just one viewing.  For example - during several of the death scenes in the film, there are different forms of an ""x"" in the background, which is what certain crime movies used in the 1930s and 1940s to show that someone was in grave danger, or about to get killed (SPOILER: see the deaths of Capt. Queenan and Frank Costello).
 
One of the things that makes Martin Scorsese so great is that he doesn't fall to the temptation of always having a Hollywood ending; the situations don't always end turn out the way we would like.  Scorsese excels in the art of unpredictability.  

It is hard to go wrong with a legenday director and top-notch acting.  With that said, [i]The Departed [/i]might be the best movie of the 00s.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-09-21","","RT","901019067"
