4.5,"Holy crap, that was awesome. The end blew me away. You know a movie's good when it manages to get you jumping out of your seat and wanting to applaud by the final credits, and The Departed did that for me. The movie built the most incredible atmosphere of fear and paranoia so well, and the cast were great. Jack Nicholson was complete OTT mad enegery. Marky Mark actually has the best role in the film, though. Matt Damon is great, even though he's the less likeable of the two central roles. Actually, he's a total douche, and it was great to see Matt play that role.

The movie had some trademark Scorcese moments - domesticity mixed in with unimaginable violence, sharp dialogue, some great camerawork, terrific soundtrack. There's some not-so-subtle symbolism - particularly in one tense scene in which Leo and Matt are dressed exactly alike and the very last shot of the film - but it works, because the story is like one of those old allegorical overarching crime sagas. Plus, in a film about deception and disloyalty, it is the honest and loyal that's left standing, and that's cool.

There are a few issues I had with the film, though. The first is that no matter how much the press likes him, no matter how much all the cool people on my flist like him, I cannot like Leo. He wasn't bad in this movie so much as he looked in pain through the whole thing. His eyes watered all the fucking time. The second problem with the movie is that Scorcese has gained some flubber over the years. His films aren't as lean as they used to be - there's plenty of scenes that could haev been cut or shortened, unnecessary bits that made this less than the best Scorcese fare. Although, less than Scorcese's best is still better than 90% of films out there. Children of Men is still my personal favourite movie of the year, but this was good. Really fucking good.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-09-25","","RT","791336642"
