4.0,"The Departed is a kind of tough, contemporary crime drama. This has turned out to be partly true. In many ways, the film is very much in the tradition of his gangster-noir classics, and a fan clearly can recognize echoes of the themes, style, dialogue and dark humor of ""Mean Streets,"" ""Taxi Driver,"" ""GoodFellas"" and ""Casino."" 

The film is the first major release of the fall worth talking about: a fast-paced, visually slick, psychologically fascinating Boston-set cops-and-crooks saga that traces the interplay between two undercover agents on opposite sides of the law. 

Mole One is Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), who is recruited by gang boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) to go through the police academy and serve as his inside cop. He has risen through the ranks to a top position in an elite special unit fighting organized crime.

Mole Two is Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio), a classmate of Sullivan's who is recruited by the cops to deliberately wash out of the academy, take on criminal tendencies and infiltrate the Costello gang, where he can serve as an informer. 

As both men become ingrained in their double lives and the police unit begins to narrow in on Costello, each mole sets out to deduce the identity of the other -- not knowing they're linked in a romantic relationship with the same woman: a police psychiatrist (Vera Farmiga). 

Scorsese has taken this cat-and-mouse plotline of the original Hong Kong film, but made the movie his own by adding some 48 minutes to its running time, beefing up the Nicholson part considerably and transforming it into much more of an ensemble character study.

Like ""GoodFellas,"" it's both ghoulishly funny and very scary in its casual violence. Like ""Mean Streets,"" it's immensely satisfying in its richness, complexity and tragic irony. Like ""The Aviator,"" it pulls us along with the strength of an industrial magnet.

It's a splendid actor's showcase and everyone in the cast comes off well. Damon is wonderfully hissable as the bland crooked cop; DiCaprio vibrates with cornered-animal volatility as his inside-the-mob counterpart; and Alec Baldwin, Martin Sheen and Mark Wahlberg all have winning moments as the more bureaucratic cops.

Not surprisingly, however, the third-billed Nicholson wipes the floor with his acting competition. Playing a satanic character named after a famous real-life gangster (and modeled after a more recent Boston crook profiled on ""60 Minutes""), the old pro steals every scene he's in and dominates this movie like a colossus.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-09-25","","RT","788454199"
