4.0,"Martin Scorsese's talent at directing gangster/crime films has not been a matter of debate since the 1970s, and [i]The Departed[/i] confirms that his talent remains. One of the most acclaimed films of his career, [i]The Departed[/i] does an incredible job of slowly building the tension and balancing several characters well enough to make them all interesting. Before seeing this movie, I was apprehensive about the potential for the film's star power (DiCaprio, Nicholson, Damon, Wahlberg, Sheen, Baldwin) to outshine the relatability its characters, plot, and realism of the experience. I should have known better. Most of these actors are so masterful at their craft and their director so experienced at this dilemna that I had no problem being drawn into the fun and drama of it all. Wahlberg was the only actor who did not have the ability to overcome his own persona. Along with the silly love triangle between the two main characters and a police psychiatrist -rediculous and entirely unnecessary- this movie is one of Scorsese's best crime epics, yet still does not surpass [i]Goodfellas[/i] and [i]Gangs of New York [/i]for artfulness and originality.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-09-28","","RT","900951615"
