5.0,"This film didn't make my personal Mount Rushmore of Film until I saw it for the second time.  I don't know that I can quite explain that without ruining the movie.  That said, this film is probably far more intellectual than a lot of people realize.  It's a shame that many will skip this movie because of the f-bombs and violence because its intellectualism juxtaposed against all of that is part of the reason it's a great movie.  For me, this is the struggle of a good person who is nearly helpless.  It's almost like Leo's character is someone in a Thomas Hardy novel for most of this movie--that is, determined by his family history, more powerful people, his own inherited personal traits, etc.  If I can be totally corny, it's as though his character is an angel placed in a filthy and dirty world, and all the gritty Scorsese-ness only serves to highlight that.  I really can't write a good review for this movie without completely ruining it, but I will say that the juxtaposition of Leo's character and Matt's character is part of the genius.  Consider scenes you might otherwise ignore.  There's a scene where Matt's character, the scum of the earth, is on a date at a fancy French restaurant, talking about fine cuisine with a gorgeous woman.  The film immediately cuts to Leo's character, who was just in a fight and is getting attended to by a female doctor.  The scene is silent, and Scorsese lets Leo's eyes tell the entire story.  He looks at the female doctor in the face, looks off in the distance (as though he realizes that his life will not allow him to date normally, get married, produce children, etc.), and then looks down as though he is resigned to his fate.  Most people probably go to the bathroom during that scene, but it might be one of the most important ones in the entire movie.  (Of course, it's complicated by events later in the movie, but I'll just let you watch it.)","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-10-03","","RT","797429616"
