5.0,"<div style=""width:550px;""><a href=""http://www.flixster.com/movie/the-departed-photos/-6779304""><img src=""http://content6.flixster.com/photo/67/79/30/6779304_gal.jpg"" border=""0""/></a><div style=""text-align:center;font-size:10px;""><a href=""http://www.flixster.com"">Flixster</a> - Share Movies</div></div>

 ""The Departed"" is a crime/drama story that takes place in South Boston, where the state police department is at war with organized crime. At that time a young guy named Billy Costigan, (Leonardo DiCaprio) who just graduated at the head of his class at the police academy, joins the boston police department and gets assigned by Capt. Queenan, (Martin Sheen) and Sgt. Dignam, (Mark Wahlberg) to an undercover job. That undercover job is for Billy to fit in with the mob syndicate that is overruned by powerful mob boss Costello. (Jack Nicholson). While Billy is going as fast as he can to gain Costello's confidence, Colin Sullivan, (Matt Damon) a hardened young criminal who also graduated at the head of his class at the same police academy, is being an informer for the mob syndicate by spying on the boston police department as he rises to a position in the special investigation unit. Billy and Colin becomes more involved as they both gather plenty of information about the plans and counter-plans of the operations. But soon as the mob syndicate and the police department finds out that they each have a mole working inside of their organization, Billy and Colin are suddenly in danger of being caught and executed by their enemy. Now, Billy and Colin must race to uncover the other man's identity in time enough to save himself.
 This was the best (Martin Scorsese) flick ive seen in a long time. It was highly entertaining; especially for a runtime of 2 hours and 30 minutes. The violent sequences were very strong, brutal and bloody. It also has pervasive strong language throughout.  All i heard from (Leonardo DiCaprio), (Matt Damon), (Jack Nicholson) and (Mark Wahlberg) was the F word. It gets very annoying towards the end, just seeing how fake it seems to be.  Eventhough, they used alot of strong pervasive language, they were all outstanding and perfect for this flick. The sexuality isn't even close to being strong. I have no clue why its rated for some strong sexual content. The sex scene wasn't even that long and im glad it wasn't. Their was no reason to put that sex scene in this. No reason at all. So, what if that inparticular scene was in this. It definetly didn't ruin it. 
 I recommend this to (Martin Scorsese) and crime fans.

