3.5,"A much more even-handed account of gangstas than the usual Scorsese fare, even if the lines between cops and robbers are often blurred. 'The Departed' could have been better but could have been so much worse. Obviously having no desire to see the original, I accept that the premise of an undercover cop vs mole mobster is enticing, and the best scenes involve these two and their cell phones. The love triangle is perhaps the movie's greatest attraction, despite the less-than-striking object of their affection, and one can't help feel sorry for both leads as the film moves on. However, surely Nicholson should have been given a better character? He seems wicked, but what do we see that he has actually done that is so bad? Ok, so he holds a dead hand, but it would have made the story more compelling to see just what makes this guy tick. Jack is of course top notch, particularly with his prenunciation of the word 'enviyonment'. The Boston setting is a nice change from Marty's usual NYNY and the film is certainly shot well. DiCaprio gives another good performance that nicely shows that crime isn't necessarily a cool thang. Alec Baldwin and Martin Sheen take the acting honors, and the final half-hour is worth the wait. Thanks Howard Shore for your 3 bars of guitar score, really appreciate that mate.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-10-21","","RT","782657117"
