5.0,"""The Departed,"" is absolutely another masterful film by Scorsese. A triumph in many ways. It's definitely his best film since ""Goodfellas,"" and shows that he definitely direct more than Italian American actors in his films. It's definitely very different in some ways too. Mostly, because this time, it takes place in South Boston, Whitey Bulger territory and home of the Irish. But what  mostly stands out the most in this film, is the all star A-list cast that dominates the screen at every given moment. From Leonardo DiCaprio (3rd film with Scorsese), who absolutely excels beyond words, as the extremely stressed out undercover cop, who works for the Boston State Police and infiltrates the Irish mob, headed by the legendary and perfectly cast Jack Nicholson. To Matt Damon, who in my view is always enjoyable to watch, no matter what movie he's in, as the mole who infiltrates the State Police and reports back to Nicholson's mob kingpin character, Frank Costello. And then there's Mark Wahlberg, who without a doubt steals the show as the tough, no non-sense cop, who runs the undercover section of the Boston State Police, with his senior partner, and excellent, Martin Sheen. The rest of the cast too plays out like a great deck of cards, from Ray Winstone, who is forceful as Nicholson's right hand man, Mr.French. To Vera Farmiga, who gives a very convincing breakthrough performance as the police shrink, who is dating Matt Damon's character, but also gets romantically involved with DiCaprio's character at one point. But that's for all you who STILL haven't seen the film yet. To finally, Alec Baldwin, who is just flat out awesome as the comic relief in this film, Capt. Ellerby, who's in charge of the organized crime section of the Boston State Police. It just feels so good to see a man of his stature finally getting his dues in this film, as I always feel he is SUCH an underrated talent. But most of the credit obviously, goes to Scorsese, for not only getting such a talented group of actors together on the big screen, but for also showing once again, that he is the absolute master on the gangster drama. NOBODY. And I mean nobody comes close when you compare Scorsese's work with other gangster dramas. I always say, there are mob flicks and then there are Scorsese mob flicks. No comparison. With also a kick ass soundtrack that just brings you right back to the Scorsese world of organized crime and the Boston dialogue (kudos to screenwriter William Monahan) that reminds yo so much of his films like ""Goodfellas"" and ""Casino"" you just can't but think that Scorsese ABSOLUTELY deserved his LONG overdue Best Director Oscar for this film. He should of won so many times before, but that's what makes this one so special, because this was the one he finally won for and he TOTALLY deserved it on all accounts! But it's the cat and mouse game between DiCaprio and Matt Damon's characters as they are both on hot ends every day trying to conceal their identity from leaking out, as they both try desperately to reveal the other's identity every step of the way, with Nicholson's mob kingpin character in the middle of it all driving him bonkers! It's a film that is clearly in the hands of the greatest living director in cinema history! Scorsese is a major beacon for cinema and he just keeps on proving it! Every frame. Every second of this feature film is an absolute DO NOT MISS, as the twist and turns in this feature are just mind blowing and will definitely have you jolting in your seat quite a bit. It's a great take on the modern day mob and everything that goes with it, from the use of cellphones and other technology that makes just about impossible for anybody in the crime world to operate today. Scorsese is king! Enough said!","d167c35d-8c80-42b9-8d4d-f29c4e4830d7",False,False,False,True,5.0,"2007-10-21","","RT","796686182"
