3.5,"Much better than Scorsese's woeful ""Gangs of New York"" (2002), but not up to the master's best efforts (Taxi Driver [1976]; Raging Bull [1980]; Goodfellas [1990]).

""The Departed"" is a remake of the highly popular 2002 Hong Kong crime thriller ""Infernal Affairs"" starring Tony Leung and Andy Lau. 

The cast is a good one: Leonardo DiCaprio as the undercover cop; Matt Damon as the mob mole in the police force; Jack Nicholson as crime boss Frank Costello; Mark Wahlberg & Martin Sheen as Dicaprio's contacts; and an awesome Alec Baldwin as SIU head Captain Ellerby. 

Scorsese likes Dicaprio (Gangs of New York [1999]; The Aviator [2004] and now the Departed [2006]), and with good reason; Dicaprio plays the emotionally tortured Billy Costigan, the undercover cop who's nerves are slowly fraying while he waits for the axe to fall and his true identity to be uncovered by the mobsters he's running with... The scene in which he freaks out in the back seat with his police contacts is brilliant, as he struggles to hold it together: ""You want him to chop me up and feed me to the poor? Is that what you want? There's a leak from the inside! It's real! Smoke him out!"" His dependence of Oxycontin, his struggle with the violence around him, his constant apprehension at being found out --- it's all too much for the young man with a number of skeletons in the family closet. Dicaprio holds his own opposite 2-time oscar winner Nicholson, and is brilliant in his role. This boy can act...

Damon does well (although his Boston Irish accent sounds forced), and Nicholson starts superbly, but soon slides into ""manic Jack"" territory and hams it up a bit. Wahlberg has a bad haircut and swears a lot, and Martin Sheen plays a wholesome father-figure foil to the darker figure of Costello (We also find out about Costigan's real father, who, while a mere baggage handler, was not for sale and maintained his integrity). Vera Farmiga is good as the love interest, but two guys, same girl? - the reason for that was lost on me...

It's a story about identity and the danger of trying to be what you are not... the story twists and turns and it's a dizzying and, at times, white-knuckle ride, but its the cast that is on show here, not the plot-line...

The film won four Academy Awards: Best Picture, Best Director (Martin Scorsese), Best Film Editing (Thelma Schoonmaker), and Best Adapted Screenplay (William Monahan). About time Scorsese took a little golden statuette home...","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-10-24","","RT","799923208"
