5.0,"Best in Show: Leonardo Di Caprio
One for the future: Leonardo Di Caprio
Stand-out scene: 
Brainer or no-brainer: Brainer
Stands up to one viewing or repeated?: Repeated
DVD commentary any good?: n/a
 
DVD
 
Martin Scorsese finally broke his Oscar duck as a director when he scooped the award for this movie, a remake of the 2002 Hong Kong movie Infernal Affairs. It had been a long time coming for Scorsese so it's just as well that the movie that finally secured him the long-deserved title was such a masterpiece (it also secured Best Picture along with two other awards). That it is also his most lucrative at the box office is incidental; the cracking storyline is intriguing from start to finish and when the end comes it hardly seems that two and a half hours have gone by.
 
It's difficult to outline the plot without entering Spoiler territory so i'll just say that an Irish-American gang headed up by Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) and the Massachusetts State Police square up against one another. Billy Costigan (Leonardo Di Caprio) wants to shake off his crook family past and make something of himself while Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is a highly intelligent but troubled soul. One is a high flier on the surface but a wreck on the inside, the other a wreck on the surface but a high flier on the inside. Between the two of them there's a decent man there but it's their conflict that forms the heart of this movie.
 
Nicholson is genuinely menacing as Costello and he draws on his own Irish-American roots to imbue the role with the necessary gravitas. Both Di Caprio and Damon are exceptional in their respective roles and its worth noting that the former declined to pursue a Best Supporting Actor nomination on the grounds that this is an ensemble piece and that he deserved no special recognition. Also nominated that year for Blood Diamond (another oustanding performance, he lost out to Forest Whitaker in The Last King of Scotland, a film i've yet to see), the studio did not pressure him to fall in line and Mark Wahlberg got the nod in this category (he lost to Alan Arkin for the brilliant Little Miss Sunshine).
 
This is one of those 'once seen, never forgotten' movies and deserves to be near the top, if not at the top of any list of Scorsese's best movies. Immaculate.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-10-26","","RT","900638434"
