2.5,"Martin Scorsese finally won a long-overdue ""Best Director"" Oscar for this Boston cops-and-gangsters drama, but in my opinion this is actually the one nomination over the course of his career that he really didn't deserve to win for.

The film is an adaptation of a Hong Kong hit (""Infernal Affairs""), but too many precious elements have been lost in translation. It is a curiosity of this film that, despite a two-and-a-half hour runtime, it actually skipped over and dropped much of what made the original such a masterpiece.

Matt Damon's character, for instance, has been turned into a straight-out weasel, a self-interested dirty cop with practically no regrets or moral conflict about his dual life. The fascinating inner conflict of his Hong Kong equivalent between a criminal background and a genuine desire to be a good cop, is nowhere to be found, and the entirety of the film (especially the ending) suffers for it.

Another misstep comes in the character of Madolyn, a psychiatrist who serves as the romantic interest of both DiCaprio and Damon's characters (she is a combination of two seperate characters from the original). Madolyn is simply an unbelievable character, though blame for this is difficult to firmly establish. Is it the acting of Vera Farmiga, far too nervous and easily flustered to be accepted as an experienced counselor of both violent criminals and troubled cops? Or is it simply the story, which asks us to believe that a competent professional would become intimately involved with not one, but two of her patients?

The film is still a fine work of technical cinema, with superb cinematography and art direction. For the most part, the acting is top-grade, apart from Farmiga and (I realize that my opinion here runs contrary to the popular sentiment) Jack Nicholson, who abuses the excess of free rein he is given with his character and ends up feeling far too over-the-top as a feared mobster.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-11-01","","RT","787579199"
