5.0,"[font=Arial][size=2]Perhaps you are familiar with the internet program on Movies.com known as From the Balcony. At the end of every episode of this five minute long program, featuring Statler and Waldorf from the Muppets, a new Balconism is defined. In one of their latest episodes, they introduced the term OS-scarred. OS-scarred is when an actor or director cannot find work after winning an Oscar. Perhaps one of the reasons that Martin Scorsese continues his string a great films is the fact that he has yet to win an Oscar. However, I believe that Scorsese will finally bring home a golden bald man, with the release of his latest film, The Departed.

Every director has their film genre that they specialize in. Steven Spielbergs being science fiction, Mel Brookss being comedy, and Tim Burtons being dark, gothic fairy-tales. The genre to which Scorsese is most famous for is the mean-street mobster movies such as Goodfellas and Casino. After taking a break from this genre for nearly a decade though, Scorsese returns to the mob genre with one of his best films to date. 

The film stars Matt Damon and one of Scorseses most frequently used actors, Leonardo DiCaprio. Damons character is a detective, secretly working for a mob boss. DiCaprio plays an associate of the same mob boss, secretly working undercover for the police. Both of their situations revolve around Frank Costello, a gangster played by Jack Nicholson. The film then results in these two rats trying to track down each other.

The Departed gathers quite possible the greatest acting ensemble of the year. DiCaprio and Damon, as I mentioned above, both earn their roles. Especially worth mentioning is Mark Wahlberg, giving a fast-talking, Vince Vaughn-like performance. And Jack Nicholson gives one of his best resent performances that deserves serious Oscar consideration. 

I suppose the question to be asked regarding the film is whether it is a drama or a comedy. While the film is purely a crime drama at heart, the screenplay is also filled with much hilarious dialog. In this case, I think The Departed can be defined with a Balconism that I like to call, Dramedy.

While Scorsese has been cheated out of many Oscar wins, The Departed should finally be his big win. Although he has been favored to win out of sympathy in the past for films like Gangs of New York and The Aviator, Scorsese seriously deserves an Academy award for this one. The Departed is currently the front-runner for the best picture of the year for the Academy and for myself.[/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-11-03","","RT","901032991"
