5.0,"This is the film which told me - Go to Film School.
I will never have anything bad to say about this film, because of the impact it had on me. Knowing full well it was a remake of a great Chinese film ""Infernal Affairs"" which was released in 2002. I was expecting a lot from this film. I got much more.

First of all, Jack Nicholson was brilliant from head to toe. Art deparment and make-up did an excellent job of making him look like a sleazebag. The dirty teeth, the grown nails, expensive clothes and messed up hair, you put that on Nicholson and give him a scumbag to portray, and Nicholson will oblige and he does oh so well in this film. 

The story is the following. Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) has placed Colin Sullivan in the Boston Police Department at an early age, as he rises up the ranks, his sole purpose becomes to report everything which is being plotted against him. Captain Queenan and Sgt. Dignam portrayed by Martin Sheen and Mark Wahlberg respectively are aware of an informant in their department, they place Billy Costigan (Leonardo Dicaprio) as an informant under Costello's group. From then on, it's a cat and mouse game, between Costigan and Sullivan.

What's so great about the film is, that Costigan and Sullivan barely share any screen time until the very end. Yet none of the overshadow each other, both get their highs and lows, helped of course by the fantastic supporting cast. The underlying themes explored in the film include the father-son relationship, betrayal, greed and doing the right thing. Sometimes doing the right thing isn't the right thing.

The character introductions are very strong. Especially Costello's,they use light sparingly for Jack Nicholson's intro. The first 4 minutes of the film, he is always shown lurking in the shadows, until he delivers his great monologue which then becomes the backbone of the film. A cool nod to the classic era of films, when film- noirs wouldn't show their antagonist fully. Billy Costigan goes through a series of edits, whilst sitting in Captain Queenan's office, we learn more and more about him and his family and why he truly wants to be a cop and Sullivan is introduced through his training and shared scenes with Costello. Really well planned and thought out.

The sound design, choice of music is fantastic. Martin Scorsese has overused the song ""Gimme Shelter"" by The Rolling Stones. But, it's still fits with whichever scene he plays it on. There's also a sense of awkwardness in the film, there are some overly silent moments in the film, which work in it's favor as they build up to something violent. Again, Scorsese uses an odd classical Jazz song while Costello beats up Costigan, brilliant scene, there's something oddly brutal about a man beating up another man with Jazz playing in the background.

Thelma Schoonmaker an idol of mine, an longtime editor for Martin Scorsese returns this time and cuts up the film rightfully. The film ranges from incredibly fast cuts to the classical formula. The fast cuts are accompanied by some great camera moves, which help smoothen the cut, kind of acting like a transition. There's also a little element of repetition too, we'll see the same shot 3-4 times, but a little but closer with every second. Fine job.

When I watch the film, I ask myself why even try man? If I have to go against stuff like this? Then why even try? 
This is one of Scorsese's best work and that's a hard thing to say, it seems everything he does is fantastic. This is different though. Goodfellas was a chronicle on the mafia. Casino was about the true workings of Vegas. The Departed is a personal story about making a name and as I said before, doing the right thing.

A true masterpiece, a film which helped me choose my career path, a very special film. If you haven't seen, you should see it.","d79eac18-58bc-45e5-a0af-96d5a8175c6e",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-12-06","","RT","797794424"
