5.0,"Hollywood's adaptation of Hong Kong's highly-acclaimed Infernal Affairs, it is a gripping cat-and-mouse police thriller, evolving around 2 rookies in the Massachusetts Police department, who are planted as moles on opposing sides of the law.

Helmed by a stellar line-up and masterful direction, Infernal Affairs was highly reverred as the film that revived the then-flagging film industry in Hong Kong. To match, or even come close to this benchmark seems ambitious, audacious even. As it is, adaptations have it tough, put under critical scrutiny, their every flaw magnified and mercilessly censured. ""The Departed"" had big shoes to fill. It did more than that, in fact, Martin Scorsese deftly transforms the remarkable masterpiece into his own without sacrificing the essential plot structure of Infernal Affairs.

The razor-sharp dialogue, high-strung tension and intrigue kept me on the edge of my seat while the enthralling performances by the A-list cast rivetted me firmly so I won't fall over. Jack Nicholson dons his role of the diabolical villain like a second skin, commanding the screen with unquestionable authority, instilling fear with unnerving ease. Leonardo DiCaprio practically sizzles as the emotionally battered undercover who spirals deep into the eye of the storm. Surely, the way he has morphed into a true thespian since his floppy-haired days in Titanic, should be a model for aspiring actors. While Matt Damon's take on his character is slightly less thorough, it is no less potent.

All in all, it is great! Although we already know the plot and twists, it is no less engaging. Good one!

p.s. I won't ever see Cranberry Juice in the same light again.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-12-11","","RT","806059198"
