3.0,"I saw The Departed within days of it opening. Being Irish, I was very keen to see what Martin Scorcese might do with the delicious prospect of an Irish Catholic Bostonian gangster movie lead by Jack Nickolson. 

A remake of Infernal Affairs, the movie mirrors the DiCaprio and Damon characters in much the same way as Face Off does Travolta and Cage, but perhaps with (much) less histrionics and hammy acting. That said, for all of the media chatter about the cast and Scorsese being ""back"" I found that the The Departed goes nowhere near the heights of Martin Scorsese's Casino, Goodfellas or Taxi Driver. 

The script has many issues. The ending for instance is quite poorly explained. 

Some of the acting sadly drifts into De Palma territory with Alex Baldwin and Martin Sheen both coming away from the film with little credit. 

While some of the dialogue is sharp and inspired, much of what we are presented is nonsensical. From reading the reviews, I had been expecting a Nicholson virtuoso performance with all manner of odd sexual ticks in his characterâ¦ all this making him an apparently frightening and a robust criminal. But other than a difficult-to-see-moment in a sex theatre (do they even EXIST anymore in the internet/DVD world?) and some rather unfortunate comments to a diner waitress,  we see nothing but rather a bastardised combination of Jack from A Few Good Men (you can't HANDLE the truth) with the crazed Jack humour of As Good As It Gets. You see, we get Jack the SHOWMAN in this movie... and a Jack whose accent bounces from Boston to LA countless times throughout. 

There is also a love triangle that involves Damon and DiCaprio and frankly this and the Face Off business felt way too HIGH CONCEPT for a Scorsese movie... as did the real rat that traipses across the screen to close the film. There is no subtlety in the film bar one exception. 

Leonardo DiCaprio. 

He is absolutely outstanding. His face contorted with a variety of anxious thoughts, pressures and stresses, DiCaprio Donnie Brasco's his way into the Boston mafia with great success. Gone are details of how difficult it is to become a right hand man to its leader (which he does remarkably quickly for someone known to have gone to Police Academy) but in spite of being let down by both the writing and direction of the film, DiCaprio captures a character that you feel genuine empathy for and for whom the audience shares the weight carried upon his shoulders. 

But unlike Donnie Brasco, the audience are given none of the inside information about the ""culture"" of the mafia bar some hokey House of Pain meets OIRISH punk music.. This from Scorsese whose detail of the mafia in Goodfellas and Casino is outstanding. Very disappointing but clearly he is on uncomfortable ground with Irish Boston. In fact, it reminded me of Atom Egoyan's own Irish mistake Felicia's Journey. 

Mark Wahlberg is good too. In his brief moments, he chews up the sets and blows pretty much everyone off the screen with charisma and presence. 

Matt Damon? We've seen this character before. Many times. And weirdly, Damon's accent makes frequent departures from Boston elsewhere... and he's FROM Boston. 

Overall, it was an entertaining enough film and admittedly it was nice to see Scorsese leave the epics of Gangs of New York and Aviator behind to get his hands dirty in what on paper should be his forte. But Scorsese need not remake Hong Kong movies to remain important... but rather find better writers and concentrate on what he knows best.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2007-12-28","","RT","784665918"
