3.0,"""The Departed"" can best be summarized as Martin Scorcese's Great Compromise, thereby finally earning him an Oscar for best direction.  ""The Departed"" finally allows Scorcese to present a work that represent's Hollywood's expetations of his general persona - the director of mob movies - while at the same time not entering too deeply into the brutally nuanced psychosis of Italian Americans which turned off voters in the past (Raging Bull, Goodfellas).  While the film has many patent Scorcese touches, including some Rolling Stones and hardened instances of violence, ""The Departed"" dwindles down into an overly long action-drama laden with snappy dialogue and ""big scene"" plot twists.   The plotline gives ""The Departed"" what may otherwise be a procedural cop/robber film a shinier, more exciting veneer.   A cut above average and miles away from the heart of Scorcese's larger body of work.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2008-01-02","","RT","799829946"
