All the pieces of interest from the original are destroyed by odd storytelling or odd cinematography.","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2008-01-06","","RT","792804593"
4.5,"Walking into a movie with high expectations is the number one way to destroy your enjoyment of the moment.  If the movie is crap, your mood plummets like life expectancy for mimes.  Coversely, if the movie lives up to and exceeds the lofty heights you had prepared for it, then it soars.

The Departed is one such film.  guerillas in a covert war between police and Not-very-organised crime, Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) is set the task of infiltrating and exploiting mob head, Costello (Nicholson).  At the same time, Costello tasks his own man, Sullivan (Damon) to infiltrate the police in order to keep them out of his hair.

As the two desperately seek each other out, the noose closes in on both of them, a time bomb waiting to destroy.

Carried by absolutely stand-out performances by DiCaprio, Damon, Nicholson... heck, everyone, The Departed explores the trauma of undercover work on both sides as each agent turns themself inside out in plans and counter-plans to trap the other.

Scorsese sets the dark and dirty tone of the movie from the first intriguing montage.  The music and script is ragged and gutteral, the acting fraught with emotion.

At the risk of losing some credibility (that I don't have cos I'm not a pro) can I just say Vera Farmiga is freaking hot.

Prepare to see a deluge of mobster/gangster movies over the next few years, because the popularity of The Departed will draw imitators and wannabees until they've milked every cent they can from genre.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2008-01-06","","RT","805787949"
