5.0,"With the The Departed, Scorsese delivers not only a great film, but a film that is the epitome of the words ""A Martin Scorsese Picture,"" and the Academy could not  ignore this chance to honorhimforamoviethatistheepitomeofthewords"" AMartin Scorsese Picture.The screenplay by William Monahan (Kingdom of Heaven) is unbelievably sharp, seething with true intelligence and ferocious intensity. It also features more F-Bombs than you might hear in twelve other films combined, or even in a twenty minute ride on the bus. Scorsese's direction is masterful, but also retrained: there are some truly amazing bits of staging and dazzling shots that showcase his finely honed skill, but it's never over the top or in your face the way Gangs was. While the film is extremely and brutally violent, it doesn't wallow in an excess of gore, and while there is strong sexual content, it is surprisingly underplayed and non exploitative, steering clear of a lot of unnecessary nudity (a welcome change of pace after the dreadfully trashy Black Dahlia).

And, under the direction of the master, we are treated to the best ensemble cast we've seen in years, a cavalcade of virtuoso performances that could fill up many of the acting nominations by themselves. Nicholson is as good as ever, giving his most entertaining villainous performance since Batman, and Sheen is excellent support, as are the aforementioned Wahlberg and Alec Baldwin, both of whom are surprisingly funny and extremely charismatic.

But the movie belongs to Damon and Dicaprio, and they are nothing short of perfect. No one can play a reluctant bad guy like Damon - he approaches Sullivan in such a way that it's really quite hard to think of him as a villain. You like him too much. Sometimes you want to root for him to win. But the fact that you don't is a testimony to the exhilarating performance by Dicaprio, in a career best performance - not even the most emphatic Titanic haters can write him off after this movie.  Dicaprio is so mesmerizing as a character who is ultra-tough yet vulnerably human, that he narrowly edges out Damon, one of the finest and most consistent actors working today. 

While the film is roughly two and a half hours long, it cruises by at such a fast and intense pace that it feels much shorter. As a thriller, The Departed will have you on the edge of your seat from beginning to end, and as a drama, it will have your heart and mind working overtime. And there is a strong moral center that remains confused, just as the characters are, but deftly plays itself out to a brilliantly satisfying conclusion. 

Scorsese is at his absolute best, never faltering, and delivering, easily, the best film of the year. Innovative and new, yet classically old fashioned at the same time, it just simply doesn't get better than this.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-01-14","","RT","791839566"
