4.0,"Martin Scorsese gives it everything, but no amount of virtuoso technique, dizzying camerawork, convoluted plotting, brilliant editing or rapid-fire dialogue can disguise the fact that the basic conceit of the ""The Departed"" is incredibly contrived, ludicrously so once Damon and DiCaprio become romantically involved with the same woman. Also, while it's entirely appropriate to the Boston setting, I could have done without that hypocritical strain of Irish-American sentimentality for the old-country, which was shitty enough, then, to leave behind and is not alluring enough, now, to ever return to. And the last minute revelation of another mole in the Special Investigations Unit was a groan-inducing twist too far, for me. I still can't decide whether Jack Nicholson is great in the movie or whether he topples over into self-parody--there's evidence to support either view--but the acting is generally outstanding. Easily Scorsese's best film since ""The Age of Innocence"", possibly since ""GoodFellas"".","",False,True,False,False,4.0,"2008-02-01","","RT","795047044"
