1.5,"[font=Calibri]Once the blood stains are bleached from the floors and the bullet casings collected, will these crimes be recorded in history books or just in a Best-Selling paperback? The truth of the matter is ""The Departed"" fills 151 minutes with gritty exit wound violence and scorching double crosses and yet is still one of Scorcese's worst films. Martin is not at his best, but his worst is still better than so many other directors bringing us crime dramas to fill our television screens and our mall theatre marquees. [/font]
 
[font=Calibri]I wanted to overlook the ""dream team"" quality on the cast; I wanted to think that the casting was honestly decided only on the basis of actors who would properly fill their rolls and not just filling box offices. Honestly I tried but Dicaprio, Damon, Nicholson, Wahlberg, Sheen, and Baldwin just made it feel like some sort of Oceans 6 based in Boston. Yes I might be being bias, and I am not saying that a second rate cast might have done a better job, I am just wondering if that was ever an option to the casting director. Not one of the performances was necessarily weak but with the exception of Damon there is proof positive that the rest of the actors have all hade much stronger performances.[/font]
 
[font=Calibri]I wonder why this was not a great Scorsese picture, and more so why so many think it was his best. The ingredients are there a complex crime flick riddled with interestingly interwoven characters and an engrossingly detailed setting but somehow it still comes off sub-par. The writing is what really made me understand what is the weak link in what could have been a strong movie. From beginning to end the pacing of this movie was distractingly uneven, the characters were developed through almost montage- esque opening sequences, then you are speed through years of their lives and through extremely important events in mere minutes this leaving time for the meat of this film to drag and just then Bam again the pace is strengthened and it all over before you know it. Here is the singular problem, no matter how genius Scorcese is you can only cook so good with rotten ingredients.[/font]
 
[font=Calibri]There you have it ""The Departed"" is by no means the one movie that Scorsese should have been awarded an Oscar for, and if you have not seen and of his other pictures go out and rent any of them. If you are looking for an entertaining movie that is superior to most of your alternate crime drama choices check out this one. My final word from me is this, how can you win an Oscar for best Adapted Screenplay (as compared to the much deserving ""Children of Men"") when in fact you fail to mention anywhere on the DVD that your film was adapted from the Chinese film "" [/font]","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2008-02-09","","RT","901075932"
