1.5,"[center][size=3][font=Times New Roman][b][u]The Departed[/u][/b][b]: Good Riddance[/b][/font][/size][/center]
 
[center][size=3][font=Times New Roman]by John D. Madden[/font][/size] [/center]
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman][i]The last temptation,[/i] intones Thomas a Becket in T.S. Eliots [u]Murder in the Cathedral[/u], [i]is the greatest treason: to do the right thing for the wrong reason. [/i]True enough, but even that does not describe the behavior of the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences in showering on Martin Scorseses [u]The Departed[/u] the Oscars that so long evaded this skilled director. They have done the wrong thing for the right reason. The Academy ignored his masterful [u]Gangs of New York[/u] (2002) but identified this irredeemable rehash as the best motion picture of 2006.[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman][u]Gangs[/u] deserved the Oscar nomination andI thinkthe Oscar himself. It looked at a brutal and violent culture and helped you understand not only the origins of the brutality and violence in a socio-economic cesspool not unlike present-day Northern Ireland but also the direction in which it was evolving, as political culture gradually seeped into a heroic, totemic (""the rabbits"") shame culture. You see the primitive Irish emerging from their subterranean lairs into urban life like cavemen into civilization. By the time federal shells start dropping out of the blue onto the climactic duel of the two tribal antagonists, the viewer could see how a national community's need for solidarity when facing civil war superseded the pride and valor of individual honor and tribal turf. And the anthropology lesson was replete with meaningful irony. While even Daniel Day-Lewiss brutal Butcher and his nativist thugs would not attack women and children gathered on the church steps, the federal troops enforcing law and order show no such compunction as they massacre them in the name of civilization.[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman][u]The Departed [/u]moves Irish culture up into the early 21st century and plants it in Boston where it infects everyone it touches with foul-mouthed cynicism and universal perfidy unredeemed by any flashes of decency and motivated by little but the scriptwriters need to keep an implausible action plot moving with yet one set of brains blown against a wall in purple foam.[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]The plot is pointless, the situations contrived, the characters shallow and the action stereotypical, creating meaningless though effective suspense through more gratuitous and implausible violence than you can shake a .45 at. It comes as little surprise, then to learn that it is a remake of the Hong Kong thriller [u]Infernal Affairs[/u] directed by Andrew Lau and Alan Mak, with other plot elements lifted from the Bollywood pot-boiler [u]Don[/u] (1978).[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]In a nutshell, the story focuses on a diabolically wily, remorseless and improbably eloquent Boston Irish mobster, Frank Costello (not to be confused with the real Frank Costello, a New York Italian) played by Jack Nicholson in his over-the-top manic mode, and his nemesis, Capt. Quinan of the Massachusetts State Police (Martin Sheen). Each manages to insert one of his own into the organization of the other (Matt Damon into the Police and Leonardo Di Caprio into the mob), each soon realizes he has a rat in the house and the respective efforts to out him generate the ensuing suspense and what little there is of a plot. Any guesses about the upshot, if youll pardon the term, of such a symmetrical construction?[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]There is actually little to give away in the film, since the identity of the respective moles, or rats, and the mechanisms of their placing, are shared with the viewer at the outset so the only puzzlement is in the minds of the on-screen characters while the audiences suspense consists chiefly of wondering when the obvious and inevitable will happen. Can you imagine John LeCarre letting you know in chapter one just who the real good guys and bad guys were? [/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]Two examples illustrate the shallowness of character and implausibility of plotting.[/font][/size] 
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]1) The young, handsome bad guy (Matt Damon) is brilliant and suave. He could have any career he wanted, so why does he choose the life of a two-bit crook? We see him once as a ten-year old being given some charity and advice by the Nicholson character. We see him next as a State Police detective loyal to the mob but about to be inserted into a crack anti-mob unit. We never see an explanation of why he has for most of his lifetime made himself the stooge of a psychopathic scumbag in a life of deception. Yet as soon as he gets a girlfriend he starts having second thoughts about his double life. Was she his first girlfriend at something like age 30? Had he never questioned himself before? [/font][/size]
 
 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]2) The first few minutes establish that the State Police have turned up absolutely everything about the family history and upbringing of the DiCaprio character, the police academy grad they want to insinuate into the mob. Why do they not seem to know anything about his evil counterpart the Damon character, also a police academy graduate who is put in charge of the whole police internal security apparatus? Don't they know the obvious--that he has spent his whole life in tutelage of the villain who is their chief nemesis? For that matter, why does it never occur to the Damon character, who is leaving no stone unturned at police headquarters trying to discover the police mole in the mob, to take a look at the half-dozen mobsters among whom the mole must be lurking? Had he done so he would quickly have recognized a near-classmate from the police academy.[/font][/size]
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Audience reaction at the matinee I attended said a lot. About halfway through, when Jack Nicholson pulls a particularly outrageous set of his patented facial expressions in the service of evil, the laughter in the theatre was obvious, and it surfaced several times more at moments of egregious implausibility. Clearly, people did not dread the dreadful Nicholson character; they thought he was just silly in much the same way that audiences do not take seriously the gravity-defying acrobatics of kung-fu movies. They are only there for the action. [/size][/font] 
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]I know that some saw [u]Gangs of New York [/u]as full of gratuitous violence but I could see an overarching anthropological model driving social evolution throughout a conceptually unified film. If anyone can bring to this film any coherence outside the hackneyed model of the B-grade action thriller, I'd like to hear of it.[/size][/font] 
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]I wouldn't waste my money unless you have a taste for being insulted and disappointed but--hey!--that's what makes us Irish so great![/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2008-02-11","","RT","901071227"
