4.5,"It took me a little while to like Leo DiCaprio after Titanic.  I'm on record as having rudely characterized his love scene in that film as being like ""girl on girl"" action.  Forgive me, it was a long time ago when I wrote that.  Many of his performances since then have been marvelous.  I loved him in ""Catch Me If You Can"" and especially in ""The Aviator.""

And he did well in this film too playing a very deep undercover cop.  Mark Wahlberg and Martin Sheen do a great job as his righteous handlers.  On the other side of the story, Matt Damon is a bad cop with major access to high level felony investigations who works for his criminal patron played by Jack Nicholson.  The movie is about the little dance as these two work their sides toward a dramatic conclusion.

I sometimes felt like I was listening to a ""stereotypical Bostonian accent tryout.""  Did any of the characters move there from anywhere else?  But there was quite a bit of good stuff in this film.  Two other actors were bright spots for me:  Vera Farmiga as the lovely psychiatrist who sits between Damon's and DiCaprio's characters, and the scarily good Ray Winstone who plays Nicholson's casually homicidal right hand man.  In many ways Farmiga's altuistic, idealistic character was the antithesis to Nicholson's icky bad guy.  The filmmaker added a few lighter moments to keep the viewer from sinking under the pressure of the main storyline:  The scene where the two henchmen on the sidewalk talk about how you can tell whether or not a passerby is a cop was funny and well done.

Oh, and DiCaprio doesn't make out like a girl anymore.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2008-02-29","","RT","799399099"
4.5,"Very good! Suspensful and thrilling. And who wouldn't love a movie with Leo, Mark, and Matt!?Only problem would be the amount of f-bombs, but I can look (or should I say listen) past them.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2008-02-29","","RT","785177641"
