3.0,"DVD Review:

The Departed.

Well, Hollywood does it again.
'Huh?  What's that?  What'd they do?' you ask.
Rip-off an original film idea, that's what.
Of course, this sort of thing has been going on for years.  [i]Ocean's Eleven[/i] and [i]The Italian Job [/i]are an obvious pair.  Then there's the less obvious;[i] I Now Pronounce You Chuck And Larry [/i](a rip-off of the Australian film [i]Strange Bedfellows[/i]) and [i]Vanilla Sky[/i] (a 'remake' of [i]Abre los ojos[/i]).
The next regurgitation from Hollywood that I came across, like those before it, also brings with it a host of 'big name' actors (perhaps I should italicize the word 'actors'?).  It is, as I have stated, titled [i]The Departed[/i].
It boasts such quality names as Jack Nicholson, and Martin Sheen, then throws in some newer blood in the form of Mat Damon and Leonardo DiCaprio.  But, let's not forget the Director, Martin Scorsese!
Heaven's no!  Don't forget him!
Martin Scorsese of such films as [i]Taxi Driver[/i], [i]Goodfellas[/i], and [i]Casino[/i].
Think of them and you'll say, yeah!  He's a great director!  Hell, he even directed a leather-clad Michael Jackson in [i]Bad[/i].
Then, you look a little deeper into his career and you see such over-hyped, pointless gore-fests as [i]Gangs of New York[/i], and then there was [i]The Aviator[/i].  Hell, he even worked with that freak-show, Michael Jackson, for gods sakes!
So, where does that leave us?
Well, like I said, The Departed is a remake (or rip-off if you prefer - I do) of [i]Mou gaan dou[/i] (Infernal Affairs), a Hong Kong film (there's a surprise.  Hollywood poaching from the massively successful Hong Kong action film studios).
Both films (and more relevant to this review) tell the story of a cat-and-mouse battle between the cops and the bad-guys.  An Irish gangster runs the streets, and even manages to rap the knuckels of the Mob.  Naturally, this has the police on edge as they try, but fail, to keep one step ahead of the badguys.  You see, while the cops have a guy on the inside, the badguys have an informant working with the cops!
Brilliant!
If only it hadn't been done before.
But, I'll move on.
DiCaprio plays the Cop playing the badguy, and Damon plays the badguy playing the cop.  With both suddenly becoming in danger of being revieled to their enemy, a tense game of cat-and-mouse ensues as both sides race to find the leak.
To be honest, I would probably find this film in my favor if it was directed by someone with more style.  Scorsese must have lost his marbles a while back, or maybe he figures he can just produce shit and still be considered a 'talent'.
The film is all over the place, with pointless chops and changes, and very strange editing points.  Moments that should be held, to add to the film are cut short, and others just jump in from no-where.  I actually found this film hard to follow, wondering at times if I was watching a flashback or something else.
It's a shame because the story, though a re-telling of a great idea, works well and is very tense.  It's just Scorsese's effort that kills it.
I found Mark Wahlberg to be over the top, in this film, too.  He doesn't play a big part, but his part is very important to the story.  I just think he could have been a little less of a prick early on, and still come across well.  In fact, considering the ending, a more subdued character in the beginning would have packed more punch.
If I had to rate this movie and give it stars, I'd give it three out of five... but it'd be a low three, and that's because of the directing - it really made it hard to watch.
All in all, The Departed tried hard, but fell well short.  Worth watching, but only just.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2008-03-14","","RT","816012139"
