5.0,"[color=Black]There are only a handful of films that win Best Picture who really deserve it.  The winning of Best Picture often depends not on the picture itself, but on the other pictures made that year.  Obviously, if [i]Titanic[/i] had gone up against [i]Lord of the Rings[/i], [i]Lord of the Rings[/i] would have won.  But [i]The Departed[/i] is one of the handful that truely deserves its win.
  
  Despite being two and a half hours,[i] The Departed [/i]is one of the most entertaining and gripping films to come out of Hollywood in the last ten years.  There was hardly a moment where I wasn't on the edge of my seat.  It's a twisted, yet simple plot, which is what makes it so enjoyable.  I feared it was going to be complicated beyond understanding (most good, ""Best Picture"" films are).
  [/color][center][color=Black][img]http://i17.photobucket.com/albums/b89/RetroAnglophile/Random%20Movie%20Pics/departed6.jpg[/img]
  [/color][/center]
  [color=Black]Martin Scorsese has never been a favorite of mine.  I couldn't stand [i]Raging Bull [/i]or [i]Taxi Driver [/i](and don't say it's because I'm a girl, I love me some [i]Fight Club[/i]), but Scorsese has his moments.  I thought he earned both Picture and Director in 2005 with [i]The Aviator[/i], and[i] Gangs of New York[/i] was pretty good.  Scorsese used hit and miss cinematographer Michael Ballhaus for [i]The Departed[/i], and produced some of the most amazing shots I've seen.  Film score genius (a few steps below Elfman and Williams though) Howard Shore produced a worth while score, with some help from Dropkick Murphys I might add, which was a fantastic touch.
  
  As for the cast, I couldn't have seen it any other way.  Matt Damon really proved his worth here, not that he hadn't already, but this sealed the deal.  Leonardo DiCaprio, an all-time favorite of mine, was fantastic, not his best, but still very good.  Jack Nicholson, while good, didn't seem to act.  Maybe that's because I find him naturally creepy, but whatever.  My favorite character (and will undoubtly go onto my re-do of my favorite characters list) was Dignam, played by Mark Wahlberg.  Despite having a small amount of lines, he was one of the key players in the film, and had many of the best moments and lines.  It was also one of Wahlberg's best performances as well.[/color]
   [center][color=Black][img]http://i17.photobucket.com/albums/b89/RetroAnglophile/Random%20Movie%20Pics/departed1.jpg[/img]
  [/color][/center]
  [color=Black]I considered giving this a nine.  The issue for me was the unnecessary sex.  Yes, knowing that Costigan and Madolyn were having a relationship was important, but the scene wasn't vital.  The peep-show, porn cinema was totally unnecessary as well.  Meet somewhere else.  Additionally, the bit where Costello is about to have sex with some young chick was pointless.  But these scenes were less than five minutes, and therefore, not worth enough to bring the film down to a nine.  So, as much as I hate giving partial points...my true rating is a 9.8.  But seriously, go rent this movie.  Now.[/color]
  [color=White]
  If you've been wondering where I am, it's finals week here at the good ol' UO, and I have had my nose stuck in book after book.  Thankfully my last final is tomorrow.  I guess I should get back to studying.[/color]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-03-18","","RT","900645941"
