5.0,"Watch It Again, Its Better Than You Remember.

Two things initially struck me when I came out of The Departed for the first time. 1) Its an American remake of Hong Kong cinema that doesn't suck and, 2) Its Scorcese's best film in 10 years. Despite these both being positive comments on the film, and ones echoed by many, they also do it a massive disservice, as neither give the film alone credit outside any kind of context.

Revisiting The Departed some 4 years on, without its forbearer in mind or the weight of expectation that greets any Scorsese film, I got excited for the film all over again; and this time for the film it is, not for the film I was glad it wasn't.

Let me get this out of the way early. Infernal Affairs is a classic of the genre. Its up there with Heat. And so is this.

What Scorsese brings to The Departed, as he has in past films, is a sense of Catholic guilt. DiCaprio's Billy Costigan is so twisted with the stuff; the loss of his mother to cancer, the mourning of his father - a man he respected for staying honest and his resentment of his extended, criminal family. He wants to be the better man and see's joining the Police force as validation of this. 

However, in one of many stand out scenes with Mark Wahlberg's Dignam, the second he graduates he is singled out for being that which he's spent his life trying not to be. As good as Wahlberg is in the scene, just pushing DiCaprio's buttons like a world class acupuncturist, its Leo's controlled reactions that impress more. Without really moving, his body contorts and tenses as we watch every last drop of hope for a better life drain out of his body. For an actor that relishes a few ""moments"" in each performance (and he has some here) this is where he earns his pay-check. 

On the flip side of this we have Mat Damon. Using every trick in his ""how to make people want you to be their best friend"" book, he disguises a character of such gutless self importance that, in the moments when that character is revealed, its like seeing a different actor.

Look at the scene in the lift at the end. He goes from cocky, self assured prick to snivelling, pathetic pussy in the space of two sentences. And, much like DiCaprio earlier, its the brief transition between  the two that he pulls off brilliantly. You can see the mechanics of his thought process written all over Damon's face without him saying a word. Brilliant. 

With three of their generations top five actors knocking it out the park (Ryan Gosling and Casey Affleck completing that list), its good to see the elderstasemen of Hollywood weighing in with some fine performances too. Well, almost.

Martin Sheen is great in his role as foil to Wahlberg's bull in a china shop. Its as if he's become deaf to his his overtly colourful bully tactics. Some men of his years and stature might wince at his outbursts. Sheen simply waits, emotionlessly, for him to finish and then carries on with what he was saying without missing a beat.

Similarly, Alec Baldwin is a blast as Damon's boss. Looking like he might just have a heart attack at any given second, without any back-story whatsoever I know he cheats on his wife, has a drink problem and is crushed that his son has come out of the closet. Its all in the performance.

In a shocking turn of events, the third of our old hands, Jack Nicholson, turns out to be the wobbly wheel on this shopping trolley. To dwell on this analogy, he's all over the place; spinning and shaking and in danger of pulling the movie of course throughout. He's not helped by a badly miscast Ray Winstone doing to the Boston accent what Dick Van Dyke did to the Cockney. Luckily, with Marty in pushing, we stay on course to the end. And oh my God, what an ending.

Despite knowing what was to follow thanks to Infernal Affairs, the sequence of events that wrap this movie up knocked the wind out of me like that fucking log trap did to matey in Predator. And it still does, each and every time.

And if Nicholson's comeuppance doesn't entirely satisfy - wait for Damon's...

After seeing him disingenuously recommend Cositgan for a medal of honour (to be fair, its the least he could do) having managed to crawl through a tunnel of shit, Andy Dufresne style, and emerge the other side without so much as a hair out of place, he returns to his apartment. As he passes a fellow tenant even the fucking dog can't stand the sight of him. He looks as pathetic as we know he is. 

He opens the door and Scorsese cuts (with deserved Oscar winning dexterity courtesy of the ever present Schoonmaker) to a pair of feet wrapped in plastic. This is only going to end one way, we know it and Sullivan knows it. He doesn't beg this time - he knows Dignam isn't hearing that.

Sullivan ""OK""

Bang.

Great last line.","66797ad3-719a-4aac-96c3-8f0ddf8c2d16",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-06-22","","RT","789580325"
