3.5,"The Departed has been compared to GoodFellas, but they're not in the same league. It's not that The Departed isn't a good film: it's tense, compelling and has at least one amazing performance, possibly more. But where one film is a definitive vision of organised crime, involvingly told and guiltily watchable throughout, the other isn't. It's just not together enough.

The tale of one mole chasing another (although ""rat"" is the word we'll stick to, since it must take up half the script) is fraught with exciting near-misses, as Leonardo DiCaprio's undercover cop is hunted by Matt Damon's crooked detective. The parallels between the two may not be groundbreaking - like Charlie Kaufman said in Adaptation, every cop movie ever made explores this idea - but they give the film a nice emotional backbone. Which is handy, because apart from Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) and Colin Sullivan (Damon), every role is skeletally underwritten. Their shared love interest, an indecisive therapist, ought to be crucial to these parallels but she's just not developed enough. Anyway, the plot gets woolly towards the end, as almost everyone turns out to be a rat and then gets executed. Is anyone *not* a rat?

The film pivots around DiCaprio, and it's a triumphant performance. Watery-eyed, troubled, angry and terrified, he's as scared of all the murder and crime as the audience, and his compliance with it to get his job done is a little like the audience's in watching it. (Reading too much into it now, perhaps; but I also like how DiCaprio and Damon look rather like each other, and DiCaprio has a passing resemblance to Jack Nicholson. It all adds to the Who's-A-Rat? mystery.) His loneliness is compelling, and the chase is satisfying.

But again, the crossed wires get too tangled in the end, and the criminal excesses of Frank Costello - Jack Nicholson, hardly taxing himself - and his gang of Irish thugs aren't terribly original. The wall-to-wall swearing gets silly (particularly with Mark Wahlberg) and the music is too heavy-handed.

It's good in parts and that central performance is a winner; it just spends a little too long chasing its own tail, leaving logical questions (like why no one suspects ex-cop Costigan, or rat-obsessed Sullivan of being moles/rats) unanswered. Not Scorcese's best, but hardly his worst either.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2008-07-01","","RT","78359253"
