4.0,"Scorsese returns to the gangster genre and once again collaborates with DiCaprio following on from 'Gangs of New York' and 'The Aviator'. Matt Damon is the criminal who has infiltrated the Police, Di Caprio is the cop who has infiltrated the mob. Suspicion, paranoia and panic reign supreme as Scorsese throws each tense and tightly packed scene at the audience like cards in a game of poker. Both Damon and Di Caprio race against time to uncover the other to survive, during which time the body count increases. 

There is excellent support from Nicholson, Sheen, Baldwin and Wahlberg amongst others. The script presents the Police as being more at odds and confrontational with each other in many ways than the gangsters. The script is peppered with crude sexual references and macho point-scoring underlining the rather neanderthal and gladiatorial male cultures on display. The only prominent female character - Farmiga, the therapist - is tossed about like a piece of clothing in a washing machine, seemingly powerless to influence the events at hand, although there are suggestions she informs Wahlberg of Damon's actions that would account for the final scene. Overall, this is a tense, well structured and well acted crime/gangster drama. The violence is sometimes over the top and unintentionally funny - almost like a comic-book representation. Nevertheless, a film well worth watching.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-07-29","","RT","802351652"
