3.5,"There is no doubt about Scorseseâs ability to use popular music to great effect (and he has the budget to back it up with the real artists).  I love the âGeez, she fell funnyâ line, followed by the little wheezing laugh from Nicholson.  Or âHowâs your mother?--Sheâs on her way out--We all are.  Act accordinglyâ.  Great script, great lines, and great deliveries.  He gives a great performance, although by no means close to his best.  His character is ridiculously smart.  Itâs not many people that have the strategic foresight to think so far ahead (i.e. gain the trust and devotion of men starting prior to their teenage years).  This is a spectacular story and script.  Scorsese has a great eye for properties.  It makes complete sense for a director like him to remake Infernal Affairs.  The main problem for me with the film is that Infernal Affairs came first and I love it.  Scorsese is a pure filmmaker though.  His camera movements and stagings are so natural and smooth.  Each flows perfectly into the next and each depends on the aim of the scene.  There is no flash; there is only purposeful mise en scene.  I love the cuts between the spraying blood from the pipe-bloodied man to the eating a banana in a sunny kitchen.  This is one of the few roles that allow Wahlberg to rise above the fray and show what he is really capable of as an actor.  Too often he takes shit roles with shit scripts.  It would be a little difficult to screw this one up, but he gives an excellent performance.  The same goes for every performance in the film.  It is a collection of perfectly cast men and women all giving it their best.  Both DiCaprio and Damon are spectacular.  One couldnât ask for better leading men here.  Everyone involved with and responsible for the film, namely Scorsese, the writer, and the actors, delves deeper into the characters than one thought possible.  There are layers of richness here that require time and patience to be uncovered and contemplated.  The high point of the film is definitely not the relationship between Nicholson and Damon.  They do not seem to have much of a rapport, except in the childhood scenes.  DiCaprio and Nicholson work much better together.  Every scene with Farmiga is golden and she is an absolute knockout.  The real problem I have is that the film starts out with an awesome first hour or so and then lags a bit too much of the second act.  This is where I felt Infernal Affairs definitely has The Departed beat.  The final action scene leaves something to be desired for me.  The denouement and final moments are the work of a great artist though.  It's the kind of final scene, music cue, and frame that leaves me wondering why I'm not giving the picture 4 stars, but I'm not there yet.   And all this from a simple genre picture.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2008-09-07","","RT","784904745"
5.0,"The best Jack Nicholson movie in years. his performance in this film i think is one of his best film performance and  worth the price of admission. this is a story about in South Boston, where the state police force is waging war on organized crime, young undercover cop Billy Costigan is assigned to infiltrate the mob syndicate run by gangland chief Costello. While Billy is quickly gaining Costello's confidence, Colin Sullivan, a hardened young criminal who has infiltrated the police department as an informer for the syndicate, is rising to a position of power in the Special Investigation Unit. Each man becomes deeply consumed by his double life, gathering information about the plans and counter-plans of the operations he has penetrated. But when it becomes clear to both the gangsters and the police that there's a mole in their midst, Billy and Colin are suddenly in danger of being caught and exposed to the enemyand each must race to uncover the identity of the other man in time to save himself. also great performances by Matt Damon,Leonardo DiCaprio,
Alec Baldwin,Mark Wahlberg,Anthony Anderson,and Martin Sheen. i think should of won the Oscar for Best Director in 2006. i think The Departed is the best
Martin Scorsese since Goodfellas.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-09-07","","RT","837406644"
