2.5,"This remake of Wu Jian Dao, or Infernal Affairs, fails to capture any of the details which made the first film superlative.  Please not that I am not going to blame this on Scorsese.  His direction was adequate, though not up to its paragon.  Rather, every single actor and writer behind this film dropped the ball big time.  Where the subtle, suave actions of the original were, loud yelling and excessive cursing came in.  Where subtle clues, small details and delicate intimacies were, blatant accentuating, over the top editing and stretched relationships came in.  Everything that made the original a smooth, flawless story was replaced with effects that screamed, in much the way 300 screamed, 'I want to be considered badass by people with too much testosterone!'  Every death lost its realistic, unglamorous realism, and instead suddenly became surrounded by ESPLODIE SOUND and a monologue with 8 f words per sentence.  The cultural gap really fucked this film over, and shoved it into a sort of mediocrity you can find in any mob film.  In no way did Monaham deserve his award for this adaptation.  Scorsese, hats off.  You did deserve the award, I just wish they'd given it to you for something you'd done better.   Like 'Innocence or Taxi Driver.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2008-09-28","","RT","838750997"
