4.0,"Brilliantly done. The film possesses an engrossingly fast pace, which is both a challenge and an accomplishment for one so long. Switchbacks and twists of the almost-predictable and left-field variety abound (most especially a shocking, if unnecessary ending), and all done with aplomb.

The acting is the biggest surprise to me: on the most part it was standard. Damon and DiCaprio were both quite good, and Nicholson performed excellently. The rest of the star-studded cast seemed almost to be going through the motions: which, interestingly enough, added to the overall feel. It seemed as an actual occurrence and not a film in some regards.

The first detraction to my mind, is that The Departed is taking a lot of credit away from the film it was based off of, the Hong Kong-cinema reviving ""Internal Affairs"". Not really Scorsese's fault, but a bit disheartening nonetheless. Perhaps I am engaging in snobbery, but should a film be given higher regard because it is a bigger-budget version of an already impressive accomplishment?

What cannot be detracted from, is how well done the whole film was. Execution was excellent. In general the film was immensely enjoyable, and easily one of the best crime dramas ever made.


Although he still ought to have won for Raging Bull. Winning best direction for The Departed seems as much a gimme as an actual accolade for Scorsese.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-10-26","","RT","788973551"
4.0,"I actually despise this movie initially like other Asian audiences. Mainly because, like others, how much I love the original HK version. However, I change my mind about this remake after repeat viewings. Unlike the original, which is a technically well-made police thriller metaphor of colony culture of HK history, this version is more a police-mafia drama about the reality about America as, no offence, a 'nation of rats' (quoted from Ray Winstone). Some changes in the plot and provide the background story for each character in a 'Goodfellas' fashion that make the movie more believable and realistic, which is something the original lack of and not provided until the excellent but underrated sequel. The decision of omitting the two female characters from the original into one is one of the best parts of the movie, and the performance of Vera Farmiga is definitely better than the female leads in the HK version. 

There are downsides. Martin Scorsese may be the best director of mafia movie, but portraying police is not his specialty. Michael Mann does a better job (well check out 'Heat'). The plot involving Costello selling microprocessor to the Chinese is laughable and the dealing sequence is less as intense as the one in the original. And what's with the ending? 'Crime does pay'? How Mark Wahlberg knows what he knows? And the rat runs across the screen? This is not 'Ratatouille'! 

Beside those downsides, 'The Departed' truly is a good adaptation that stands on it's on. Comparison to the original is really not necessary. Both are great movies.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-10-26","","RT","796052578"
