5.0,"When Infernal Affairs was released in 2002, no one expected it to be a big hit.  I mean really, what movies do you usually hear coming out of China?  Martial arts films?  Jackie Chan?  Yet, surprisingly, the film stood up against the odds and came out as one of Hong Kong's most engrossing and smart films to come out in a long time.  So was it a big surprise when we found that there was going to be a remake?  No.  Were we surprised when we heard the director was none other than Martin Scorsese, as in [i]the[/i] Martin Scorsese who directed Taxi Driver and Goodfellas?  Let's just say that at around that point our interests were peaked.

The film is set in modern day Boston.  The films two main characters are totally oblivious to each other, yet connected in more ways then one might think.  Matthew ""Billy"" Costogan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is a mole for the Massachusetts State Police, embedding himself deeper in the mob, and slowly getting closer with the main main, Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson).  Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is a Special Investigations Unit State Trooper, but actually a mole placed there by Costello.  So guess whats gonna happen when Costello starts to smell a rat in his gang.

The Departed marks the return of Martin Scorsese, no he hadn't stopped making films.  This is Scorsese's best film since Goodfellas, for such a high profile director, sixteen years is a long time to release near hits and misses.  But damn what a return to form!  The Departed is the best film of 2006, and one of the best films in recent years.  Infernal Affairs was an above average, B-movie cop thriller, diverting and entertaining, but The Departed is so much more then that.

The films plot is thickened, but not padded.  The acting is as top-notch as you are gonna get, these actors are all at their peak with this project. Leonardo DiCaprio and Matt Damon both give career defining performances.  Jack Nicholson gives one of the best performances of his career alongside Jack Torrence in The Shining. The characters are all given a new depth that makes them interesting and likable.  Even the side characters have personalities.  The writing of the film is A+ work.  The film may be 150 minutes long but the pace is nice and speedy and the film is never boring.  If there is one film you should not miss in 2006, The Departed is it.



**** out of ****","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-10-29","","RT","901167279"
