4.0,"More twists and turns than a twisty turny thing, let your attention lapse for five minutes while watching The Departed and you'll likely have to start again at the beginning.  Not that there will be much chance of  attention deficit as this is a compelling and gripping thriller from start to finish.
A veritable feast of who works for who and who doesn't work for whom, Martin Scorsese crafts a well oiled machine in a tale of police and mob corruption.  Leonardo DiCaprio and Matt Damon both carry their parts well.  They're both accomplished actors and you'd expect nothing less. Jack Nicholson makes sure he is the largest presence in the movie.  He does his usual mad eyed, cackling kind of thing but he is so damn good at it, it's always a pleasure to watch.  The real stand out performance though is Mark Whalberg's foul mouthed sergeant who gets a fistful of the best lines from what is an absolutely cracking script with some excellent dialogue.
The pace of the film does not let up and comes with genuine shocks and surprises along the way.  The only part that doesn't really gel in the film is DeCaprio's relationship with Damon's fiancÃ©.  It feels sort of tagged on and unnecessary and does let the film drop on occasion.
The Departed is a fine piece of work.  Scorsese does gangster movies like no one else and this is another excellent addition and one of the finest police thrillers in recent years.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2009-02-09","","RT","797064395"
