3.0,"Bad things about this:
The hollow Yank romanticism of the Irish (Freud never said the Irish were impervious to psychoanalysis btw.)
Dicaprio has a sulky face on him throughout. 
Nicholson hams it up. He is diverting but not convincing.

The good things:
Matt Damon's great performance.
Some of the cosmetic editing touches are diverting (the freeze frame shotgun blasts are one example.) Despite the fact that the editor has been around for donkeys years, the cutting is full of energy, occasionally making an interesting departure from conventional styles.
----
Dialogue: 

Goodfellas dialogue almost felt improvisational in the way it spilled from the characters. The dialogue in the Departed is significantly more sharp, but feels contrived in comparison.

Casting:

The big flaw for me is Dicaprio (from here on in to be known as whiney snotface). He spends most of the film with a whingey scowl on his face which is simply not convincing. not to mention irritating. Vera Farminga feels a bit wasted here also as 'the love interest'.  

Matt Damon on the other hand really makes up for these aspects with his slimey and intelligent portrayal of Colin Sullivan, perfectly evoking the characters morally bankrupt charisma (and eventually desperation) as the film goes along. His performance really deserved more praise then it got (although some might consider it understandable seeing that the character is so unlikable). For me it was the best performance of the film.

While you can argue (quite reasonably) that Jack Nicholson in overacting psycho mode falls into self parody almost all of the time, he is still Jack Nicholson (who has buckets of charisma even when on autopilot). 
On the other hand, 
he is still Jack NIcholson (rather then an actual underworld kingpin.)","f41cc039-4cfc-46ab-aa5f-d0b5f0af5d11",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2009-02-23","","RT","850101658"
