3.0,"THE DEPARTED

The Departed(), managed to find instant success upon it's release, grabbing itself four oscars. The film follows a very typical ""cop and mobsters"" storyline, and in all honesty there is very little to differentiate it from others of this genre.

Leonardo DiCaprio plays a cop from a rough background who goes under cover to infiltrate mobster Jack Nicholson's organisation, however Matt Damon plays a character who is working as a respectible detective, but secretly working for the mob. The tension builds as the two mirrored counterparts try to uncover each others identies.

Whilst the performances here are good, they fail to be anything spectacular, because the protagonists are very much cliche'd charicatures of characters we've seen before in other movies of the genre. Jack Nicholson does his usual badguy routine, and whilst the acting on the whole is believable, it's nothign outstanding.

When looking at direction of this film, two things spring to mind, firstly that Martin Scorsese has managed to keep tempo and pace for most of the movie, which is something he has stuggled with all his career. Secondly, there are some, shots that are completely nonsensical. In the middle of a meeting, the camera will suddenly have a couple of quick tracking shots that seem out of place, or cut to a longer distance shot just for a few seconds before returning. There are some poetic moments though, and the final shot is wonderful, but there's so much excess movement at times that seems to be there just to keep the viewer interested when not much is happening in a scene. Better than Scorsese's usual direction, but still in much need of imporvement.

The narrative itself is quite compelling, as we find ourselves drawn deeply into the plot, once we get past the over the top profanities that are apparently part and parcel with the police force, we do build up a slight empathy with the protagonists. The love interest of the piece though, seems to almost become a more integral role, before being dismissed, making us not see much use for her inclusion for a great deal of her screen time. Certain parts of the story are merely glanced at, whilst we spend too long with others, eventually building to the hopelessly anti-climactic finish, which leaves the audience detached and overall dismissive of the movie entirely.

All in all however, The Departed is a good film, but far from great. We get a few beautiful shots and sequences, as with most Scorsese films, but like his other work, they are strung together very poorly with a lot of wasted time and weak narrative choices. A fine example of the genre however, but nothing more than decent.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2009-03-18","","RT","793592447"
