2.5,"Martin Scorsese's next adventure after the ill received muddling crime epic 'Gangs Of New York' was once again supposed to bring the supreme master of the urban crime genre back to his best. In essence The Departed promises much more than his mixed reviewed '95 epic that was Casino, but I felt unconvinced overall that his big budget remake of one of the best received Hong Kong Thriller's (Infernal Affairs) in existence really added anything new. It's full of Scorsese's style, but the endless copying and little re-routing from the original left me slightly disappointed. Considering this was the film that finally won Scorsese a whole batch of Academy Awards (including Best Picture) I felt he could have done more with the template he had been left by Lau and Mak. And as for the female role that Sammi Cheng Sau-Man played in the original was a much stronger part than that of the poorly scripted in Vera Farmiga, a female angle was lost in translation. Another problem the tale had this time around was the length. Expanding the original running time of about 100 minutes to 151 minutes was, in my opinion, a very silly thing to do when considering how little there is extra added in. Surprisingly under rated actor Leonardo Di Caprio fits his role very nicely as the goodie undercover mole, while Matt Demon continues to amaze me with his 2D performance. I've said some very bad things about Mark Wahlberg on this site, and gave him the worst performance award in my own version of the oscars for The Happening, here he plays rude, arrogant and loud mouthed Staff Sergeant Sean Dignam to great effect. The story is strung along well enough, though some parts may bore the average movie goer. Another reason to watch this film would have been to see Jack Nicholson but quite frankly I was disappointed, his usual witty drunk-type self didn't work, partly because he lacked any power in the role but also because his acting was flat, a first experience for me to witness. Overall its competent and well above average, but the increase in the violence, and other explicit content (Infernal Affairs was a 15) doesn't add to the so-so characters and loosely shaped story. The blame unfortunately lies with Scorsese himself, though this does really feel more of a consistent tale than one with powershot montages. My advice would be to watch the original. It's better, more concise, better acted and more interesting than the overplayed Boston environment.
75/100","e0da6a5c-b236-47d5-a3ca-ffc9777e68f3",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2009-04-20","","RT","901012285"
