4.5,"Scorsese likes to stick with his leads, and if it wasn't already apparent to you after Gangs of New York and The Aviator that Leo DiCaprio is Scorsese's new Robert DeNiro, then The Departed is your pleasant wake up call. I have a confession. I've never given DiCaprio the proper credit for his acting chops which, honestly, he's wielded since childhood. I think I've never been able to get past his boyish charm. Anyway, whenever he lands a role as an adult, I instantly think he's been miscast. It doesn't matter to me if he's grown a beard or toned his pecs or sports a tat-laden torso. I can't help thinking, ""What a cool Halloween costume!"" With The Departed, however, he really grew into his role. It was the first time I was able to take him seriously. Fortunately for us, he's not the only shining star in this flick; he's surrounded by a lot of talent. Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, and Martin Sheen also dial in great performances. Jack Nicholson is...well, he's Jack. He doesn't try very hard anymore, but nobody's sending him to Juilliard for it.","e6f07600-7e48-4527-87a8-df8914ba8c7d",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-06-12","","RT","901008652"
