4.5,"The Departed is much more mainstream than some of [Director] Martin Scroceses other workespecially his early workbut its no less entertaining.  This is an engaging, riveting and involving crime thriller fueled by outstanding performances by the lead actors and slick, fast-paced direction, with a touch of comic relief sprinkled throughout.

Matt Damon plays Colin Sullivan, a fatherless kid who happens to live in the same neighborhood as notorious organized crime boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson).  Costello takes Sullivan under his wing at an early age and becomes a father figure of sorts.  He helps Colin keep food on his familys table and helps keep him mostly out of trouble; when the time comes, he forces Colin to join the Massachusetts State police and serve as a mole.  Colin excels at the police academy and quickly rises through the ranks to join the Special Investigation Unit, a division assigned to organized crime.

Meanwhile, at around the same time, William Costigan Jr. (Leonardo DiCaprio) is about to finish his time at the police academy.  Just before he graduates, hes approached by Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sergeant Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) for a deep undercover assignment to infiltrate the Boston Mob.  Costigan is chosen because his fathers ties to the mob make him a more believable criminal.  Hes imprisoned on a phony assault charge and, upon release, uses both his fathers name and his own phony criminal record to infiltrate Costellos organization.  Costigan rises as quickly as Sullivan, becoming one of Costellos most trusted lieutenants.

Soon both the mob and the police realize theyve been infiltrated; both informants are tasked with helping to find the traitor in his respective organization.  What ensues is a race by each informant to discover the others identity.  Complicating the issue is a love triangle that inadvertently develops between Costigan, Sullivan and a police psychiatrist (Vera Farmiga).  Further complicating things is the fact that only Queenan and Dignam know of Costigans true assignment; hes so deeply undercover that there are no official records of his involvement in the case.

The Departed is crime drama elevated to Shakespearean-like tragedy.  Everyone in the cast is very strong and certainly no one is eclipsed; for my money though, DiCaprio gives the most impressive performance.  Until this role, I couldnt help still viewing him, to some degree, as Hollywoods Teen Beat cover boy.  Say what you will about not believing him as a thug, the man can act.  Plain and simple.  Alec Baldwin provides the comic relief here and Matt Damon is terrific as Sullivan, a mob mole whos far from in control of his own life.  Sullivan is a mildly sympathetic figure in some ways, but in the end, he proves little better than the thugs he works for and I wasnt sorry to see him get his comeuppance.  Mark Wahlberg is also good as Dignam, a police sergeant with an attitude and a decided dislike of both green cops and Costigan in general.  And Jack Nicholson is, well, Jack Nicholson.  He's demented and edgy and menacing as mob boss Frank Costello.

There are some issues here with the plot and the love triangle seems a bit contrived, but I hardly noticed any of that.  The Departed is fun, involving, true edge-of-your-seat entertainment.  Highly recommended.","1ca92bf0-3826-4b9d-894a-f9c05002f2b3",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-07-15","","RT","901273474"
3.5,"Nicholson hams to great effect and the supporting cast is stellar. Darkly enjoyable.","73339baf-0220-4ea8-be33-623a82374898",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2009-07-15","","RT","856253987"
4.5,"I was late to the scene on watching The Departed, and I ended up watching it when it was released in Japan, typically late as most movies are when they are brought to this market.  It had already been to the Oscars and there was some press that I read about how the families of the victims of Jack Nicholson's character were deeply offended by the glorification of the man.  It also employs two actors that I feel ambivalent about (Matt Damon and Leonardo DiCaprio).  So I wasn't expecting to like it so much.  What should have been at the forefront of my mind, however, was a phrase that has never steered me wrong.  ""Trust in Scorsese!""  

The Departed turned me around on DiCaprio.  What I remember is the mildly unpleasant-to-look-at boyish tweener idol from Titanic and the made-for-teenage-girls Romeo + Juliet (actually not a bad movie, despite DiCaprio).  I actually (yes, VERY regrettably) skipped Gangs of New York specifically because DiCaprio was so prominent in the movie (another phrase, in conjunction with Trust in Scorsese, should have been Trust in Day-Lewis! when I initially skipped it).  Anyway, it was quite something on DiCaprio's part to turn around about ten years of admittedly unjustified hatred on my part in this one movie.  It was good enough to get me to go back and watch Gangs of New York, and he did well in that, too.  

I don't care much for Matt Damon, but for his part in The Departed he's playing a character that is supposed to be unlikeable, so it was actually perfectly cast.  In that regard it was not hard to get past my general dislike of him as an actor.  

Jack Nicholson is marvelous.  It may be my favorite of his roles (though I have an admittedly small Nicholson cache from which to make that exclamation).  While I can understand the feelings of the families of his character's victims, it's really hard not to like Nicholson in this kind of campy style (as long as you are detached from the reality of the man he's playing).  

There are many very good supporting roles, including Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg (maybe his best role since Three Kings), and Alec Baldwin, that add to the overall greatness of the movie.  While Nicholson is stealing the show, these other actors are providing strong underpinnings to the movie to keep us from losing interest when Nicholson is not onscreen.  

The ending was...shocking, to say the least.  I don't know about the true story around Nicholson's character, but if this is what really happened to the other people, then it can't be helped that that is the ending Scorsese had to settle with.  If it was Scorsese's decision independent of reality, I applaud the audacity to pull it off in such a mainstream film, even if it is less than crowd-pleasing (Wahlberg makes up for it a little bit in the end).  

One little thing that I don't know if other people noticed was in the police department, fairly early in the movie, the instructor is talking about what happens when a bullet is shot into someone's head.  There's a graphic picture of the process on the chalkboard.  It foreshadows the rest of the movie and how most of the deaths in the movie will be carried out.  I liked that little touch.

I think the only reason that I can't give The Departed the 100% that it probably deserves is I have the tiniest of reservations against the ending and the sometimes callous glorification of the despicable true mobster in Nicholson's character.  If I can keep myself detached from the reality, though, there's nothing to dislike in Nicholson's performance itself.  Also, I do give credit to the gutsy ending, but the side of my brain that likes a nice Hollywood ending and the side that appreciates originality and anti-Hollywood endings are constantly fighting and I can't FULLY forgive it.  There are better Scorsese movies out there that he deserved to win the Oscar for Best Director for, but it's good to see him get his long overdue accolades.  The Departed is entertaining, funny, brutal, and original, and is a must-see for mobster-, crime drama-, Scorsese-, and Nicholson-enthusiasts.","8c3dbb17-39a7-46f1-bd79-d783d195f919",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-07-15","","RT","901286725"
