3.5,"<i>""When you decide to be something, you can be it. That's what they don't tell you in the church. When I was your age they would say we can become cops, or criminals. Today, what I'm saying to you is this: when you're facing a loaded gun, what's the difference?''</i><p>
<i>The Departed</i>, directed by Martin Scorsese, is the Hollywood remake of the Hong Kong crime thriller <i>Infernal Affairs</i>. Yes, I <i>hate</i> Hollywood remakes. Why can't they come up with their own ideas instead of taking ideas from another film. Truth be told, <i>The Departed</i> is a lot better than most remakes that are produced nowadays, but it seems Scorsese has fallen into Hollywood's influence.

<u>SUMMARY:</u><p>
Ever since his childhood in South Boston, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) has been in debt to gang boss Frank Costello (Frank Costello). Sullivan is an admired member of the Massachusets State Police; his closest friends don't realise that he's in the pay of Costello. Meanwhile Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio), who has had a troubled childhood and is alone in the world, has enrolled in the police academy. But he's persuaded by Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and his deputy, Sergeant Dignam (Mark Wahlberg), to feign disgrace and endure a prison sentence in order to infiltrate Costello's gang. While Sullivan tips off Costello whenever the police are getting close, Costigan in turn tries to warn the police of Costello's activities. Both men are looking for the traitor in their midst. And both men become involved with government psychiatrist Madolyn (Vera Farmiga).

<u>REVIEW:</u><p>
It seems ridiculous that everyone thinks that this is <i>so</i> 'original'. It's a remake! Part of the reason comes Scorsese - the film doesn't fully explain that <i>The Departed</i> is a remake of <i>Infernal Affairs</i>, though it does label that it is in association with Media Asia (the studio that made <i>Infernal Affair</i>). Good thing is - <i>The Departed</i> isn't a shot for shot copy. There actually are some changes. It's been Americanised - which was obviously necessary, and each of the characters behave differently. However, the biggest problem comes from the romance with Madolyn. In the original film, the love interests of the two central characters were seperate. Here, they're the same, and a bit of an 'unknown' love triangle forms. It doesn't help the character of Madolyn in terms of how likeable she is. The romance she shares with Costigan and Sullivan isn't particularly interesting either. On the plus side, screenwriter William Monahan does a great job with the script. It feels like a classic mob movie, and the dialogue is brillaint - almost every line is drenched with the word 'fuck'. The events of <i>The Departed</i> follow the events of <i>Infernal Affairs</i> quite closely, and for those who have seen <i>Infernal Affairs</i>, there shouldn't be a lot of surprises.

The movie boasts a really strong cast, and everyone is perfectly cast. Leonardo DiCaprio and Matt Damon are both great as the central characters, but the rest of the cast seem to upstage them. Jack Nicholson is so good as Costello - it's hard to find anyone else to play him, he's perfect in the role. Mark Wahlberg creates an amusing character with Dignam, who swears and insults people almost all the time. Martin Sheen is great, and so is Ray Winstone as Costello's right hand man, Mr. French. Alec Baldwin plays Captain Ellerby, who is Sullivan's boss at the State Police department, and he does seem to have a lot of fun with the role. Vera Farmiga, however, was the best of the performances for me. While her characters wasn't particularly interesting, she created something quite sincere in her character. She's gorgeous, and provides some really moving moments throughout the film.

There's no sense of style here. <i>Infernal Affairs</i> had a good sense of style throughout the film, and <i>The Departed</i> seems to go for a more realistic approach. The city of Boston works well with some crabby looking streets, and the film is generally well-shot by Michael Ballhaus. The soundtrack is excellent and gives the film a classic crime movie feel. There are some truly explosive, and quite violent, action sequences, and though they are short, they're well staged by Scorsese, whose direction is sublime. While it isn't as subtle as his previous films, it does allow for some great performances. Edited amazingly by Thelma Schoonmaker, the film feels quite tense, but it <i>DRAGGGGGS</i>. This film is quite long at 2 and a half hours, and there are some really unnecessary scenes which weren't needed at all.

<i>Infernal Affairs</i> is one of my favourite films, so maybe I'm biased on this one. <i>The Departed</i> is a solid and quite enjoyable mob movie, but I'd still have to recommend you see <i>Infernal Affairs</i> first. This ain't a classic, though everyone else says it is, and it ain't Best Picture. When will Hollywood remakes come to an end?

