5.0,"The Departed is another masterpiece from Martin Scorcese, the kingpin of organized crime dramas/thrillers. The film stars a crowd of big-name actors, and is written by William Monahan, formerly known for Kingdom of Heaven.

The Departed tracks the Boston Police Department organized crime unit, and the organized crime group they're hunting (Frank Costello and crew), via two ""moles"" or ""rats"". One, played by Leonardo DiCaprio, is the son of a recently deceased mobster, but is now an undercover cop deep inside the Costello organization. The other, played by Matt Damon, is the adopted son of sorts of Costello, now climbing the ranks in the organized crime unit of BPD. Through the 150 minutes there are numerous crossings and double crossings, murders, beatings, taps and tails. That barebones plot looks like many other crime thrillers, but Scorcese would never let it end up like that. 

Two and a half hour movies generally tire out in the last 40 minutes or so, causing me to think back about what should have been left on the cutting room floor. The Departed, however, rips through the minutes quickly, and I was engrossed the whole way through. Scorcese and editor Thelma Schoonmaker cropped each shot close, only dragging out when there was an apparent reason for doing so. The pacing is rapid but not action movie pace, which lets the viewer digest and interpret as the film ticks along, finding some details and motifs that Scorcese put in. For example, the use of envelopes is something worth tracking for careful viewers.

The acting truly steals a show, and that's why the entire cast was nominated for a ""Best Cast"" Screen Actor's Guild award. Mark Wahlberg got a supporting actor Oscar nom for his performance as the fierce-tongued police lieutenant, but I didn't find him particularly outstanding. I thought Leo DiCaprio put up the best performance of the show by far. He was wicked and evil when his undercover job called for it, but also conveyed the hidden vulnerability and panic when called for. He's fast becoming my favorite actor, and it's movies like The Departed and Revolutionary Road that got him there. Jack Nicholson is of course, great as the boss, Frank Costello. I would have like to see a bit more madness later in the film, even panic, but Jack is very convincing. Martin Sheen and Alec Baldwin as police superiors are solid but not at all standouts.
The Departed was nominated for five Academy Awards and won four (Picture, Director, Editing, Adapted Screenplay), and was nominated for and won myriad other awards from around the world. It's well deserving. It's a fantastic example of a mature, engrossing thriller, and a showcase of some legendary talent.


PS: I would rate it 95%, but the scale only allows 10% increments. To give it a 90%, an A-, is too low, so it gets the nod for 100%.","7534f271-3090-4b0b-990d-d52e7132be81",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2009-11-24","","RT","901279574"
