4.5,"X marks the spot.

And down the rabbit hole we go. The Departed is one of the bleakest films I can remember, even counting Taxi Driver. Scorsese's lament on personal descent is practically maddening and completely immersive. Even upon multiple viewings it's as potent as it ever was. The complex story follows parallel lives of a criminal posing as a cop and a cop undercover as a criminal, both making choices that they cannot turn back from. Scorsese is above all else a lover of films and keeps it entertaining (in a car wreck on-looker sort of way.) The pacing is brisk, it speeds by despite its running time and never feels slow. Top it off with an ensemble of amazing actors, (yes even Walberg,) and you've got yourself one hell of a film.

http://thisguyoverhere.com/2006-films/the-departed-2006/","8286808a-2070-43a6-974e-afb8603a471c",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-08","","RT","901165916"
4.0,"Pour ceux qui, comme moi, ont revu l'Oscar du meilleur film de 2007 dimanche soir, un seul conseil:
Precipitez vous sur Infernal Affairs, le polar hong-kongais dont ce film est un remake!
Encore plus tendu que sa version americaine, l'intrigue est ressere sur les deux personnages principaux. Et le decor de Hong-Kong se prete parfaitement a ce scenario a la fois simple et ici parfaitement exploite.","52035426-82c1-4170-9b08-2235847eacd9",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2009-12-08","","RT","800135598"
4.5,"X marks the spot.

<a href=""http://thisguyoverhere.com/2006-films/the-departed-2006/"">This Guy Over Here picks this as one of the best films of the 2000s</a>","6b06ae5c-5cfe-4b4b-b4a8-537a8f0caecb",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-08","","RT","890562071"
4.5,"I must tell you how happy I was to find out that Martin Scorsese's The Departed was an absolute piece of work. Most of these classic directors begin to die off and run out of ideas like Spielberg is slowly starting to do with releasing certain flops like War of the Worlds. Scorsese is known for releasing certain classics like Goodfellas (one of my favourites), Raging Bull, and Gangs of New York. However, he has created a few flops like New York New York that came out to very luke-warm reviews. For The Departed, I did not know what to expect. All I knew was that it was a movie that paralleled various movies like Infernal Affairs and Mean Streets.

It stars DiCaprio as a wanna-be cop who goes undercover for the Boston PD to take down a notorious mob boss played by Nicholson. However, also inside the PD is a snitch who keeps tipping Nicholson off when the cops want to take him down. Once discovering that there may be a rat in Nicholson's crew, Nicholson starts to really show his dark side (his Shining like personality). It sounds like a basic plot, but it isn't, it develops into a work of art that has a creative and well-directed beginning and an unpredictable and shocking conclusion.

What I loved so much about this movie is how intense and unique each scene is. For example, there's a scene when DiCaprio tries to arrest the corrupt cop in an alleyway, but just before striking him, his cell goes off, blowing his position. 
The reason why I felt so immersed into this scene and every other scene is because each scene deserved that moment due to other amazing scenes in the film.
Another unique aspect about this movie is that it seems to play out a lot like an opera because there's music playing through ¾ of the movie (and it's good music; you get Gimme Shelter by the Rolling Stones, Comfortably Numb by Pink Floyd, etc.) and for one other reason, which I won't give away. There's various other positive comments I could deliver to you about this movie, like its top-notch and career-best performances by the whole cast and it's clever use of symbolism. I highly recommend this movie; it's one of the best movies I have seen so far this year. And as a closer, let me just say that this movie called The Departed definitely lives up to its title.","2a4b566e-c6e3-42dc-9aac-2e2680ecf6b1",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-08","","RT","901342919"
