4.5,"The Departed is an instant classic from the master of the gangster genre. Matt Damon gives an especially good performance as the inside man for Jack Nicholsons Frank Costello. This movie has one of the best ensembles I've yet to see, and all of the actors and actresses are in top form. Scorsese also shows us some of his best directing since Goodfellas. And also that the guy can really tell a story. This is for sure a must have for any Scorsese fan, or movie fan in general.","9ceed6e9-4055-4c14-a7f1-72f72c9a6d2c",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-17","","RT","901347910"
4.5,"Will Martin Scoresese ever fail? Not this time. 
The departed is enthralling; gut wrenching, eye gluing filmmaking. 
Probably, some of the best I have seen in years. 

Leonardo DiCaprio, another poor sod whose flawless looks have convinced his critics he can not possibly have talent to accompany them, proves everyone wrong here. 
I can feel the sheer stress and energy of DiCaprios characters panic, as he falls deeper and deeper into the dark sorted double crossing, backstabbing and terrifying world of Mob Boss Frank Costello (played brilliantly by none other than Jack Nicholson). 

Nicholson, always brilliant at being the bad guy, does not disappoint. His performance is charismatic, sexy and highly provocative. You simply cannot take your eyes off his energy and feel as though his talent is bathing you in bright lights, much like the performance of fellow Oscar Winner Matt Damon.
  
There is no lacking in any character here. Performances are all highly commendable and legendarily well drawn. Writing is clever and potently memorable. I feel DiCaprio may be inching closer to that coveted Oscar statuette with this searing performance, and it is only a matter of time before Nicholson takes home another. 

The storyline is incredibly complex, and yet the ending is so final and so complete, with no lingering questions. Perfectly wrapped up and pristinely well written. The Departed is truly a film that you will remember as representing everything brilliant about filmmaking, acting, directing and writing.
Absolutely Brilliant. 




RECOMMENDATIONS 

WATCH IT IF YOU LIKED: The Bourne Identity trilogy, the godfathers or Goodfellas. 
	
CHILDREN: Send them to bed, and shut the door. Some teenagers may find the story hard to understand so stick to 15+
 
RELIGIOUS COMMUNITY: Some blasphemy, heavy swearing, drug use and high level violence so no, probably not a good idea as is definately not a 'crowd pleasing' sort of movie. 

FREE ADVICE: Pay attention! If the phone rings, pause the film or the plotline will lose you. Do not watch with people who are notorious for not paying attention, and then asking annoying questions. You will get a headache.","6c9b5661-71fe-48be-a7a4-02b6c945edcf",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-17","","RT","901347767"
3.5,"Really enjoyable, although Scorcese winning the oscar for THIS film was a bit of a joke.","28d25bd1-a1df-4897-864e-5f5bfd92fc4e",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2009-12-17","","RT","806637075"
5.0,"Competes with goodfellas and the godfather in its genre and quiet possibly the best movie of all genres this past decade!! scorceses stamp on this movie is undeniable hes at his peak here as is all the cast who put in performances of a life time to make this a must see movie. The story is edge of the seat stuff and the tension scorcese creates in some scences is refreshing in a era when movies just dont suck u in anymore. This movie will be looked back upon in the coming years as a true classic and with the same light as other scorcese heavy hitters as raging bull and taxi driver","b7decdbc-bf43-4351-af51-97e52dfafb18",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2009-12-17","","RT","901347993"
5.0,"The Departed is an epic tale of good cop vs. bad cop. Leonardo Di Caprio plays an undercover cop who is chosen for the job because of his troubled background. Matt Damon plays a sucessful cop who is picked for one of the highest positions in the Boston Police Department. Here's the thing, he's a rat who secretly works for the mob. Nobody suspects him though because of his high position. Frank Costello, the mob boss, is played gloriously over the top by Jack Nicholson. He runs Boston like it's his playground from first grade, it's almost too easy. The police can't seem to figure out why they can't touch this guy. The two guys who hire Di Caprio, played by Mark Walhberg and Martin Sheen, make it their personal duty to find the rat in the police department. Nicholson becomes suspicious and begins to think that there is a rat in his mob, so Damon begins to look for the rat in his own department. The two moles end up looking for eachother.

The Departed is yet another thrillingly intense Martin Scorsese picture. William Monahan's script is masterful, adapted from the Hong Kong film Infernal Affairs. Scorsese takes the script and absolutely makes it his own. The Departed is full of compelling sequences and offbeat humor that only Scorsese could pull off. The cinematography in this film is absolutely amazing. The editing is spot on, the film works at a brisk pace despite it's lengthy running-time, there is not a single boring moment in the film. The production for this film is Grade A. It doesn't get much better than this. This film is nearly flawless.

There are so many amazing performances in this film, that it's hard to pick out the finest. Leonardo DiCaprio is wonderful in the complex role of William Costigan. Matt Damon is deliciously evil as the corrupt cop Colin Sullivan. Damon is masterful in the role in which he takes complete command. He creates some very intense and dynamic scenes with Nicholson, in which you can't help but to hate his guts because of how corrupt he is. Yet, he creates some very solid chemistry with Vera Farmiga, in which he gains your sympathy and you wish he could forgot his past and live a good life with his girlfriend. It's a masterfully multi-layered performance. Jack Nicholson is gleefully hysterical as Frank Costello, he is basically doing ""The Joker"" again. But it works, the performance is so driven and scarily over-the-top, that you understand why this man is so feared on the streets of Boston. His scenes with DiCaprio is so intense that it becomes beyond chilling. The relative newcomer Vera Farmiga is great in a somewhat small role. She's electrifying in the therapist sessions with DiCaprio. She's also sexy and incredibly seductive in her scenes outside the office with DiCaprio, which leds to a forbidden night between the two. She's also lovely and soft in her scenes with Damon, which gains also helps gain Damon's character some sympathy because your simply rooting for the couple. The ""forbidden"" night between Farmiga and DiCaprio led to quite an interesting subplot at the end of the film if you can catch it. Finally, the best supporting performance comes from Mark Wahlberg, who is hilarious as the extremely vengeful and angry Dignam. His character shows up in unexpected moments of humor and ease. Ray Winstone is great, Alec Baldwin is great. The ensemble for this film is amazing, it's a truly perfectly cast film.

The Departed is a masterfully crafted film, proving that Scorsese is once again at the top of his game.","5729c6b6-234a-4012-a307-5797a31b611b",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2009-12-17","","RT","901344976"
4.5,"Most of Martin Scorsese's films have been about men trying to realize their inner image of themselves.The Departed is about two men trying to live public lives that are the radical opposites of their inner realities. Their attempts threaten to destroy them, either by implosion or fatal betrayal. The telling of their stories involves a moral labyrinth, in which good and evil wear each other's masks.","53c7bdec-328c-4729-a380-2d9879ae81a0",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-18","","RT","901345792"
