4.5,"The Departed marks Martin Scorsese's greatest film since Goodfellas. The 2006 irish crime drama revolves around three major players. In South Boston, the Irish mob boss Francis ""Frank"" Costello(Jack Nicholson) befriends a then young Colin Sullivan. Now all grown up and now a state trooper for the Massachusettes State Police, Sullivan(Matt Damon) is a double agent for Costello. In another state police training academy, William ""Billy"" Costigan Jr.(Leonardo DiCaprio) has gradguated, but cannot become an undercover agent due to his unfortunate crinimal family ties. After convincing Captain Queenan(Martin Sheen) and Staff Sergeant Dignam(Mark Wahlberg), Costigan gets arrested and goes on probation in order to possibly join the ranks of Costello and his mob. The intensity in the film goes to great lengths, especially scenes with Costello's right hand man, Mr. French(Ray Winstone.) His tolerance for violence and inflicting it on other people could make any person sick to their stomach. The Irish mob has never looked so bad-ass. Nicholson's performance is a powerful and neurotic potrayl of a mob boss. He plays his role to the top of the hill, as does Damon and DiCaprio. This film has its share of violence, so if you have a light stomach and can't bare to watch gruesome bar fights, I suggest you leave this one alone. AS for the rest of you, I highly recomend it.","26dd5417-13f2-44ba-944f-79fab081bdcf",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-12-19","","RT","901301492"
