5.0,"Yes this is Martin Scorsese's best film since GoodFellas and the best crime film ever to grace the screen. This film also delivers great performances by Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon and Jack Nicholson, as well as great supporting performances by Mark Wahlberg, Alex Baldwin, Ray Winstone, Martin Sheen and Vera Farmiga. The film is a dark crime saga, with Scorsese's traditional trademarks, as well as great cinematography. Jack Nicholson's character is based off of real life gangster James ""Whitey"" Bulger, while the violence is realistic and follows Scorsese's use of staying true to gangster violence. The film's ending will make people jump out of their seats, while Nicholson still manages to bring a great performance, with Wahlberg portraying a totally different character. This film is a true Scorsese crime saga.","6604a8d7-ea6c-44e7-a909-7a018b243607",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-01-26","","RT","901365990"
4.5,"""A man makes his own way. No one gives it to you. You have to take it.""

So begins Martin Scorsese's most recent gangster flick. For the first five minutes, you can just barely make out the face of the film's sinister key central figure, crime boss Frank Costello. But the voice and swagger are unmistakably Jack. That's right. Jack Nicholson is back doing what he does best, reprising his bad boy Hollywood persona as a sadistic, but charming, crime kingpin.

Director Martin Scorsese takes a departure from the mean streets of his beloved New York City and travels about 200 miles north across the Eastern Seaboard to another notoriously tough American town. Set against the backdrop of modern day Boston, ""The Departed"" is a carefully orchestrated drama that carefully shifts between the tumultuous life of one man, and the relatively comfortable upbringing of another. Early on in the film, there is a level of sympathy that develops toward one of these two men, and a heated contempt toward the other. Leonardo DiCaprio and Matt Damon play the fresh police academy graduates from two entirely different backgrounds. The world that these two men live in is a world that Scorsese knows all too well; a world where senseless violence runs rampant, and where trust is always the key issue. In Scorsese's world of organized crime, deception and betrayal always seem to come in the form of a familiar face and a friendly smile.

Scorsese proves himself once again as a powerhouse of American cinema. Not only is he a great storyteller, but Scorsese may very well be the greatest director of the gangster film. ""The Departed"" is bold and daring, but not as flashy as, say, ""Casino"", for example. The movie concentrates more on developing its story and its wide array of various characters. The film also has its sublime cinematic moments. The Chinatown on-foot chase sequence comes to mind, as it showcases Scorsese's brilliant eye for thrilling film-making.

The movie's storyline twists and turns, and thrills and surprises, up until its poetic final images. Four and a half stars. ""The Departed"" will leave you highly entertained.","bdbbe2e0-90d1-4b21-bb5c-aa5ab6795463",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-01-26","","RT","897799655"
