5.0,"Director Martin Scorsese takes on Boston's organized crime, where Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) runs the show.  The state police take some drastic steps, with the Special Investigation Unit heads Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Dignam (Mark Wahlberg in an Oscar-nominated role) bringing in Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) from the academy to work as an informant from inside Costello's gang.  Unbeknown to them, Costello has a similar plan: he has Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) on the inside working his way up through the ranks at SIU.   With both sides trying to stay one step ahead, but constantly undermined by the other, each tries to uncover the mole on the inside.  A gritty modern crime drama in the style only Scorsese can capture, The Departed is the best movie of its kind since The Godfather (and unsurpassed except perhaps by The Dark Knight).  The story is great, based on the series Infernal Affairs.  What sets it apart is the cast, each a powerhouse performance.  One of DiCaprio's finer roles, if not his best ever.  Damon is also a great presence.  Nicholson seems, by this point, able to do these sorts of roles in his sleep.  The true stars of the movie, however, are the smaller roles of Dignam (Wahlberg) and Ellerby (Alec Baldwin).  This is probably Wahlberg's finest performance, and the one that truly showed he was capable of being taken seriously as a dramatic actor.  The ending of the movie is priceless with loads of twists, turns, and 'serves you right' vengeance that will make you cheer.

My rating: A / 5 stars","4fca6425-0def-4422-969c-3935f96ec7c4",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-04-01","","RT","814860780"
