4.5,"If you forgot already, this film won the Academy Award for Best Motion Picture, and it completely deserved it. The craft involved in this film was almost perfect, and Scorsese definately earned his Best Direction Oscar.

At its core, its a cops vs. Irish gangsters flick; the only problem is that both sides are so corrupted and entangled with eachother that when one falls, it drags the other one down with it. A lot of people are ""whacked"" in this film in ways that one expects with a Hollywood production, but because of Scorsese, they are ""whacked"" in a way that resembles a masterpiece.

Again, this is a gangster film, and a damn good one at that. Scorsese has visited the genre several times before from different angles (see Gangs of New York: a period ganster drama). This one sort of hits  from all angles, and only because of Scorsese's imense skills as a filmmaker does the film stay on track and not unravel.

Its main theme is corruption (not surprising for a ganster film), but it is corruption at all levels. Social corruption, bureaucratic corruption; this movie isn't exactly uplifting, because this corruption is actually happening as we speak. 

The story of The Departed occurs within a viscous cycle of murder and betrayal that leads to more murder and betrayal. To cut to the chase, there are a lot of dirty rats in this movie, and a lot of them are taken out, even in the very last scene.

The pacing was mostly smooth sailing, though like a lot of Scorsese pictures, its long, and some things could of been left on the cutting room floor. At times, the accents are terrible; Matt Damon is the biggest offender in my book, and isn't he from Boston? Leonardo DiCaprio's accent was also pretty bad, but its sort of like Sean Connery's Irish Accent in The Untouchables: who gives a f--k? 

The only casting problem I had was Damon, but I think that's mostly due to overexposure. Mark Wahlberg deserved his Oscar nomination, and he should ""act"" more often (if its like Planet of the Apes, just say no!). 

Vera Farmiga really blew me away in this, and she really stole the spotlight in every scene she was in. She reminds me of Faye Dunaway, except she isn't so manic; its more subdued, like Meryl Streep. If there are more actresses like her, they need to step up to the plate right now because they are the future of female roles in movies.","b2ec36e9-a664-4ae0-90ab-701b5e682fa2",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-04-04","","RT","901395610"
