5.0,"It is a special thing when you watch a movie and know when the credits start to roll that you just witnessed something incredible. That is an extremely rare feeling one gets while watching cinema, especially with modern films, but after watching The Departed, I had such a feeling. The Departed is one of those rare movies that other filmakers envy because it is so good, they gaze upon it with jealously because it is so meticuously crafted, so expertly told that other filmakers must bow their heads in shame at the crap products they produce when compared to a film such as this. This is grade-A cinema, plain and simple.

The Departed is director Martin Scorsese's Oscar winning(he finally recieved his directing Oscar for it) gangster film about the Irish Mob in Boston. It is a story of men and their qualms with their identity; how they appear to the world around them is vastly different from who they actually are deep down. Colin Sullivan(Matt Damon) grew up as semi-normal kid, but he was taken under the wing of a man name Frank Costello(Jack Nicholson) at a young age. Costello is a mob boss and he slowly built Sullivan up to being one of his goons, but instead of following Costello around like a normal henchmen, Sullivan joined the Boston Police Department to work as a mole for Costello. Meanwhile, a new cadet at the Police Academy named Billy Costigan(Leonardo DiCaprio) is looking to become a police officer, and after taking an interview, he is asked to go undercover into Boston's underworld due to his familial ties to the Irish Mob. Costigan accepts, and pretty soon he is infiltrating his way into the crew of his target; Frank Costello.

From here, the plot becomes a game of cat and mouse as Costigan tries to find the mole in police department, while Sullivan tries to find the rat in Costello's crew. This sounds like a rather simple set-up, and taken on a base level it is, but the film is nothing if not complex, with the story constantly taking twists and turns that leaves the viewer on the edge. That is one of the best things about the movie, the tension. Few movies nowadays can muster up actual tension when that is what the film needs, but Scorsese separates the boys from the men with this movie by keeping a nonstop nail-biting atmosphere throughout the entire two and a half hour runtime. I truely had no idea where the movie was going to go, and that was an extremely refreshing feeling because most movies give you the entire story in the trailer or TV spot, but this flick keeps you guessing all the way up until the last second.

A lot of the tension that is generated in the film must be given credit to the actors that portray the characters, and there is not single actor in this movie who does not bring their abosolute A-game. DiCaprion and Damon are great as two guys who are in a constant state of paranoia, knowing that there is somebody on the other side trying to find out who they are, and that if they are made, it is over for them. Nicholson is also fantastic as the mob boss Costello, a guy who is smart and resourceful, but also on the edge about who it is in his crew that is a rat. The rest of the supporting cast, which includes Mark Wahlberg, Vera Farmiga, Martin Sheen, Ray Winstone, Alec Baldwin, and many others, are all great in their respective roles as well. That is another thing that blew me away about this film; the way all of the characters, even the small side ones, play a part in the story. No matter how big or small the part is, all the characters get their pivotal moment in the film that totally shakes up everything that is going on at that point.

This is a brilliant film made by one of the greatest directors of all time. Scorsese does not let the story drag for a second, he makes two and a half hours seem short with his excellent pacing and storytelling. This a must see movie for those that enjoy crime films, great dramas, thrillers, or just a damn good movie. Thumbs way way up!","5d80a0ab-44ac-45f0-a8af-209d955a63fb",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-04-06","","RT","901340530"
