5.0,"Martin Scorsese has been labeled the king of the crime genre. When he makes a movie, he knows he wants to give the audience a great description of the real crime world. He describes every detail of gangsters, cops, even their families to make sure we all know who these people are and what their objectives are. His masterpiece of crime movies is ""Goodfellas."" That movie is an entire description of the true crime life.

Now, he has released his new crime masterpiece, ""The Departed."" The movie is a remake of a foreign film from 2003 called ""Mou gaan dou"" (""Infernal Affairs). The setting takes place in Boston, Massachusettes, set in present time. It begins with a monologe from Jack Nicholson's character, Frank Costello, describing the way crime has progressed during the years, and his view of his own crime world. Frank is a man who knows what he wants, and how to get it. He tends to have a smart tendency to make sure he isn't caught by the cops. That's when we get an introduction to his new guy under his wing, Collin Sullivan (played by Matt Damon). Collin takes Frank's advice on everything as he grows up during the years. Now, grown up, he enters the Massachusettes State Police Department, already promoted to Sgt. He isn't the cop you want though. He's only in the department to be a rat for Frank and his mob. The MSPD has been trying to nail Frank for years. They've tried so many times to find a way to finally capture him, to stop the violence and crime he has created in the state. This is when we are introduced to rookie, undercover cop, Billy Costigan, Jr. (Leonardo DiCaprio). Billy is unable to get back into the regular police department, so his only option is to go undercover, joining Frank's mob. He gets hired and guided by two police officers, Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sgt. Dignam (Mark Wahlberg). They make Billy do the works to make sure he is not suspected by anyone. From going to prison for a fake crime, to getting aquainted with Frank, to seeing a therapist, the whole nine yards. It drives Billy crazy doing all of this for a living, just aching to just get Costello out of the way. But he knows it's the only way it will do justice for the department and for himself to be a full pledged cop.

Viewing the technical side of the movie, it's camera work is clean cut with greatly shot scenes. The editing is fast paced, with a few minor problems, but balances out where it is really solid. The music score by Howard Shore creates a good dramatic mood in this crime world we view. Scorsese's directing was absolutely brilliant as it always is. He goes back into the crime roots, making the movie in a more artistical sense, involving the script and the setting of the movie.

The players in this movie was the correct pick for all of their parts. Leo gives possibly one of his finest performances since ""The Aviator"" as Billy Costigan, capturing the strugglement of being an undercover cop working for one helluva mob. Matt Damon plays out nicely as Collin Sullivan, being able to keep his cool working for the police and being a rat for the mob at the same time. Of course, Jack Nicholson is awesome in this as he always his. It's about time he finally got a great role as a villain in this movie, because he does a good job being Costello. I will say, I'm not too big for Mark Wahlberg, I never thought him or some of his movies were all that good. I got to say though, I was impressed the way he took his character to something we can being attracted to through out the film. 

It seems Scorsese was trying to invent something different for the crime genre, making this a film where you have to think hard on whose playing who, and whose working for who. It's what builds the excitement of the movie, watching all of the character's lives intersect at a crossroads, with tales behind them being revealed. ""The Departed"" is one of Scorsese's best movies he has released in recent years, and beyond words one of the best movies of the decade. With great direction, fast-paced movement, and an all-star cast, featuring Martin Sheen, Alec Baldwin, Ray Winstone and Vera Farmiga, the movie will be remembered as a modern crime masterpiece of our time. It recieved five Academy Award nominations, with four of them winning for Best Motion Picture, Best Adapted Screenplay, Best Film Editing, and Best Director (finally), and it's last nomination being for Best Supporting Actor, Mark Wahlberg. I think the critics were thinking, ""It's about time we give him the win.""","675f44e8-8138-42b0-9cbd-61e1733db9da",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-05-01","","RT","904360413"
