5.0,"A twisted plot and a controversial yet engrossing ending, makes the Departed one of the best movie of the decade (In my opinion). Exploiting the word ""Fuck"" in ways where they do don't even fit gives a hilarious yet serious atmosphere. At first, one may perceive that the film takes a straight line towards normality, but throughout the film, it begins to alter and shift and bend into a mind blowing ""Guess who"" chase. Towards the ending of the film the ""Perception line"" suddenly explodes. Martin Scorsese flawlessly kills off one of the main characters in the most articulate and fascinating way. Then at the very end of the movie; he goes on and kills off the last main character. Everything in the movie tied in and nothing was left out. At first the strings scattered off into no where but then somehow found their way back and knotted into this magnificent and insane driven bow.  Maybe...Maybe not... Maybe fuck yourself (A quote from the movie)","ca567c50-d6b4-4ff6-88b5-b4e958f3f743",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-05-17","","RT","901397266"
4.5,"When writing about ""The Departed"", it's easy to get excited about the dream cast, the flawless soundtrack, and the fact that the legendary Martin Scorsese finally won his Oscar with this film. While this is all true, the real winner of this gritty crime-drama epic is screenwriter William Monahan.

Monahan puts together one of the most fascinating screenplays ever put to film. Not only is ""The Departed"" essentially a combination of three previously-released movies (Alan Mak & Andrew Lau's ""Infernal Affairs"" trilogy), but it's a dense, complicated plot that avoids being confusing. Monahan weaves a lot of themes into the story including identity, family, and instincts, and his outstanding work got him the Best Adapted Screenplay Oscar.

The most gratifying part of this film though, is the number of high-caliber actors who are working together in each scene. From character actors like Ray Winstone and Alec Baldwin to leading men like Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, and Mark Wahlberg to acting legends like Jack Nicholson and Martin Sheen--each scene features big time performers trying to outdo eachother.

Of course Scorsese is the man that pulls it all together for a serious experience rather than just another star-studded goof fest. This movie doesn't quite have the energy of ""Goodfellas"" but then again, what film does? I've always said that nobody does violent killings like Scorsese and this film is no different, each blood-splattering gun shot is a work of art...and there are a lot of them in this one!

My biggest knock on ""The Departed"" comes with Matt Damon, who I feel doesn't quite hold up his end of the dramatic bargain to his counterpart DiCaprio. Damon's criminal buried in the Boston PD doesn't struggle with nearly any of the self-doubt and personal angst that DiCaprio's policeman undercover in the crime world does. Of course, maybe that's a knock on Monahan for fleshing out one part more than another.

You will see a good deal of ""Heat"" and ""Mystic River"" in ""The Departed"", but that's pretty tall company to find yourself in. Even with all the misery and darkness in this film, it's still a fun watch and one that gets better each time you see it.

Watch If You Like: Goodfellas, Casino, Heat, Mystic River.","73bf015c-bdf7-41bb-9c1d-805f10736d13",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-05-18","","RT","895357829"
