4.5,"(from The Watermark, 10/19/06)
     In this quintessential Martin Scorsese film, he returns to his roots: crime, justice, revenge, and life in the underbelly of the big city. Leonardo DiCaprio is an undercover Boston cop working for Irish mafia kingpin Jack Nicholson. At the same time, Matt Damon is one of Nicholson's boys now working from inside the police department. What follows is an intricate and compelling story that keeps each imposter teetering just on the edge of exposure.
     Scorsese juxtaposes the two parallel stories beautifully, with lots of nervous tension and edge-of-your seat suspense. (Interesting fact: with forty-something directing credits including Taxi Driver, Raging Bull, and Goodfellas, Scorsese has never won an Oscar. Very sad.) Nicholson is his brilliantly weird self, but the film really belongs to DiCaprio, who is heartbreaking as the cop trapped in a secret identity from which he can't escape.
     The great supporting cast includes Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin, and Martin Sheen -- all with believable Boston accents no less. The body count and blood factor is pretty high; everybody shares in the cussing, fighting and killing. Two-hours-plus of testosterone-infused criminal violence -- would you expect anything less from Mr. S?","912c119e-f74f-44ac-bd78-23cd183de6f5",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-06-06","","RT","900967750"
