3.5,"Martin Scorsese's 256th movie The Departed is very long. Its long, long, long. And its violent. But what great Scorsese movie isn't extremely long and brutally violent. Think Bringing out the Dead, which was certainly long, but not quite violent enough. Though The Departed is by no means Goodfellas, it's a step in the right direction or whatever direction leads Scorsese away from The Aviator and Michael Jackson's Bad video. Yes he did direct that Wesley Snipes driven horribleness, look it up. 

Similar to his excellent Gangs of New York, Departed is loaded with colorful characters that have extended backgrounds and deal in elaborate plots. But more important than that textbook Cinema 101 drivel, this movie is violent, very very violent, particularly in its Hail Mary ending. The story rotates around two men from the similar backgrounds who end up on different sides of the law, though both undercover. Billy Costigan (Total Eclipse's Leonardo DiCaprio) is an undercover police officer whose posing as a worker in an Irish crime syndicate lead by Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). At the same time Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) becomes a member of the Massachusetts State Police Task force on organized crime, though reporting to Costello. The rest of the movie rolls out mostly as you would expect. It doesn't really become too surprising until the shocking end, which though predictable, is shocking nonetheless. 

But what really propels this movie is the acting. Nicholson, as the dangerous Costello, wasn't onscreen nearly enough, but when he showed he was hardcore. Ray Winstone co-starred as Costello's deranged right hand man Mr. French, which was very reminiscent of his great role as Gal Dove in Sexy Beast. Martin Sheen pulled off the concerned Police Captain Queenan well, though his death was not the best. Even Marky Mark Walhberg, who's acting I abhor, and Alec Baldwin, whose voice is great, graced the film, albeit brief, but profound moments. 

But the two that everyone came to see were Damon and DiCaprio. Both pulled off their characters perfectly, Damon as the all around golden boy with a dark secret and DiCaprio as the drop out who wants to help. The only problem I had with them was their love interest plotline with the luscious Vera Farmiga's Madolyn, who though well-acted, was a wasted character in the film. She falls for both lead characters, and spins a web of connection between the two that really never materializes and is somewhat of a pointless addition to the plot. Scorsese should've cut out all the scenes with her clothes on, and saved the film a good 30 minutes. This would've appeased me at least. 

Overall great movie, which won 4 Oscars, but long. If you're a Scorsese fan, this is a no-brainer. If you loved Casino, rent it. If you loved Alice Doesn't Live Here Anymore, best steer away.","f4e88619-2596-4175-9c25-afd202e590b9",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2010-06-11","","RT","904376207"
