5.0,"Irish mob, Irish cops. At the time people said Scorsese got the statue for other films. I don't think so. The Departed is every inch a great film. What stands out is how the imported story gets re-scripted into the American Irish idiom. Being a hybrid-Irish crazyman myself I can relate to these Boston Irish sorting their way through layers of deception and betrayal as the whole nest unravels. Remarkable is basing a decent chunk of Nicholson's character on true-life bad guy Whitey Bulger. As always, Scorsese grounds his stories in the real world, and they are richer for it. The story is airtight, a beast of a script, muscular, yet lean, all those wonderful things we look for in great drama. The Catholic layout of sin and betrayal is in evidence, neatly layered in. This time around I saw oodles of associations and connections I'd missed, such as when Vera remarks ""And I thought I was lying."" Deception is the theme here, and Di Caprio's is the most problematic as his is self-deception, the most dangerous kind, and he pays the biggest price. As always, Crime Doesn't Pay, and yet in this film the message runs deeper. The story exists in a world characterized by the statement ""We live in a nation of rats"". This refers to gangsters as well as pedophilic priests, as well as friends and family who lie to each other to protect what they fear losing. This blanket statement reflects the point of view of the film insofar as everyone in the film lies to some extent. The psychiatrist, the health-centered figure at the heart of the film, discusses lying from her first scene, and specializes in her own style of lying. It's a style that is the least on the price-to-pay rung of lie-telling, but it's still lying. Everyone lies, and everyone pays the price might be what the film is getting at, and when it reaches systemic levels, when our police forces and government protection agencies, such as the FBI, are engaged in wholesale deceptions, it's no wonder that the little guys get chewed
up in the machinery. So much more than a cop movie, more than political nose-thumbing, rather a telling mirror of our modern era, and how technology, mere cell phones and data tracing, are erasing our ability to lie, even as we use them to perpetuate the malfunctioning systems that employ and govern us. This hardly touches on performances and editing and smoothness of execution, but needless to say, we love it. Hugely funny too.","8517c411-b77a-404b-95d4-8b00491bb0d3",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-03","","RT","799738945"
