5.0,"Based on the Hong Kong Thriller, Infernal Affairs 

Both sides of the law, the Boston Police and the Irish Mafia, go head to head in an all out battle that could leave any of them in a departed state. Undercover cops are out to infiltrate this Irish mob, while a young criminal by the name of Colin Sullivan is infiltrating the Boston police as an informant. You watch them obsessively go at each other in trying to gain as much information on them without succumbing to the reality of death. But, what any of them don't know, is that anyone on either side of the law could betray them and instantly take them out without even realizing it. Sacrifices are made, betrayal is within the grasp of causing paranoia, and death, well death is just a shadow away. 2006, the year Martin Scorsese was finally honored with the prestige he has been long overdue. Winner of the Academy Award for Best Picture, The Departed is such a superb gangster flick, that the motion picture is one of the few mobsters flicks to psychologically grind within your skin. It is so deep in its storytelling that I had to watch it twice in order to be able to gain every single piece of content in this picture. Scorsese will go down in the record books with Francis Ford Coppola as one of the great masterminds of mobster movies. Forever will I remember this as one of the great mobster movies ever made. Just like he did in Goodfellas and Casino, Scorsese uses high profile actors to go along with a story that features brutal and gritty violence. Not to also mention the deepness and authenticity that goes along with the story. The filming locations are not to be left out as well, for they show us that it is not just about what story you are making, but where you are filming the story. Everything in this movie is top notch: the writing, directing, and the grittiness of the violence to go along with it.","98e5eb17-eab6-47ca-93d0-0c0b1e2266ef",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-11","","RT","904387507"
