5.0,"A tale of deception, betrayal, sacrifice and pride. The film is set in Boston where a crime boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) plants Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) as an informant within the State Police. Whilst the State Police assign an undercover cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) to infiltrate Costello's crew. This proves difficult for both men in living a lie, but the stakes rise when both men must find out the other's true identity before being discovered themselves. The Departed is a remake of a Hong Kong film Internal Affairs. I've never seen this film so can't compare the two. What I can about The Departed is that it's stylishly shot by Martin Scorsese and the screenplay written by William Monahan is rife with testerone. The films boasts a stellar cast including Vera Farmiga, Martin Sheen, Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin & Ray Winstone all giving solid performances. Nicholson was brilliant as the vile yet seeming to be untouchable Costello, but I felt the performances by Damon and DiCaprio were particularly impressive. The struggle which both their characters had to deal with maintaining a facade which their lives were on the line for, never became melodramatic or far fetched. Although the soundtrack seemed religiously for those who love the Rolling Stones, it never got tiresome and was pretty good. I enjoyed the backdrop of Boston, and references to culture and religion which made the film gritty and somewhat believable. Martin Scorsese is quickly becoming my favourite director, he definitely deserved landing an Oscar for this film which is a Modern Day Classic.","2a4c6b82-6c65-4c49-8536-79d29aab1003",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-13","","RT","901382412"
4.0,"Leonardo DiCaprio is just getting better with age.","5e0fcc9b-cb09-44cd-9969-326443d2df41",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-07-13","","RT","805680011"
4.5,"engrossing and intense thriller, charecters are real and full of moraly gray choices, as opposed to black and white decisions.","a0bb609a-a59d-482d-99cf-3ce1901a7cf4",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-07-14","","RT","904381537"
4.5,"The Basics: So there's one cop who's a mole for the mob, and there's one non-cop who's a mole for the police.  Then they tiptoe around each other trying to figure out who's the mole in the police department so the police can arrest the mob boss.  And then there are twists and turns that confound and confuse.  

What's the Deal?: Sometimes, as a director, you'll make a lot of good movies, and become a legend, and then people will take you for granted.  Then you'll make a lot of crappy movies that no one really notices, and people think you're done.  Then you'll make a really awesome gangster movie, and then you're back.  That's probably how Martin Scorsese must feel.  And believe me, this movie is pretty awesome.  

Awesome awards: Mark Wahlberg for being a total jerkoff who is really believable in his relatively minor role, Alec Baldwin for being that guy he is on 30 Rock with a Boston accent, Matt Damon for being totally unlikeable, and Jack Nicholson for being able to play Jack playing this character without it seeming too ridiculous.  

The Verdict on Leonardo DiCaprio: Look, he's a good actor and all, but he looks so young.  He's not believable as someone who's any older than 20.  Seriously, watching him try and grow facial hair is funny enough to totally delegitemize his performance.  I'm going to call this the Joseph Gordon-Levitt rule, and it applies to all of his performances as well.  Bottom line: Inception's going to be very interesting.  

How to watch this movie: First on DVD, then on cable to watch how they remove all the swears.  Even my sister figured it out after 10 minutes.

Final Verdict: Really good, really fun, and really intriguing.  All of the actors are great, and it's so much fun to watch.  It sucks you in really easily, and it doesn't let you go until the end.  A-.","7e6ddcaa-9341-4f98-a4aa-3d2fa27217c7",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-07-14","","RT","901389253"
