4.5,"8.6/10

    The Departed is an ingenious crime drama from Martin Scorsese. Being only the second film that Ive watched to be directed by Mr. Scorsese, Id say that Im essentially ready to explore more of his ingenious works. The Departed often feels like many things. It can feel like both an intoxicating crime drama and a violent chase thriller at the same time. It can also feel like a completely new film all together that deserves to be remembered in every possible way. And finally, The Departed feels like a very dedicated love letter from director Martin Scorsese. What Martin seems to understand is that you can make a violent, entertaining crime drama, but you can almost never make it incredibly unique. Well, since he understands it so well, hes obviously one of the only directors out there who makes films in times such as this, and still manages to make most of them nothing short of awesome. Mr. Scorsese manages to combine superb acting with solid thrills and never-ending perfection in order to create a film that can only truly be called one thing: A stunning, thoroughly entertaining masterpiece. I like the way that Scorsese details all his characters to seemingly have something in common and its our job to discover what that thing is. I also admire the fact that Scorsese was game for having a song such as Shipping out to Boston in his film. Any movie with such a fine song as that is essentially something rather special on its own right. So basically, Im saying that this is an absorbing piece of cinema. I truly loved it, and it is note perfect filmmaking. Its the rare crime drama/thriller that really works on all fronts.

           So theres this boy. Hes just a boy, and thats whats so damn interesting about him. His name is Colin, and at a young age he is introduced to crime via a man named Frank Costello. Frank is a ruthless, sadistic and tricky mobster. Colin is influenced heavily, but in the end grows up to become a police man who informs Mr. Costello of whats going on (such as the plans and progress) in his work. Enter a failed cop figure by the name of William, who is asked by the force to work as a sort of undercover agent. His job is to befriend Costello and his gang, and uncover certain information through his relationship with the mob. This leads to William experiencing murders and deaths, some of which are caused by him alone. Soon, William seems to be experiencing the effects of anxiety and (possibly) depression. He confronts a psychiatrist, who happens to be Colins former girlfriend. The two spark a romance, although for a while the rat doesnt have a clue. I need not spoil anything else. Its far too complex and rich for that. Essentially, its one of the best crime dramas that Ive seen in a long time. Hell, it may just be the best ever. Then again, I have yet to see Goodfellas or even The Godfather (I know shame on me). I admired Scorseses characters the most. They were all so well fleshed out, and they pretty much fit in perfectly with the setting. This is also a very well written piece of work as well. Its a perfectly paced film that will have your heart racing at one point, and have it beating harder another. So basically, this is not to be missed. Hence why I didnt spoil the goddamn thing.

          Scorsese once again scores big with Leonardo DiCaprio. It seem as if their collaborations just keep getting better and better (Shutter Island wasnt too shabby, to be honest). Among all the films Ive seen Leonardo DiCaprio act in, this is among my favorites. For once, its a very, very good film. Plus, DiCaprio knows how to summon dramatic energy out of thin air half of the time, as well as straight forward intensity. Expect nothing less from ol Leo boy here. Hes at his best. Matt Damon (while nothing strictly unique) is still as good as ever, whether you like him or not. Luckily, hes pretty damn convincing as an Irish man. Now Jack Nicholson is as convincing as ever. Nicholson essentially steals the show every time you see him. His voice, his character, and his performance combined are incredibly endearing, believable, and border-line powerful. Plus, hes often times quite funny. Even Mark Wahberg is able to show just how much potential he has in a supporting role. Genius! The supporting cast is pretty damn colorful as well. Alec Baldwin, Ray Winstone, Martin Sheen, and Vera Farmiga also co-star in this gripping hell of a thriller.

         The Departed is set (interestingly enough) in Boston. Whats even more interesting is the film itself (as you could probably notice). The Departed is essentially a tour-de force throughout. Its violent, perfectly paced, and relentlessly entertaining. It never slows down to catch up with the audience, not that it speeds by too fast to begin with. First off, being a violent thriller is sure to loose a bit of the audience. Some people just dont like violence, period. None the less, the core audience will like it anyways. Any film enthusiast will probably notice how dedicated this film is. It feels much like Scorseses homage to past successful crime thrillers. It is partially inspired by Internal Affairs, to the point where it is almost a remake of the film. Nevertheless, it takes a masterful director to make such a satisfactory production come to life such as this. Now lets get to the heart of this thing. The production design for this film is pretty darn impressive. Scorseses recreation of Boston is one of the best yet (in my book), and he surely put a lot of heart into it. Second of all, the music in this film was also pretty incredible. I mean, Im Shipping Out to Boston- Dropkick Murphys is just plain ingenious. It is a film set in Boston after all. Aside from that very awesome and pleasing song, the licensed soundtrack was pretty damn good. The original score is also pretty solid as well (if not a bit boring at times.but then again I have to give it another listen), featuring radical and satisfactory tunes. And lastly, the themes of The Departed are pretty good as well. The theme of the film seems to deal with identity and morality as well as relationships between (blood-wise) unrelated characters. The identity piece becomes obvious in time, as well as the morality part, but the relationship aspect of the film is all too clear from the beginning. Its up to the viewer to decide just how awesome each of these themes is, but my interpretation has already been explained. So Ill leave it at that.
 
           I loved The Departed. In the end, I saw it as a thought provoking, intelligent hell of a crime thriller. Its even quite dramatic at times (hence why I refer to it as a crime drama various times). Nevertheless, it is a superbly acted, flawless film by all means. I mean, what flaws can this film possibly have? Tell me. I dont think most people can muster good reasoning, if they were to dislike the film. But then again, there are some people who think most films featuring any form of action violence are essentially brainless all together. But I must warn you. Those people are most likely loons. Stay away from them, for gods sake. Itll be worth your while if you do. Well, I think thats enough for tonight. See The Departed when possible, because you flat out wont regret that you experienced it in the end. Youre essentially bound to enjoy yourself.","9bf581fc-ee3a-4a81-bea6-9515ab6aeb8f",False,True,False,False,4.5,"2010-07-30","","RT","901286764"
3.5,"It's all about rats. Rats here, rats there..

HUGE fans of DiCaprio.. damn him and his good choice of movies..","3df8c97d-ec33-47dd-b9c7-f381c0a3bec7",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2010-07-30","","RT","797319937"
4.0,"Ive no doubts that Leonardo DiCaprio is probably the most important actor of our generation. Not only has he proved us more than one time that he has a natural talent for acting, but he has a thing of choosing interesting projects, characters, and directors. He has now officially become Robert De Niros succesor in being Martin Scorseses muse, I dare to say also one of the most important directors of the past generation that is making his way through the new decades. Personally, he is not my favorite director (ore one of my favorites, in that case), as Im not very fond of his recurring themes (especially the ones in this newer films) and the genres he covers, or rather the way that he does it. As I said, in previous reviews, the action genre is probably my least favorite, but Scorsese managed to create a story and an environment that, cutting off all the explosions, gunshots, deaths, and car chases, could capture me inside the essences, motives, and emotions of the characters and the situations, something pretty unsual for a film like this.

Definitely, the highlight of the films and its most important feature is its story. Adapted from a foreign film that was adapted from a novel, the story is not original by any means (weve seen tons of mafia films before) but its told in a way that definitely is. Two persons, both from different sides of the law (the mafia and the police) are infiltrated by the leaders of their respective companies into the opposite one. This leads to a person from the mafia working inside the police department, and a cop working inside the drug deals. This creates a very unique and extremely interesting mouse-and-cat game. Treachery, lies, embodiment, lose of identity for both the characters and the audience, confusion and questioning. One thing that this movie can do is that it keeps go interested and it never bores you. A deep drama or just a mafia blockbuster; the movie can go as deep as you wish to, and thats something I always appreciate.

The acting is magnificent here. From an all-male ensemble cast (except for Vera Farmiga in her breakout role. Not a bad way to enter the industry), everyone stands out as his own character, and I dare to say no one is better than anybody. Sure, the on-screen time is different for everyone, but the effort, embodiment, and acting work is there. 
Leo DiCaprio delivers another great, deep, emotion-moved performance as he usually does. I dont think Ive seen him done a role where he doesnt delivers himself into in the last decade, or probably more. Matt Damon also gives a very strong performance as the bad guy who is working for the good guys, but also suffers a very dramatic change of tides near the end. My personal favorite has to be Mr. Jack Nicholson, who, being the incarnation of evil, as he described it, still manages to keep himself entirely likeable, and charming throughout the story. I could never get to hate him. 
	The rest of the cast also does a remarkable job, from Mark Wahlberg, to Martin Sheen, to Alec Baldwin.  This movie is entirely based on its performances, and without them no other elements could have worked.

The music is good, the cinematography is quite impressive, and the direction is flawlessly taken. For others this could be the perfect movie, and I understand why. It got Scorsese the Oscar for Best Direction, as well as for Best Picture. Though if it was based on this movie alone or a recollection of all his past merits, Ive doubts about that. 
	The Departed is an undeniably interesting, flawlessly done movie. The performances are superb as well as the way the story was handled. Again, is not a type of movie that I personally would call a favorite, but I cant deny its successes. One of the best handled action films Ive seen, this movie proved to me that, just as any of the characters inside the story, no everything is quite what is seems","342fb613-5867-478f-8790-662e81335d38",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-07-30","","RT","901354041"
5.0,"It took 30 years and it may seem like a billion films, but Martin Scorsese has finally won an Oscar for Best Director. He won it for The Departed, which is, in my opinion, the greatest crime film of all time. An Boston cop/mob crime saga, it follows the stories of two undercover agents from the opposite sides, as they slowly close in on each other. Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is the undercover cop, as he positions himself closer and closer to the top in the criminal empire of Irish mobster Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). Along the way, he figures out that there must be a rat in the Boston PD leaking info back to Costello. Little does he know that it is the golden boy of the SI unit, Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon). As Costigan receives guidance from IA head Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sgt. Dignam (Mark Wahlberg), he feeds critical information to the cops as they come closer and closer to Costello, as Sullivan secretly undermines them every step of the way. DiCaprio continued to validate himself throughout the 2000s, and he goes a long way in doing that is Costigan. Damon is as good as usual, blasting out a Boston accent he hasn't used since Good Will Hunting. Nicholson continues his excellence of the past four decades, giving out his best recent role in a long time. Wahlberg (aka Marky Mark) gives out his best acting job ever, and fully earns that Oscar nomination with his thick Boston accent. I know that when it comes to crime films, others will pick the Godfather or Goodfellas, but the cop vs. mob saga is what makes me put The Departed over the top.","afe4d947-1cf7-418d-af7b-c6f1f3a0e1c9",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-30","","RT","884729337"
5.0,"Another perfect crime-epic by Scorcese, and probably better than Goodfellas, The Departed features a surprising plot, amazing performances by all leads (and in my  opinion Leonardo Dicaprio's best work), The Departed is a gritty but perfect crime film","3f62a322-7304-46af-b1f5-ff9b50725ffe",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-30","","RT","902095231"
4.0,"While it remains oddly detached in emotion, this Scorsese film is intriguing, smart and brings out the best in some of the actors. Nicholson's role as the sadistic mob boss Costello is the highlight of the movie. Wahlberg however, is almost pathetically bad, and leaves me to wonder how the hell he garnered a nomination for best supporting actor. If you're a fan of Scorsese (I'm not afraid to say that, while I enjoy his work, I was never a die hard fan, and I'm still not) then it'll fit the bill.","03dc3327-9525-419c-936e-b7c319d953d5",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-07-30","","RT","860033645"
