5.0,"This my favorite movie of the last decade. Of course ""The Departed"" won Best Picture so I won't say it's underrated, but I think it's been almost universally misunderstood. 

At heart this movie is a comedy. It's a parody of.. well.. Scorcese pictures. It's done so slyly and competently that people think they're watching a regular mob picture. They like it but then, later, when they think back on the movie, doubts start to creep in. Wasn't Nicholson a little too hammy? Wasn't that rat crawling on the windowsill too ""on-the-nose""?  Boy, there sure were an awful lot of coincidences. Everyone but Marky Mark dies? EVERYONE? Scorcese is using the Stones ""Gimme Shelter"" AGAIN? 

They begin to doubt the movie. They think these are flaws in a realistic gangster picture. They're not, this is a comedy. It's over-the-top on purpose. Don't believe me? Watch/rewatch ""After Hours"" which establishes the subtly dark Scorcese humor and then sink deeper with ""The King of Comedy"". But especially rewatch ""Cape Fear"", another remake like ""The Departed"", that deconstructs itself as it plays.

If ""The Departed"" is approached as a dark send-up of the crime genre, which I contend this movie is, it will make more sense and you will see a much different, better movie. And funnier. Nicholson is pulling a human hand out of a ziplock for crying out loud! Scorcese is saying ""I can do what Tarantino does, subvert the genre, and I can do it better and throw in some old school suspense while I'm at it."" Personally, I think the old man beat Tarantino at his own game. 5 solid stars. 100% Tomatoes.","08bcd226-a8e5-4916-8899-72ae941bf615",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-08-12","","RT","901395601"
