5.0,"For anyone who doesn't know, I f**cking love Martin Scorsese.  He's probably gotten more 100%'s from me than any director (Mean Streets, Taxi Driver, Raging Bull, Goodfellas, The Aviator & This).  When I heard in 2006 that he was doing another mob movie I just about flipped my lid.  I was so excited.  When it was finally realeased I couldn't believe what I just saw.  Not only did I think The Departed was easily his best film since Goodfellas, I knew and still think that it's the best film of the 2000s.

The story was adopted from the modern chinese classic Internal Affairs.  The plot is shifted to Boston which it seems thanks to this movie is now the setting for every modern crime drama.  Irish mob boss Frank Costello rules Boston with an iron fist and knows the cops are trying to get him.  His solution, find a kid, help him out and then when he grows up make him cop so that you have a guy on the inside.  When that kid named Colin Sullivan finally gets into the police force as one of the cops trying to hunt Frank down, Billy Costigan is hired.  Billy grew up in the good part of town with his mom, but his down still lived in Frank's turf and his uncle even worked with Frank which made him perfect to try to get him into Frank's team.  He's sent undercover and remains there for years.  The rest of the film is pretty much about the two rats trying to figure out who the other rat is.  The plot is incredibly good.  It's intense and really keeps you on the edge of your seat.  A lot of the twists are clever and the movie as a whole has a lot more appeal to the average film goer than other Scorsese movies liek Mean Streets or Goodfellas which have very little plot.

The film's script is actually dead on perfect.  It's got all the Boston slang right, the cop terms, the angry rants, the psychotic rages, the mundane conversations, it's amazing how pefect it is.

The film's acting is topnotch.  Leonardo DiCaprio plays Billy Constigan in what I consider the best performance by the greatest actor of his generation.  He plays a stressed out, insomniac, who is paranoid to the point of insanity and he does it so amazingly I can't even describe it.  How he wasn't nominated for an oscar for this is beyond me.  Matt Damon plays Colin Sullivan and he's pretty good but takes a more laid back approach.  Whenever the false identity drives DiCaprio crazy, Damon keeps it inside.  It's also kind of cool to see him as a villain which is very out of character for him.  Jack Nicholson plays Frank Costello.  It's nothing new for Nicholson, a crazy guy, but he does the part very well.  Matt Damon has the defining part of his career as the hot headed but dedicated cop Dignam.  Another one that I can't believe he didn't win an oscar.  The rest of the cast is all great with notable performances by Martin Sheen, Vera Farmiga & Ray Winstone.

The film's music is pretty awesome aswell.  It has what you'd expect from a Martin Scorsese movie, Rolling Stones & 60s Pop, but it also has some other good uses of songs like Comfortably Numb and the films perfectly suited theme There Shipping Me Off To Boston.  The film also unlike most Scorsese movies has a musical score.  For some reason it's all Spanish Flamenco guitar & elevator music for.  It's bizarre but works surprisingly well.

The movie's editing is amazing as always.  Like Goodfellas & Casino before it the entire movie is made up of a lot of short scenes that don't have a whole lot to do with eachother and for them to flow this seemlessly well is quite a feat.

I think there's way to get by it, Martin Scorsese it arguablt the best director of all time and this is one of his best directed movie and won him that long deserved oscar.  The camerawork is fantastic.  It combines his signature tracking shots with some genuinely cool looking shots, and does it all with the precision and control you wouldn't expect from a crime drama.  Most of them now all use that handheld kind of stuff to make it more ""realistic"" but I like it this way better.  The hand held stuff is ripped off of Scorsese anyway from Mean Streets.  Simply put The Departed is the best directed movie of the 2000s.

The film also has the fantastic theme of Identity.  Nobody has the Identity they want.  It drives Billy crazy not to be able to bust these guys he watch murder and steal everyday, and it drives Damon crazy not to be able to just bust a cap in Dignam's face, but they have to keep cool.  If you go back and watch Goodfellas you'd see that Henry Hill wantd to be nothing but a gangster.  He wanted the glamour, the fortune, the life, but in this movie Billy is the exact opposite.  He hates all of it.  Where Henry wanted to be a gangster, Billy wanted to be a cop, but he's stuck with the other.  It really shares more with Mean Streets.  In Mean Streets Charlie doesn't really want to be a gangster but he's born into it and it's just expected to.  Sort of like how Billy's father and uncle were involved and that's how they got Billy in.  I think there's no doubt that all three have something in common, but I think all 3 should be watched and compared for full enjoyance of all.  There almost like a trilogy.

All in all I can't recommend The Departed enough to anyone.  It's got everything you would want.  It's action packed, suspenseful, tragic, realistic, well acted, well directed, well everything.  It's just simply one of the greatest movies I've evers seen.","78d4480e-b4ff-4eed-941e-1103f1a24583",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-08-18","","RT","901176202"
