1.5,"A completely uninspired and miscast film from a very overrated filmmaker. It might just be the worst mystery movie that I've seen since Christopher Nolan's predictable ""Memento"", but here I didn't have to stare at pretty boy Guy Pearce with no shirt on for half the film. 

The film follows two rats in the mob and the police department (played by DiCaprio and Damon, respectively) and their colleagues who are too busy fighting over the Best Supporting Actor Oscar to actually develop as characters. Plenty of annoying twists ensue and lead up to one of the worst plot turns in the history of film. I guess I won't ruin it for you, but I will say that the writer(s) obviously threw it in at the last second.

Nearly everything about this film bugs the hell out of me. The editing makes me nauseous because of its frantic cuts, and Leanardo DiCaprio is horribly unconvincing as a thug. In addition to that casting disaster, Matt Damon clones his character from the slightly better Bourne Trilogy, and Jack Nicholson makes it hard to believe that he once shined in films like ""Wolf"". 

It is clear that Scorsese struggled with the direction of this movie, perhaps due to his age. I wouldn't be surprised if he hired a stand-in or something like that, because that would explain why it doesn't attain the barely above-average quality of his yarn ""Goodfellas"". 

I only recommend this movie to die hard Scorsese fans, as many bipartisan viewers would simply be perplexed by the film. I give it an extra 20 percent for Mark Wahlberg's decent performance and the fact that it is a somewhat better movie than Scorsese's other thriller ""Taxi Driver"".","407f9a33-1d29-472c-af17-ec809fda0b6a",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2010-08-27","","RT","904938371"
