4.5,"For Fans Of: Boston (the city), Scorsese, gangster flicks
Date Night: Nope
Art Factor: Medium
Fun Factor: High
Emotional Factor: Low
Intelligence Required: Medium
Essential Viewing: Yes

The Plot: Gangsters in Boston try and out flank cops hot on the trail and behind enemy lines

My Take: So here we find the the picture that finally put an end to Scorsese's Oscar curse.  As you will see I did not give this a 100 rating even though it is the best picture winner from my second favorite director and marked it as essential viewing.  Why, you might be wondering?  Well, ole Michael well tell you.  When I give out scores I do not use the same criteria for the final score for all movies.  In most cases I use a director's body of work as a comparision to the current film.  Judd Apatow is never gonna have to face Martin Scorsese in my mind for a rating.  With The Departed, it came down to how would I rank this in general against other Scorsese films and in a sub evaluation, against Scorsese's other Gangster outtings.  I for the life of me could not rate this higher than Goodfellas.  Goodfellas is Scorsese's 100 gangster film.  But don't let any of this take away from The Departed.  Start with Scorsese himself.  The director knows just when to let that camera be patient and wait, almost linger a second long on a particular shot for added weight.  But he also knows when it is time to drop the hammer and break out the quick cuts, immpecable dolly shots and his always plotted to perfection steady cam layouts.  The soundtrack is great, just not on the same level as Casino or Goodfellas.  Remember - I still said great.  The script is water tight like a duck's ass.  Our two leads, DiCaprio and Damon, are in top form, with Damon outshinig Dicaprio by a hair.  When you see the film you will understand how big a compliment that is to Damon and in no way a slight on DiCaprio.  The supporting cast is where this thing goes from your ""average great"" cast to something really special.  Hall of Famer (seriously, we need a motion picture Hall of Fame) Jack Nicholson is a treat playing the heavy.  In a career out of making us laugh and frightening the shit out of us, all in the same role most of the time, this is one of his best and easily along with his performance in About Schmidt rank as his best work since As Good As It Gets.  The rest of the supporters, Baldwin, Whalberg, Sheen, etc. all come out to do Boston proud.  There is no reason to not see this film.  Hell, if you haven't maybe you should reconsider saying ""I'm into film"" for a bit, or until you do.  A classic.","2d52bef1-ce1e-4a3f-bef7-149dd92ddeea",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-09-04","","RT","905210325"
