4.5,"Review #400 Oscar Winner, Great film all-around, Drama, Crime-Thriller

""This is abosuletly, with-out-a-doubt, without question, one of the best crime films ever, if not a great film all around. Leo isn'; the greatest actor, but I guess Scorsese makes a man out of him!""

I know I always rag on Leonardo, but for some odd reason, he does his best acting in a Scorsese film. He really works well with the rest of these good actors. ""Martin Sheen, Mark Whalberg,Matt Damon"", to name a few. Nicholson was good also.

I can't stress enough on how well this film was made. Vera Farmiga was superb in her role(Although she did better in Up In The Air)This film was adapted from a forgien material source(Internal Affairs). I have to  check up ont hat before I can start compring the two.

""The Departed is a slick, and witty Crime Drama, about mob, and police tensions in South Boston, but even more so, a character study into the lives of an undercover agent, a mole, his mob boss, and the way they interwine. It even gives us a suspenseful, and dramtic conclusion. This is a must see for everyone, and unlikee some films, that are unappealing, and headturning, this film will only grab you into the film, and not let you out until the very end.""

Trust me. This film is awesome.","c1ba9c86-526b-430c-83ed-ee18dec92cac",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-09-27","","RT","901339138"
5.0,"Not only is this a well made motion picture directed by one of my favorite film makers, but it is filled with nothing but pure TESTOSTERONE","ba66ac72-a076-49e4-9b8a-5a715273b263",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-09-27","","RT","905281111"
4.0,"From award winning director Martin Scorsese comes his finest film since Goodfellas, with a superb all star cast that includes the likes of Matt Damon, Leonardo DiCaprio and Jack Nicholson, the Departed is a superb police-gangster drama that is brilliantly scripted and wonderfully directed resulting in a hugely enjoyable and occasionally brutal drama.

Based on the Hong Kong story internal affairs, the plot concerns rookie cop Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is assigned to go deep undercover to help catch local Mafia godfather Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). Meanwhile, Costello has groomed Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) since puberty to be his spy on the force.

The acting is outstanding, Nicholson is in his best role for years, and he plays Frank Costello with considerable menace and skill any scene involving Nicholson is gripping. DiCaprio is also is his finest film with Scorsese. Matt Damon is great as the godfathers mole and DiCaprio is equally impressive, but its the magnificent supporting cast of Mark Wahlberg, Alec Baldwin and Ray Winstone who are quite clearly fit their characters perfectly and hugely enjoying their role. Mark Wahlberg is the finest actor in the film stealing every scene he is in as the VERY foul mouthed police sergeant. 

The actors are boosted by a cracking and compelling William Monahan script, it is intelligent and occasionally funny. Alec Baldwin has much of the comedy elements, as well as Mark Wahlberg who can be quite funny with his rather in your face approach to interviewing. There are moments of high tension, as each of the Boston police and the mafia getting ever so closer to finding out who is the mole in their organizations. DeCaprio successfully shows his fear about his chances of being found out. Cinematography, with some impressive and gloomy views of the dark areas of Boston, and character development is terrific as Matt Damons charms, somehow, win you over. 

The film has a few niggles, the film does, on very rare occasions tend to drag on a little bit, but these problems have a very little impact on the film. At a running time approximately two half hours the film is arguably a bit too long and most of the female characters play second fiddle in the story (apart from Vera Farmiga). 

Not for the faint hearted, with a huge number of swear words a large amount of violence, and brains being graphically blown apart, its not a surprise its an 18.  
 
Martin Scorsese finally won the award he truly deserved, an Oscar for best director, which he deserved for Goodfellas and Taxi driver but never won (he was not even nominated for best director for Taxi Driver.) If he continued to be nominated but never won he would have been of the greatest directors never to win an Oscar for best director.  The Departed was also the winner of Best Picture, Best Film Editing and Best Adapted Screenplay.

The Departed proves that Scorsese is not just the one of the best directors in the crime genre, but one of the best directors in general. Even Scorsese at only his half best he is quite clearly better than many directors at the highest point in their respective careers (Shutter Island is a good example.) 

This is a fantastic watch, not the best film of 2006 (that award does to Pans Labyrinth) but it one of the best of the year.

4/5.","6c2e49d3-f8cc-4da1-8dc2-7d508accd3cd",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-09-28","","RT","904833129"
