5.0,"I'm doing this review for an English project. As much as I hate my English class (seriously, nobody really needs English, I'm pretty sure everyone knows it) I'm really, really excited to do this review. Partly because this movie is brilliant, but partly because of how much I liked it, it's going to be a lot easier to get a good score. This movie is amazing all around.

The Departed is based on the Hong Kong movie, International Affairs. Now, I haven't it (although lately I've been dying to), but I've heard that Scorseses version tops it. I can definitely see why. The amount of work, the amount of effort put into this film is simply breathtaking. 

The story is FULL of twists and turns. It tells a very  though out, but yet complicated story, but I'm going to try and sum it up. Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is a 20-something guy, who trained to be a police officer, but because of his past, has to first be a mole in gangster/psycho Frank Costello's gang. Collin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is one of Frank Costello's moles, and is located in the same FBI agency where Billy Costigan is. Also in the mix are: Mark Whalberg, Anthony Anderson, David O Hara, Vera Farmiga, Alec Baldwin, and Ray Winstone.

The whole cast is excellent, and one of the main attractions of the movie. Leonardo DiCarprio is outstanding. He really demonstrates his acting chops here, his performance evokes emotion, and you can seriously tell he tried. Matt Damon, in one of his more villainous, evil roles is also outstanding. Even though you hate him, you're still cheering for him. He's very subtle, very interesting in the film, and it's pretty amazing how great of an actor he is.

Jack Nickolson plays a gangster, and you could not have picked a better person to play the character. He succeeds at seriously creeping you out. To me, it seems weird that Nicholson and Scorsese have never worked together. The fit almost perfectly. Jack Nicholson fits in flawlessley with his character, and it shows, because of how great the writing is.

The rest of the cast is also flawless. Vera Farmiga throughout the movie comes to actually know both Billy and Collin. With Collin, she has a relationship. They meet through work, and have chemistry, too. They start a relationship that is also very, important in the film. Billy knows her as his councilor, and later it transcends  into an affair that makes you go ohhhh crap!. 

My favorite character throughout the film, though, was Mark Whalberg. Every time his character was on screen, I found myself laughing to death. His character, assistant to director of the FBI, can make you laugh even in the most intense situations, and adds some MUCH needed comic relief to a film thats extremely serious. Also, with a couple cameos, are Anthony Anderson and David O Hara. Even though they had little screen time, they added a bit of flair to the film. The acting overall is outstanding, one of the main reasons to see the film. Scorsese could not have picked a more talented, excellent cast.

And yet, another brilliant part of the Departed is the writing and dialogue. I believe that a good actor can take a bad script and make it as good as he can, but with a fantastic script and fantastic actors, the levels of great-ness sky-rocket. Screenwriters William Monahan, Siu Fai Mak and Felix Chong blend subtlety, action and emotion into a fantastic looking and tasting smoothie. You could not have asked for better writing. Seriously, it's one of the best examples of great writing in a film these years. 

The direction in the Departed is superb. Even though the plot has a bunch of twist and turns, there are zero holes. You can actually tell whats happening. Scorsese explains to you whats happening , whats going one throughout the film, without any weak links, without anything  getting  confusing. The plot is flawless, original, and smart. 

Yep! And some more bonuses: the cinematography and score. Every shot, every landscape, every detail, every action/suspense scene are beautifully and masterfully shot. You could not have asked for a more talented cinematographer. You can actually TELL whats happening (yeah, Im referring to Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen, Mr. Bay), and you  never take your eyes off the screen. The score, composed by Howard Shore, is nothing short of beautiful. It fits in with the action scenes, the love scenes, the brutal parts, and the dialogue scenes with perfection.

You know, I could go on and on and on about this movie. But I won't, because then I would run out of space, so I won't. To sum up everything. You need, desperately need to go and rent (well, actually buy) The Departed. With a flawless cast, perfect acting, smart, coordinated writing, excellent direction, bloody yet interesting violence, great cinematography and an superbly composed score, I'm sure you'll love the Departed. It's my favorite film of all time, and I'm sure it will be in your Top 5, even Top 3. I am in love with The Departed. Please! Do yourself a favor and see this, it is a cinematic triumph, from the mastermind Martin Scosese.","a6eec430-31db-42dd-93ea-8bde201c798d",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-09-30","","RT","901390340"
