4.0,"Huh, so this is what happened to Jimmy Hoffa. I guess if you can't beat the mafia, then you may as well join it, and then lead it, because when you're Jack Nicholson, nobody better mess with you, or else Johnny's gonna have to make an appearance. Man, he always played such a great crazy guy, so expect to get plenty of that craziness in this film, though maybe not as much as you might expect from looking at the title, which sounds like you're about to walk into a horror movie that's just riddled with those brought back from the dead. Well, needless to say that this film is definately not about that, because if it was, then they probably would have brought back to life acting material for Matt Damon, because although he's as good as he always is in the film, he hasn't had anything to do in forever, and a great actor like him deserves better. Seriously though, the point is that this film isn't about bringing careers back to life, and if is was, then it sounds like it probably should have come out after ""The Bucket List"", so maybe we would have had a chance of bringing Jack Nicholson's career back to life, because after that film, he did close to nothing, and after this film, he did stuff that close to no one saw, like ""The Bucket List"". Actually, come to think of it, maybe we should hold on to that film idea of bringing dead careers back to life, just in case, one day, Leo DiCaprio's and Martin Scorsese's collaborative partership cuts off, because as good as their movies together are, we can not afford to let this dream team die, and this film is further evidence of that. Still, as awesome as this film is, maybe it gets a little too hardcore for its own good, especially at that abysmal ending, which isn't to say that's the only time the film goes a little overboard.

Getting the biggest problem out of the way first, this film is plagued to no end by the fact that the editing is so glowingly good that it's bad, in that the editing will get too frantic and overly snappy to the point of leaving exposition to feel overwhelmingly rushed, and to add insult to injury, with all of the intensity in the aura that goes amplified by the rapid-fire editing, things get so agonizingly overbearing to the point of only diluting the intrigue. Sure, those overly overbearing moments are far and few between, but they are pretty bothersome; but hey, at least they're consistent with the film's tone, because another misstep that this film will fall into is the occasional overly slow sequence, a flaw that is not only bothersome by its own right, but in the midst of all of this unrelenting intrigue and tension, it's wildly inconsistent with the tone. The film is all over the place, and well, all of that overwhelming messiness is clearly a theme; it's just a very problematic one, and it's a them that faults the most where it matters most: the ending. The resolution - such as it is - is so messily overlong, being dragged to no end, and yet, it still leaves some glaring holes in the story. Still, what makes the ending dissatisfying the most is the bull that it pulls with the characters, who contradict themselves one after another, only to suffer fates so messily handled, so abrupt and so embarassing disrespectful to both the film and the audience that what should be an ""Ouch"" ending comes off as some kind of cruel parody. It's probably one of the worst, most embarassing mistakes in recent film memory, standing as one of those gut-churning moments where you look back and wonder why you put so much time and investment in these characters and this story, only for it to come down to this... and then you remember that you gave this film your investment to begin with because it's still a just plain awesome film, nevertheless. Yeah, I know, speaking of inconsistent, yet as much as this film faults, and embarassingly so at that, it hits so many more hight points, and hits them so hard that although you'll walk away with some disappointments in a few departments, at the end of the day, you still embrace the strengths thoroughly enough to see this as the truly excellent that it is, even from a technical standpoint.

Earlier, I stated that the film's editing will get so good that it's bad, in that it will get a little bit too frantic, but even with those occasional missteps, make no mistake, this is some top-notch editing. The film snaps and pops, and sure, there is the occasional seam, but more often than not, the jumps are air tight and fluid to where the shifts are slick, but palpable, to where you do know when the focus switches. The snap and pow in Thelma Schoonmaker's editing jolts and enthralls much more often than it overbears, supplementing both the frantic tension, as well as the entertainment value, which is also supplemented pretty thoroughly by Howard Shore's excellent score, matched only by the classic soundtrack, which is pretty darn awesome, the Van Morrison version of Pink Floyd's ""Comfortably Numb"" notwithstanding. Okay, I still kind of like Roger Water's version with Van Morrison, especially in how it fits into the film, but seriously, if this film's ending isnt' enough of an awesome testament to Scorsese's clearly being insane, who in their right mind would pick the Waters/Morrison version of ""Comfortably Numb"" over the awesome, slam-bang, actually makes you kind of numb original version? Of course, what keeps you with the film the most has got to be William Monahan's screenplay, and I know now is around the time where I say, ""even with its missteps"", but really, it's the execution of the script that's kind of spotty, whereas the script itself is consistently slam-bang, riddled with razor-sharp snappy dialogue and gritty, audacious intrigue, as well as mind-boggling complexities that engross and make tension all the tighter. The script is pretty blasted glowing, even if its execution faults here and there. However, as great as the script is, the film would not be as good as it is if its execution was nothing more than workmanlike with a few glaring missteps (*cough*Rid*cough*ley*cough*Scott*cough*), so although Scorsese makes more than a fair share of mistakes, more often than not, the boy knows how to deliver on that crime-thriller tension as well as he ever has.

Scorsese is not the greatest storyteller ever, something made clear as day with this spotty film, especially with that abysmal ending, but when the guy does deliver, oh man does he kill it dead, and sure enough, he pumps this film to the rim with hardcore intrigue and tension that rarely overbears, but still draws your investment in. The film is consistently tight in its atmosphere, boasting charm when it's not delivering grit, yet it never doesn't punch you with convolutions, and good ones at that; the ones that make the film all the more engaging, as well as rewatchable, as you just can't wait to go back and see all of the cool notes that you missed before. Of course, even with all of the offscreen talent, one of the biggest things that keep you coming back to see this film again and again is, of course, the onscreen skill from everyone in this star-studded cast. Whether it be Mark Whalberg's hardcore but justice-respecting Sean Dignam character, Alec Baldwin's authoritative but flawed George Ellerby character, or Vera Farmiga's clever but humanly imperfect Madolyn Madden character, every person in this cast is distinctive, engaging and memorable, and they all pull off some pretty good Boston accents on top of that, and yet, the real stars of the show are, well, the real stars of the show: the veteran Jack Nicholson, the always charming Matt Damon and, of course, the actor to end all actors himself, Mr. Leonardo DiCaprio. As I said, Nicholson always played a great crazy guy, and while his Frank Costello character is not off-the-wall, he's clearly disturbed, yet still clever enough to know what he's doing as a powerful threat, and as you would expect, Nicholson nails that character with his typical punch of charming but disturbing effectiveness as an antagonist. As for Damon, again, he is, as always, with very little to do, yet he still delivers on electric charm, as well as a still fairly palpable degree of compelling tension in his presence as this man that has fallen into the fine, comfortable life that he's always wanted, only to have his dark secrets threaten it all. Still, the most engaging character is easily the one who's under the spotlight the most: Billy Costigan Jr., and I'm not just saying that he's the most engaging character because he's being played by my all-time favorite actor; I'm saying that he's the most engaging because this film offers further evidence of why DiCaprio is my all-time favorite actor, for he, like always, delivers what is easily the best performance of the film (Clearly, he made the Oscars mad, because they have to hate him), effortlessly and emotionally drenching Costigan with the cleverness and desire for redemption that defines him, and he does it all while nailing Costigan's constant fear of death, shame and lord knows what else as he finds himself trapped in an impossibly horrifying situation that constantly slips into things more brutal than he ever imagined, so if you see this film for no other reason, then see it to for the powerful experience of watching Costigan go through this life-affecting dance of unrelenting anguish that no one could possibly nail as phenomenally as DiCaprio.

To close this case, the film occasionally hits a slight inconsistency in pacing and often hits overbearing points in its frantic style, and it all comes down to an embarassingly unrewarding ending; yet through all of those missteps, the film, as a whole, is thoroughly rewarding, boasting generally razor-sharp editing and an excellent soundtrack, as well as, most importantly, a snappy, audacious, complex and overall glowing screenplay that Martin Scorsese executes with constant grit and intrigue, while his colorful cast of star performers all deliver enthralling, memorable and charismatic performances, with Leonardo DiCaprio, in particular, killing it the most as he always does and helping greatly in making ""The Departed"" a generally wildly entertaining crime-thriller that you will walk away from remembering for its greatness far more than its missteps.

