3.5,"Scorsese finally gets his Oscar for a remake of the Hong Kong classic Infernal Affairs. Top director, top actors, great script, great source material... but somehow the film ends up being a bit less than the sum of it's parts. 

The first thing you notice about The Departed is the amazing cast. Leo DiCaprio and Matt Damon take the leads as two opposite number cops with shady backgrounds. It's one of Damon's best performances to date, while DiCaprio is intense, as always, without really pushing himself. It could be argued that the support cast overshadow the leads by times. Particular credit goes to Ray Winstone who plays the part of a badass geezer gangster (a role he seems to relish) and Markey Mark Wahlberg who puts in a great shift as an overbearing alpha male dtective. Wahlberg steals every scene he is in. It is perhaps a shame that he doesn't feature more in the film. 
Jack Nicholson plays the villain. While it is clear that he loved every minute of it, his character is a bit too 'larger than life', to the point where he comes across as being cartoonish. There is no real rhyme or reason to anything he does. He just seems to be an asshole for the sake of being an asshole. He does not so much chew up the scenery as he does devour the entire film reel leaving the audience scared and confused, but not in a good way.

The plot to The Departed is full of twists and turns which leave you guessing and keep you engaged, which is impressive as the film clocks in at about two and a half hours. The pacing is very good and the film never really feels dragged out.
One thing which took a lot away from the film was over the top symbolism which takes away much more from the film than it ads. Not least when the camera zooms in slowly whenever the word 'Departed' appears on screen. You jnow, just in case you forgot which film you were watching. It is also very patronising that Scorsese seemed to have some sort of rodent fetish while making the film, jamming in images of rats at every opportunity. To show us that the film is about rats (the two legged kind) he shows us rats (the four legged kind). This is in addition to the fact that the word 'rat' is said exactly 64 (number made up) times in the film. One particularly infuriating part is the final shot of the film, which is so unnesessary it feels like the director is hitting you on the head with a large mallet saying, ""Do you get it yet dumbass?!?!?!?"". A shame, since the ending sequence is actually a very cool scene (bar the shameless symbolism of course).

Do not get me wrong The Departed is overall a good film, it's just not the classic a wave of Oscar 'for your consideration' style hype made it out to be.","1edd1ea5-1ec7-4f47-a15f-ea9df913b001",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2010-12-26","","RT","855623049"
5.0,"just amazing if you enjoyed American gangster and road to perditon you well love this film","a8d112cc-f090-43d9-889e-3ab07a7ba28a",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-12-27","","RT","898319276"
5.0,"The Departed"" is by far my favourite film of 2006! It's absolutely brilliant. It marks a return to form for Jack Nicholson, too long has he languished in rom-com hell! Jack's Back! Boy, is he EVER! With by far the best lines of the movie and he delivers them with such relish. But it's by no means Jack's movie, Departed is strictly an ensemble piece. Leonardo Di Caprio is captivating, putting in an anguished, haunted performance of a tragic figure. Matt Damon puts in his best performance since ""Talented Mr Ripley"", with a character that is completely immoral and self-serving it is easy to loathe him. Even Jack Nicholson, the villain of the piece, is more sympathetic than Matt Damon's character. Mark Wahlberg has a ball with his character delivering his lines with aplomb and even Martin Sheen puts in a stellar performance. Vera Farmiga, easily recognisable from ""The Manchurian Candidate"", puts in a good performance of the female lead. (Lucky girl, getting to kiss both Matt and Leo!!!) The Script is excellent, the accents impeccable and the soundtrack, Oh man, the SOUNDTRACK!! Brilliant! The Dropkick Murphys are a perfect accompaniment to all the Irish gangsters. All in all, Marty Scorsese delivers what is his best since ""Goodfellas"".","90eb1d15-03ac-47d0-a465-9bed23637664",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-12-28","","RT","788601893"
