4.5,"With so many Ace cards dealt to the audience in this hand (A-list cast, legendary director, critically-hailed source material), filmgoers might have expected The Departed to be a stacked deck much like recent high-profile flops Black Dahlia and All the King's Men. Instead, the gambit results in a winner-takes-all 4-of-a-kind viewing experience. The film lays down the action like a perfectly played card that manages to trump the scene before with even MORE quotable lines and intrigue. The performances pack amazing heat as well. The film doesn't rank among director Martin Scorsese's best (Taxi Driver, Raging Bull, Goodfellas), but screenwriter William Monahan does the film on which it was based one better, amazingly wringing some great laughs out of an out-and-out gangster film. The rest just falls into perfect cadence. 

In Scorsese's R-rated remake of the Japanese hit Infernal Affairs, a South Boston crime boss (Nicholson) plays both sides of the law when two moles-an undercover cop (DiCaprio) working within his organization and his own soldier (Damon), who has infiltrated the state police force-try to sniff each other out.

The magnificent poetry of this latter-day Hail Mary pass by Scorsese is in the fractured yet altogether satisfying storytelling. Weaving between the dueling serpentine storylines of DiCaprio's tortured undercover cop and Damon's duplicitous undercover crook, the story receives the perfect anchor in the guise of Nicholson's brilliant turn as Irish crime boss Frank Costello. While Infernal Affairs concentrated little attention on this character, The Departed makes him the fulcrum, which only informs the film's South Boston Irish personality all the more. It works, as does the inclusion of a mutual love interest (Vera Farmiga), albeit to a lesser degree. The climax and ending, however, is simply a veritable Dead Man's Curve of viewing pleasure.       

Bottom line: Full of grace.","d4c27747-336e-4ad8-aee6-46d92cc006be",False,True,False,False,4.5,"2011-03-07","","RT","904377541"
