5.0,"While many people will disagree with my view on this film I think this is Martin Scorsese best film to date. Don't jump to any conclusions though, I still believe that Casino, Goodfellas and Raging Bull are some of the finest films ever made (I cannot vouch for Mean Streets or L.A confidential however because as of this date I have yet to see them, although I have a great desire too). When making this decision I got Goodfellas, Raging Bull, Casino and The Departed all off my DVD shelf. I then had to have a good think about which is the best. I narrowed it down to The Departed and Raging Bull and after a very hard depth think, I concluded that The Departed was superior. I think this because while both films are near perfect, I had one critiscm about Raging Bull, Jake La Motta. I found him extremely unlikeable in certain parts of the film and while the film is still incredible it is hard to sympathise for a character who is very self destructive, similar to Dicaprio's character in Blood Diamond.
So, on to the film, Departed is incredible, it is one of my favourite films of all time and at the moment stands at number #18 in my all time favourite films, below 'The Bourne Ultimatum' and above 'Indiana Jones and the Raiders of the Lost Ark'. It is a true masterpiece, everything about it is crafted to near perfection, the Soundtrack, Performances, Location, Story, every minute detail is absolutely stunning and the film had my attention for the full two and a half hour running time, which can be a very hard task to accomplish when you have to include the talking sequences to make sure the dramatic parts of the film don't lose their impact.
Saying this however, every part of the film is breathtaking and serves a purpose; there are many things within the film which need to be talked about.
The first point which I need to make is the two main leads, Dicaprio and Damon. They are both excellent in playing their parts, and they are definitely no easy characters to play. They both play out as Polar opposites to one another. Damon's character has had a childhood of being raised by a father figure (Nicholson) who crafts him to being his rat inside the police office. He is the bad guy playing the good guy. Damon's character is very confident in himself, he has a great apartment and he wants for nothing, everyone around the office like him and he has a beautiful girlfriend (Farmiga) who he manages to hide his dark side from. Dicaprio's character on the other hand, was raised in the slum of Boston, he has very little family left and he is seen as an outcast by the police. One incredibly touching scene at the beginning of the film shows you Dicaprio with his mother, who is dying of cancer, and although you only see her for two seconds, you are left with a horrible image in your head which sticks with you throughout the rest of the film. Ike I said earlier, he is the polar opposite of Damon, and he is good guy, who is forced to be a bad guy. He also shares a love interest with his therapist, who also happens to be Damon's girlfriend. However, instead of being the confident tough guy, he is the emotional wreck; he lives in fear but cannot express it other than in his therapy sessions.
The supporting cast in this film is also superb, easily on par with other films such as 'The Dark Knight' and 'Aliens'. Jack Nicholson, as always, plays his part perfectly. He is an incredible actor and he shows just again how versatile he can be, as the head of the crime organisation in the Boston Area, his persona is also damaged and cracked, all the years of drugs, deceit and destruction have started catching up with him. Other Characters such as Sheen, Wahlberg, Farmiga and Baldwin all deserve incredible amounts of credit for their roles, each of them served a specific purpose in the film and weren't put their just to fill space.
The story is also perfectly crafted, there are major twists and turns which I couldn't see coming but were believable and related to the specific restraints or circumstance a character was in. The story itself is fairly simple; it basically follows the lives of two characters who are exact opposites to each other and the effects and repercussions of their actions on each other. In my opinion, I think the best part of the film comes in the last half an hour, one thing (which I am not going to specify but I guarantee will shock you) happens which starts a domino effect. All the pieces come into play and Damons character who at the beginning as a confident man, is almost completely ruined, his armour has all been broken off if you will and it shows, especially in the roof scene which is one of my favourite scenes in a movie ever.
The soundtrack also is very very immersive, my favourite song is 'Shipping up to Boston' by the Dropkick Murphy's. It was only used twice and at both times it was used exactly the right amount of time, it was also used for appropriate sections of the film.  The beginning and towards the part where the big shock comes.
However, all of this wouldn't have been possible without the absolutely outstanding direction of Martin Scorsese. He shows us that he still is one of the best directors around and I'm sure that he will stay cemented at joint first as my favourite director of all time. Each one of his films stays in familiar ground but adds something new and never feels like a case of Déjà Vu. I look forward to watching his future films as well as his old ones such as LA confidential and Mean Streets. Scorsese is nearing 70 now and I will weapon on the day he dies, because he is a truly terrific person, in every interview I've seen him in, he seems like a down to earth person and he researches his films to a ridiculous extent, making sure every tiny detail is crafted to perfection, because Scorsese is a perfectionist and even long after his death, Scorsese will live on as one of the best directors of all time.","b02e0e31-cb8b-4b1e-9464-1f6759770f86",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2011-03-21","","RT","906331983"
