4.0,"When this film ended, I felt the same way I do after watching a lot of George Clooney films: there were so many stars packed into this film, it worked against itself. This film was, at the best, intense and captivating. The twisted (no understatement) plot was very well spun. All the leads were exceptional and fit right into the self-destructive, trust no one plot that Scorsese has generated. However, at the worst, The Departed is utterly confusing and relies too heavily on the word ""fuck"" (237 times, 1.5 times/min), as well as the actors to fill in the fairly mediocre plot. Cops betraying each other whilst fighting the mob? I can't say that is the most original piece of work I have ever beheld. Overall, while a very good film, it is kept out of true stardom by an overly-long run time, too many A-list actors, an overly twisted yet underly simplistic plot, and slew of lewdness that cannot be naturally human.","140861f3-8375-4842-9940-677bb65d531d",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2011-04-20","","RT","887162276"
4.5,"Martin Scorcese's 'The Departed' is vintage Scorcese, a film that will bring back memories of his past ventures while mixing it with a contemporary style that is bold and brilliant. The performances are all completely top notch, with Damon, DiCaprio, and Nicholson turning in brilliant performances. Whalberg, Sheen, Farmiga, Baldwin, and Winstone are also a delight to watch every time they are on screen. Scorcese clearly had fun choosing his ensemble cast. This movie is also made brilliant by its Editing, beautiful work that deserved to win the Oscar. Another great (classic?) from Scorcese!","57883a5b-341f-4c05-9cfa-4056929f3199",False,True,False,False,4.5,"2011-04-20","","RT","905658398"
