3.5,"Love the suspenseful moments. Some things felt a little odd when you watched them, like the the constant abrupt endings to the music or the entire ending which was totally sudden and fast-paced. But over all those were stylistic choices and they made for an interesting movie even if you want to criticize those things at first. It took a pretty long time for Leo and Matt Damon's characters to finally become really connected so it was kind of distracting following two stories for a while, but in the end it made for those intense moments. I'm not sure I'd watch this again, but it was worth watching once.","5168932f-2310-4a26-b402-68cba9b8f324",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2011-05-21","","RT","908312115"
4.5,"'The Departed' is a gritty and interesting crime thriller from director Martin Scorsese, this really did go above and behind my expectations. The plot is a web of rich and unexpected twists. 
 
 The Plot??? As a child Colin Sullivan is introduced into an organised crime ring through Irish mobster Frank Costello. Costello trains him up to become a mole inside the police force. Sullivan goes under intensive training and eventually is accepted into the Special Investigations Unit. Meanwhile Billy Costigan, another trainee, is asked by Captain Oliver Quuenan and Staff Sergeant Sean Dignam to go undercover into Costello's mob, as his family's history in crime circles will make him a good infiltrator. He accepts, dropping out of the academy early and does time in prison for a fake charge to make his past more believable. When he gets out he sets to work infiltrating Costello's organisation whilst Sullivan is passing information on from inside his department. Enter psychiatrist Madolyn Madden, Sullivan starts a relationship with her but before they move in together Costigan also falls in love with her and they have an affair. This has effects later.
 After Costello barely escapes a sting operation he orders Sullivan to find the rat within his organisation. At the same time the police know they have a mole but order Sullivan to find him/her! As each man delves deeper into their respective organisations and the police draw closer to nailing Costello their paths slowly twist, these twists involve well filmed chases, a shocking death, a suspicious Dignam is put on leave and both men risk their lives to: uncover the truth in Costigan's case or protect Costello in Sullivan's case. This plot may seem a little confusing but it works beautifully and you begin to wonder how it will all end.

 The last sequences are phenomenal as the twists of the plot merge into one, the deaths of specific characters are shown very well and the blood splats are of a pretty good standard. The soundtrack to the film is also great, the music fits the picture!
 
 And, most of all, the cast which is amazing! My favourite actor in this was Jack Nicholson as Frank Costello, the head mobster. He played the evil but sophisticated role very well. Matt Damon played Colin Sullivan, Costello's mole. Leonardo DiCaprio plays William Sullivan the 'rat', Mark Wahlberg plays Staff Sergeant Sean Dignam, he also performs well. And Martin Sheen plays Captain Oliver Charles Queenan. Other performances come from Alec Baldwin as Captain George Ellerby, Vera Farmiga as Dr. Madolyn Madden, Ray Winstone as Arnold French and Anthony Anderson as Trooper Brown.
 
 The final seconds of the film were genius, we see the view from Sullivan's appartement, which is referred to in the scene in which he buys it, and a rat crawls along the railing. This is a brilliant way to end.
 Overall this film is awesome, it features great actors, has a complex but engaging plot with plenty of twists, the level of acting is high, the soundtrack is good and the ending is perfect. I think this may be looked back on in the future as a classic; or at least a really good crime thriller!","83cbd23a-7b6b-4db7-b7e7-2f0f1dce8f8e",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2011-05-22","","RT","909566726"
