4.0,"I remember seeing this after it first came out and not liking it very much. Maybe it was the poor viewing environment in which I saw it. Maybe I was in the wrong state of mind. But I'm glad to say that after seeing it again, I have a newfound appreciation for this fantastic film. It's an epic cops vs. organized crime movie that's right up there with Heat (one of my genre favorites) in terms of the character development, the scope of the story, etc. There are just so many levels to it, and all of the threads that are started are seen through to fruition... Except for that envelope at the end. That damn envelope... Moving on. I wasn't a big fan of the way it was edited before the title card was introduced. It was bizarre, and I didn't get a sense of cohesion from it. It felt really sloppy. Thankfully that got a lot better as the film went on. Excellent acting all around. Entertaining from start to bloody finish. It may not have the happy ending everyone wants, but that's life, and I think that it makes a better movie by being honest about how this sort of thing really works (or so I would imagine). [062611] [4 STARS]","7c4edcdc-afb0-4b3c-9c86-01a4d4a0c7b0",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2011-06-27","","RT","788349272"
5.0,"There is no mistake, The Departed is more than amazing, it blows you away every single moment, making the 150 minutes worth every second. This remake of ""Infernal Affairs"" exceeds the original in every possible way, with one of the best castings ever, and a story that surpasses most of the plots involving gangs, cops, spys, treason, and based on a script meant to be complicated and intriguing at the same time.","8db045a6-5dd7-460c-aacd-0abfe1108118",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2011-06-28","","RT","908101851"
