3.0,"I'm always a fan of Martin Scorsese and I'm really glad that he finally won his long deserved Oscar, BUT I still have to say that The Departed is an overrated movie. From a point of view of a guy who watched and loved The Departed's Hong Kong predecessor Infernal Affairs (aka Mou gann dou) even before The Departed came out, I have to say that The Departed is just another scene-by-scene American remake (and even that, there's a lot of scenes in Infernal Affairs that is far well-crafted than The Departed, especially the ending) that didn't even bother to add something new except for a lot of F-word and Mark Walhberg's annoying performance (why the hell did he got an Oscar nod for being so annoying like this anyway?)
Speaking of the performances, Leo DiCaprio and Jack Nicholson did an okay jobs (even though Jack Nicholson's performance in this was so, well, Jack Nicholson's stereotype, he's still a joy to watch), but for Matt Damon, he just can't hold a candle to Andy Lau's performance in Infernal Affairs. 
No offence to Mr. Scorsese by any mean, but he should've won his Oscar(s) from his TRUE masterpieces like Taxi Driver, Raging Bull, or Goodfellas.","5717612e-3ea4-4754-a06d-0b3e69fd9c14",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2011-09-09","","RT","914593140"
4.5,"This movie shows that the gang-drama genre is still very much alive.","d68c63e7-2ea5-40b5-8ea3-d0185495d9d2",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2011-09-11","","RT","906213096"
4.5,"Church vs. state; cops vs. robbers; a loaded gun vs. an unloaded gun; an honest man in a bad situation vs. a dishonest man in a good situation. These morals are where the Departed, Martin Scorsese's 2006 Best Picture Oscar Winner (superseding Goodfellas, Raging Bull, and Taxi Driver), flourishes in cinematic value. Filmed on location in the southern district of Boston, Massachusetts, the story follows the life of mobster Frank Costello and the law's strenuous attempt to bring him to justice. But Costello owns the mob, the city, and the drugs. He even has an informant of his own, Sgt. Sullivan (Matt Damon), stationed in the police unit. Sullivan is an overprivileged and spoiled boy who grew up to be an overprivileged and spoiled adult. He works for nothing; his integrity is fickle. 

On the opposing end, in the special investigations unit ran by Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sgt. Dignam (Mark Wahlberg), the police conceive their own ""rat"" in that of Billy Costigan Jr. (Leo DiCaprio). Costigan, an honest working individual who graduated with high accolades from the police force, is to erase his identity, assimilating a new one just for purposes of the mission. 

The Departed is expertly directed by Martin Scorsese. It's fast-paced and never maintains a dull moment. It's done similarly to Goodfellas, but without the narration. I have never been so enthralled by a cops and robbers story before. The constant bickering of cops, the backstabbing of the mob and the police force, and the themes of identity, conceit, and deception are constantly in motion. The film has a star-studded cast that can recognizably appeal to most audiences (Sheen, Baldwin, Wahlberg, DiCaprio, Nicholson, and Farmiga, among others). The constrasting scenes of Sullivan and Costigan's ups and downs is skillfully crafted with enough juxtaposed irony to delight those viewers with literary penchants. This was a well deserved win for Martin Scorsese, who finally claimed his best director (and best picture) awards.","4a0c9164-645a-484a-a0c2-b09935c1e754",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2011-09-12","","RT","904206639"
