4.5,"After decades of unruly and raw cinema, Scorsese has finally toned his brand of hard-boiled gangster film down enough to create a resonating- and still very wild- piece of highly engaging mob drama.","ae8337d0-b362-4962-ba7a-fa8bdd77f6dc",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2011-10-06","","RT","906837262"
4.5,"When you look in the credits and you discover that a gifted filmmaker named Martin Scorsese is directing a gangster picture, you will know that shits are going to happen. An innovative artist has added another gangster picture in his career - that picture is called The Departed. Apart from being informed that Scorsese is directing the project, I am aware that Departed is a great film from viewing the first thirty minutes of the film. And yes, the work is a masterful act; it is a Scorsese's masterpiece.

Departed features a wholly talented, distinguished cast. Not to mention only Scorsese, the film too benefits from its stars. Leo Dicaprio, Matt Damon, Jack Nicholson, Mark Wahlberg, Martin Sheen, Ray Winstone, Alec Baldwin - All bring their A game for this one. I had trouble figuring out on who shines above others. It's like, everyone is in their top form, and everyone gives a near perfect performance. Hands down, The Departed is one of the best acted movies of the decade.

Excellent writing, as a whole, is there. The film never drags and remains to be highly entertaining; all are highly due to its clever writing. Put writing and acting aside, the film would not be as effective without Scorsese tight direction. Scorsese simply chooses to be effective, and the picture turned out to be good. Another treasure of the film would be its brilliant editing being delivered for the work, and too, the film is being superbly shot.

The departed tells us about on how riveting and exciting a film could possibly be. Although the film runs for 151 min, it never gets dry or boring. Simply, The Departed is just another Scorsese's masterpiece.

