3.5,"My initial recollection of Martin Scorsese's ""The Departed"" was only in bits and pieces. Now, I can say I have viewed it. I was quite pleased with it, but not something singular. Thoughts on the film and Scorsese: The acting of DiCaprio was unconvincing in the early parts of the film, but he picks it up as the film goes on. As expected, Damon and Wahlberg were satisfactory. Vera Farminga's character was predictable. I was simply in awe of Jack Nicholson's portrayal of Frank Costello, just hearing his voice made me tremble. This film would not have been as good without his charisma. I also noticed references to ""Goodfellas,"" ""Taxi Driver"" and ""The Third Man."" There is a scene where Winstone is beating up a guy, which is very much like De Niro in ""Goodfellas."" Or, Matt Damon and Nicholson, being in the porn theater, which is what Travis Bickle did. Most significantly is the reference to the beautiful ending of ""The Third Man,"" with Matt Damon being completely ignored by Farminga's Madolyn. ""The Departed"" is in no comparison to the great Scorsese films: ""Taxi Driver,"" ""Raging Bull,"" ""Goodfellas,"" ""The Last Temptation of Christ."" Scorsese seems to have lost what he had back in the day. (I do need to watch more of his recent films) To me, there was a special quality about his earlier films; he had a world. ""The Departed,"" as good as it is, felt awfully common. Yes, there are some Scorsese moments, but it is not what it used to be. Not to sound one sided, I do love Scorsese. In my mind he is probably one of the top three greatest living directors, and that is not just because of his masterpieces, it is more because he has been so dependable. Not every director can create such good (or great) works like ""Mean Streets,"" ""The Age of Innocence,"" ""After Hours,"" ""The King of Comedy,"" ""Casino,"" ""New York, New York."" Still have to see ""Gangs of New York,"" ""Hugo,""  ""The Aviator,"" etc.","4b0048bd-e9d7-40f1-b3a2-69d033415e82",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2012-03-02","","RT","853804398"
