3.5,"It's a spy blast definitely; ""The Departed"" had shown the expertise of Martin Scorsese, tactic-wise. It just brings you right into the action, with rationality. Colin Sullivan (Damon), a part of the gang owned by Irish mobster Frank Costello (Nicholson) from South Boston, is trained to be a mole for Costello inside the Massachusetts State Police. He was later accepted in Special Investigation Unit, where he signals Costello whenever the police is about to impede his illegal transactions. On the other hand, a secret cop named Billy Costigan (DiCaprio) is conducted by his superiors, Queenan (Sheen) and Dignam (Wahlberg) to infiltrate Costello by joining his mob; he will be a mole for the police in short. Now, the bottomline is who will gain his own victory, will it be Costigan and his troopers or Sullivan and Costello's mob. One thing is credibly sure; it won't end without either one of them getting killed or neither the two of them departing.

âThe Departedâ? has a slow start that ends in a huge, dramatic, and traumatic way. Leonardo DiCaprio gave all he has in this one, I never saw this dirty and bad-mouthed Leo before, all I know is that the sweet little Jack from Titanic came up as a madcap cop without knowing the word fear. In Jack Nicholson's part, we always see that personality that he always brings on, it's a typical him. Matt Damon, the spy whom we loved because he doesn't like to be killed as Bourne, ends up being a great adversary to Leo. Last but not the least; Mark Wahlberg has a great assist to make this mob-fuck-ster film work more. The weakness, we saw this couple of times way before Scorsese did this, and he himself had made this agenda on that before. But still, the best of 2006, can't give loathsome comments.","804ad57c-7dcb-449f-b256-c0359e8f29bd",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2012-05-07","","RT","916102212"
