4.0,"Martin Scorsese, sure, is an old timer but the kind of movies he delivered in the past has left an indelible impression not only on people but also on the word called âcinemaâ?. Weâ(TM)ve seen him deliver some of the best and awesomely gruesome crime epics in his long lineage of legendry. With âThe Departedâ?, the legend is back to his best forte â"" street crime, espionage, and thuggery bounced back against basic human instincts of survival, fear, courage and treachery.

Plot:

Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon) is trained to be injected in to Massachusetts state police by organized crime boss Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson). On a parallel beam police academy dropout Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio) is drawn in to the world of spying and debauchery given his ineffable family background. The film kicks pace when both these characters come to know about their existence and line of work ultimately culminating towards the concept of âroot outâ?. 

Analysis:

The film is loosely based on 2002 Hongkong film âInfernal Affairsâ? but is totally limited to the padding from it. The free flowing screenplay by William Monahan infuses more vigor and vitality to the movie compared to its original counterpart. The director and writer take a lot of time in setting up the lead characters rendering a draggy feeling to the initial run. But once the lead characters get pitted against each other, the film veers off like a speedy demon. All characters in the movie are given a perfect human aspect carefully treading the path of crime vs. feel.

DiCaprio as Billy Costigan is the best package of the film and truly delivers one of the best performances of his life. The insular moments of marauding between fear, depression, helplessness and gumption are exceptionally well designed and âBillyâ? aces them like a spade. As a kid, with troubled family background bustling up to the ranks of police academy, who gets thrown in to the world of organized crime laced with blood, guts and glory heâ(TM)s as delicate as a cotton thread.

Matt Damon as Sullivan does justice to the role and brings his usual panache to the character. Jack Nicholson as Costello brings forth a âtouch of the devilâ? feel but inconsistently. Heâ(TM)s given a pure devilish faÃ§ade that fades away as we sail through the gruesome plot. Irrespective of his character he portrays it with his usual ease and expertise. Mark Wahlberg as Sergeant Dignam is ruthless, condescending and funny at the same time.

Scorsese as the primary helmsman is downright excellent. He is one of the best directors when it comes to dramatizing street crime for the silver screen. Just like every other movie there are gaping holes in its screenplay and character setup but the movieâ(TM)s picked up pace and adrenaline covers for everything.

Tail piece:
Scorsese always teams up with individuals epitomized by uncanny portrayal abilities. We have seen the legend partner with Robert DeNiro, in the past, and deliver some of the best classics of crime/gangster genre. Itâ(TM)s pretty nice to see him hit it off with DiCaprio in todayâ(TM)s era and the grapevine promises more cinematic outings from the duo.","a0be1d50-09c6-408e-ab0c-e5fa3921f4d1",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2012-09-13","","RT","934161907"
