5.0,"Martin Scorsese is certainly a Director whose stamp can be felt anywhere, since he stopped writing his own scripts sometime after Goodfellas its been less pronounced but even as the voice feels less and less like his own he still manages to make his presence as a Director which is pretty special in and of itself. And weirdly enough, nowhere in his surprising amount of work in the past decade for a man who's now 70 years old has his influence over the proceedings felt more present than in 2006's The Departed a film which adapted the Hong Kong crime Thriller Internal Affairs into modern day Boston. The Academy Awards certainly thought so and at long last rewarded Scorsese that year with the coveted Best Director and Best Picture one two punch for the film which makes the movie of a certain significance in the History of Cinema for that fact alone as well as marking a return to Gangster movies and the Directors Third outing with current favorite Actor Leonardo DiCaprio. The movie is seen as a career defining triumph, it certainly bears all the markings of past Scorsese Gangster flicks but one can't help but question how much of that is done through wish fulfillment, circumstance and careful marketing. Even with its overwhelmingly positive reception if the movie hadn't netted Scorsese his Oscar Glory would people still be so quick to heap such high accolades upon it? And a cynical mind can't help but think how a good team of Hollywood Producers could have fooled people into viewing this as more of a pure Scorsese movie than it is, the strategic casting of Jack Nicholson a beloved Actor from Scorsese's heyday who'd yet to work with the Directing Giant was certain to generate buzz by itself as well as some posters which featured a simple design of The title in the shape of a smoking handgun recalling Scorsese's breakthrough picture Mean Streets is poster. But Marty's a legend and so many love this movie it's certainly worth checking out so let's maybe take a look. Or Maybe Not. Or Maybe Fuck Yourself.

Of the myriad ensemble of characters that inhabit this film debatably the central one is Irish Mobster Frank Costello (Nicholson). Costello is the head of the Irish Mob which has run Boston since the 70s keeping other groups in check and in this time has managed to while generating massive Police Attention place a high level mole in the Special Investigation Department of the Boston PD in the form of smooth talking Colin Sullivan. In a web of conspiracy and lies the Undercover unit headed by Captain Oliver Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Staff Sergeant Sean Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) have managed to create a cover for and plant a plain clothes cop, Billy Costigan (DiCaprio), who grew up in the area in Costello's inner circle and both groups are tightening the noose on one another. As the Boston PD under Captain George Ellerby (Alec Baldwin) closes in on Costello's latest racket of selling microchips to Chinese buyers offshore Costigan and Sullivan struggle to discover each others identities before the other does. And all the while Costigan, who is slowly losing his grip on sanity and control of his situation, carries on an affair unknowingly with Sullivan's lover The Department's Psychiatrist Madolyn Madden (Vera Farmiga). 

The undeniable great strength of The Departed rests in the story it creates, not necessarily how good or relatable its characters are we'll get to that later, but more how its complex set up pays off with insanely compelling and good drama. It ups the cat and mouse, cop and criminal game to a whole new level one that is still fairly simple on the surface but insanely rewarding to watch unfold. All it does is add the element of cop chases criminal a cop who is pretending to be a criminal, and a criminal who is pretending to be a cop but the brilliance in that simplicity really pays off. And the biggest way it pays off is the ingenuous way it melds cell phones and modern technology into the mix the way many other cop and gangster dramas today are scared to which is why so many of them are period or pretend the Internet does not exist. The movie builds an eerie at times almost oppressive sense of tension in the anonymity and hands off nature of the technological world, in multiple scenes Sullivan's character orchestrates counter intelligence moves that suit his ends without ever leaving his office at the Police Station and all the cops rely on technology to aid them bust Costello to a fault. And this make the visceral and stripped down scenes all the more intense one of the most nail biting parts of the whole movie is a foot chase through a crowd between Sullivan and Costigan in the Chinatown District which is pretty low key stuff writing wise. But that's not to say its a movie without its flaws, the characters though technically written well behave like they're in an over wrought morality play even by Scorsese standards. The characters that are bad are raving lunatics and out of control psychopaths or else sniveling impotent weaklings without an ounce of humanity while the good characters who are honest cops can at worst be accused of having some abnormally salty language and being liberal with dispensing beatings when frustrated. It creates a kind of bizarre dichotomy considering how besides that these are really well fleshed out and engrossing characters you kind of wish it weren't so quick to label them good and evil. The incredibly solid script is also aided by universally fantastic performances, particularly Matt Damon who has never lost himself so much in a role before or since nor taken on a role as juicy. Mark Wahlberg and Jack Nicholson talk circles around the rest of the admittedly great cast though dispensing memorable and often hilarious witticisms and one liners that I suspect along with the violence are part of the reason for the film's mass appeal.

The Departed is one of those movies that succeeds and fails mightily and luckily for it though it could have gone either way it succeeds enough those failures are rendered all but moot. It's pacing is at times damn near break neck, I've never seen Infernal Affairs but its a Hong Kong Cop Thriller so I assume part of that is being an adaption of it but honestly along with the technology thing it just serves to make it feel fresh and unique even among Scorsese movies. And Scorsese leaves his imprint their enough while I wouldn't call it career defining the 2 and a half hour near epic could indeed be viewed as a career spanning thesis. We get the sudden burst of violence, mixed in with exposition on the nature of morality, overt Catholic imagery and a dirty but ultimately colorful portrayal of a major city. Hell we even get a significant scene taking place in a porno theater like his classic Taxi Driver which this reminds me of in style more so than any other. All in all luckily this movie is far from all hype, it's at once exhilarating and fucking fantastic a mind bending crime thriller that will be viewed and discussed though maybe mostly in relation to Scorsese's other work for years to come.","d93fd183-dc4a-4e96-9a2c-c7ea03f289f2",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2013-02-11","","RT","904841004"
