5.0,"It's odd to think that after three decades (going on four fairly soon) Martin Scorsese hasn't skipped a beat.  He's mastered every genre he's tried, consumed plenty of nominations, and absorbed spectacular work from the actors in his films.  Of all of his films though, Scorsese has always made crime dramas like no other director, and ""The Departed"" is his masterpiece of the decade, and one of his best films.  His talents form together to make this film, with flawless results.  His unusual artistic style has never worked better.
	The plot revolves around Billy Costigan (Leonardo DiCaprio), Colin Sullivan (Matt Damon), and Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson).  Costigan and Sullivan are both cops, while Costello is a crime lord that rivals the evilness and power of Marlon Brando in ""The Godfather"".  The twist is, though, is that Sullivan just happens to be Costello's ""son"" (after meeting him as a child, he trained Sullivan to play a policeman his entire life), who's quickly shot to the top of the police world and now serves as an informer.  In the meantime, Costigan is a man with a bad background who becomes a cop, but before long is assigned by Captain Queenan (Martin Sheen) and Sergeant Dignam (Mark Wahlberg) to use his family ties in order to work as an undercover policeman-- this time to join Costello's entourage and work as an insider.  Before long, everything will be at stake.
	""The Departed"" is so perfect it's almost hard to begin.  What appears to be an ingenious story molds into brilliance, climbing to a breaking point of a climax, and ending with tragedy that rivals any play Arthur Miller could write.  While we think we know what will happen when the film begins, every second thereafter is instead heightened by a sense of reality, thereby making it constantly thrilling, and unpredictable.  The dialogue all feels real, making us feel, without a doubt, that this is what real life criminals talk like.  At this point, Scorsese is one of the few to master this difficult task.
	The actors all give one of their best performances.  While Nicholson is clearly the veteran here, his psychopathic and utterly unnerving portrayal of Costello simply brilliant, it's only a side performance compared to both DiCaprio and Damon.  DiCaprio constantly plays his character with regret and anxiety, making Costigan the most compelling character because we can never tell whether his past will catch up with him.  Damon is excellent as well, throwing away his usual charisma, and playing a villain just as morally evil and Costello, while creating a façade that's both believable and subtle.  Though Wahlberg was the only actor nominated for the film, his performance does not compare with the three leads.
	""The Departed"" is phenomenal.  On one hand, it's a terrific piece of entertainment.  On the other, it's a masterpiece that will surely be up to the ranks of ""Taxi Driver""-- it may as well already be.  Highly recommended.","f83de334-a81a-41c8-bcb0-d48d9f94cc45",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2013-02-14","","RT","909783806"
