5.0,"A little too violent and over the top, but it has a lot of grit and truly good storytelling. Driven by a very fast paced and intense plot, with deep characters (played by some of the best actors in the business), this is a great movie.","5ea00f96-e117-4d14-9962-e3ffa097f584",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2013-04-11","","RT","908414511"
4.5,"When compared to Martin Scorsese's earlier crime classics such as Taxi driver, The Departed displays that Martin Scorsese is a man who constantly has the idea in his head of how to structure the dynamics of a film so well that it shocks the audience, no matter what decade. And it's about damn time he earned the Academy Award for Best Director for it. 

The Departed was just great, and the dynamics were so powerful and quick that I could barely figure out who was who, and so the product was mesmerising in a great way.
And the general thrills of the atmosphere was perfect, and invoked a sense of mystery that proved that whatever the viewer thinks is going on is wrong, much like in the 1973 classic The Sting, except in a differently complicated way because in The Departed, the story structure takes place in a way that chronicles the dynamics focusing on the heroes, the villains and the rest, ensuring that the audience gets a full feel for character motives and understanding. The assistance with this is primarily thanks to the great actors that are all so talented that there is not one that stands out, because it features many in some of their greatest performances.

Leonardo DiCaprio makes a stunning lead, with character development and line delivery iconic of him when he combines forces with Martin Scorsese. His role in The Departed is one of his most powerful to date, and features a chance for the audience to witness one of his richest characterisations available.
Matt Damon also equally mirrored the skill in a largely against type role as a villain that embraces an image so alike a combination of Leonardo Dicaprio's and Mark Whalberg's that I more-or-less became unsure of who was who, so it was like a mole was planted in the film and there is no way to figure it out which is all a part of the mesmerising experience of The Departed. Matt Damon emphasises every point of his character in a great manner and stuns the audience with his performance as well as the entire film itself. This is also one of his best performances.
And although I didn't witness enough complicated situations or screen time for Mark Whalberg to deserve an Academy Award nomination, his performance was sufficient and strong and ensures that the audience always knew that there was a determined backup character in case anything happens, and so he ensured that The Departed wasn't entirely a series of negative plot dynamics killing each other. He also made an excellent transition from being an ass-cop to being a real cop.
And Jack Nicholson Meade a great premise and ensured that the audiences consistently viewed him in nothing less than a creepy manner only to mesmerise the audience in many ways. His presence ensured that the audience's beliefs of events was constantly unbalanced.
Also, the general presence of Martin Sheen provided the perfect feel of nostalgia for The Departed and he was a strong and solid character in one of his best performances in years. His mere voice was nostalgic for the story to take the audience back to the time of the film, and after that was a chance for him to work with Martin Scorsese and he evoked a great performance.
Lastly, I also enjoyed the brief roles played by Anthony Anderson and Alec Baldwin who portrayed much strength in their brief respective time on screen. 

So The Departed is one of Martin Scorsese's greatest modern works, and is composed of a good soundtrack, an excellent cast and some great cinematography, and the praise can all be directed to the mind of masterful director Martin Scorsese who is long overdue for an Academy Award.","1c8c02a0-8e3b-4e5b-b3f0-884b7d18d946",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2013-04-12","","RT","891495465"
