4.5,"Review In A Nutshell:

The Departed's plot is about two individuals, one a mole for the Boston Irish gang and the other an informant stationed in the Boston police department.

I have seen this film when I was much younger and it didn't impact me that much, though quite a few of its scenes did leave an imprint on my brain. I recently watched the film that inspired this, ""Infernal Affairs"", and going through this again I was recalling memories of the original Hong Kong film and the memories of my past experience with this film. Though they were running through my mind, none of it actually affected my perspective of the film negatively.

The film is essentially a replica of Infernal Affairs, aside from a few changes, and I was happy to go through the story one more time. In rewatching this, I wasn't searching for thrills or surprises to engage me; instead I was investing my focus on the characters, which was certainly one of the film's biggest changes from the original film. First off, the film's antagonist gains much more exposure as compared to the original film and taking some of the focus away from the two other leads. I was happy with what Scorsese and the writers have done as it does gain us a sense of idea on why this figure is so important, which was lacking a bit on the original film. Another aspect that was different from Infernal Affairs was the lack of focus on the original's core themes, Identity and Morality. This is not to say that they don't appear at all as they are certainly present during the third act of the film, but it only makes an appearance during this area of the film, with no focus on it whatsoever during the 2 hours. This left me a tad disappointed as those were elements that made the first film feel so effective. The third and most obvious is the character Dignam. All of the frustrated policemen that were found in Infernal Affairs was squashed together and moulded into this one character here, which I thought, was a bit of an odd choice, and turns out by the end of the film his role is only present to provide a twist into the story, which I actually didn't mind.

The Departed core strength is found in its characters, they were written in such an interesting way that I can't keep my eyes off them. The film lets us in deep with the lives of these characters, allowing us to gain a sense of connection in order for the final act to truly pay off. The film lets us in our characters in a psychological level, allowing us to see how their profession and current position affects their personal lives and state of mind. Though I felt it was a bit unnecessary for the character Billy to develop a relationship with his psychiatrist as it felt forced and didn't really do much in changing my perspective or making his story more impacting.

The film does contain its thrills and for the most part, they still similarly produce the same impact they had on me when I watched it years ago. As this is a Scorsese film, there would be violence, and when they do appear, boy are they bloody. One can feel that this is a Scorsese film, but thankfully he doesn't let this film be overwhelmed by his obvious trademarks and instead let the film's story take centre stage.

The acting in this film is powerful and is one of the core reasons why I favour this film over the original. The actors in this film immerse themselves to the role in such a way that my preconceived ideas of the actors suddenly went away from the first frame I saw them in. Scorsese uses his actors here the same way that David O. Russell uses his actors for almost all of his films; he brings out the most concentrated of performances from his cast in order for the film to feel truly authentic and to distinguish itself as the more powerful film when compared to the original. Each of the principal cast deserved at least a nomination for their performances, aside from Mark Wahlberg who I felt was a bit campy in a couple of moments.

The Departed is a must watch as it gives the audience a glimpse of the master filmmaker and his actors in top form.","9b7295ca-fccb-4ccc-849f-05e15f2f3280",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2014-07-23","","RT","954573411"
