Already considered a modern-day classic, this Scorsese movie is one of his best, featuring great performances from everyone involved and a twisty-turny screenplay that's vastly entertaining, all backed up with his unique style","0ec44d74-7e24-4d52-9893-4836598922e4",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2014-08-16","","RT","944148775"
5.0,"The Departed is considered a drama film that was originally a remake of a foreign film. The film submerges the viewer into the life of the Massachusetts State police and the Irish organized crime in Boston. The key roles in this movie include gang members, police officers, undercover officers, mob boss, and a therapist. The films genre falls into the drama category and meets the requirements for the drama genre. A typical genre film involves an emotional buildup of characters and the characters approach to dealing with problems in the film. IN this film the audience is connected to Leonardo Dicaprio (Billy) and follows the conflict of him being on ""off the books"" undercover officer. 
The sound use in this movie is outstanding. The music is the primary atmosphere setter and uses the music to set the emotion for key plot points. For example the band Dropkick Murphy's plays Shipping up to Boston. This song is played while showing the main group of gang members and helps signify the gangs success and ""don't care"" attitude. 
Cinematography of the film is interesting because it gives a dark gloomy look to Boston. This appearance made me want to eat a bowl of England Clam Chowder while watching this great action packed drama. 
Editing provided and overall setting during the action scenes and assisted to contributing to twists. Without giving away any part of the movie the editing is used to give a twist in last parts of the movie. For example there is an elevator scene that turns deadly and the editing assists on changing who the shooter is. 
As a rating this film receives a high rating and is suggested for any drama go getter.","59b98c74-5453-4fd4-a795-8a16495678b0",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2014-08-16","","RT","966342845"
4.5,"Featuring one of the best casts of great actors I've ever seen, this movie was bound to be a hit.  Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Mark Walberg, Jack Nicholson, all being some of my favourite actors, and having then all in one movie is awesome, and one about the gangs and cops of a corrupt city is the perfect setting.  They all preform great, the script is good, and doesn't lay low off the profanity, which I also enjoy, which makes for some odd laughs.  The action is good, and extremely violent, which although is maybe above and beyond that of real life, it's entertaining and makes the action better in my opinion. At first the story is kinda confusing, who is doing what and where, but after a bit it all makes sense, but still is a complex film that required attention. The love story works and doesn't get in the way, and for a movie so violent and full of swearing I was surprised at the relative lack of sexuality. The ending is what I've heard as typical  Martin Scorsese fashion, everyone getting killed, at first I didn't really like that Matt Damon was the survivor of the famous elevator executions, in which no fewer than three main characters ended up on the ground after a bullet to the head from one of their friends. But then Mark Walberg made a surprise appearance in Matt Damond apartment and put him down too.  I thought it was a good way to end a good movie, different that that we are used too.  Overall a very good movie, not a true gangster movie or cop movie but in between, that achieved good results.","f513ffac-dcb5-4f41-8985-cd16aec4a8ba",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2014-08-17","","RT","962768994"
