5.0,"Probably my favorite crime drama of all time (yes, even better than The Godfather and Pulp Fiction). From the moment I saw this film I was blow away by the amount of heart and detail. Everything from dialogue, to story, to Jack Nicholson's disturbing performance as a psychotic crime boss. The monologues Nicholson gives are deeply disturbing and engrossing, mostly because of how morally true they are, and this is easily his second best role (right behind The Shining). Now yes, I know this is a remake of the Hong Kong movie Internal Affairs, but why should that take away from the movie? Martin Scorsese really grabs you with his amazing direction. Plain and simple, nobody makes a gangster flick like Scorsese. Plus I can't forget to mention Mark Wahlberg playing the biggest dick bag I've seen in a movie. He's amazing at playing an asshole and I really wish he'd play assholes more often, I mean, after all, he uttered one of the film's most famous lines: ""You're no fuckin' cop!"" Masterly crafted on every level, and with a climax that is so exhilarating I felt like I would explode, The Departed is a film that should be seen by anyone, where gangsters are your thing or not.","7201989c-465f-49f2-8e7b-1f94ad24c44f",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2015-01-31","","RT","953188119"
5.0,"The Departed is not a a tale of morality; it is an amazing and purposely morally-contrived film that allows the audience to get to the core of its characters with a simple look of their actions. Billy's vulnerability is evidenced in his sudden violent outrages, Colin's vile nature is shown in his glances at Madolyn (I mean was he actually sorry? It didn't look like it) and Frank's caring side is exposed in his exchanges with Gwen. You will not know who to root for and might be disappointed by how it all turns out, but could it actually end in any other way?","01cdb18a-8087-4b21-b361-446a7fe003b1",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2015-01-31","","RT","946182912"
