5.0,"How strange that Martin Scorsese, one of the most respected American directors of all time, has only directed one Best Picture winner but I suppose that's the Academy for you, it's always too little, too late. That is not to say that The Departed isn't fantastic it's easy to forget that the story is so complex and meanders so often that it would have been incredibly difficult to cut this together into a coherent crime/drama film. This is deservedly considered one of Scorsese's best later works and whilst it was not my personal favorite film of 2006, that honor would go to Friends with Money, it is a worthy Best Picture Winner.

The film features an ensemble cast including Leonardo DiCaprio, Matt Damon, Mark Wahlberg, Jack Nicholson, Vera Farmiga, Alec Baldwin and Martin Sheen, each cast in roles that fit them like a glove. DiCaprio plays Billy, a cop who is forced to go undercover in the Irish Mob and his adversary Damon, becomes a mole in the Special Investigations Unit. Both men work under Mob boss Frank Costello, Nicholson being very Nicholson-y, who may not be all he seems and they both enter into a romantic relationship with Farmiga's Dr. Madolyn. Supporting players within the police force, Sheen and Wahlberg, attempt to aid DiCaprio in his mission whilst struggling to find the mole in their unit.

The film is a remake of Infernal Affairs (2002), which I confess to not having seen, and the twisty plotline seems like something taken straight out of a Hong Kong crime thriller. This influence is clear throughout as the cinematography echoes that of Cheng Siu-keung in Breaking News (2004) and Fight Back To School (1991), the pure black values and scatological shots of the cityscape create an environment of thick tension. The look of the film is gorgeous as you would expect as Scorsese's camera captures the environment of South Boston, not the most traditionally beautiful area in the world, and finds the special nooks and crannies that possess beauty and the gritty toughness necessary to define the area. Michael Ballhaus, who produced his best work in The Age of Innocence (1993), is on fine form here and the extra effort taken to find beauty in unlikely places enhances the film in a way that helps to drag the film out of being a standard film of this genre.

The William Monahan screenplay is also special in that it adds extra pieces of characterization that allow the audience to emotionally connect to figures who are otherwise carrying out objectionable actions. The date scene between Farmiga and Damon in particular features writing that hints at Damon's sleaziness, ""So now you're gonna fuck with me?"", whilst making it clear what would make him attractive to Farmiga. Similarly to Before the Devil Knows You're Dead (2007) the rivalry is dramatic without feeling pained and the motivations of each character are similar but crucially different. Another deserving Academy Award win came for this screenplay and it is disappointing that Monahan's future screenplays were mostly duds with Sin City: A Dame to Kill For (2014) and The Gambler (2014) being particularly awful.

Wahlberg gives his greatest performance, yes I think this beats his turn as a murderous porn star in Boogie Nights (1997), as a cop with a giant chip on his shoulder who ultimately gets the job done. No, Wahlberg is not a great actor but he can be well-used when playing someone striving to be the best who does not have the skills required. Damon is equally brilliant as a scarily manipulative charmer, similarly to The Talented Mr. Ripley (1999), and he injects his character with a ï¿ 1/2 1/2~creep factor' that plays on his reputation as a ï¿ 1/2 1/2~nice guy' movie star. Despite essentially playing himself Nicholson is darn effective as a two-faced Mob boss and Sheen is dependable as a man with a strong moral center pushed to his limits in dire circumstances. DiCaprio, the star of the film, is largely overshadowed by Damon and Wahlberg, but we still root for him as he chases Farmiga and attempts to maintain his dignity whilst losing so much of himself to his mission.

This is one of his most popular films, it made $291.5 million, at the box office and is sure to be a film that will be passed down from one generation to the next therefore I don't need to say that it has mass appeal. The film does not reach the heights of Scorsese's greatest films but it is still his eighth best and better than most modern crime thrillers and certainly an enjoyable watch that produced a deserving Best Director win for Scorsese and was the right choice for Best Picture especially when considering it's weak fellow nominees.","fefd890f-3e1b-4260-bc65-2e3650dfe360",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2019-04-19","","RT","977990772"
