4.5,"For some bizarre reason the 2000's ended up being a decade in which the films released weren't always properly met with the appropriate praise or concern, (look no further than the 2004 disaster ""Crash"" winning Best Picture at the Oscars to see my point); accordingly, I've been consciously trying to re-visit & interrogate many of the films that have been some of the most memorable movie-going experiences of my life in order to see how many films from the 2000's were ""imposters"" — so to speak. It's been 13 years since I first saw The Departed in theaters, and I think almost 95% of film critics have already decided they won't praise any Scorsese film made after Goodfellas as an ""instant classic"" ever again out of some weird pretension or whatever. That being said, The Departed holds up just as well as Goodfellas does, and in many ways BETTER than Casino. (Yeah I said it, Sharon Stone & James Woods just aren't able to pass as anything but dead weight these days, however The Departed boasts an ensemble cast or such ridiculous talent that there isn't a single talking role in the whole film that was anything less than stellar.) ","6f2ebc85-aeb7-4e19-8247-98f3b1dedf06",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2019-09-28","","RT","1134597b-1547-4c60-98f5-f475d7c87a0d"
4.5,"Possibly the best ensemble cast in a Scorsese movie ever. The contrast is set up so greatly and equal in a sense between the mob and the police force - giving pretty equal screen time between Damon and DiCaprio. So many twists and turns and dead bodies in the wake of a different kind of Boston Massacre. Finally giving Scorsese his best director Oscar, it was well deserved.","f5dedff3-3418-4c43-9fe8-b81be9126aeb",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2019-09-30","","RT","978112576"
