4.5,"Not a while ago, I did a Martin Scorcese marathon, that is watching one of his films every day, and the one thing I observed is that Scorcese makes one masterpiece every decade. 2006's The Departed is destined to be one of those opuses, one about betrayal and corruption in the Boston Police Department and the Irish Mob. Frank Costello (Jack Nicholson) hires Colin (Matt Damon) to be a mole in the police department while Billy (Leonardo DiCaprio) is assigned as an undercover cop weaving through Frank's men. Eventually, the two rats collide, discover each other's identities and they find out Frank has ties with the FBI. Another thing I took note of during the Scorcese binge is his frequent use of Catholic symbolism to establish either a sign of spiritual dominance, family tradition, or redemption. In The Departed, set in Boston, the city with the most catholic families and churches in the United States, the Catholic ideology links to the mafioso. This dynamic motif alluded to Scorcese's previous films Goodfellas and Mean Streets. The final trope I discovered in his filmography is his homage and tribute to other films. The foreshadowing of death is symbolized as the letter ‘X,' which is a direct reference to Howard Hawks's Scarface. Moral of the story, go see The Departed. 

(4 ½ Mark Wahlberg blows to the head out of 5)","411424e1-5722-46bb-803a-9912abfaa75c",False,False,False,True,4.5,"2020-07-23","","RT","909109136"
