3.0,"I am thoroughly entertained by this movie which also happens to be a terrible, awful film. The writing, except for Mark Wahlberg and Alec Baldwin, is dreadful. Marty does not know how to write for women, and here he shows himself to be one of the worst directors in that realm. Farmiga's character is one of the stupidest people I have ever encountered, and an awful therapist to boot. Nobody practices this way. Every line of hers makes the viewer dumber. Jack Nicholson delivers one of his worst performances since he chewed all the scenery in The Shining. His inconsistent intelligence is either owing to the awful script, or to Jack's insistence on ad-libbing. The only interesting thing in the film is the idea that maybe he's a really good draftsman, but we don't get a clear view of the obscene drawing he gives to the costumed catholic actors. DiCaprio couldn't act his way out of a paper bag, and nobody is there to tell him which end is open. Haven't seen such an unconvincing addict since the theater department at my high school. Nicholson's girlfriend has a different bad accent everytime she comes on screen, like she's trying out to be the lost member of the Kennedy, Bouvier, and Hepburn families. The plot devices are less complex than an episode of The Bloodhound Gang, and none of the scenes of the criming add up. Not to mention the insanity of having a whole film revolve around microprocessors. Opening scenes in the deli were shot in Greenpoint, Brooklyn. President Bartlett was thrown off a rooftop in DUMBO, Brooklyn. Funeral scenes shot at Greenwood, in Brooklyn. Costigan got his period at a bar in Brooklyn. The skin flick is in a theater in Brooklyn. Only convincing performances delivered by locals. A very bad no good performance by Damon puts it over the top. And whenever this comes on the television, I will watch every minute. This movie sucks ass and it's marvelous. Garbage. Three stars.","3b560e07-425f-4a88-968e-c626190a53bf",False,False,False,True,3.0,"20