5.0,"Let the rats scurry.

Crossed encounters and rampant corruption lead director Martin Scorsese's popular crime thriller The Departed (2006). Scorsese weaves an intricate web of deception like the characters that are plants in The Departed with shots set up to distract you away from suspicious characters. It's no wonder why everyone who watches The Departed walks away from it blown away as the twists and reveals continue to astonish as Scorsese shoots and kills his way through his incredible cast.

Leonardo DiCaprio gives the greatest performance here as an honest cop named Billy Costigan Jr., who truly wants to change Boston to have less crime. In all his decency, he goes undercover and DiCaprio's face when witnessing mob murders is priceless. You see all his shock, horror, and pain as he's conflicted on what to do in a basically helpless position for over a year. DiCaprio proves he can play a tough hero with a sensitive kindness and warm empathy. Vera Farmiga has real romantic chemistry with DiCaprio, understandably. She's really breathtakingly gorgeous in The Departed as this foolish shrink Dr. Madolyn Madden, blind to her love's treachery. You feel all her frustrations and see where she's missing the mark of her characterizations of these various nefarious men.

Matt Damon is a true blue scumbag rat Staff Sergeant Colin Sullivan  in The Departed. You see how this little prick is influenced by Jack Nicholson's Irish mob boss Frank Costello through charisma and money. Damon plays his crooked cop with a nervousness that feels so real as he's questioned and tries to undermine the investigations into Nicholson. Meanwhile, Nicholson is chewing the scenery with his cruel and menacing jerk crime lord, who threatens and tosses aside his own men like they are nothing. Nicholson's crude and abrupt jokes are so shocking and entertaining as you can tell Nicholson is having a blast insulting every character and running the show.

Mark Wahlberg is surprisingly effective as this awful Boston Staff Sergeant Sean Dignam with his crude remarks and endless belittling comments with Wahlberg's realistic bluntness and mean spirited command. Martin Sheen is interesting as the ineffective head of the undercover unit Captain Oliver Charles Queenan with an earnest and thoughtful performance. Ray Winstone is mighty and fearsome as Nicholson's evil henchman and hitman Arnold ""Mr. Frenchy"" French. Anthony Anderson is here as the funny and stupid cop on the force Trooper Brown. Alec Baldwin is excellent as the powerful head of Damon's detective group Captain George Ellerby with his funny offhand remarks and fierce delivery.

Brad Pitt was right to produce The Departed. Writers William Monahan, Alan Mak, Felix Chong, and Man-Keung create this perilous crime narrative that judges all rats and asks the audience is the crime and deception in these professions worth the risk. Editor Thelma Schoonmaker effortlessly cuts from cop murders and gang killings with her classic slick style. The Departed is a massive 151 minutes and feels half that length to me. It's so fast paced as it blasts through tense conversation after conversation wherein anyone can be killed. 

Cinematographer Michael Ballhaus finds all these fun angles to shoot scenes, so you can only focus on the characters in the frame, while you're forgetting about all the killers just outside the frame. His beautiful shots make every encounter, dialogue scene, or chase as frightening as possible because anyone can die. Composer Howard Shore creates very tense scenes with his striking music. I liked Scorsese's very up front soundtrack that bursts into the scenes like a police raid. Sandy Powell's costumes look like real detective and cop uniforms, while the crooks all have this realistic casual appearance.

In all, The Departed remains a fantastic film from the cinema master Martin Scorsese. Scorsese's fearless and perceptive direction lets you pay attention to only what Marty wants you to see and The Departed is a great trick.","6c94d7b5-e401-42bd-9859-a55d1b5258a4",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2021-12-10","matthew d","RT","d755d5eb-f128-4600-b73a-ace69c2f7808"
