4.5,"I think this films sometimes falls into that strange category of films that are critically underrated because of their success. Of course, this was Scorsese's long overdue first Best Picture Oscar; and whilst it's not Raging Bull, there's still so much to enjoy here. The parallelism between various sets of characters is brilliantly established; I loved on this rewatch noticing the many camera pivots around the 180-degree line emphasising shifting perspectives and loyalties. The performances (even Jack Nicholson is reigned in a little) and technical craft are near faultless, and Scorsese guides us through a complex plot with elegantly constructed ease. It's not perfect - we really do need more than one female character with agency and significance, and it does lack something of a sense of place. It may not have the moral or intellectual depth of some of his work, but it still leaves you with that giddy, satisfied feeling of being in the hands of master story-teller in a film of such propulsive momentum that you barely notice the long running time. And that is not a small thing.","dc243005-bd75-4be2-ab77-184c0e6d48b1",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2022-02-27","","RT","35cfdf5d-b9dd-40f3-b358-c10d277df220"
