5.0,"One of the greatest final shots to a film ever!

What do you get when you include the cops in your usual Martin Scorsese gangster drama flicks? You get The Departed. Now, the plot of the film is pretty simple, there are two guys who've been planted into the police force and into an irish mob and both of them are working for the other side, that is, they are rats. This playing the part of rats leads to a film that is so amazing, you sometimes question yourself as to why is this film so good to watch?

First off all, right of the bat, the performances in the film are all flawless. Not even a single loose thread. Leonardo, Matt, Jack (The Powerhouse). Alec, Mark (he's probably my favourite among them all) everyone is so good it's mind-boggling. Leonardo plays the most troubled character in the film and he displays every emotion that is needed masterfully, Matt Damon plays the character who has to act like he's a good guy but in actuality isn't and he does it with skill, I always had a smug when I saw him on-screen because of the steps he took to hide his identity. Jack Nicholson though, brings a powerhouse performance here. I am actually surprised why he didn't win an oscar for this because this here is a definitive performance. The only scene necessary for the academy to give him an award is when he imitates a ""gnawing, cheese-eating, f**king rat"". Mark Wahlberg is probably my favourite amongst this entire roster of talent because his character's just got this style and charisma which I instantly grew a liking towards. You don't wanna get into a fight with or disobey orders from this guy because he will fuck you up.

The editing in this film is something I really liked. Being a video editor myself, I would actually study this film several times to understand how to tell a story this complicated with such ease to an audience. The story arcs of both the leads are shown with the help of cross cuts and intercuts which really helps the audience to see how both of them are on similar pathways and what each of them is feeling at certain moments is just brilliant.

The score provided for this film and the music that has been used in certain sections is also something that works wonders. The particular use of Shipping Up To Boston by the Dropkick Murphys in this film is just brilliant. The song is literally on loop right now as I'm writing this.

Overall, this film is just a blast to watch because of the story and its characters. And ofcourse, there are several of those trademark Martin Scorsese Unexpected moments sprinkled here and there throughout the film which take the intensity of the film up a notch.

To finish I'd like to say that the genius of this film is that you know both sides of the story and yet you just feel that tension getting cranked up with each passing minute.","c147185a-9024-46b1-903d-41a69a4f8b35",False,False,False,True,5.0,"2023-03-11","Sarthak B","RT","b30b1665-cfab-4aef-ba04-1ec012526b41"
